/* combos-work.js — classic script; assigns to window.TD. No ES modules, so file:// works.
   What two cards say together in a reading about work, career and money. For now it covers every pair that
   includes a Major Arcana card: Major with Major (231) and Major with each minor suit (4 x 308), and
   every pair of two cards from the same suit (4 x 91); 1,827 pairs, keyed
   "idA|idB" in deck order like combos.js (see TD.pairKey in js/spreads.js). Pairs of two minor cards
   from different suits show the general reading from combos.js, labelled "General reading".
   Written fresh for this demo; a live site edits or replaces them. Loaded only when a reading needs it. */
window.TD = window.TD || {};
window.TD.COMBOS_WORK = {"major-00|major-01":"Newness and know-how are lined up on the same desk. Volunteer for the project nobody has done before, since you have the tools to figure it out. Ask for a small trial run and show what you built instead of describing it. A working sample persuades faster than a long explanation.",
"major-00|major-02":"A new direction may be forming in you before you can defend it in a meeting. Keep your impressions to yourself for a week and jot them down, then compare them with the facts. Do not announce a switch until you can explain it in one clear sentence to a skeptic.",
"major-00|major-03":"A fresh venture needs nourishment as much as ambition. Look at how you feed the new effort: sleep, decent meals, a workspace you like, colleagues who are kind. Build those in from week one. Work that comes from a cared-for person tends to last longer than work fueled by adrenaline.",
"major-00|major-04":"A bold idea meets a boss, a deadline or a set of limits, and the meeting is useful. Bring your pitch in a form that respects what is already decided: scope, timeline, who approves what. Ask about the frame before you present the picture. Ideas that arrive with structure get taken seriously.",
"major-00|major-05":"Someone experienced holds the map to a place you have never worked. Sign up for the training, take the veteran to lunch and learn the unwritten rules before you bend any. Once you know how things are done and why, your fresh angle will be welcomed as a contribution, not an interruption.",
"major-00|major-06":"A crossroads is opening, and it is less about which role sounds grander than which one fits who you are becoming. Write down what you want from work in plain terms, then hold each option against the list. Pay attention to colleagues who share your values, since that match matters daily.",
"major-00|major-07":"Raw enthusiasm has been handed a destination. Choose one goal for the next quarter, name the first milestone and trim the projects that pull you sideways. Energy is an asset only when it points somewhere. Tell a colleague your target so someone can notice when you drift.",
"major-00|major-08":"Being new is uncomfortable, and quiet courage helps more than a loud performance. When you make a beginner's mistake, own it briefly and fix it. Patience with your learning curve, and with colleagues who are slow to trust you, builds a reputation faster than trying to prove yourself in month one.",
"major-00|major-09":"A career leap looks different once you have thought about it alone with a notebook. Set aside a few evenings to ask what you want your days to feel like, and write plain answers. Talk to a trusted mentor afterward, once you know your own mind, and then choose.",
"major-00|major-10":"A reshuffle, a shift in circumstances or an unexpected opening may arrive on a schedule that is not yours. Keep your skills and contacts fresh so you can say yes quickly. Avoid pinning all your hopes on one outcome, and treat this as one turn of a longer cycle.",
"major-00|major-11":"A fresh start comes with fine print, and reading it now spares you frustration later. Ask about terms, expectations and how performance is judged before you commit. Treat colleagues evenly, keep your promises and write down what was agreed. A beginning built on clear, fair terms holds up when things get busy.",
"major-00|major-12":"You are eager to move, and the situation asks you to wait and look at it differently. Pause a pending decision for a few days and describe the problem as a customer, boss or rival would. A new angle may turn a leap into a smarter step, or reveal a better door.",
"major-00|major-13":"A role, routine or way of picturing your career has run its course, and the space it leaves is workable. Clear your desk, refresh your materials and say a proper goodbye to the old identity. Then try one small experiment in a new direction, and allow yourself to be a beginner again.",
"major-00|major-14":"Enthusiasm is more useful once it is measured out. Introduce your fresh approach in small portions alongside what already works, and watch how colleagues respond before adding more. A new role takes shape over weeks, not days, so find a working rhythm you can actually sustain.",
"major-00|major-15":"Something keeps you tied to a job, routine or belief about your worth, and a fresh option is asking you to test that hold. List what genuinely keeps you where you are, then sort each item into real obligation or habit. Take one small step that loosens the least essential tie.",
"major-00|major-16":"A surprise, such as a reorganization, a lost project or a sudden change of plan, can sting and free you at once. Take a day to absorb it, then list the skills and relationships that are still yours. Begin from what is real and be willing to build differently this time.",
"major-00|major-17":"Renewed hope makes a fresh professional start feel possible, even if recent months were discouraging. Aim at a modest, honest goal you can share, such as learning a skill or offering help where it is needed. Steady, generous effort builds a reputation more surely than a flashy announcement.",
"major-00|major-18":"The path ahead is not fully visible, so gather more information before committing. Ask colleagues plain questions, read the details of any offer twice and hold off on irreversible moves until the picture sharpens. Your instinct may be right, but for now it is a hunch that deserves checking.",
"major-00|major-19":"Your enthusiasm is contagious and the timing is good. Share your new project openly and let colleagues see how much you enjoy it. Ask for feedback while the mood is bright. Good work is easier to do when people can see you like it, so do not hide the pleasure.",
"major-00|major-20":"Your work history is calling for an honest review so you can answer a new opportunity from a stronger place. Write down what you have done well, what you have outgrown and what you still want to try. Then apply, ask or pitch for the role that fits who you are now.",
"major-00|major-21":"A chapter of your work has come to a satisfying end, and that is what frees you to start something else. Close out loose tasks, thank the people who helped and note what you achieved. Then, with your record in hand, open a new project or role, knowing you are not starting from zero.",
"major-00|wands-01":"An idea has caught fire and the only thing missing is a first move. Put twenty focused minutes into it today, whether that is an outline, a call or a rough prototype. Enthusiasm fades if it sits, and a tiny visible start gives colleagues something concrete to respond to.",
"major-00|wands-02":"You have outgrown your current corner and can already see a bigger field. Draw up two or three possible routes on paper, note what each would require and choose which to test first. Being a beginner in a larger arena is fine as long as you read the map before setting out.",
"major-00|wands-03":"Your idea is out in the world, and you may be tempted to keep tinkering while you wait. Use the gap to learn something adjacent, tidy your records and prepare answers to the questions people will likely ask. Send one polite follow-up if enough time has passed, then get back to other work.",
"major-00|wands-04":"A moment of stable footing invites you to enjoy what has been built before heading somewhere new. Mark the finished project with your team, even briefly, and mention what you would like to try next. Goodwill earned around a shared table often turns into support for the next venture.",
"major-00|wands-05":"Crowded opinions and competing ideas surround your fresh proposal. Treat the noise as a free test of it: note which objections come up more than once and adjust for those. Stay curious rather than defensive, and remember that lively disagreement usually means people care about the result.",
"major-00|wands-06":"Your early efforts are being noticed, even though you are still learning. Accept the praise, write down what earned it and keep the note for days when confidence dips. Let a manager or peer know what you would like to take on next, since visibility now can open doors.",
"major-00|wands-07":"A new path draws critics, and you are asked to choose your stand. Pick the two or three points that truly matter and defend those with facts and a calm tone. Let smaller objections go, since you cannot win every debate. Your persistence will be more convincing than your volume.",
"major-00|wands-08":"News, replies and opportunities may all arrive at once. Keep a short list of your top priorities so that quick movement does not scatter you. Answer time-sensitive messages first, and take a moment before saying yes to anything big. Speed serves you best when you still know where you are headed.",
"major-00|wands-09":"You are worn from a long effort and yet ready for a fresh attempt. Rest properly before making big commitments, and start the new thing with modest expectations. Keep the protections you have earned, such as clear limits on your time. Still being here is proof of stamina, and it counts.",
"major-00|wands-10":"A new opportunity has arrived while your arms are already full. Before accepting it, list your commitments and decide which one you can hand off, postpone or decline this week. Ask a colleague for help with one specific task. You will do better work on the new thing once you carry less.",
"major-00|wands-11":"A new interest may point toward a career shift, and it deserves a low-stakes try. Take a short course, build a rough sample or shadow someone for a day before deciding what it means. Your enthusiasm is a resource, and a colleague or newcomer with a similar spark may offer useful company.",
"major-00|wands-12":"Fast energy is available, and so is the risk of running past the details. Channel it into the task that matters most today, and slow down enough to read the brief. Tell your team what you are aiming at so nobody is surprised. A short pause before you send is a cheap way to avoid repair work.",
"major-00|wands-13":"You can be new and still carry yourself with authority. Speak up in meetings, share your enthusiasm and be clear about what you offer. Colleagues respond to someone who is warm and sure. If a confident mentor is nearby, ask for a candid opinion of your plan, and take it seriously.",
"major-00|wands-14":"A vision has found someone with the nerve to lead it, and that someone could be you. State the goal in one sentence, invite others to help with the details and take responsibility for the direction. Aim to be the leader people would choose to follow, giving credit freely and admitting errors early.",
"major-00|cups-01":"A satisfying offer may be on the way, such as a project you care about, a kind colleague or a role that feeds you emotionally. Notice what excites your heart as well as your resume, and let that guide your response. Work you love tends to bring better energy to everyone involved.",
"major-00|cups-02":"A partnership is forming or renewing, with each side offering something. Talk openly about what each of you wants and what each will do, and agree to it in writing. Collaboration works best when both people bring something and feel heard, so ask what your partner needs from you as well.",
"major-00|cups-03":"A team that likes one another gives your new idea a friendly reception. Celebrate small wins together and include newcomers. Bring a fresh proposal to a casual gathering first, where people are relaxed, before taking it to a formal meeting. Shared enjoyment builds the goodwill that carries you through busy weeks.",
"major-00|cups-04":"Restlessness at work may cause you to overlook a modest but real chance. Before you say no, ask what your curiosity would say about it. If your job feels flat, look for one new responsibility or skill that would make it interesting again, and give that a real try before deciding anything.",
"major-00|cups-05":"A setback still stings, yet other options remain standing behind you. Give yourself a fixed time to feel bad about a lost project or missed chance, then list what you still have: skills, contacts, ideas. Start something small from that list and let progress rebuild your confidence.",
"major-00|cups-06":"Something from your past, such as an old skill, a former colleague or a childhood interest, carries a clue to your next step. Reach out to someone you once worked well with, or revisit a project you loved. Familiarity can offer a warm place to begin something new.",
"major-00|cups-07":"Plenty of exciting options are on the table, but few are solid yet. Pick the one whose first practical step you can name, and try that before pursuing the others. Ask what each idea would demand week to week. The ones that survive that question are worth a real experiment.",
"major-00|cups-08":"A quiet sense that this is no longer enough has been growing. Rather than acting on it in one dramatic move, spend a month exploring what you would move toward: talk to people, take a class, look around. Leaving with a destination in mind, and on good terms, serves you better than leaving in a fog.",
"major-00|cups-09":"Some satisfaction with your work is already present, and a fresh wish is stirring on top of it. List what feels good in your role, since that is your foundation. Then tell your manager or a friend what you would like to add next, and treat it as a new chapter, not an escape.",
"major-00|cups-10":"You are not starting from nothing, since a community stands behind you. Ask friends, family or a former team for introductions and honest advice. Choose the path that lets you keep the relationships that matter. Work that fits the life you want is worth more than work that wins praise while draining you.",
"major-00|cups-11":"A creative or kind-hearted idea is asking for a hearing. Follow it for an afternoon to see whether it holds up, then share the rough version with a friendly colleague. Take feedback with an open mind, since the idea is still tender. A sensitive newcomer or a younger voice may offer a helpful view.",
"major-00|cups-12":"An attractive proposal has come in, and its charm may outshine its details. Enjoy it, then ask what the day-to-day duties, timeline and expectations would be. Get the terms in writing and give yourself a day before answering. A good offer holds up under plain questions, and a poor one usually does not.",
"major-00|cups-13":"Emotional intelligence is a real professional asset right now. Listen for what colleagues or clients are not saying, and use it to shape your new approach. Set a limit on how much of other people's feeling you carry home. A caring, intuitive presence at work, yours or someone else's, can smooth a fresh start.",
"major-00|cups-14":"A calm, kind approach helps you start well. Keep your composure when others are stirred up, and let your decisions be shaped by care as well as facts. If a seasoned colleague seems level-headed, ask how they handle pressure. Being both compassionate and clear is a good foundation for a new role or team.",
"major-00|swords-01":"A clear idea or hard truth arrives, and it points to a new beginning. Write down the one sentence you have been avoiding about your situation, then act on it in a small way. Communicate plainly in emails and meetings. Clarity is your advantage now, so use it with courtesy.",
"major-00|swords-02":"You may be stuck between two options and shielding yourself from information that would help. List the facts you have been avoiding, such as details of an offer or how your current role really feels, and look at them. A decision made with open eyes, even a small one, ends the stalemate.",
"major-00|swords-03":"A workplace disappointment, such as criticism, a lost role or a broken promise, is still tender. Do not act as if it did not hurt. Talk it through with someone you trust, then choose one small forward step. You can begin again while still feeling the sting, and honesty about it speeds recovery.",
"major-00|swords-04":"A period of rest is what turns a restless urge into a real start. Take a few days off from big decisions, and if possible from the screens that feed them. Recover, then reassess your idea with a clearer head. If it still pulls at you afterward, you can trust it more.",
"major-00|swords-05":"A win that leaves others resentful is not really a win. If you are competing with a colleague, ask whether the fight deserves the fresh energy you have. Choose your battles, share credit and keep your integrity intact. Walking away from a petty conflict can be your strongest first move in a new role.",
"major-00|swords-06":"You are moving from a stormy situation toward a quieter one, and the crossing is gradual. Leave behind what belongs to the old problem, and keep the skills you gained. Give your new environment time before you judge it. Peace at work usually arrives in stages, through small changes in routine and in how you speak.",
"major-00|swords-07":"Being clever is useful, and being quietly sneaky is not. Before you go around a rule or a colleague, ask whether you could explain the plan aloud without embarrassment. Take credit where it is due, and be careful with anyone who avoids straight answers. Trust at work is slow to build and quick to break.",
"major-00|swords-08":"A story about why you cannot move may be more confining than your actual circumstances. Write down each reason, then test the weakest one with a small step, like asking a question or applying for something. Many walls at work turn out to be assumptions, and you may find more room than you expected.",
"major-00|swords-09":"Nighttime worry about a new step often exaggerates the risk. Write each fear down and decide in daylight which ones are actual problems that need a plan. Talk to a mentor or a trusted colleague about the biggest one. A restless mind is often asking for a clear next step, not a catastrophe.",
"major-00|swords-10":"A painful work chapter, such as a project that failed or a role that ended, is over, and that is also a release. Take a day to let the ending register, then gather what you learned. You are starting from a cleared floor, so begin with one small task and let momentum return.",
"major-00|swords-11":"Bright, alert curiosity is your best tool as a beginner. Ask the questions others are shy about, and notice who holds influence and who does the work. Take notes and hold off on announcing plans until you know the landscape. A sharp-eyed newcomer, or your own inner student, sees more than veterans expect.",
"major-00|swords-12":"A direct, fast approach cuts through delays, and it can bruise colleagues. Say what needs to be said, then check in on how it landed. Read each message twice before sending it. If someone near you moves fast and speaks bluntly, ask them for specifics and try not to take their tone personally.",
"major-00|swords-13":"You can see what is real and what is wishful in your plans. Ask for an honest assessment from a colleague who will not flatter you, then act on what makes sense. Keep your communication direct and courteous. Someone with clear sight, whether a mentor or your own inner critic, can save you wasted effort.",
"major-00|swords-14":"Reason and principle are guiding a fresh start, and you may be asked to explain your choices. Prepare a clear rationale in plain words, and be ready for tough questions. Base decisions on evidence and fairness rather than mood. An authority figure, or your own inner judge, is weighing your plan, so answer thoughtfully.",
"major-00|pentacles-01":"A genuine opening is here, with real material to build on. Say yes to what is concrete, such as a defined role, a real project or a proper trial. Then handle it with care and follow through on small promises. Solid beginnings grow best when you attend to them from day one.",
"major-00|pentacles-02":"Several demands are competing for your attention, and a new one has joined them. Make a simple weekly plan with clear blocks of time for each. Be willing to adjust it as things change, and tell colleagues what you can and cannot take on. Staying flexible, and a little playful, keeps juggling from becoming strain.",
"major-00|pentacles-03":"Skilled colleagues can shape your fresh effort into something durable. Share an early draft and ask for specific, constructive critique from people who know the craft. Be open to correction, and credit those who help. Good teamwork and careful workmanship take longer, and they give you a result you can stand behind.",
"major-00|pentacles-04":"The wish for security and the wish for a fresh start are tugging at each other. Sort out what you truly need to keep steady and what you are clutching from habit. You may be able to step forward while keeping a firm base. Be generous with your time and knowledge, and see what comes back.",
"major-00|pentacles-05":"A lean stretch has left you feeling shut out, and a new start can seem out of reach. Look for the lit window nearby: a colleague, a mentor or a professional network that could help. Ask for one specific piece of help this week. Reaching out is a practical step, and nothing to be ashamed of.",
"major-00|pentacles-06":"Help flows both ways in a good working relationship. Accept mentoring from someone more experienced, and pass on what you know to someone newer. Watch whether the give and take in your team feels balanced, and mention it early if it does not. A fair exchange of effort and credit builds lasting trust.",
"major-00|pentacles-07":"A new effort needs a fair season before you judge it. Take a moment to check what you have planted and what it needs, then adjust your tending, not the whole plan. Keep a record of small signs of progress. Patience with honest assessment beats both constant tinkering and total neglect.",
"major-00|pentacles-08":"The first day is exciting, and the hundredth is where skill lives. Choose one small, repeatable block of work on what you are learning and keep to it. Track your improvement over weeks and welcome feedback. Enjoy being a beginner, since it is where every good practitioner started.",
"major-00|pentacles-09":"Your own efforts have created a comfortable position, and a fresh interest can grow alongside it without threatening it. Look at what you have built through your own skill and discipline, then pursue a new project at a pace you can enjoy. Independence at work is a strength when it comes with curiosity.",
"major-00|pentacles-10":"A fresh step taken inside an established structure, such as a family business, a long-standing employer or a tested profession, has firm footing. Ask what your predecessors built that you can rely on, and what you might add. Respect the history while bringing your own touch.",
"major-00|pentacles-11":"Being new is an advantage when you treat it as an apprenticeship. Sign up for the course, shadow the person who does the job well, or take the small paid gig that teaches you the trade. Keep notes on what you learn each week. Curiosity plus a real first attempt builds a skill people will pay for.",
"major-00|pentacles-12":"Excitement about a new direction needs a slow, reliable engine behind it. Break the idea into dull weekly tasks and do them on schedule, even when the thrill fades. Track hours and results in a simple log. Nobody notices steady progress at first, and later it is the thing that gets you trusted with more.",
"major-00|pentacles-13":"A capable, nurturing approach makes a home for your new plan. Take care of the basics first: a workable schedule, a tidy space, good habits and supportive colleagues. Being resourceful and generous with your time gets people on your side. A practical mentor nearby, or your own organized side, can turn an idea into a routine.",
"major-00|pentacles-14":"Experience and reliability share the road with your fresh start. Ask a seasoned person how they handled their early years and what they would do differently. Take a grounded step in that direction instead of aiming for a huge result at once. Mastery of a craft grows through patient, generous work that others can depend on.",
"major-01|major-02":"Skill and instinct are teamed up. Do the research, then check it against your gut sense about the people and the project. If the facts say yes and the room feels off, look further. Act on your read of the situation, and use the tools you have, not every tool available.",
"major-01|major-03":"A project benefits when skill is combined with patient care. Choose work that grows with steady attention and give it regular time each day. Look after your energy, your team and your workspace, since a nurtured effort tends to bear more fruit than a forced one. Let your craft develop at a natural pace.",
"major-01|major-04":"Talent needs a framework to produce results. Set a schedule, define clear goals and agree on who decides what. Use your inventive streak inside those lines instead of arguing with them. Ask a person with authority to hold you to your timeline, and deliver something finished, not just clever.",
"major-01|major-05":"Learn the established method before you improve on it. Find a mentor, a recognized course or a respected practitioner and study how the craft is done properly. Once you understand why the rules exist, you can bend them with purpose. Respect for tradition makes your original ideas more persuasive to people in charge.",
"major-01|major-06":"Two job paths may both be within reach, and your skills would fit either one. Before you commit, write down what you want your working days to feel like in a year, then pick the role that serves that list. Talent is not the question here. Fit is.",
"major-01|major-07":"Your resources finally have a destination. Choose two or three priorities and put your tools behind them. Tell a colleague your target and hold the reins firmly so scattered demands cannot pull you sideways. Progress comes from steady direction and focus, not from adding more tools.",
"major-01|major-08":"Real mastery is often quiet. Handle a tense colleague or an unruly project with calm, patient skill instead of forceful display. Do the small, skillful thing well and repeat it. A steady presence and a gentle hand tend to win people over, and a reputation for composure lasts longer than one for flash.",
"major-01|major-09":"Take your tools somewhere quiet and ask one honest question about your craft: what do I actually want to make? A stretch of focused, solitary work will show you things a busy calendar hides. Come back when you have an answer, and share it with someone whose opinion you respect.",
"major-01|major-10":"Preparation and chance shake hands. Keep your portfolio updated, your skills sharp and your contacts warm so that a sudden turn finds you ready. Put effort into what you control and let go of the rest. If the timing does not favor you this round, your readiness still belongs to you.",
"major-01|major-11":"Good work is about to be measured fairly. Keep clean records of what you built, what it cost and who helped, because a review, contract or pay conversation will go better with evidence on the table. Skill gets you noticed; being straight about the numbers gets you paid.",
"major-01|major-12":"Set your tools down for a few days and look at the problem from the other side. Imagine how a customer, a critic or a coworker sees it, and note what changes. A willing pause often produces a simpler and kinder next move than another burst of effort. Let the delay work for you.",
"major-01|major-13":"A project, role or income stream is finishing, and your abilities are not leaving with it. List the tools, contacts and know-how you are carrying out the door. Some of them belong in the next thing, and some only made sense in the old setup. Sort them before you start again.",
"major-01|major-14":"Steady progress beats a big launch right now. Put your best effort into a routine you can keep for months: a set number of hours, a fixed budget line, one skill practiced weekly. Mixing ambition with patience is slower, but it holds up when the first excitement wears off.",
"major-01|major-15":"Watch where your talent is being spent. A job, client or habit may be using your ability in ways that pay but leave you stuck, or you may be using clever shortcuts that are starting to cost more than they save. Name the trade you are making, then decide if it is still worth it.",
"major-01|major-16":"Something at work may give way without much warning: a plan, a structure, an arrangement that looked solid. Your ability to adapt is the real asset here. Rebuild from what is actually true now, not from the old org chart, and you may find a better fit than the one that fell.",
"major-01|major-17":"A sense that things can improve is joined by the skill to help them along. Put real effort into repair instead of waiting for it, by resuming a paused project, mending a working relationship or tidying a neglected process. Small, careful acts of restoration carry more weight than they appear to.",
"major-01|major-18":"Not every offer or figure is what it seems. You have the skill to act, but details are unclear, so ask for terms in writing and check the numbers twice before you put your name on anything. Hold off on the big move until the picture sharpens a little.",
"major-01|major-19":"Your work is in full light, with energy, warmth and honest pleasure in it. Show it to people who will enjoy it, and do not dress it up with false modesty. Ask for the recognition your results have earned. Being visible and cheerful about your skills tends to bring more of the work you enjoy.",
"major-01|major-20":"Your track record is being looked at, possibly by you. An appraisal, a portfolio refresh or an honest look back at the last few years could show which skills people really rely on. Answer that call by putting those skills forward and letting go of work that no longer reflects you.",
"major-01|major-21":"A long piece of work is ready to be finished and shown. Tie up the loose ends, ship it and let people see the whole thing. Completing this cycle well puts your abilities on record and opens the door to a bigger stage, possibly one that reaches further than your current circle.",
"major-01|wands-01":"A burst of motivation is meeting real competence, which is uncommon. Pick the task you have been circling and finish a first version this week, however plain. Then ask a colleague you trust for a reaction. Something imperfect that exists gives you more to work with than a flawless plan that does not.",
"major-01|wands-02":"You have the tools in hand and a map of where they could take you. Pick one market, client type or role beyond your current patch and write down what it would take to reach it: the skill to sharpen, the person to call, the hours per week. A plan with numbers beats a daydream with a view.",
"major-01|wands-03":"Your work has left the shop and is on its way to others. Rather than reworking what is already sent, use the wait to prepare for what comes back, such as questions, feedback or the next request. Follow up politely once if enough time has passed, and let the process take the time it takes.",
"major-01|wands-04":"You have earned a celebration, so hold one. Invite the people who helped, show what you made and let their pride feed yours. Recognize contributors by name. A shared pause after good effort builds team spirit and restores energy for the next round, so treat it as part of the work.",
"major-01|wands-05":"Meetings with clashing opinions are raw material, and you know how to shape it. Gather the loudest suggestions, pick the two that hold up, and build something small from them by Friday. Frustration turns into a product when one person decides what actually gets made.",
"major-01|wands-06":"Skill that has been practiced quietly is about to be seen. Prepare a short, clear account of what you made and how you made it, so credit lands where it belongs. Share the win with the people who helped, and your reputation will outlast this one moment.",
"major-01|wands-07":"A competitor or a critic is circling your project, and your craft is the best defense. Keep your position by showing the work, not by matching their volume. Choose the one point worth defending this week, prepare your evidence, and let the smaller skirmishes pass.",
"major-01|wands-08":"Momentum is building and you have the tools to ride it. Clear the small blockers today, send the email you have been drafting, and finish what is half done. Fast news is coming, so keep your materials ready and your calendar a little looser than usual.",
"major-01|wands-09":"You are close to the finish and short on energy. Use your resourcefulness to cut, not add: drop the one task that no longer earns its place and protect your best hours for the last stretch. A short, deliberate rest now will keep the project sound.",
"major-01|wands-10":"Because you can do it all, everyone has let you. Write out every task on your plate, keep the three where your skill matters most, and delegate or postpone the rest. Say the new limit out loud to your manager or team, with a plan attached.",
"major-01|wands-11":"A fresh interest is worth turning into something you can show. Give yourself a small experiment: one prototype, one sample, one trial project. Your abilities are stronger than you think, and curiosity gives them a direction. Keep the scope modest so you finish and learn from it.",
"major-01|wands-12":"Your ability and your impatience are running at the same speed. Channel it into one important project rather than five. Check the details before you announce anything, and allow colleagues time to catch up. Fast moves work best when the groundwork is already done.",
"major-01|wands-13":"Confidence and capability are visible in you right now. Lead with a project you love and explain it in a way that makes people want to join. Being warm does not mean being soft: hold your standards and your prices. Colleagues follow someone both generous and definite.",
"major-01|wands-14":"Vision and the skill to carry it out are both at hand. Decide what you are leading toward, explain it in a sentence, and assign the first three tasks. People are ready for direction. Make sure your decisions are backed by real work, not only by charisma.",
"major-01|cups-01":"Enthusiasm for a project has arrived along with the means to start it. Pick the version you care about most and build a rough draft before doubt sets in. Work you love holds your attention longer, so let that guide which offer or task gets your best hours.",
"major-01|cups-02":"A good partnership becomes possible when each side names what it brings. Propose a split of tasks in writing, with who does what and by when. Whether it is a colleague, a client or a business partner, fair terms agreed early save you from resentment later.",
"major-01|cups-03":"Team spirit is a tool you can use on purpose. Organize the small celebration, the lunch or the shared kickoff, and notice how ideas flow afterward. Your skill combined with a group that likes each other gets more done than a lone push. Credit people out loud.",
"major-01|cups-04":"Tools and opportunities sit in front of you while your interest drifts. Look at what you have skipped past: a side task, a course, a colleague's offer. Set a small experiment for this month with a clear finish line. Curiosity often returns once you are making something instead of waiting to feel motivated.",
"major-01|cups-05":"A setback has your attention, yet the resources for a rebuild are still yours. Name what was lost, whether a project, a role or a budget, then list what you kept: skills, contacts, knowledge. Start on a smaller version of the work this week. Mourning and rebuilding can happen side by side.",
"major-01|cups-06":"Something from your past experience is useful now. An old contact, a former boss or a skill you set aside could be the resource this project needs. Reach out with a friendly, specific note. Just be careful not to insist on how things used to be done when the setting has changed.",
"major-01|cups-07":"Ideas are plentiful, and the skill is choosing one and finishing it. Write down every option, then rate each on effort, time and what you would learn. Commit to the top one for thirty days. Splitting attention across five appealing projects means none of them gets the polish you are capable of.",
"major-01|cups-08":"You are outgrowing a role or a project, and you have the skill to change direction on purpose. Update your portfolio, talk to people in the field that draws you, and be clear about what you need next. A planned shift beats a sudden storming off, and it keeps your reputation intact.",
"major-01|cups-09":"A wish about your working life is closer than it feels. You have the skill, and you seem settled enough not to panic. Write down the specific result you want from this year, then take one concrete step toward it. Enjoy the good stretch without letting comfort turn into coasting.",
"major-01|cups-10":"A workplace where people feel they belong is something you can help shape. Use your skills to build a team routine, a process or a shared goal that people care about. Small actions count: a clear welcome for new hires, a fair way of sharing credit. Good work grows best in a place that feels like home.",
"major-01|cups-11":"A creative idea is arriving, along with the know-how to try it. Draft it quickly and show it to a trusted colleague. The Page can be a newer teammate with a fresh view, or your own beginner's excitement. Treat first drafts as gifts, and spend your effort on making the idea usable.",
"major-01|cups-12":"A proposal that sounds exciting is on the table, and you are well placed to test it. Ask for specifics: scope, timeline, who is responsible for what. The Knight may be a persuasive colleague or client, or your own enthusiasm. Keep the charm, add the details, and decide once you can see the plan.",
"major-01|cups-13":"Emotional intelligence is your professional strength right now. You read the room, and you know how to act on what you sense. Use that to steady a tense meeting, mentor someone or handle a client with care. The Queen may be a colleague who does this well. Learn from her, or be her.",
"major-01|cups-14":"Leadership with heart is on offer. You have the skills, and the King adds composure under pressure. Handle a hard conversation with a teammate by being honest and kind in equal measure. If someone like this is your boss, study how they decide. If it is you, lead the way.",
"major-01|swords-01":"A sharp idea meets the tools to build it. Write the goal in one sentence before you start, then use what is already on your desk to make a first version. Clarity about what you are making matters more than having extra materials.",
"major-01|swords-02":"Plenty of talent sits idle while you stall between two options. List what each path needs, pick the one you can begin this week, and treat it as a test rather than a lifelong vow. Skill unused because of indecision helps nobody, including you.",
"major-01|swords-03":"A disappointment such as a turned-down proposal or harsh feedback stings, but your skills are still yours. Let yourself feel it for an afternoon, then turn the criticism into a list of fixes. Your craft is intact and ready to be used again.",
"major-01|swords-04":"Skill without rest turns into sloppy output. Step back from the project, protect a real day off, and let your mind sort the problem quietly. You return sharper, and what you make afterwards will need fewer repairs than what you would have rushed.",
"major-01|swords-05":"Talent used to outmaneuver a colleague earns a short victory and a long reputation cost. Aim your skill at the shared result instead. If a dispute is brewing, offer a fix rather than a rebuttal, and let your results speak for you.",
"major-01|swords-06":"A move toward calmer conditions is possible, and your skills travel with you. That might mean a smoother project, a friendlier team, or a lighter workload. Make a practical plan for the transition, and gather your best work samples before you go.",
"major-01|swords-07":"Strategy is fine, sneaking is not. You may be tempted to work around someone instead of with them, or to take a shortcut that skips credit. Use your resourcefulness in the open, share the plan with the right people, and your standing stays solid.",
"major-01|swords-08":"You feel boxed in, though the tools to get out are on your own table. Write down the rules you believe apply, then ask which ones a manager or a contract really enforces. Often the fence is thinner than it looks, and one request tests it.",
"major-01|swords-09":"Anxiety about a deadline or a review keeps you awake, even though you are skilled enough to handle it. Write the worries down, then beside each one note a small action you can take tomorrow. Doing something practical calms the mind.",
"major-01|swords-10":"A project, role or way of working has finished, and you cannot revive it by force. The good news is that your abilities remain. Review what you built, close it out properly, and start sketching what you would like to make next.",
"major-01|swords-11":"A learner's alertness meets real ability. Ask the pointed question in the meeting, study how the process actually works, then apply it to something small. You get further by observing first and building second, and people notice good questions.",
"major-01|swords-12":"Skill plus speed gets things launched, but blunt delivery can leave colleagues behind. Move quickly on the part only you can do, then spend five minutes telling others what is happening. Momentum helps most when the team is carrying it with you.",
"major-01|swords-13":"Clear judgment and real capability make you the person others ask. Trust your read of the situation, state it without hedging, and back it with a working example. Being direct about what is realistic saves the team from spending effort on wishes.",
"major-01|swords-14":"Authority and know-how make a strong pair for leading a project. Set clear standards, make the decision you have been circling, and explain your reasoning. People follow a plan more readily when they understand the principle behind it and can see you doing the work too.",
"major-01|pentacles-01":"A real opening has appeared, and you have the skill to use it. Choose one project, name what done looks like, and give it your undivided hours before taking on anything else. Focus turns a good chance into something you can point to when it is finished.",
"major-01|pentacles-02":"Several demands are competing, and you can handle them once you stop treating them as equal. Rank the tasks, set a fixed hour for the most important one, and let smaller things wait. Managing the pace is the craft now.",
"major-01|pentacles-03":"Your ability meets a team that values quality. Ask a colleague whose work you respect to look over what you are making, and take their notes seriously. Doing the job properly, with others watching the details, is what puts your name on good results.",
"major-01|pentacles-04":"You know how to make things happen, yet you may be sitting on your best work or your time. Protecting what you have built is reasonable. The next step needs you to show it, share it, and let other people use it.",
"major-01|pentacles-05":"A stretch of scarcity, whether of hours, recognition or steady work, is real, yet you are not helpless in it. List the skills you already have and name three people who could use them. Ask directly. Resourcefulness beats waiting by the door.",
"major-01|pentacles-06":"You can put your skill to use for others, and a fair arrangement is possible. Be clear about what you give and what you expect back, in time, credit and pay. Terms spoken plainly and early save resentment later on.",
"major-01|pentacles-07":"You have been building something, and now it is fair to check the results. Look at what has grown, what has stalled, and where your hours went. Then choose where to put your next efforts. A calm review, not a panicked one, shows the way.",
"major-01|pentacles-08":"Talent gets sharper through repeated hours, and you have both. Pick the one skill that would most improve your work and give it a fixed slot every day. Progress will feel dull for a while, then suddenly show.",
"major-01|pentacles-09":"Your own effort has produced real comfort, and you can be proud of it. Use that footing to choose projects that interest you, not only the safe ones. Independence is a strength, so share what you know when it suits you.",
"major-01|pentacles-10":"Skill applied over years leaves something lasting. Ask what you are making that will still be useful long after today: a process, a team, a reputation. Put steady effort there, and write down what you know so others can carry it on.",
"major-01|pentacles-11":"You are ready to learn a new craft, and you already have a talent to build on. Sign up for the course, ask for the shadowing shift, or start a small practice project. Your first real attempt will teach more than more planning.",
"major-01|pentacles-12":"Consistency is your edge. You have the skill and the follow-through to finish what you started, one dull task at a time. Pace yourself, keep the routine, and let colleagues learn that you deliver. Reliability builds trust faster than any pitch.",
"major-01|pentacles-13":"You can create a workplace where things run smoothly and people feel looked after. Use your ability to organize what the team needs, and put your practical knowledge to work for them. Caring and competence go together. Anticipate what the group needs.",
"major-01|pentacles-14":"Mastery of your material is within reach. You know the craft and how to manage resources, so consider a leadership or mentoring role where your steadiness helps others. Decide slowly, act firmly, and set an example of how good work is done.",
"major-02|major-03":"Your quiet read on a project is worth trusting, and it needs feeding to grow. Give the hunch time and resources: a test run, a notebook of observations, a week of steady effort. Ideas at work often ripen when someone tends them patiently instead of announcing them early.",
"major-02|major-04":"Trust your read of the room, then build a system around it. Perhaps you sense a process is off, or a colleague is holding something back. Write down what you notice, propose one simple rule or checkpoint, and let the structure do the speaking for you.",
"major-02|major-05":"What you sense privately and what the workplace teaches may point in different directions. Learn the official way well enough to know where your instinct genuinely adds something. A mentor or a training course can help, as long as you keep your own judgment in the room.",
"major-02|major-06":"A decision about who or what to commit to is best made from values, not pressure. Sit with the options in silence before you compare pay or titles. Ask which one you would still respect in a year, then tell a trusted colleague your answer out loud.",
"major-02|major-07":"Momentum is available, but your gut is asking for a moment before you press ahead. Check the direction before the speed: is this the goal you actually want? Spend an hour on quiet planning, then commit and move with confidence. Work goes further when the driver knows the route.",
"major-02|major-08":"Stay steady with a tricky person or task and let calm do what force cannot. You sense more than you say, which is useful in a tense meeting. Hold your tone, listen longer than feels natural, and let the quieter approach win people over gradually.",
"major-02|major-09":"Step back from the noise and let a problem come clear. Block time without meetings, ask one honest question, and write down whatever surfaces. A colleague who sees you working more quietly is not necessarily seeing you disengaged. The answer likely comes from you, once you make space.",
"major-02|major-10":"Circumstances at work are turning, and your instinct picked up on it early. Use that head start calmly: quietly update your skills, tidy your records, and stay flexible. You cannot control a reorganization or a market mood, but you can meet it prepared.",
"major-02|major-11":"Facts and intuition need to agree before you act. If something feels off about a contract, a review, or how credit is being shared, check the records and the wording. Trust the hunch enough to ask questions, and trust the paperwork enough to hear the answers.",
"major-02|major-12":"Stalled progress may be a gift for now. While the project sits, turn your view around: what would the client, the newest hire or the skeptic see? Use the delay to gather insight rather than pushing. The next step will make more sense after you have looked properly.",
"major-02|major-13":"A chapter of your work life is closing, and the inner knowing has been there a while. Say it to yourself plainly, then take small tidy steps: finish loose ends, hand off what you can, and leave space before filling it. Endings handled well make new starts possible.",
"major-02|major-14":"The middle of a long project is where quiet judgment matters most. Balance workload, energy and relationships instead of sprinting. Trust your sense of what is enough for one week, and adjust as you go. Steady, measured effort will serve you better than heroics.",
"major-02|major-15":"You suspect a routine or a dynamic has more hold on you than it deserves. Whether it is the comfort of a title, a difficult boss or an unhealthy pace, take your private doubts seriously. Write down what it costs you, then choose one small step toward more freedom.",
"major-02|major-16":"A surprise change at work may land like a fire alarm, yet part of you was half prepared. Do the immediate practical things first: read the messages, ask what is expected, protect your records. Later, reflect on which warning signs you noticed and how to trust them sooner.",
"major-02|major-17":"Renewed confidence is growing after a rough patch, and it does not need an audience. Keep a modest, steady routine that restores your energy and skills. Small, honest progress adds up. Share your plans with people who support you and hold back from those who drain you.",
"major-02|major-18":"Not everything is visible yet, and your unease deserves attention without becoming a story. Collect facts before reacting: ask for the missing details, reread the fine print, and hold off on big commitments. What feels unclear today usually resolves when you keep looking steadily.",
"major-02|major-19":"A hunch you have kept to yourself is ready to be shared, and the room is likely to respond well. Present it clearly, without hedging, and let people see your enthusiasm. Good news at work often arrives when quiet preparation meets an open moment.",
"major-02|major-20":"You have a clear inner sense of whether your current path suits you, and it is asking for a proper look. Review your past year honestly: what you did well, what you avoided. Then respond to the pull you feel and take one concrete step.",
"major-02|major-21":"A big effort is wrapping up, and your quiet observations helped shape it. Finish properly: document what you learned, thank those who helped, and reflect on what you would do differently. Completing well gives you confidence for whatever comes next.",
"major-02|wands-01":"An idea you have kept to yourself is ready for its first step. Trust your read on the situation, then take one small action: draft the outline, make the call, book the meeting. You know more than you have admitted, so start before you feel fully certain.",
"major-02|wands-02":"Your instinct and your planning need to agree. Before you commit to a direction, sit quietly with what you already sense about the options, and then map the wider picture. A decision made from both the gut and the spreadsheet holds up better than either alone.",
"major-02|wands-03":"You have done the groundwork and now comes the wait. Do not fill the silence with noise. Trust what you know about your work, and use these days to prepare quietly for whatever the reply brings. Patience with a clear mind will serve you better than checking your inbox.",
"major-02|wands-04":"After effort, a pause on familiar ground does you good. Let the team or your own mind settle before the next push. Use the quiet to notice what is working and what your instincts say about the next move. Small rituals of closure help a workplace feel stable.",
"major-02|wands-05":"Office friction can bury your best judgment. Step out of the argument and listen for what you already understand about the problem. Then offer one calm point instead of another loud one. Discretion often persuades better than volume, and you may see what others miss.",
"major-02|wands-06":"Recognition is coming, and you may prefer to stay a bit in the background. Accept it graciously, but keep your own counsel about what is next. What you know that others do not is still your edge. Let the win speak while you plan quietly.",
"major-02|wands-07":"Protect your position by saying less. Under pressure from rivals or critics, your quiet knowledge is a strength: do not reveal every card. Make one calm point, stand by it, and let the results do the rest. Trust your read of who can be counted on.",
"major-02|wands-08":"Fast news may arrive, and your first reaction is not always the wisest. Take a breath, then check it against what you know. Move quickly on tasks that are clear, and pause on the ones your instincts question. Quiet judgment inside a busy week is valuable.",
"major-02|wands-09":"Fatigue has made you watchful, and that watchfulness is useful. Trust what you sense about the last stretch and cut anything that drains without paying back. You do not have to prove endurance to everyone. Keep your reserves for the work that truly matters.",
"major-02|wands-10":"Some of what is heavy is unspoken. Sit quietly and sort what is truly yours from what you absorbed by default. Then say what you cannot carry to whoever can help. Your intuition already knows which task should go first, so act on it this week.",
"major-02|wands-11":"A new interest calls, and you sense it matters. Study it privately before announcing it. Read, watch, ask a few questions, and see whether your gut agrees with the excitement. Learning quietly is a strong start, and you can share what you find when it is clearer.",
"major-02|wands-12":"One part of you wants to move now, another wants to wait and watch. Let the quiet part vet the plan before the fast part acts. A short pause to check your read of the situation can prevent a rushed error and still keep your momentum.",
"major-02|wands-13":"Quiet judgment and visible confidence work well together. Speak up with what you have read in the room, and speak plainly. Colleagues respect someone who is warm and also knows more than they say. Use that standing to steer a decision that others have avoided.",
"major-02|wands-14":"Vision needs private clarity behind it. Before setting direction for others, spend time alone with what you truly believe about the goal. Then state it simply and lead. People follow someone whose confidence comes from thought, not noise. Keep some strategy to yourself until timing is right.",
"major-02|cups-01":"An offer may come with a gut feeling that arrives before the facts do. Write down what you sense, then gather the details that confirm or challenge it. Do not rush a reply. A day of quiet reflection before you answer can reveal what you really want from the role.",
"major-02|cups-02":"A working partnership may rest on unspoken understanding, which is a strength until it causes a misunderstanding. Put the important agreements in writing. Notice what a colleague leaves unsaid in meetings, and ask about it privately. Trust built quietly over time is worth protecting with a few clear, shared terms.",
"major-02|cups-03":"The team is cheerful, and you are noticing things they do not. Listen at the office event for what is not being said: tensions, hopes, quiet plans. You can share what you pick up privately, and with tact. Good social awareness makes you someone people trust with sensitive things.",
"major-02|cups-04":"Lack of interest in your current tasks carries information. Instead of pushing through, spend an hour writing down what drains you and what still interests you. Something in your job is asking to change. The answer may be a different project, a talk with your manager or simply a rest. Listen before you act.",
"major-02|cups-05":"A disappointment at work deserves quiet processing before a reaction. Do not fire off the reply or announce your feelings in the meeting. Write it out privately, then decide what remains: skills, relationships and options. The loss is real, and the understanding that comes later will help you plan what to protect next time.",
"major-02|cups-06":"An earlier chapter of your career holds a lesson you have not fully absorbed. Think back to when work felt meaningful and ask what was different: the people, the pace, the purpose. Bring one of those things into your present role in a small way. Past experience can inform without controlling your decisions.",
"major-02|cups-07":"Choices multiply and none look solid, so ask what your quiet instinct says once the noise stops. Take a walk, sleep on it and test each option against a plain question: will this hold up in six months? Do your homework on facts too. Intuition works best when it is checked against reality.",
"major-02|cups-08":"A quiet certainty may be telling you that a role, project or working arrangement is finished for you. Do not act in anger. Spend time listening to the feeling, then plan the change in stages: skills, contacts, timing. Moving on well takes preparation, and your instinct is a good place to begin.",
"major-02|cups-09":"Satisfaction with your work may be an inward thing that others do not fully see. Notice what specifically makes you content: the autonomy, the craft, the people. Protect those things when new demands arrive. Keep your wishes for the next stage private for now, and let them take shape before you share them.",
"major-02|cups-10":"A workplace where you belong needs more than good perks. Ask yourself what kind of culture suits your temperament, and notice where you feel calm and respected. Trust that feeling when weighing a role or a team. Being part of something shared works best when you also keep space for your own private thinking.",
"major-02|cups-11":"A new idea is arriving softly, so give it quiet before you present it. Jot it down, sleep on it, and let it mature. A junior colleague may have an insight worth hearing. Your own early hunch is also worth noting, even if you cannot yet explain the reason for it.",
"major-02|cups-12":"A tempting proposal is coming, and it deserves a quiet look beneath the surface. Ask what the person offering it wants and what is left unsaid. Give yourself a night before you answer. If the offer still feels right after reflection, it probably is. If you feel uneasy, keep asking questions until you know why.",
"major-02|cups-13":"You pick up on what others feel, and that is a professional asset. Use it to notice who is struggling, which client is uneasy or what is really behind a tense email. Verify your impressions with a calm question. The Queen could be a colleague with that gift, or the role you are being asked to play.",
"major-02|cups-14":"A composed, discerning presence is helpful in your work now. It may be a mentor or manager, or a role you grow into. Listen more than you speak in the next meeting, then offer one thoughtful point. Decisions made after reflection tend to hold up better than those made in the heat of a moment.",
"major-02|swords-01":"Your instinct about a situation at work and a clear reason to support it have arrived together. Write the hunch down as a sentence, then look for two facts that back it. Bring it to the meeting once you can explain it simply.",
"major-02|swords-02":"You are torn between two options while your gut leans one way. Give yourself a quiet hour without input from others, note which choice brings relief, then check it against basic facts. Putting the decision off costs more than a decent imperfect one.",
"major-02|swords-03":"Something you suspected at work, perhaps about a colleague, a decision or a shift in tone, turns out to be real, and it stings. Take the evening to absorb it privately. Then choose one calm question to ask, and keep your response measured.",
"major-02|swords-04":"Step out of the noise for a day or two. The insight you want will not arrive through more meetings or messages. Protect quiet time in your schedule, take a walk, and let your mind sort the problem. Your next move will feel clearer afterwards.",
"major-02|swords-05":"A workplace dispute has undercurrents that no one is naming. Pause before you respond, and ask what the other person is actually worried about, such as status, workload or recognition. Addressing that quietly can end a contest that arguing would only prolong.",
"major-02|swords-06":"Trust the sense that you need a calmer setting, then plan for it with real steps. Update your resume quietly, talk to someone who knows another team, or ask about a change of duties. Leaving a strained place well starts with private clarity.",
"major-02|swords-07":"Your instinct says something is being handled out of view, such as a decision made without you or credit quietly claimed. Keep notes of what you observe, stay calm, and seek clarity from the right person. Facts, calmly presented, steady you more than hunches.",
"major-02|swords-08":"Feeling stuck often means a belief is doing the trapping. Sit quietly and ask what you honestly want your work to look like, then list what you assumed you could not change. Choose one assumption to test, gently, with a person you trust.",
"major-02|swords-09":"Nighttime dread about work exaggerates. Your quieter intuition, the one you hear on a calm morning, knows more. Separate facts from fears on paper and take just one practical step. Rest, a walk and a short list help more than rehearsing worst cases.",
"major-02|swords-10":"A role, project or arrangement has ended, and you probably saw it coming. Allow yourself a quiet pause rather than jumping straight into the next thing. Listen for what you actually want next, and let that guide which door you knock on.",
"major-02|swords-11":"Listen more than you speak for a while. Notice who really decides things, what goes unsaid in meetings, and where the gaps are. Then ask one well-chosen question. Your patient attention will show you the opening, and your question will show you are worth hearing.",
"major-02|swords-12":"A colleague pushes hard and fast while you sense something is being overlooked. Trust that instinct and speak up before the plan locks in. One calm sentence such as, 'I want to check one thing first,' can save the team from a quick mistake.",
"major-02|swords-13":"Clear thinking backed by strong intuition makes your read of a situation unusually reliable. State your assessment plainly, and do not soften it until it says nothing. When the room prefers the comfortable version, calmly offer the accurate one and the reasons behind it.",
"major-02|swords-14":"You are being asked to make a considered call, and your inner sense matters as much as the numbers. Take time alone to weigh it, then decide on principle and explain the reasoning calmly. Decisions made from both facts and instinct usually hold up.",
"major-02|pentacles-01":"An opportunity has arrived, and part of you already senses whether it fits. Before saying yes, spend a quiet hour writing down what you notice about the people and terms involved. Your instinct has collected details that a pros-and-cons list would miss.",
"major-02|pentacles-02":"Competing tasks are pulling your attention, and the sharpest guidance is internal. Step away from the noise for twenty minutes, then decide which duty truly matters more. Trust the priority your quiet mind picks, and let the rest bend around it.",
"major-02|pentacles-03":"A team project rewards the person who listens before speaking. Notice what nobody is saying in the meeting, then raise it gently. Your quiet read of the room, paired with careful craftsmanship, can improve the whole result without any drama.",
"major-02|pentacles-04":"You may be sitting on what you know, from caution rather than wisdom. Silence has value, but so does sharing. Consider which of your insights would help your team, and offer it, even if the offer is small and unpolished.",
"major-02|pentacles-05":"A lean period can make you doubt yourself, yet part of the answer is already inside you. Think back to what has worked before. Quietly write down the skills and contacts you have, then ask one of them for help.",
"major-02|pentacles-06":"Look beneath the stated terms of any arrangement. What is offered, what is expected, and what is left unsaid? Your instinct will pick up unfairness before the paperwork does. Ask the question plainly, and let the answer guide the deal.",
"major-02|pentacles-07":"Progress is happening below the surface. Rather than checking hourly for results, give the project quiet time and then assess it honestly. Your intuition about what is working and what is not is probably right. Write it down, then test it against facts.",
"major-02|pentacles-08":"Deep skill comes from daily practice and from listening to yourself as you work. Notice which tasks feel right and which feel forced. Keep showing up to the craft each day, and let your own sense of quality steer the hours you put in.",
"major-02|pentacles-09":"You have built independence, and your quiet judgment is a real asset. Protect time to think without interruption. What you have learned by yourself is worth sharing with others when the moment is right, though you need not hurry.",
"major-02|pentacles-10":"Long-standing structures at work have unspoken rules. Learn them by watching before acting. Then ask what you want to contribute over years, not months. Your quiet understanding of how the place truly runs is an advantage.",
"major-02|pentacles-11":"New study and quiet reflection go together. Take a course or start a practice project, and keep notes on how you learn. Your first real try will teach you more than the manual, especially if you review it calmly afterward.",
"major-02|pentacles-12":"Reliable effort and a good sense of timing beat force. Keep to your routine, and listen for the moment when a task is truly ready to move. Your quiet dependability will be noticed. Do not mistake it for going unseen.",
"major-02|pentacles-13":"Practical care and quiet insight make a strong pair. You can sense what colleagues need without being told, and you have the skills to meet it. Support someone behind the scenes this week, and pay attention to what that teaches you.",
"major-02|pentacles-14":"Authority grounded in real knowledge does not need to announce itself. Decide with care, keep your own counsel until you have the facts, and lead by example. Colleagues will respect calm mastery more than any speech.",
"major-03|major-04":"Creativity needs a container. You may have plenty of ideas, resources or goodwill, and the job now is to give them a schedule, a budget and clear roles. Build a simple structure around what is already growing, and keep it flexible enough to nourish the work.",
"major-03|major-05":"Learning from people who came before you feeds your growth. Find a mentor, join a training or study how experienced colleagues handle their work. Then bring your own care and creativity to it. Skills passed down thoughtfully turn into something you can pass on yourself.",
"major-03|major-06":"Choose the work you are willing to nurture. Options may look equal, but one matches your values and will grow with attention. Ask which project or team you would happily tend for years. Then give that choice regular, generous effort.",
"major-03|major-07":"Resources are there and so is the drive to use them. Point the effort at one clear goal, and look after the people and energy that carry it. Steady progress needs meals, rest and encouragement. A well-fed team moves farther than a hurried one.",
"major-03|major-08":"Gentle persistence is winning here. A difficult colleague or a slow project responds better to warmth and consistency than to force. Keep showing up, keep your temper even, and reward small steps. Over time, that steady care turns into real trust and results.",
"major-03|major-09":"Step away from the busy hive to look after your own energy. A short retreat, a day without meetings or a walk alone can feed your best thinking. Return with one clear question or idea. Nourished focus beats constant output.",
"major-03|major-10":"Growth cycles come and go, and you seem to be entering a fruitful one. Prepare by looking after your resources and relationships, and keep your plans flexible. Not every good turn can be forced, but you can be ready, generous and well rested when it comes.",
"major-03|major-11":"Fair treatment and steady care go together. Look at how credit, workload and pay are shared, and raise concerns kindly with facts. A workplace that feeds its people also keeps honest books. Be generous, but keep clear records so generosity does not become resentment.",
"major-03|major-12":"A pause in progress may be feeding the project rather than starving it. Let the idea grow while you look at it from a fresh angle. Rest, ask for other views, and resist forcing a result. Sometimes a stalled season is where the deepest ideas take root.",
"major-03|major-13":"Something in your work is ending, and it is clearing room for growth. Let the old routine, role or project go with thanks. Then plant something new in the space: a skill, a contact, a smaller experiment. Composting the old makes the next season richer.",
"major-03|major-14":"Sustainable effort wins over bursts. Blend your resources, time and energy in moderate portions, and keep a pace you could hold for months. Look after your health and your team's, and make small adjustments as things change. Good work grows from an even hand.",
"major-03|major-15":"Watch for comforts that hold you in place: a familiar role, an easy perk, a habit of overworking to feel needed. Ask honestly what you are getting from it and what it costs. Then loosen your grip on one thing, and see how that feels.",
"major-03|major-16":"Something comfortable at work may be disrupted, whether a routine, a team or a plan. Protect your footing first: rest, eat, talk to allies. Then look at what remains and what you can grow from it. Rebuilding with better foundations takes time.",
"major-03|major-17":"Fresh hope is taking root after a tough period. Feed it with regular rest, orderly records and a plan you can actually keep. Tell supportive people what you are aiming for. Nurturing small, real progress builds a base of confidence for later.",
"major-03|major-18":"Plenty of potential is here, but the picture is not clear yet. Avoid locking in plans on guesses. Ask questions, request missing details, and keep your energy steady while things settle. Grounded routines protect you from worry spirals while facts come in.",
"major-03|major-19":"Good energy and real results can go together. Celebrate what is going well, thank the people who helped, and let your enthusiasm show. A cheerful, generous team culture makes hard tasks lighter. Keep enjoying the work while it is working.",
"major-03|major-20":"Take an honest look at what your efforts have produced and what they cost you. Are you nurturing the right things? Answer the pull toward change with a practical step, such as a conversation, a new routine or a project you have deferred.",
"major-03|major-21":"Sustained care has led to something finished and good. Take time to enjoy the result and thank everyone who helped. Then rest properly before the next thing. A project handled with generosity leaves a team ready and willing to work together again.",
"major-03|wands-01":"A fresh project has appeared with real potential to grow. Feed it: give it time, care and steady attention, whatever resources you control. Start with a modest first step this week, and look after your own energy, since growth needs a healthy gardener.",
"major-03|wands-02":"You have a stable base and the chance to plan larger. Ask what your current work has given you, then map where it could go next. Growth is best when the roots stay healthy. Take a wider view this month, and choose the next step that builds on what already thrives.",
"major-03|wands-03":"Seeds sown earlier are growing, and returns need more time. Keep tending: follow up, support your team, and take care of your health while you wait. Offers from farther afield may open up later. Patience here is active, not passive.",
"major-03|wands-04":"A stable, generous spell is here. Enjoy the results of steady effort, and share the good mood with colleagues: a meal, a thank-you, a shared break. Settled teams do better work. Use the calm to strengthen the base before the next challenge.",
"major-03|wands-05":"Creative disagreement is happening around a project you care about. Stay warm and steady while others argue: listen for the useful point in the noise. Look after the people involved, and food and rest in long meetings help. Good ideas often grow from messy debate.",
"major-03|wands-06":"Your generosity and steady effort are getting noticed. Let credit come, and share it with the people who helped things grow. Reputation built on care lasts longer than quick applause. Use this good standing to ask for what would help your work flourish.",
"major-03|wands-07":"You can be generous and still defend your position. When someone tries to take credit or push on your resources, answer calmly and firmly. Protect what you have grown. A boundary stated with warmth is easier to keep, and it earns respect.",
"major-03|wands-08":"Things are speeding up, and you can stay grounded through it. Handle the rush by looking after the basics: rest, food, a clear desk. Send what is ready and let fast news arrive. Abundant work is good, so long as you do not burn your own supply.",
"major-03|wands-09":"Your resources have been stretched, and you are still going. Refill before the final push: rest, a proper meal, a day off if it is possible. Then protect what you built. Being nurturing at work means treating your own stamina as part of the project.",
"major-03|wands-10":"You have been caring for everyone, and it has piled up. Decide who else can take on part of the load, and say so plainly. Nurturing a team does not mean doing their tasks. Drop one commitment this week to give your own health and work room to breathe.",
"major-03|wands-11":"A curious beginning is worth feeding. Give a new skill or side task regular, gentle attention instead of dramatic bursts. Learn from someone who has grown something similar. A small, well-tended experiment this month can become steady, dependable work later.",
"major-03|wands-12":"Energy is high and the project needs nourishing, not just pushing. Speed is fine, but check that people, budgets and health can keep up. Ask how the team is doing before adding another deadline. Growth that lasts comes from drive and care in equal parts.",
"major-03|wands-13":"You are at your best when you lead with care and decisive energy. Host, mentor or back someone, and do it with authority. People bloom around a leader who is confident and kind. Take a role that lets you grow others while your own work is visible.",
"major-03|wands-14":"A leader with vision and a caring hand builds something lasting. Set direction, and also look after the people who carry it out. Plan resources for the long term, not just the next quarter. Confidence is strongest when the team is fed, rested and clear on the goal.",
"major-03|cups-01":"A generous opportunity is coming, and it suits your capacity to grow things. Think about whether it feeds your energy, your finances and your interest, not just one of them. Treat the start like a seedling: give it steady attention, patience and realistic timing. Rewarding work often builds slowly and then surprises you.",
"major-03|cups-02":"A collaboration that is fair and generous can flourish. Agree on how you will share the workload and the rewards, and keep checking in on how each person is doing. Care for the people you work with as much as for the results. A supportive partnership often produces steady, lasting satisfaction.",
"major-03|cups-03":"Plenty is present, along with people who enjoy each other. Celebrate a finished task with the team, share the credit and notice who has been doing quiet work. A well-fed, appreciated group tends to be more productive and loyal. Consider how you can make the workplace more welcoming, and treat it as part of getting results.",
"major-03|cups-04":"Comfortable circumstances may be making you flat. Notice whether you are actually tired, undernourished or short of variety. A better lunch break, a walk or a change of scenery may restore some energy. Then ask whether the work itself needs a new challenge. Growth usually asks for a bit of fresh soil.",
"major-03|cups-05":"A setback has drained you, so start with basic care: sleep, meals, time outdoors. Once your energy is back, look at what survived the loss, such as skills, clients and goodwill. Replanting takes patience. Do not rush to prove you are fine. Tend the work in small daily steps, and progress will return.",
"major-03|cups-06":"Familiar ways of caring for a team may serve you well now, like a mentor's advice you still carry or a habit from an earlier job that worked. Revisit it and adapt it to the present. Be mindful not to let sentiment keep you in a role that has stopped growing.",
"major-03|cups-07":"Plans multiply, and few have soil to grow in. Ask of each idea: who will do the daily work, how long will it take, and what will it need from you every week? Choose the one with steady footing and give it regular attention. The rest can wait on a list.",
"major-03|cups-08":"Walking away from a draining setup may be healthy, but do it with care for your practical needs. Plan how you will protect your time, energy and relationships through the shift. Review what you have grown here and carry it with you. A change made with support and preparation tends to land softly.",
"major-03|cups-09":"Satisfaction and steady reward are within view. Enjoy the results of consistent effort, and make your workspace or routine a little more pleasant. Then ask what would sustain this: rest, fair boundaries, good relationships. Comfort lasts when it is tended, so keep doing the quiet things that got you here.",
"major-03|cups-10":"A stable, nurturing work environment is possible, and you can help grow one. Look after people's basic needs: fair schedules, real breaks, thanks. Family businesses and close-knit teams especially benefit. Build habits that last, and treat colleagues as members of a community, not just resources. Long-term contentment comes from consistent care.",
"major-03|cups-11":"A creative seed of an idea wants care rather than pressure. Give it regular, small amounts of time. A newcomer on your team may bring something fresh, and welcoming them will pay off. Your own beginner's enthusiasm is an asset. Protect it from cynicism, and let it develop before you judge the result.",
"major-03|cups-12":"A proposal arrives with appealing perks. Enjoy the good feeling, then read the details: workload, hours, and how people are treated. The Knight may be a charming person offering it, or your own eagerness. Choose what supports your wellbeing and growth over whatever looks flashiest.",
"major-03|cups-13":"Care and competence combine in a role that supports others. You may be the person who keeps the team fed, calm and growing. Set boundaries so the caring is sustainable, and make sure your contribution gets noticed. The Queen may be a generous colleague, or the part you are stepping into.",
"major-03|cups-14":"Providing well for others while staying level-headed is the theme. The King may be a supportive senior colleague or the leader you become. Make fair decisions, look after your people, and keep your own limits clear. Prosperity in working life is helped by calm, generous handling of time and effort.",
"major-03|swords-01":"A sharp idea has the right conditions to grow. Decide its purpose in one line, then give it steady attention, like a plant that needs regular water. Support from colleagues and a bit of time would help, so ask for both plainly.",
"major-03|swords-02":"Generosity or comfort is keeping you from choosing between two paths. Set a decision date, list what each option would nourish, and stick to it. Effort and resources grow better with a direction, and a kind, clear answer helps others plan around you.",
"major-03|swords-03":"A setback at work lands on something you had invested care in. Give it a proper moment of sadness, then look after your basics: sleep, meals, a friendly colleague. You handle the next step better when you have been kind to yourself first.",
"major-03|swords-04":"A restful pause helps your work grow. Take the vacation day, sleep properly, and stop overfilling the calendar. Fields need fallow seasons, and so do you. You will return with better ideas and a steadier hand, and the team will benefit.",
"major-03|swords-05":"A contest over credit or resources leaves everyone with less. Instead of squeezing a colleague, look for how to grow the pie: share the workload, offer help, invite ideas. Abundance mostly comes from cooperating, and reputations grow from being generous.",
"major-03|swords-06":"A shift toward a healthier, more supportive setting is within reach. Think about which conditions help you flourish: reasonable hours, friendly people, room to grow. Plan the change in stages, and bring the goodwill you have built along with your reputation.",
"major-03|swords-07":"Someone may be taking more than their share of the credit or resources, or you may be tempted to. Keep a simple record of who contributed what, and speak up warmly but clearly. Protecting what you have nurtured is fair, and sneaking around it is not.",
"major-03|swords-08":"You feel tied to a role by duty or comfort, and the ties may be mostly in your mind. List what you actually need from work, such as pay, growth or kindness, and check what is truly fixed. Ask for one change and see what happens.",
"major-03|swords-09":"Money and workload anxieties gather at night, but a tired mind exaggerates. Have a proper meal, sleep, and take a small practical step in daylight, such as a budget review or a request for help. Looking after your body is part of solving the problem.",
"major-03|swords-10":"A cycle of work has ended completely. Treat the space it leaves like an empty field: rest, clear the ground, and decide slowly what to plant next. Look after your health habits and your household budget through the change, and draw on the goodwill you built.",
"major-03|swords-11":"Learning is fertile ground right now. Ask questions, take notes, and give yourself time to grow into a new skill or role without demanding instant results. A patient, watchful start builds the foundation a career stands on, and mentors will notice your care.",
"major-03|swords-12":"Speed and steady growth pull against each other on a project. Push where a quick decision helps, but leave time for the parts that need patient care, like training a newcomer or testing a product. Rushing everything tends to trample the good ideas.",
"major-03|swords-13":"Practical care and clear sight make you a natural mentor or manager. Support your people, but do not soften the facts they need to hear. Honest feedback given warmly helps a team grow, and honest budgeting given calmly keeps everyone steady.",
"major-03|swords-14":"Abundance thrives under fair rules. If you lead, reward good work openly, decide difficult questions on principle, and explain them. If you follow, look for the leader who combines care with justice. Steady, principled stewardship of a team's time pays off over the long run.",
"major-03|pentacles-01":"A new chance to grow something has been placed in your hands. Give it steady attention, patient care and enough room to develop. Do not rush it to prove itself. Good early habits, like regular check-ins and clean records, matter more than early results.",
"major-03|pentacles-02":"Nurture your projects, but do not let them all demand equal feeding. Sort what needs daily care from what can wait, and build in rest. Handling the load sustainably is a skill, and it protects your health, your team and your budgeting habits.",
"major-03|pentacles-03":"A creative, cooperative project is ripe. Bring generosity to the team: notice who needs encouragement, praise good work by name, and take pride in the details. Workplaces grow best when people feel cared for and skill is respected.",
"major-03|pentacles-04":"Generous resources are at hand, yet you may be clutching them. Ask what your team, or your own growth, would gain from a freer hand. Careful budgeting is a good habit, and openness about it keeps trust healthy.",
"major-03|pentacles-05":"A lean time asks for care of the person doing the work. Rest, eat properly, and keep simple routines. Then approach a colleague or mentor for practical support. Abundance returns more easily to someone who has looked after their own energy.",
"major-03|pentacles-06":"Giving and receiving fairly is the theme. Share credit, mentor someone, and accept help yourself without embarrassment. A workplace where people look after one another is stronger. Be clear about what is fair, and say it kindly.",
"major-03|pentacles-07":"Something you planted months ago is showing what it can become. Walk the rows: what is thriving, what needs more care, what should be let go. Adjust your effort with a gardener's patience, not a critic's impatience.",
"major-03|pentacles-08":"Skill grows through repetition, and so does a healthy working life. Give your craft a fixed hour each day, and protect meals, sleep and breaks as part of the job. Steady, cared-for effort wins out over frantic bursts.",
"major-03|pentacles-09":"Your own effort has grown into real comfort. Enjoy it without guilt, and keep the habits that made it possible. Consider how you might help others grow something of their own, through advice, mentoring or generosity with your time.",
"major-03|pentacles-10":"Something you nurture now can outlast you. Think about legacy in practical terms: a team you have trained, a system others can follow, a reputation for fairness. Steady, generous work over years creates lasting security for many people.",
"major-03|pentacles-11":"A growing skill needs good soil. Study something practical, try a small trial project, and be gentle with mistakes. Your early efforts do not need to be perfect. Consistent attention and encouragement will help them grow into real ability.",
"major-03|pentacles-12":"Reliable, patient work is nourishing to the people around you. Show up, do the task in front of you, and treat colleagues well as you go. This kind of steady contribution earns trust and gently raises the standard of the whole team.",
"major-03|pentacles-13":"Capable, warm leadership is available to you. Look after both the people and the practical details: workflows, supplies, follow-ups. Colleagues thrive when a competent person supports them. Use your skill to make things easier for others, and accept the same in return.",
"major-03|pentacles-14":"Prosperity built on care lasts. Manage resources with a steady hand and treat your people well. Lead by providing what the team needs: clear direction, fair treatment, and space to grow. Trust, like any resource, increases when it is handled well.",
"major-04|major-05":"Rules, hierarchy and established methods are strong forces here. Learn how the organization actually decides things and follow its proven paths while you build authority. A mentor or formal credential can help. Question outdated rules respectfully, with a better alternative in hand.",
"major-04|major-06":"A significant decision about partnership or direction needs a clear framework. List the values and non-negotiables, then compare your options honestly. Choose what fits, and back it up with written agreements. Good working relationships rest on shared principles and clear terms.",
"major-04|major-07":"Authority and momentum are on your side. Set the plan, assign the roles, and drive toward the target with confidence. Keep listening as you go so speed does not outrun sense. Leadership works best when people know where they are going and why.",
"major-04|major-08":"Lead with steady calm rather than volume. Authority comes across best when paired with patience: keep standards clear, hear people out, and correct without shaming. Difficult situations at work respond to consistent, kind firmness better than to pressure.",
"major-04|major-09":"Step away from daily management to think about the larger structure. Are the systems, roles and goals right? Take quiet time to review, jot down one honest question, and return with a simple change. Good leaders take space to see clearly.",
"major-04|major-10":"Plan carefully, then accept that conditions will move. Build a structure that can bend: buffers, backups, honest check-ins. Not every change is yours to control. Steady leadership means responding well to whatever turns up. A written plan with room for surprises holds up better than a rigid one.",
"major-04|major-11":"Rules, agreements and fairness are central. Make sure roles are defined, commitments are written clearly, and decisions follow consistent standards. Own your part of any outcome. A well-run workplace treats people evenly and keeps accurate records.",
"major-04|major-12":"A planned schedule stalls, and that may be useful. Instead of forcing the timeline, take a step back and question your assumptions. Consider how the problem looks from a customer's or a junior colleague's chair. Then adjust the structure with what you learned.",
"major-04|major-13":"A structure at work is being taken apart, and it may be time. A role, policy or leadership habit has run its course. Shut it down cleanly, document what it taught you, and design something simpler. Firm endings make room for better foundations.",
"major-04|major-14":"Combine structure with adaptability. Set firm goals, and blend methods and people so the whole runs smoothly. Pace yourself for the long haul. Steady, measured leadership, with small corrections along the way, gets more done than intense pushes.",
"major-04|major-15":"Power and ambition may be pulling harder than you think. Ask whether status, a title or the need to control has become a trap. Look at who benefits from rigid rules. Choose one habit of over-control to release, and give someone else a genuine say.",
"major-04|major-16":"A structure you relied on gets shaken, whether a plan, a leader or a policy. Steady yourself, gather facts, and do the next necessary task. Later, redesign with what the surprise revealed. Foundations built on real needs, not appearances, hold up better.",
"major-04|major-17":"A renewed sense of direction can be built into daily habits. Set modest, regular goals and protect the time for them. Hope becomes real when it has a plan. Ask for guidance from steady people, and let your confidence rebuild on evidence.",
"major-04|major-18":"Structure is being tested by unclear information. Resist rigid decisions based on rumor or worry. Keep your routines steady, gather concrete facts, and check assumptions with reliable sources. Clarity usually arrives, and calm leadership in the meantime holds people together.",
"major-04|major-19":"Solid leadership and a good result meet. Clear plans and firm standards are paying off. Share the credit, keep your structure simple, and let your confidence be visible. A well-run team that enjoys its work is a strong asset.",
"major-04|major-20":"A serious review of your leadership, goals or systems is due. Face the results honestly, own what you did, and decide what changes. Then set new standards and follow through. Accountability that starts with you tends to spread.",
"major-04|major-21":"Long, disciplined effort reaches a finish. Structures you built are working, and a major goal is met. Record how you did it, thank contributors, and take a real breather. A well-completed project becomes a template for the next one.",
"major-04|wands-01":"A new idea needs a frame to become real. Set a scope, a deadline and a person responsible before you begin. The spark is valuable, but plans give it a place to grow. Write down what done looks like and start on the first piece.",
"major-04|wands-02":"Long-range planning is the task now. Draw the map, assign roles, and decide the rules of the road before you move. You have the authority to set the direction and the wider view to choose well. Write it down so others can follow it.",
"major-04|wands-03":"The framework is in place and you are waiting on results. Keep systems steady, track progress, and avoid rewriting plans out of impatience. Reliable process is what carries a project through a slow spell. Check in at set times, and leave gaps for things to arrive.",
"major-04|wands-04":"Solid structure gives your team a reason to celebrate. Recognize what you built: the processes, the trust, the calm. Then keep it maintained. A settled workplace lets people relax and do good work. Mark this stage before you set the next target.",
"major-04|wands-05":"Competing ideas need a referee, and that may be you. Set a fair process for the debate: who speaks, who decides, and by when. Then hold everyone to it. Friction inside clear boundaries becomes productive, while friction without them just wears people out.",
"major-04|wands-06":"Authority you have earned is being recognized. Accept the title, the praise or the responsibility, and use it well. Credit the systems and people that made your results possible. Reputation for reliability opens doors, so keep your standards visible and consistent.",
"major-04|wands-07":"You have position and you are being challenged. Rely on your rules, records and clear responsibilities rather than raised voices. Defend the decisions you made for good reasons, and be willing to adjust where evidence says so. Steadiness earns more than aggression.",
"major-04|wands-08":"Fast-moving work needs a firm frame. Set priorities, owners and cut-off times before the rush hits. Messages and deadlines will pile up, and order is what keeps you effective. Decide what gets answered today and what waits, and stick to it.",
"major-04|wands-09":"You have been the steady one for a while, and it shows. Trim the structure to what is essential and let the rest go. Delegate tasks that others can own. A lighter, well-ordered load is more sustainable than heroic endurance, and it protects the finish.",
"major-04|wands-10":"Too many obligations are sitting on your shoulders. Review your responsibilities with an organizer's eye: which are yours by role and which crept in? Reassign, defer or drop the extras, and set a firm limit on new requests. Order is a relief, not a cage.",
"major-04|wands-11":"A beginner or a new idea needs your framework to grow. Offer structure and clear expectations, and allow room for questions. If you are the beginner, look for someone with authority who will teach. Curiosity paired with discipline turns interest into skill.",
"major-04|wands-12":"There is drive, and there is a need for order. Aim your momentum at goals that have been defined, with roles assigned. Rushing past rules and colleagues causes rework. Channel the heat into a disciplined push, and check your plan first.",
"major-04|wands-13":"Confident, structured and warm, you hold a room well. Use that authority to set fair expectations and make decisions people trust. Support your team and be clear about the rules. Leadership that combines care and order is rare and effective, so use it.",
"major-04|wands-14":"Vision and structure meet, and you are the one to direct them. Define the mission, set clear rules and choose the people you trust. Lead by example, and adjust when facts change. A well-run plan with a bold aim can carry a team a long way.",
"major-04|cups-01":"An enthusiastic opportunity needs a plan to survive. Set the scope, the hours and the deadline before you say yes with your whole heart, and put the terms in writing. Passion for the project is a good sign, and clear structure keeps it from turning into overwork or vague promises.",
"major-04|cups-02":"A fair partnership needs terms. Define roles, decision rights and how credit and rewards are shared, and write them down. Respect each other's authority in their own area. Trust between colleagues grows faster when boundaries are clear from the start and disputes have a known path.",
"major-04|cups-03":"A well-run team celebration or group project shows leadership. Organize it with clear roles and a simple schedule, then let people enjoy themselves. Good morale is built by structure that feels friendly. Steady management can turn a group of individuals into a team that trusts and likes working together.",
"major-04|cups-04":"Boredom in a well-defined job invites restructuring. Ask whether you can change your responsibilities, schedule or goals to make the work interesting again. Bring your manager a proposal with clear benefits for the team. Discipline helps too: set a challenge with a deadline, and rekindle interest by finishing it.",
"major-04|cups-05":"A setback calls for order. Write a plain account of what happened, what is still working and your next three steps. Keep to a steady schedule while you recover your footing. Ask for help through proper channels. The loss is real, and a calm structure lets you rebuild without more chaos.",
"major-04|cups-06":"Established methods and old relationships deserve respect, and they should serve today's goals. Reconnect with a former colleague if it helps, but agree on present-day terms. Keep the procedures that still work and update the ones that do not. Loyalty to how things were done should not block improvement.",
"major-04|cups-07":"Too many options call for criteria. Set three requirements a project or role must meet, such as steady hours, room to grow and decent colleagues, and drop whatever fails. Put a deadline on the decision. A firm framework turns a foggy list of possibilities into a plan you can act on.",
"major-04|cups-08":"Moving on is best done with structure. Finish your obligations, hand over your work clearly, and document what you know for whoever follows. Decide your criteria for the next step before you begin looking. An orderly change protects your reputation and lets you go with your head high.",
"major-04|cups-09":"Success built on good structure deserves recognition. Notice which systems and habits produced this satisfaction, and keep them. Set a wish for the coming year with a specific measure and a timeline. Enjoy the results without letting comfort loosen the discipline that earned them.",
"major-04|cups-10":"A cohesive organization is built on clear structure and shared purpose. If you lead, define roles, rules and rituals that make everyone feel included. If you are part of a team, learn its unwritten rules and contribute. Steady leadership and a warm culture together create the belonging people stay for.",
"major-04|cups-11":"A fresh idea from a junior colleague, or from your own beginner's mind, needs a process to grow. Set up a small pilot with clear goals and a short timeline. Offer guidance without stifling it. Good leaders give beginners both encouragement and a framework, so creativity produces something usable.",
"major-04|cups-12":"A polished proposal calls for careful checking. Ask for the terms in writing, look at the track record of whoever is offering, and compare it with your own priorities. The Knight may be a charming colleague or your own eagerness. Say yes to what fits your framework, and negotiate the rest.",
"major-04|cups-13":"Compassion works best inside limits. You may be the person others bring problems to, so set aside specific times for listening and protect your own workload. The Queen might be a colleague who leads with empathy, or the role you play. Caring and organized together make a trusted, effective presence.",
"major-04|cups-14":"Fair, composed leadership is the theme. The King may be a boss who combines decisiveness with kindness, or the leader you become. Make clear decisions, explain the reasons and treat people with respect. Structure and heart together create a workplace where people feel safe to do their best work.",
"major-04|swords-01":"A sharp insight needs a structure to carry it. Turn the idea into a plan with an owner, a deadline and a simple measure of success. Bring it to whoever has authority to approve it, and state your case in a few clean sentences.",
"major-04|swords-02":"A deadlock over policy, process or reporting lines is stalling progress. Ask what rule everyone is actually protecting, and whether it still serves the goal. Propose a trial period for a compromise, with a review date. Structure can flex without collapsing.",
"major-04|swords-03":"A harsh decision from above, such as a restructuring or a policy change, stings. Acknowledge the disappointment, then ask for the reasoning and the next steps. Focus on what you control, like your role, your work and your boundaries, and keep a record of agreements.",
"major-04|swords-04":"Build rest into the plan instead of hoping to find time for it. Block out recovery days, delegate a routine task, and let the system carry some weight. Good leaders know that sustainable structure includes downtime, and a rested team makes fewer errors.",
"major-04|swords-05":"A power struggle over turf, process or credit leaves scars. Stand by your legitimate boundaries, but do not press every advantage. Ask which win matters, concede the small ones, and keep the working relationship intact. Authority used gently lasts longer.",
"major-04|swords-06":"A carefully planned transition is likelier to succeed than an impulsive one. Map the steps, the timeline and the people you need on your side. Move toward the calmer setting with an orderly handover, and leave a good record behind.",
"major-04|swords-07":"Someone may be working around the rules, or you may be tempted to. Check the policy and the chain of command, and keep your own conduct clean. If you spot a shortcut that hurts others, raise it through proper channels, calmly and with specifics.",
"major-04|swords-08":"You feel confined by procedures, hierarchy or your own strict standards. Separate the rules that truly apply from those you assumed. Ask a manager which ones are flexible, and propose an alternative in writing. Authority often has more give than it appears.",
"major-04|swords-09":"Anxiety over responsibility grows when things are vague. Put your worries into a written plan with dates, owners and next steps. Structure quiets the mind, and a clear list of what you control and what you do not is a strong place to start.",
"major-04|swords-10":"A system, role or reporting arrangement has come to its end. Review the situation, hand over cleanly, and document what worked. Then design what comes next on purpose, with clear roles and realistic limits. Endings give you a rare chance to build the frame right.",
"major-04|swords-11":"Learn the structure before you try to change it. Watch how decisions are made, ask about the reasoning behind rules, and note who holds influence. A curious newcomer who understands the framework is far more effective than one who pushes against it blindly.",
"major-04|swords-12":"A fast-moving colleague or a quick decision meets established rules and roles. Channel the energy through the proper process instead of around it. Move promptly on approvals, be direct about constraints, and keep the team informed. Speed inside a good structure goes further than speed alone.",
"major-04|swords-13":"Sharp judgment and a firm framework make a formidable combination for planning and review. Give honest assessments backed by clear criteria. Set standards fairly and hold them consistently, including for yourself. People trust a process they can see, even when it delivers unwelcome news.",
"major-04|swords-14":"Authority and principle are strongly present. If you decide, do so on clear standards, explain them, and stay open to review. If you answer to someone who does, present your case in their terms. Fair, well-defined systems keep organizations steady, and consistency builds trust.",
"major-04|pentacles-01":"A promising opening deserves a solid framework. Before you start, write down the goals, the timeline, and who is responsible for what. Clear structure at the beginning makes the real work easier, and protects everyone from misunderstandings later.",
"major-04|pentacles-02":"A schedule is your best tool for juggling competing tasks. Build a weekly plan with fixed blocks, limits on meetings, and clear priorities. Then defend it. Structure makes a heavy workload manageable and gives you calm for surprises.",
"major-04|pentacles-03":"A team does best with defined roles and high standards. Clarify who owns which part, set expectations, and inspect the quality of the work together. Good structure lets talented people do their best without stepping on each other.",
"major-04|pentacles-04":"Control over resources can slide into rigidity. Check whether your systems still serve the work or only your comfort. A steady budgeting habit is wise, and refusing all change is not. Adjust one policy this month and see what happens.",
"major-04|pentacles-05":"Scarcity calls for order, not panic. Write down what comes in, what goes out, and what commitments you have. Build a simple routine and stick to it. Structure gives you a base from which to ask others for help.",
"major-04|pentacles-06":"Clear terms make fair exchange possible. Put agreements in writing, define who does what, and honor them. Being generous inside a firm framework works well. Support people who deserve it, and hold the boundaries that keep the arrangement healthy.",
"major-04|pentacles-07":"Time for an orderly review. Look at the results so far against the plan you made. What worked, what fell short, and what needs a new approach? Decide on the next steps with a clear head, then commit to them.",
"major-04|pentacles-08":"Disciplined practice builds mastery. Design a daily schedule that protects time for your core skill, and keep to it even on dull days. Set a standard for yourself and measure against it. Craft grows inside a good routine.",
"major-04|pentacles-09":"Self-reliance and good structure have earned you comfort, and that is worth respecting. Keep the systems that work. Also consider stepping into a mentoring or leadership role, where your discipline can help others build their own steady footing at their own pace.",
"major-04|pentacles-10":"Building for the long term takes a framework. Establish processes, roles and standards that will outlast any one person. Write things down. A well-organized team keeps working through change, and offers steady security to those in it.",
"major-04|pentacles-11":"Study the rules of your field before you bend them. Take a structured course, follow a proven method, and get a mentor's feedback. Your first serious attempt at the work will benefit from clear standards and honest correction.",
"major-04|pentacles-12":"Methodical, reliable work wins out. Follow the plan, meet the deadline, and finish what you start. It may not be exciting, but it builds a reputation as someone whose word holds. Steady progress is still progress.",
"major-04|pentacles-13":"Competent, caring management is on offer. Organize the details, set clear expectations, and look after your people while you do. Good leaders create systems that make work smoother. Put yours in writing, so others can rely on them too.",
"major-04|pentacles-14":"Mastery and authority meet here. You can lead with skill and set the framework in which others succeed. Be fair, be clear, and be open to input. The strongest leaders build structures that outlast them and treat people well.",
"major-05|major-06":"Ethics and belonging shape a choice. An organization's traditions and your own principles may or may not match. Weigh which culture you can wholeheartedly join. Ask mentors and read the written values, then choose the place where your integrity and your role align.",
"major-05|major-07":"Proven methods and forward drive combine well. Use the established process, get the training or certification, and push toward your target with focus. Follow the rules that help and question the ones that do not. Discipline within a system can move you fast.",
"major-05|major-08":"Learn the ropes patiently and keep your temper. A mentor, a training program or an old-school colleague may test your composure. Respond with calm and curiosity. You can honor the way things are done and still quietly show a better way.",
"major-05|major-09":"Retreat from the group's opinions to hear yourself think. Study quietly, read the source material, and form your own view of how your field works. Then consult a trusted teacher for a second opinion. Independent understanding makes learning stick.",
"major-05|major-10":"Institutions change, and the trusted route may shift under you. Stay close to your principles and keep learning. Accept that promotions, rules and markets turn on their own timing. Being adaptable within a solid foundation helps.",
"major-05|major-11":"Formal rules, policies and agreements are prominent. Read the handbook and the process, and make sure you follow them and that others do too. If something is unfair, raise it through proper channels with facts. Integrity in procedure earns respect.",
"major-05|major-12":"Pausing your usual training or route can reveal a better one. Question a standard practice and consider it from an outsider's view. A delay in advancement may give you time to learn something unofficial but valuable. Return with a fresher perspective.",
"major-05|major-13":"An old institution, method or mentor relationship is coming to a close. Thank it for what it taught you, then move on cleanly. A qualification or tradition that once defined you may give way to a new path. Endings like this are a graduation.",
"major-05|major-14":"Combine established methods with your own judgment, in measured amounts. Learn from teachers, then adapt the lessons to your situation. Steady, patient practice beats cramming. A balanced approach to rules and creativity makes for dependable work.",
"major-05|major-15":"Rigid dogma or an unspoken system may be trapping you. Ask whether you follow a rule because it works or because you fear stepping out. Look at what the institution gets from your compliance. Seek an outside opinion, and test one small change.",
"major-05|major-16":"A trusted institution or method may be shaken by sudden change. Stay steady, verify what the rules now say, and ask questions. It can be unsettling when the known way fails. Afterward, you can build a more honest understanding of how things work.",
"major-05|major-17":"Guidance from a mentor or a proven method restores your confidence. Study, ask for advice, and follow a plan that has worked for others. Hope grows when you see a path. Share what you learn as you go, because teaching reinforces your own progress.",
"major-05|major-18":"Official information may be murky or contradictory. Rely on documented facts and trusted advisors, and avoid acting on rumor. Ask for clarification in writing. When the way forward is foggy, established procedures can help you keep your footing.",
"major-05|major-19":"Learning and belonging are going well. A supportive mentor, a good training program or a healthy team culture gives you confidence. Show appreciation and share what you have learned. Work that respects tradition and welcomes newcomers tends to shine.",
"major-05|major-20":"A formal evaluation, a review of your qualifications or a reconsideration of your field is in play. Be honest about what you have learned and where you have stalled. Answer the call to grow, perhaps by seeking further training or a role that fits.",
"major-05|major-21":"Study and practice culminate in a real accomplishment. A degree, certification or long project may be ready to be recognized. Take part in the ceremony, thank your teachers, and consider how to pass knowledge on. Completion brings standing.",
"major-05|wands-01":"A new idea can grow inside an established method. Learn how things are usually done, then take the first step in a way the organization can accept. A mentor or a set procedure may give your spark a safe start. Ask before you improvise.",
"major-05|wands-02":"Look at the standard route and at the wider options before you commit. Ask a mentor or read up on what has worked, then plan your own version. Training, credentials or an established path may still serve you. Choose it knowingly, not by default.",
"major-05|wands-03":"You followed the process, and results take time. Keep to the proven steps and do not skip stages out of impatience. Ask for feedback from a teacher or supervisor while you wait. Established channels may be slower than expected, but they lead where you are headed.",
"major-05|wands-04":"A team ritual or an accepted achievement is worth marking. Recognize milestones the way your workplace understands them: a ceremony, a thank-you, a shared meal. Established routines create belonging. Enjoy the stable footing, and note what the group values most.",
"major-05|wands-05":"Disagreements about the right way to work are stirring. Ask what convention says and what evidence says, and where they differ. A neutral senior person or an agreed guideline can settle it. Use the conflict to update a practice that no longer works.",
"major-05|wands-06":"Formal recognition suits you now: a title, a certificate, a public thank-you. Accept it as a sign that your steady following of good practice paid off. Thank the mentors who taught you. Your reputation with institutions and senior people is strong, so use it carefully.",
"major-05|wands-07":"Standing by your principles matters when the group leans the other way. Cite the standards, the training and the record behind your position. Do not attack tradition, and do not give in to pressure that ignores the facts. Respectful persistence tends to earn a hearing.",
"major-05|wands-08":"Things move quickly, but the process still counts. Send it through the proper channels so it does not bounce back. Get approvals early, follow the format, and speed will come from doing it right the first time. A fast reply that ignores protocol may cost days.",
"major-05|wands-09":"When energy is low, established routines do the work for you. Follow the checklist, use the templates and ask an experienced colleague for their method. There is no shame in the standard approach. Finish the job the proven way and rest afterward.",
"major-05|wands-10":"Rules, procedures and inherited responsibilities have piled up. Review which of them still serve the work. Ask your manager or mentor which can be retired, and propose a simpler version. Duty is worth honoring, but not every old obligation needs to stay.",
"major-05|wands-11":"A new interest needs a teacher. Find someone experienced in the area, sign up for training or ask to shadow them. Established knowledge gets you further than reinventing the wheel. Show curiosity and respect, and you will learn faster than you expect.",
"major-05|wands-12":"You want to move fast, and the system asks for procedure. Get to know the rules, then work quickly inside them. A supervisor or mentor may open doors that speed alone cannot. Impatience with process usually costs time, while informed effort saves it.",
"major-05|wands-13":"Confidence with principle makes you a natural mentor. Share what you have learned, and lead by example. Colleagues will trust someone who is warm, sure and consistent about standards. Take on a teaching role, or back a newer colleague, and enjoy being a reference point.",
"major-05|wands-14":"Leading with a vision that respects the institution is powerful. Learn the rules, then guide change through them. Credibility with established people gives your ideas weight. Set an example of standards and teach others how you work, so the direction outlasts you.",
"major-05|cups-01":"An offer arrives through an established path, such as a company program, a mentor or a professional association. Learn how things are done before you try to change them. Ask a seasoned person for guidance on the offer. A proven route can carry your enthusiasm further than improvising.",
"major-05|cups-02":"A formal agreement between equals is favored. Use standard contracts, clear procedures and the proper channels. A mentor or an established organization may help bring both sides together. Following the accepted way of doing things reassures everyone and builds the trust that supports a long working relationship.",
"major-05|cups-03":"Company traditions, team rituals and professional gatherings can be valuable now. Attend the training, the annual dinner or the association meeting, and talk to people who have been there a long time. A professional community gives you access to knowledge and goodwill that a solo effort cannot.",
"major-05|cups-04":"Boredom with the standard path may be a sign to ask questions. Is the career script you are following actually yours? Talk to a mentor about alternatives within your field before you set aside your training. Learning something new inside a known framework may renew your interest without starting over.",
"major-05|cups-05":"After a setback, lean on established support: a mentor, a professional network, a union or an HR process. Ask how others in your field recovered from similar losses. Proven steps can make a difficult time more manageable. The disappointment is real, and you have more guidance available than you may think.",
"major-05|cups-06":"An old mentor, a former teacher or an early lesson comes back with value. Contact someone who trained you and ask for advice on your current path. Methods you learned long ago may still serve you. Bring them up to date, and pass on what you know to someone newer.",
"major-05|cups-07":"Numerous options can be sorted by asking experienced people. Talk to a mentor, or study how established professionals in your field chose their path. Favor reputable routes over the shiniest idea. The tried and tested option may lack glamour, but it often has firmer ground.",
"major-05|cups-08":"Departing from the expected path can be a considered choice. You may be leaving a conventional route, such as a set career ladder, for something that suits you better. Seek advice from someone who has made a similar shift. Respect what the old path taught you as you move toward the new one.",
"major-05|cups-09":"Contentment comes through doing things the established way. Your steady adherence to good practice, training and professional standards is paying off. Enjoy it, and consider teaching what you know to others. Your wish for the next stage is best served by continuing to learn from those who have gone ahead of you.",
"major-05|cups-10":"A workplace with strong traditions and a sense of community is highlighted. Learn its history, take part in its customs and find a mentor. If you have authority, pass on knowledge generously. Belonging in a professional community is built through participation, and it provides support long after a single project ends.",
"major-05|cups-11":"A keen beginner, perhaps you, is eager to learn. Find a teacher or a training program and follow it faithfully for a while before improvising. Written guides, apprenticeships and structured courses suit this energy. Curiosity paired with a good mentor grows into real skill.",
"major-05|cups-12":"A proposal arrives through a respectable channel, and its polish may impress you. Check that the organization's actual practices match its promises. The Knight might be a persuasive recruiter or client, or your own idealism. Ask a mentor to read the offer with you before you respond.",
"major-05|cups-13":"Caring leadership guided by principles suits your workplace. The Queen may be a mentor who teaches with warmth, or the role you take on. Share what you have learned with others, patiently. Empathy and established best practice together make you the person colleagues ask when they are unsure.",
"major-05|cups-14":"Ethical, experienced leadership is highlighted. The King may be a mentor or manager who leads with heart and integrity, or the leader you grow into. Uphold fair standards, explain your reasons and teach as you lead. Steady, respected guidance is valued in any organization and builds trust over time.",
"major-05|swords-01":"A fresh insight meets established methods. Learn the proven way first, then see exactly where your idea improves on it. Present it in language your organization already uses, and cite the precedent. A clear proposal that respects the process is likelier to be heard.",
"major-05|swords-02":"The way it has always been done clashes with what you sense is needed, and you are hesitating. Ask a mentor or senior colleague how they weighed similar choices. Then run a small trial of the new approach within the rules, and report the results.",
"major-05|swords-03":"Following the accepted route did not spare you a disappointment. Feel it, then consult someone with more experience. Mentors and professional communities can help you make sense of a setback, and learning how others recovered will speed your own steps.",
"major-05|swords-04":"Respect the tried-and-true rhythm of rest that your field and colleagues follow. Take your leave or the slow season, and do not apologize for it. A trusted mentor can tell you when to pause, and returning after proper rest is standard practice.",
"major-05|swords-05":"A quarrel over procedure or seniority is draining. Stand by what is fair, but appeal to shared standards rather than personal victory. Use the formal channels, such as a mediator or HR, if you need them. Winning by the book but losing goodwill is a costly result.",
"major-05|swords-06":"A move to calmer waters can follow a proven route, such as training, a qualification or a transfer through established channels. Ask someone who has made a similar shift how they did it. A well-worn path is often the smoothest way to leave a difficult situation.",
"major-05|swords-07":"Formal rules exist, and someone may be sidestepping them. Check the policy, keep your own record clean, and raise concerns through official channels. If you are tempted to bend a rule quietly, ask a trusted mentor first. Integrity matters more than any clever workaround.",
"major-05|swords-08":"You feel bound by convention, hierarchy or an unwritten career script. Ask a mentor which limits are real and which are habit. Study how others in your field found room to move, and take an approved course, certificate or lateral step that widens your options.",
"major-05|swords-09":"Fear of falling short of expectations wakes you at night. Ask a mentor or a trusted colleague what is actually expected, since it is often less than you fear. Follow a familiar routine to wind down, and write the standards down so you can measure yourself by them.",
"major-05|swords-10":"An established path, such as a role, a track or a program, has finished. Mark it properly with a handover, a thank-you or a farewell. Then ask mentors and peers how they moved forward, and find the next recognized step, even if it is a fresh start.",
"major-05|swords-11":"A student's mindset serves you well. Ask thoughtful questions of a mentor, watch how seasoned people handle situations, and take notes. Learning the standard methods carefully before improving them earns respect. Being known as someone who listens and asks well is a real career asset.",
"major-05|swords-12":"Impatience meets an established process. You may be tempted to bypass the committee or the old procedure. Move fast within the rules, ask for approvals early, and explain your urgency. Direct communication with the people who own the process will help more than working around them.",
"major-05|swords-13":"Clear judgment and respect for professional standards work well together. Give your assessment plainly, cite the accepted practice, and be candid where it falls short. Mentoring others with honest, well-grounded feedback will build your reputation as someone whose word can be relied on.",
"major-05|swords-14":"Authority, precedent and principle line up. If you lead, decide according to clear standards and explain them. If you work under such a leader, learn the rules of the house and follow them well. Institutions run best when fairness and continuity are both taken seriously.",
"major-05|pentacles-01":"A recognized route into something solid is available: a training program, an apprenticeship, a formal position. The known way may be slower, but it gives you a firm base. Ask what qualifications are expected, and start on the first one.",
"major-05|pentacles-02":"Juggling tasks goes easier with proven methods. Ask someone experienced how they manage it, and borrow what suits you. Following an established workflow may free your mind for the parts that actually need original thought.",
"major-05|pentacles-03":"Learning from a respected team pays off. Follow the group's established standards, ask for guidance, and take pride in getting details right. Belonging to a good tradition of work makes you better, and lets you eventually pass it on.",
"major-05|pentacles-04":"Loyalty to the old way may be holding you back. Rules protect, and so does learning. Talk to a mentor about which practices matter and which have simply become habit. Adjust one procedure, and see how it goes.",
"major-05|pentacles-05":"In a lean stretch, established support systems can help: professional associations, training programs, mentors, community resources. Do not be too proud to ask. Others have walked this road before you, and their guidance can shorten yours considerably.",
"major-05|pentacles-06":"A mentor or teacher may offer guidance, and you may offer help in return. Fair exchange in learning and pay is the theme. Respect the customs of your workplace, and speak up politely when an arrangement seems uneven.",
"major-05|pentacles-07":"Look at your progress against the proven path. Are you where someone following this route would expect to be? Seek a mentor's assessment, then adjust. Patient, orthodox effort tends to pay off, and honest review keeps it on track.",
"major-05|pentacles-08":"Apprenticeship is the theme. Study under someone with experience, follow their methods, and repeat the fundamentals until they are second nature. Mastery of the basics gives you freedom later. Give the routine time before you decide it is not for you.",
"major-05|pentacles-09":"Independence built on solid training is a good place to stand. You know the rules and can apply them your own way. Consider teaching what you know to someone starting out. Handing on your knowledge deepens your own.",
"major-05|pentacles-10":"Institutions and long-standing practices matter. Consider how your work fits into a larger tradition, and how you might help it endure. Documenting methods, mentoring newcomers and keeping high standards are ways of building something lasting.",
"major-05|pentacles-11":"Formal learning is favored now. Enroll in a structured course, find a supervisor, follow the curriculum, and do the exercises. Your first real attempts will be rough, and a good teacher will help you refine them. Respect the process.",
"major-05|pentacles-12":"Following the proven method, step by step, will get you there. It is unglamorous, and it works. Keep to the procedures, meet your obligations, and let colleagues see how consistent you are. Reputation grows from such habits.",
"major-05|pentacles-13":"Combine practical skill with respect for how things are done. You can support colleagues, teach newcomers, and keep standards high, all while keeping the place running smoothly. Being both capable and kind makes you the person others turn to for advice.",
"major-05|pentacles-14":"Recognized mastery and formal standing go together. You may be a teacher, an authority in your field, or someone being asked to act as one. Lead with integrity, honor the standards of your craft, and pass on what you know.",
"major-06|major-07":"A clear decision unlocks momentum. Choose the option that reflects your values, tell the people involved, and commit your energy. Half-hearted effort scatters. Aligned choice plus focused drive gets a project or a career move rolling.",
"major-06|major-08":"A choice about people or values calls for calm courage. Stay steady with a difficult colleague, and hold to what you believe without hardening. Patience and integrity make working relationships that last. Consistent kindness earns real trust.",
"major-06|major-09":"Before choosing between paths or partners, spend time alone with the question. Write down what you value and what each option costs you. Consult one wise person, not a crowd. A decision made from clarity feels lighter.",
"major-06|major-10":"An opportunity or change forces a choice. Timing is partly outside your control, so decide by your values and keep options open where you can. Say yes to what matches who you are becoming, and let the rest pass.",
"major-06|major-11":"A decision has consequences, so look at it clearly. Weigh facts and values, and make sure the choice is fair to the people it touches. Put agreements in writing. Choose what you can stand behind when asked about it later.",
"major-06|major-12":"Decision time arrives, and you are stuck. Rather than forcing it, view it from other angles: what would each option look like in a year? Wait for missing information, and consult the people affected. Good choices sometimes require patient delay.",
"major-06|major-13":"A decision means closing one door. Choose the path that fits your values and release the other with respect. Tidy up commitments, and communicate the change plainly. What ends now makes space for work that suits you better.",
"major-06|major-14":"Good partnerships form through steady collaboration. Blend your strengths with a colleague's, and stay flexible about method while agreed on values. Share the load fairly and adjust often. Balanced cooperation makes the long middle of a project easier.",
"major-06|major-15":"A tempting offer or working relationship may not match your values. Consider whether you are drawn by real fit or by comfort, status or fear of missing out. Look at the fine print honestly. Choose the path that lets you keep your integrity.",
"major-06|major-16":"An abrupt change forces a hard decision about people or direction. Take a breath, gather facts, and choose in line with your principles. What falls apart may have been a poor fit. Rebuilding with better alignment is possible.",
"major-06|major-17":"A hopeful partnership or shared vision is forming. Choose collaborators who share your values, and put your energy into what inspires you. Recovery from a hard patch is easier with good company. Keep your goals honest, and keep them modest at first.",
"major-06|major-18":"A decision feels uncertain because information is incomplete. Hold off on committing until the picture clears. Ask for details, and pay attention to gut discomfort without letting it snowball. Your values remain a steady guide.",
"major-06|major-19":"Working with people you like and respect is a real asset. A choice that reflects your values is paying off. Say thank you, share your happiness in the work, and build on what is going well. Enthusiasm is contagious on a good team.",
"major-06|major-20":"A call to reassess your path aligns with what you value. Review your record honestly, then choose the work or partnership that fits who you are now. Act on the answer with one visible step, such as a conversation or an application.",
"major-06|major-21":"A meaningful collaboration reaches its goal. Choices made from shared values have paid off. Celebrate with the people who helped, and record what worked. Completion is also a good moment to decide what you want your next project to say about you.",
"major-06|wands-01":"A promising opportunity comes with a choice attached. Before you leap, ask if it fits what you value about your work and the people involved. Take the first step only when the answer feels honest. Aligned effort lasts much longer than a burst of enthusiasm.",
"major-06|wands-02":"You are choosing between directions, and the planning matters. Lay out each option with its cost and how well it fits your values. Ask a trusted colleague for a second view. The best choice is the one you can still stand behind a year from now.",
"major-06|wands-03":"You have chosen your direction and now the work has to mature. Stay aligned with what matters while results come in. Check in with partners or collaborators to keep everyone on the same page. Patience is easier when you know why you chose this.",
"major-06|wands-04":"Agreement and a sense of belonging are in the air. A partnership, team or role that suits your values is worth celebrating. Take a moment to acknowledge it, and thank those who helped. Work that matches what you care about feels lighter, so protect that fit.",
"major-06|wands-05":"Conflict at work may be a values clash rather than a personal one. Ask what each person is trying to protect. Name the shared goal, and decide together how to pursue it. Sorting out the real difference will save more time than winning the point.",
"major-06|wands-06":"Recognition arrives for work that reflects what you value. Accept it without apology and credit your allies. Being known for integrity has real weight. Let this win guide which projects and partners you say yes to next.",
"major-06|wands-07":"You are defending a decision that reflects your principles. Keep your reasons clear and calmly hold your position. Not every challenger deserves a long reply. Focus on the allies who share the goal, and let your steady conduct answer the critics.",
"major-06|wands-08":"Events are speeding up and decisions are due. Make choices from your values instead of the pressure of the moment. Respond quickly to what is clear and ask for time on what is not. Alignment lets you move fast without regretting where you landed.",
"major-06|wands-09":"After a long push, ask if you still believe in the goal. Take a real break before deciding anything big. If the values still line up, finish strong. If they no longer do, that is worth noticing now, and you can plan a change thoughtfully.",
"major-06|wands-10":"Too many yeses have piled up. Review your load against what you value: which tasks fit your real priorities, and which are habit? Keep the ones that match. Say a courteous no to the others, so the ones you hold are ones you chose.",
"major-06|wands-11":"A fresh interest could become a direction if it fits your values. Test it with a small project before you rearrange your schedule. Ask what draws you to it, and whether it aligns with the people and purpose you care about. Curiosity can guide choices.",
"major-06|wands-12":"Fast momentum needs a clear reason behind it. Before you push, confirm that the direction fits your values and your partners' as well. Aligned drive is powerful, while unexamined speed creates cleanup. Choose the goal, then go at full speed.",
"major-06|wands-13":"Warm and self-assured, you make choices that people respect. Lead in a way that shows your values, and invite collaborators who share them. Choose partners and projects carefully. Others will follow someone whose confidence and principles point the same way.",
"major-06|wands-14":"Vision is stronger when it matches your values. Set a direction you can defend, and choose the people who share it. Lead by making clear decisions and explaining why. A team that understands the purpose will move with more heart, and less friction.",
"major-06|cups-01":"An attractive offer arrives, and it asks you to pick. Compare it with what you value about work: purpose, people, pace. Let your first gut response count, then check it against your priorities. A good choice usually feels right in both the gut and the plan.",
"major-06|cups-02":"A partnership works best when both sides choose it freely and share core values. Discuss what you want to achieve and how you want to treat people along the way. A collaboration based on aligned principles tends to weather disagreements. Choose colleagues you would be proud to work beside.",
"major-06|cups-03":"A decision about which team, group or circle to join is at hand. Choose the one whose values and mood match yours, not simply the most fun. Building alliances at work is a choice with lasting effects. Be honest about what you offer and what you want in return.",
"major-06|cups-04":"Restlessness may point to a mismatch between your work and your values. Write down what matters most to you, then compare it with your day-to-day tasks. The gap shows what to negotiate or change. Choosing on purpose, instead of drifting, often brings interest back.",
"major-06|cups-05":"A setback puts a decision in front of you: how to respond. Grieve what was lost, then choose the values you want steering your next steps. Pick the response you will still respect a year from now. Your integrity is what remains intact, and you decide how to use it.",
"major-06|cups-06":"A past connection or former role presents a choice. Consider whether to return, reconnect or leave it as a good memory. Compare it with your present goals and values. Familiarity is comforting, so name what you truly want from the next chapter and choose the path that serves it.",
"major-06|cups-07":"Too many appealing options call for a clear choice. Rank them by how well they match your values and abilities, not by how exciting they sound. Choose one, tell someone about your choice, and give it real effort. Deciding well means letting go of the rest.",
"major-06|cups-08":"A deliberate move away from a situation that does not match your values is highlighted. Name what you want to keep and what you cannot accept. Talk to trusted people and take the time to choose well. Leaving for reasons you can explain calmly tends to lead to a better fit.",
"major-06|cups-09":"Your work choices line up with your values, and it shows in your contentment. Recognize the decisions that got you here. When your next wish comes, test it against the same standards. Fulfilling work usually comes from a series of honest choices rather than one lucky break.",
"major-06|cups-10":"A workplace that fits your values gives a real sense of belonging. Choose employers, teams and projects that match what you believe in. If you lead, hire and reward people who share core principles. When personal values and organizational culture align, effort feels meaningful rather than draining.",
"major-06|cups-11":"A creative idea or fresh start brings a choice about which path to follow. Choose the one that reflects your values and interests. The Page may be a newcomer to your team, or your own beginner's mind. Say yes to what excites you, and say it with a plan for the first small step.",
"major-06|cups-12":"An appealing proposal requires a decision. Weigh it against your values: does it fit the kind of work and people you want? The Knight may be a persuasive colleague or your own enthusiasm. Ask questions, sleep on it, and answer in a way you can stand behind.",
"major-06|cups-13":"Compassionate decision-making is highlighted. The Queen may be a colleague who weighs people's feelings when choosing, or the role you play. When you face a decision, consider its impact on others and on your principles. Empathy guides you toward choices that are both ethical and effective.",
"major-06|cups-14":"Composed, values-driven decision-making is the theme. The King may be a leader who chooses wisely under pressure, or the leader you are becoming. Weigh the facts and the human impact, decide, and stand by it. Good choices made calmly build the respect of colleagues.",
"major-06|swords-01":"A sharp insight about what you value clarifies a career choice. Write down which priorities matter most to you, such as pay, purpose, people or time, and test each option against them. Then speak to the person who can act on your decision.",
"major-06|swords-02":"Two options have equal pull and you are stuck. Write your top three values, then score each option against them. Talk to someone you trust, set a decision date, and commit. Taking neither path is also a choice, and it usually costs more.",
"major-06|swords-03":"A decision that seemed right has brought disappointment. Grieve it briefly, then review what you valued when you chose. Learn from it without rewriting history. Talk with the people affected, and use the experience to make your next choice better aligned with your priorities.",
"major-06|swords-04":"Do not decide while exhausted. Take a break, sleep on it, and return when you are rested. A well-aligned decision comes more easily to a settled mind. Put a deadline on the rest so it does not become avoidance.",
"major-06|swords-05":"A workplace conflict turns on differing values, such as speed against quality or loyalty against fairness. Identify what each side is really protecting. Look for a solution that respects both, and be willing to give up a point. Being right is less useful than being aligned.",
"major-06|swords-06":"A move toward calmer conditions reflects a choice about what matters. Decide which values you will not trade for the sake of status. Plan the transition in steps, and let your priorities guide which offers or roles you pursue.",
"major-06|swords-07":"A choice between the easy shortcut and the honest route is on the table. Ask whether the path aligns with what you stand for. Discuss the plan openly with the people affected. Choices that need secrecy to work usually conflict with your values.",
"major-06|swords-08":"You feel trapped by a choice you believe you cannot make. Write down what you value, and what each option costs and gives. Challenge the story that only one path exists. A single honest conversation with a mentor or manager may widen the options.",
"major-06|swords-09":"A career choice weighs on you at night. In the morning, list what matters most and what each option offers. Small fact-finding steps, like a conversation or a trial task, can ease the dread. Decisions made from values rather than fear tend to hold.",
"major-06|swords-10":"A path you chose has run its course. Acknowledge what it gave you, and close it thoughtfully. Then ask what you value most in the next chapter of your work, and let that guide the direction. Endings clarify priorities in a way that few other things do.",
"major-06|swords-11":"A good question can guide a choice. Ask colleagues, mentors or prospective employers how they decide things and what they value. Observe carefully before committing. Learning what an organization or partner truly stands for saves you from a poor match.",
"major-06|swords-12":"Fast, blunt action meets a choice that needs care. Decide quickly on the small things, but give the big one proper thought. State your position directly, invite responses, and agree on the values that will guide the decision before you commit to a direction.",
"major-06|swords-13":"Clear, unsentimental judgment supports a values-based choice. Weigh each option by what it offers and what it costs you in principle. Speak plainly with those affected. Choosing the right fit over the flattering offer is often the wiser decision.",
"major-06|swords-14":"Principle guides a decision that matters. Set out your criteria, weigh the options fairly, and explain your reasoning to those affected. A decision aligned with your values and made on sound judgment tends to earn respect, even when others would have chosen differently.",
"major-06|pentacles-01":"An opportunity asks you to choose based on what you value, not just what flatters you. Compare it with your real priorities: the kind of work, the people, the way you want to live. Then say yes or no clearly.",
"major-06|pentacles-02":"Competing commitments force a decision about what matters. Look at each one in light of your values. Which fits the work and life you want? Drop or postpone one, and put your energy where it counts most.",
"major-06|pentacles-03":"A good team is one where people share values. Look at who you work with and whether you agree on standards and purpose. Where you do, collaborate closely. Where you do not, say so honestly and find a way to align.",
"major-06|pentacles-04":"Security is one value among several. If you are staying where you are out of habit or fear, check it against what you actually care about. A steady position that fits your values is worth keeping. One that does not deserves a fresh look.",
"major-06|pentacles-05":"A tight time can tempt you to compromise on what matters. Stay clear about your values while you look for support. Ask for help from people who respect you, and choose your next step based on principle, not panic.",
"major-06|pentacles-06":"Fair dealing is a matter of values. Consider how you treat colleagues and how they treat you when nobody is watching. Choose to work with people who deal fairly, and be one. Speak up if an arrangement no longer sits right.",
"major-06|pentacles-07":"Now is a good time to assess whether your efforts match your values. Look at your results and ask if this is the crop you wanted. If it is, continue with patience. If not, this review is your chance to choose a different field.",
"major-06|pentacles-08":"Devote your practice to work you believe in. Repeating the same task is easier when it matches what you value. If it does not, take that seriously. Pick a skill worth developing, and commit hours to it.",
"major-06|pentacles-09":"You have earned comfort by working in your own way. Check that your current path still reflects what you care about. If it does, enjoy it. If something feels off, listen. Your independence lets you adjust course thoughtfully.",
"major-06|pentacles-10":"Think about what you want your work to add up to over decades. Does the path you are on reflect what you value? Choose commitments, such as a team or a field, that you can stand behind for the long run.",
"major-06|pentacles-11":"A young career benefits from clear values. Study what you enjoy and what troubles you about a field before committing. Try small assignments to test the fit. Your first real experience of the work will show you what matters.",
"major-06|pentacles-12":"Show your values through reliable work. Doing what you said you would do, day after day, tells colleagues who you are. Choose tasks and employers whose principles match yours, and give them consistent effort, even when nobody is checking.",
"major-06|pentacles-13":"Caring and competent people choose their work carefully. Make sure your effort goes to something that reflects what you value, and to people you respect. Use your skill to support them well. Good fit brings out your best.",
"major-06|pentacles-14":"Mastery joined to integrity earns respect. Choose responsibilities that let you use your experience in ways you believe in. Lead by example, treat people fairly, and make decisions you would be content to explain openly to anyone.",
"major-07|major-08":"Drive and gentle mastery combine. Keep moving toward the goal, but manage your temper and your team with steady patience. Setbacks become manageable when you stay calm. Persistent, warm leadership wins cooperation that pressure cannot.",
"major-07|major-09":"Pause the sprint to check your bearings. Take a quiet day, ask one honest question about whether the goal is still yours, and look at the map. Then resume with better direction. A short retreat can save months of misdirected effort.",
"major-07|major-10":"Your plan meets changing conditions. Keep your direction, and adjust tactics as needed. Some things, like market swings or other people's schedules, are outside your control. Focus on effort and readiness, and take good openings as they come.",
"major-07|major-11":"Push ahead while keeping the books straight. Ambition is fine when your methods are fair and your records clear. Own the results, credit others properly, and meet obligations. Progress with integrity holds up when someone looks closely.",
"major-07|major-12":"Progress halts, and forcing it may make it worse. Treat the delay as a chance to reconsider the approach. Look at the project from a different role's viewpoint. When you restart, you will move with better information.",
"major-07|major-13":"A goal or route is done, and pushing on would waste effort. Close it out cleanly, then point your energy at something new. Endings feel abrupt when you are moving fast. Give yourself time to regroup before setting the next target.",
"major-07|major-14":"Sustainable momentum beats sprints. Pace your effort, mix urgency with reflection, and adjust as you learn. Balance workload and recovery. A project moved forward in measured stages usually arrives in better shape. Set a pace you could keep up for a whole quarter, not just a week.",
"major-07|major-15":"Ambition may be steering you. Ask whether the push for status, money or approval is serving you or trapping you. Overwork can feel like progress. Choose a boundary, such as a fixed finishing time, and see what that changes.",
"major-07|major-16":"An abrupt event derails your plan. Breathe, look at what is intact, and decide on the next practical step. Do not push ahead on autopilot. After the shake-up, you can set a more realistic course.",
"major-07|major-17":"Recovery meets forward motion. Point your energy at a goal that inspires you, and keep the pace sustainable. Hope is practical when tied to steps. Ask for support, celebrate small wins, and let your confidence rebuild as you move.",
"major-07|major-18":"Momentum is there, though the map is blurry. Push hard now and you may drive confidently down the wrong road. Get the missing details in writing, check the assumptions behind the plan, and let a trusted colleague test your route before you spend more hours on it.",
"major-07|major-19":"A clear aim and a good mood on the team make hard work feel lighter. Put your momentum into the project people already believe in, and say out loud what is going well. Visible progress now builds the trust you will need for your next request for more responsibility.",
"major-07|major-20":"Your effort is about to be measured, and you may sense a bigger question about where you are going. Review what you have actually delivered, own the gaps, and then choose a path on purpose. Meet the moment with focus instead of defensiveness, and let results speak.",
"major-07|major-21":"Sustained drive is paying off in something finished and whole: a launch, a certification, a project closed out well. Take credit, thank the people who helped, and write down what worked. Then pick your next destination on purpose instead of sliding into whatever is nearest.",
"major-07|wands-01":"Fresh energy gets a driver. Choose a target, put your whole effort into the first push, and keep your hands firmly on the direction. Momentum is an asset now. Start with a concrete deliverable this week, and let the path clear as you go.",
"major-07|wands-02":"Drive is high and the next question is where to aim it. Take a step back and look at the wider field: markets, roles, timing. Then commit to a route and go. Knowing the destination lets you use your energy well and reduces wasted effort.",
"major-07|wands-03":"Your effort has launched, and results take time. Stay disciplined: keep the routine going, keep sending updates, and do not swerve because of the delay. Momentum is built in the waiting as well as the moving. Use the pause to refine your route.",
"major-07|wands-04":"You have reached a base camp. Take a break, celebrate the progress, and refresh the team. Momentum is not lost by resting. Use this stable ground to check your route before the next stretch. A well-timed pause makes the next push cleaner.",
"major-07|wands-05":"Many strong drivers, one road. Friction is likely while several people push different ways. Set a common target and agree on who has the reins for each stage. Competition can sharpen the work as long as everyone knows where the finish line is.",
"major-07|wands-06":"Victory for a well-driven effort is arriving. Take the credit that is due, and share it with those who helped push. Reputation for finishing what you start opens doors. Use this moment to decide the next goal, while people are watching and ready to back you.",
"major-07|wands-07":"Pressure from rivals or critics is a test of your control. Hold your direction and keep your temper level. Pick the objections that deserve an answer, and ignore the rest. Disciplined progress is the most convincing reply you can give, and it costs nothing.",
"major-07|wands-08":"Speed and direction are both on your side. Deadlines, news and offers may come quickly, and you can handle it if you stay organized. Choose priorities and move with force. Keep a firm hand on the plan so speed does not carry you off course.",
"major-07|wands-09":"The finish is near and you are running on determination. Cut what is not essential and protect your energy for the last stretch. Ask for help if you need it. A controlled ending beats an exhausted sprint, so plan your rest as carefully as your work.",
"major-07|wands-10":"You are pulling too much weight in one direction. Lighten the load: share tasks, cut extra commitments, and set your route in phases. Determination is admirable, but a full cart slows you down. Pick the most important delivery and steer only that this week.",
"major-07|wands-11":"Curiosity can be aimed. Choose one new area to learn and give it a defined goal, such as a certificate, a sample or a trial run. Focused interest turns into skill quickly, whereas scattered interest does not. Set a first milestone for the month.",
"major-07|wands-12":"Full speed ahead, so someone had better hold the reins. Set the destination, then let the energy run. Watch for reckless corners and skipped steps that will slow you later. Fast, focused effort can move a stubborn project if you plan the route.",
"major-07|wands-13":"Poised and determined, you lead in a way that others want to join. Set your course and communicate it warmly. People will follow someone who moves with purpose and treats them well. Take charge of a project that needs both energy and a personal touch.",
"major-07|wands-14":"Vision plus drive make a powerful team. Name the goal, map the road, and set out with conviction. Bring your people along with clear roles and a shared timeline. Leadership shows in how consistently you pursue the target, and how you treat those beside you.",
"major-07|cups-01":"Excitement about an opportunity gives you drive. Set a clear goal and a timeline, then move. Use the boost to finish a draft or make the call you have been putting off. Enthusiasm is fuel, and direction keeps it from burning out in a week.",
"major-07|cups-02":"A partnership pulling one way moves fast. Agree on the destination and split the driving: who leads on what, and when you swap. If you work with a colleague, coordinate your efforts so you are not steering against each other. Shared momentum wins the day.",
"major-07|cups-03":"Team energy is high, and a shared push can achieve a lot. Rally your colleagues around a clear goal and keep the pace lively. Celebrate wins along the way to keep spirits up. Make sure the drive is steered by a plan, so the effort goes toward something worthwhile.",
"major-07|cups-04":"Effort without enthusiasm suggests a course correction. Take a short break and ask whether you are working toward your own goal or someone else's. Adjust your route: a new project, a different role, a new target. Forward motion feels better once it points somewhere you care about.",
"major-07|cups-05":"After a setback, you may want to push forward fast. Allow yourself time to absorb the loss, then set a realistic new goal. Take one step toward it each day. Driving on with determination is admirable when it is paired with honest acknowledgement of what happened.",
"major-07|cups-06":"A good memory of past work can motivate you as you move ahead. Draw on former successes, and keep your eyes on the present goal. Do not let nostalgia steer. Use your old network for a boost, then continue with new energy toward what is next.",
"major-07|cups-07":"Many appealing directions call for a decision. Choose one destination, map the first stretch and go. Progress comes from sustained effort in one direction, not scattered bursts in five. Give yourself a checkpoint in a few weeks to see whether it is working, and adjust from there.",
"major-07|cups-08":"A determined change of direction is underway. Leave the old setup with a clear sense of where you are headed. Prepare the route: skills, contacts, timing. Move with purpose so the shift feels like progress and not flight. Plan the next leg before you fully leave the last one.",
"major-07|cups-09":"Hard work is paying off, and you can feel it. Take a moment to enjoy the results of your persistence. Then set the next target so your momentum continues. Reward yourself and your team for what you have earned. Satisfaction fuels the next stretch of effort.",
"major-07|cups-10":"Determined effort toward a strong, happy workplace pays off. Lead the team with a clear goal and treat people well along the way. Build routines and traditions that keep everyone moving together. Success feels best when shared with people who helped, and you can create that atmosphere on purpose.",
"major-07|cups-11":"A creative spark meets the energy to pursue it. Begin now, with a short deadline and a simple goal. The Page may be a newcomer at work, or your own beginner's enthusiasm. Move steadily and let the idea take shape through action, not endless planning.",
"major-07|cups-12":"An exciting proposal arrives with speed behind it. Two energetic forces are heading the same way, so check the destination before you accelerate. The Knight may be a driven colleague or your own eagerness. Ask about the plan, agree on milestones and enjoy the momentum once the route is clear.",
"major-07|cups-13":"Compassion and determination together make a capable professional. The Queen may be a colleague who cares deeply and gets things done, or the role you play. Bring warmth to the way you lead a project. Empathy shows you where the team needs help, and drive makes sure it arrives.",
"major-07|cups-14":"Composed, decisive leadership is the theme. The King may be a manager who stays calm while driving results, or the leader you are becoming. Keep your emotions steady, set the direction and keep the team on track. Calm authority makes people willing to follow through challenges.",
"major-07|swords-01":"A focused idea meets the drive to pursue it. Define the target in one sentence, choose the first route, and go. Keep your eyes on the goal and check your progress weekly. Clear direction turns effort into results faster than extra hours.",
"major-07|swords-02":"Drive is present, but two routes are competing. Choose one and commit to a short trial. Momentum will show you whether it is right. Standing still, waiting for perfect certainty, costs more than a modest course correction later.",
"major-07|swords-03":"A setback has stalled your progress. Let yourself feel the disappointment, then review what went wrong without blaming yourself. Adjust the plan, and restart at a sensible pace. Determined effort works best after honest processing, not in place of it.",
"major-07|swords-04":"Driving harder without rest lowers the quality of your work. Schedule a proper break before the next push, and protect it. Rest is part of the plan. You will have more focus, better judgment and steadier energy when you come back.",
"major-07|swords-05":"Aggressive drive can win a battle and lose the team. Keep your ambition, but do not flatten colleagues on the way. Aim for results that others can share in, and let a gracious approach protect your reputation. Sustainable progress requires allies.",
"major-07|swords-06":"A deliberate move toward better conditions is underway. Keep the momentum but do not rush the handover. Plan the timeline, tell the right people, and finish current commitments well. The transition works best as a planned journey, not an escape.",
"major-07|swords-07":"Ambition may tempt you toward a shortcut. Check whether it costs someone else, or your integrity. Keep your direction, but move openly. Progress that depends on concealment tends to stall, and progress that others can see and trust has staying power.",
"major-07|swords-08":"Ambition is pressing against a sense of limits. Check which limits are real, and which are assumptions. Find the smallest step that moves you forward, whether it is a request, a course or a conversation. Motion, however modest, breaks the feeling of being stuck.",
"major-07|swords-09":"Worry about a target or a deadline is loud at night. Convert it into a plan: three steps, each with a date. Sleep, then act on step one. Drive works best when the mind is rested, and a small, concrete action will quiet the dread.",
"major-07|swords-10":"A push toward a goal has reached its end, whether it finished or was closed. Acknowledge the effort, and review what you achieved. Rest before choosing the next target. A clean stop makes the next start stronger.",
"major-07|swords-11":"Energy meets an inquiring mind. Point your drive at learning, ask sharp questions, and test ideas quickly on a small scale. Being known as someone who is both motivated and observant will open doors. Learn what you need, then move.",
"major-07|swords-12":"Momentum and bluntness combine into rapid action. That helps with a deadline, but check that the team is with you. Say what you need clearly, listen to objections, and adjust. Fast progress that leaves colleagues behind tends to be undone later.",
"major-07|swords-13":"Drive guided by sound judgment gets results. Set the destination, assess the obstacles honestly, and adjust the route without ego. Make your case with facts, and be candid about risks. Progress built on clear thinking is faster than progress built on hope.",
"major-07|swords-14":"Leadership and momentum combine. Set a course on clear principles, explain it, and keep the team moving. Hold yourself to the same standards you ask of others. Decisive, fair direction earns loyalty, and a shared destination makes hard work easier to sustain.",
"major-07|pentacles-01":"A real opening has appeared, and you have the drive to seize it. Choose a clear goal, plan the first few concrete steps, and start moving. Direction plus a solid foundation gives you traction that scattered effort never will.",
"major-07|pentacles-02":"Managing competing demands is the challenge. Set your destination, then judge every task by whether it moves you there. Say no to the ones that do not. A firm hand on the reins keeps the whole load rolling.",
"major-07|pentacles-03":"Team effort with a clear destination gets results. Agree on the goal, assign roles, and hold quality high while you move fast. Your drive and their craft complement each other. Keep everyone pointed the same way.",
"major-07|pentacles-04":"Drive is strong, yet you may be clinging to a safe position. Progress asks you to release something. Ask what you are protecting and whether it is worth the cost of standing still. Move one step toward the goal.",
"major-07|pentacles-05":"A hard stretch does not stop you from choosing a direction. Set a goal, however small, and take action every day. Reach out to people who can help. Forward movement, even slow, restores confidence and opens doors.",
"major-07|pentacles-06":"Ambition works better when shared fairly. Give credit, help others reach their goals, and accept help yourself. Success gained with fair dealing lasts. Be clear about your aims, and open about how the benefits will be shared.",
"major-07|pentacles-07":"Halt briefly to assess your route. Are your efforts producing what you hoped? Study the results with a clear head. Then set your direction again and press forward with adjusted plans. A moment of review saves months of wrong turns.",
"major-07|pentacles-08":"Focused, repeated effort takes you far. Choose your goal, set a regular practice schedule, and keep driving forward even when progress feels dull. Discipline turns ambition into skill. Trust the process, and measure progress weekly.",
"major-07|pentacles-09":"Self-made comfort gives you a base to aim from. Choose your next destination deliberately. What would you like to build or learn next? Use your steady footing to move ahead with confidence, knowing you have earned your position.",
"major-07|pentacles-10":"Point your energy at something built to last. Set a long-term direction for your career and pursue it with determination. Each day's work is a brick in the structure. Keep your eye on the horizon, and build steadily.",
"major-07|pentacles-11":"Ambition and study go hand in hand. Set a learning goal, sign up for a course or project, and commit to it. You will be clumsy at first, and that is the price of entry. Keep pressing forward.",
"major-07|pentacles-12":"Determination and reliability make an unbeatable pair. Pick a target, then work toward it methodically, one task at a time. It will not look dramatic, but it works. Colleagues will notice you finish what you begin.",
"major-07|pentacles-13":"Capable, caring leadership can drive a team forward. Set the direction, organize the resources, and take care of your people as you go. Progress that also looks after those doing the work is progress that lasts.",
"major-07|pentacles-14":"Mastery plus drive makes a strong leader. Set a clear course for your team, manage resources wisely, and hold yourself to high standards. Move with confidence, and stay open to advice. Authority earns respect when it serves the goal.",
"major-08|major-09":"Calm persistence plus focused solitude is a strong recipe for deep work. Block out interruptions and give a hard problem the time it needs. Come back with one thoughtful answer instead of five rushed ones. Your steadiness will be noticed more than any loud contribution.",
"major-08|major-10":"Conditions are changing around you: budgets, staffing, a market mood. Meet the turn with a level head instead of fighting it. Keep doing the reliable parts of your job well, and stay flexible about the rest. Calm people are the ones others lean on when things move.",
"major-08|major-11":"Firm, gentle handling of a fairness issue gets further than heat. Gather the facts, state them evenly, and keep your tone level. It may involve credit, workload, or a policy that treats people differently. Steady persistence with clear records is your best tool.",
"major-08|major-12":"A stalled project asks for patience, not force. Use the pause to look at the problem from a different side: the client's, the newest hire's, the person downstream. The fresh angle may solve what pressure could not. Hold steady and keep your temper.",
"major-08|major-13":"A role, routine, or team arrangement is finishing. Face it with composure rather than clinging or bitterness. Tidy your handover, keep your relationships intact, and stay steady while the shape of your work changes. What you build next rests on how gracefully you close this out.",
"major-08|major-14":"Slow, even effort wins here. Balance your workload so you can keep going for months, not just this week. Mix up your tasks, share duties, and stay level in meetings. A reputation for calm consistency is worth more than a sprint you cannot maintain.",
"major-08|major-15":"A habit at work keeps repeating: overwork, a difficult dynamic with a boss, chasing approval, or a paycheck you resent. You have the inner strength to loosen it. Name the pattern, set one small boundary this week, and hold it calmly.",
"major-08|major-16":"News or events break the plan without warning. Your job is to stay level while others panic. Sort what is actually damaged from what merely feels shaken, and take the next practical step. Composure now will be remembered once the reshuffle is over.",
"major-08|major-17":"After a rough stretch, steady effort begins to restore confidence. Keep showing up with patience, and let renewed hope guide small improvements: a better routine, a skill you practice, a relationship you repair. Progress may be quiet, but it is real and it builds.",
"major-08|major-18":"Part of the picture at work is hidden, such as unspoken politics, vague expectations, or a rumor. Resist reacting to shadows. Stay composed, ask direct questions, and wait for facts. Your steadiness will help you see more clearly than a hasty move would.",
"major-08|major-19":"Confidence built on real patience is showing. Colleagues feel at ease around you, and your steady handling of pressure has become a strength. Take on something visible, share the credit generously, and let your good energy help a struggling teammate.",
"major-08|major-20":"A moment of reckoning asks how well you have handled pressure. Review your own conduct calmly: what you did well, where temper or avoidance crept in. Then respond to the call with quiet confidence. A fair appraisal, taken gracefully, strengthens your standing.",
"major-08|major-21":"Long, calm effort has ended in a result you can be proud of. Acknowledge what it took: the restraint, the follow-through, the many small decisions. Present the finished work with quiet confidence, then rest before you start a new cycle.",
"major-08|wands-01":"A new venture needs steady hands more than loud ones. Start small and stay with it. Remain calm when the first attempts wobble, and let calm authority carry the team. Quiet confidence in the first move usually wins more trust than a grand launch.",
"major-08|wands-02":"Longer plans reward patience over pressure. Look at the wider picture and decide where steady, gentle persistence will get you further than force. Work out what you can manage calmly over a year. Strong, quiet planning beats panic, and it keeps your options open.",
"major-08|wands-03":"Your patient effort is paying off slowly. Stay calm during the wait, keep doing good work, and answer delays with courtesy. Steady, gentle persistence shows character that others remember. Use the time to build skills and relationships, not to fret over the outcome.",
"major-08|wands-04":"Your quiet consistency has earned a stable base, so use the breather well. Thank the people who kept things calm, then decide what deserves protecting before the next push. A settled team follows a patient lead much further than a loud one.",
"major-08|wands-05":"A noisy meeting or a clash of styles is useful if you stay steady. Let others push while you keep your tone level and your facts ready. Patience reads as strength here, and it often turns the argument into a better plan.",
"major-08|wands-06":"Recognition is arriving for effort you made without fuss. Accept it graciously and resist the urge to shrink it. Then use the goodwill: ask for the responsibility or the resources that fit the person people now see you to be.",
"major-08|wands-07":"Someone is testing your position, and you do not need to shout to keep it. Know exactly what you will and will not agree to, state it evenly, and repeat it as often as needed. Calm persistence outlasts pressure more often than force does.",
"major-08|wands-08":"News, deadlines or opportunities are coming in a rush. Do not match the panic. Sort what truly needs action today, answer that first, and give the rest a measured reply. Staying composed while everyone else scrambles is the advantage you bring.",
"major-08|wands-09":"You have been carrying a long stretch and you are still upright, which says a lot. Take that as proof of resilience, not a reason to grind on. Ask for one specific bit of help and protect one real hour of rest this week.",
"major-08|wands-10":"The pile on your plate has grown because you are dependable, not because it is fair. Name what you can hand off, and say it plainly without apology. Choose two tasks to carry well instead of ten to carry badly.",
"major-08|wands-11":"A fresh subject or side project is pulling at you. You have the temperament to learn it slowly and well instead of rushing to prove something. Give it a small, regular slot in your week and see whether the interest holds up.",
"major-08|wands-12":"A fast, impatient colleague or your own restless drive needs a steady hand. Channel the heat toward one clear goal instead of five. Slow down long enough to check the details, then move. Momentum guided by patience gets there intact.",
"major-08|wands-13":"Confidence with a soft touch is your best tool. Lead by being warm, direct and unbothered by drama. A person like this, in your team or in yourself, wins trust that no title can give. Speak up where you would normally soften.",
"major-08|wands-14":"Real leadership here is vision held with patience. Set the direction plainly, then give people room to reach it their way. Your calm under pressure will do more for morale than any speech. Decide what you are building and keep returning to it.",
"major-08|cups-01":"An opportunity arrives that appeals to you personally, not just on paper. Meet it with calm confidence instead of rushing to prove yourself. Let your composure do the persuading in meetings, and notice which tasks quietly energize you. Those are worth building a steadier path around.",
"major-08|cups-02":"A working partnership benefits from patience more than pressure. Whether it is a colleague, a client or a business partner, steady goodwill will get you further than pushing. Agree on terms clearly and kindly, and let trust build through small, reliable follow-through over the coming weeks.",
"major-08|cups-03":"Team morale gets a lift when someone stays calm and generous, and you may be that person. Celebrate a shared win, credit others by name, and keep your temper even when colleagues get rowdy or competitive. Being liked and being steady at once makes people follow you.",
"major-08|cups-04":"Routine has dulled your interest, and forcing enthusiasm will not fix it. Use your patience differently: ask for a different project, learn something adjacent, or reshape your role a little. Small changes made with steady effort can bring back the spark without upending a job that otherwise works.",
"major-08|cups-05":"A setback stings, and the loss deserves a moment. Then turn around and look at what remains: skills, colleagues, projects still standing. Handle the fallout calmly instead of blaming yourself or anyone else. Steady work on what is left will rebuild confidence faster than dwelling on the mistake.",
"major-08|cups-06":"Something familiar returns: an old colleague, a former field, a skill from years ago. Treat it as useful experience rather than a place to hide. Bring your gentle, steady manner to the mentoring or collaboration it invites, and check that the past is not being romanticized over present openings.",
"major-08|cups-07":"Ideas are plentiful and none feels solid yet. Your discipline is the missing ingredient. Pick the one option you can test cheaply for a month, and let the others wait. Calm follow-through will show which possibility has substance and which was only a pleasing daydream.",
"major-08|cups-08":"Something at work has lost its meaning, and you are ready to walk away from it on purpose. Do it with composure: finish what you owe, leave good relationships intact, and know what you are heading toward. A calm exit carries more strength than a dramatic one.",
"major-08|cups-09":"Steady effort has produced a satisfaction you can feel. Look over what you have built, from skills to a reputation for calm reliability, and let yourself enjoy it. A wish fulfilled is also a moment to decide what you want next, without grabbing for more from habit.",
"major-08|cups-10":"A team or workplace that feels like a community rewards those who stay patient and kind. Your steadiness is part of what holds it together. Speak up for shared wellbeing, keep workloads humane where you can, and see that lasting satisfaction comes from good people as much as from pay.",
"major-08|cups-11":"A young, creative or sensitive person, or your own fresh idea, needs a calm hand. Encourage without taking over. Give a new colleague patient feedback, or nurture your own early draft without judging it too soon. Gentle guidance brings out more than criticism, deadlines or pushing hard.",
"major-08|cups-12":"An attractive offer or proposal arrives, full of promise and charm. Your composure is the asset here. Ask practical questions gently, read the details, and see how the person or company behaves over time. Enthusiasm is welcome, but let steady observation confirm it before you say yes.",
"major-08|cups-13":"Empathy and composure make a strong professional combination. Someone with a caring, intuitive style, perhaps you, can settle a tense room and read what clients really need. Use it in negotiations, mentoring or customer work, while keeping boundaries so other people's stress does not become yours.",
"major-08|cups-14":"A leader who is warm yet level-headed shapes this situation. You may meet one, or need to be one. Listen to people's concerns, stay fair when emotions rise, and make decisions on facts as well as feelings. That balance builds trust and is rarer than it should be.",
"major-08|swords-01":"Your best argument is the one delivered without heat. Get your point down to two clear sentences, then present it steadily, even if someone pushes back. Calm conviction persuades more than volume, and it keeps a working relationship intact while the decision gets made.",
"major-08|swords-02":"A standoff softens when one side stays calm and says what it needs. If a decision is stuck between two options, ask what information is missing and go get it. Patient, plain talk with the person holding things up will move you further than another round of debate.",
"major-08|swords-03":"A stinging remark or a setback can be met without hitting back. Acknowledge that it landed, give yourself a day, then respond in the steady voice you would want from a manager. Composure after a disappointment is remembered longer than the disappointment itself.",
"major-08|swords-04":"Recovery is a skill, and you have earned some. Block a real pause after a demanding stretch and protect it from small requests. A rested, even-tempered version of you handles the next project better than a depleted one pushing on out of pride.",
"major-08|swords-05":"You may be able to win this round and still lose goodwill. Pick the battles that matter and let the small ones pass without comment. Staying composed while others posture gives you quiet authority, and the colleague you spare today may back you next quarter.",
"major-08|swords-06":"A move toward steadier ground is underway, and it rewards a slow, careful hand. Do not rush the handoffs or burn bridges while you transition. Bring your good habits and leave the grudges. Calm waters at work are usually built by people who stayed even-tempered on the way out.",
"major-08|swords-07":"A tempting shortcut or a quiet workaround is on the table. Strength here means doing it in the open, or not at all. If you sense something is being kept from you, ask calm, direct questions and keep notes. Integrity that stays steady tends to outlast clever moves.",
"major-08|swords-08":"You may feel boxed in by a belief that you have no options. Test it gently. List three things you could actually try, and pick the smallest. Slow, repeated effort loosens a stuck situation more reliably than a dramatic leap, and a trusted colleague can help you see the gaps.",
"major-08|swords-09":"Anxious thoughts about performance or money tend to crowd out sleep. Write the worry down, then write the next small action beside it. Calm, repeated habits steady a racing mind. Talk with someone you trust at work if the load feels heavy, because carrying it alone helps no one.",
"major-08|swords-10":"A project, role or way of working has run its course. Rather than fighting the finish, close it with quiet dignity: tidy the files, thank people, take what you learned. Your steadiness during the ending will be remembered and carries you into the next thing.",
"major-08|swords-11":"A new voice, perhaps yours, is asking sharp questions, and calm handling makes the difference. Welcome the questions instead of bristling. If you are the learner, watch closely and ask one thoughtful thing per meeting. Steady curiosity earns trust faster than a display of knowing everything.",
"major-08|swords-12":"A fast-moving colleague or your own urge to charge ahead needs a steady hand on the reins. Take a breath before hitting send. Keep the drive but soften the delivery, and check the plan with one calm person first. Momentum with patience reaches the goal with fewer bruised feelings.",
"major-08|swords-13":"Clear judgment and a calm manner are a strong pairing. Give feedback that is direct and kind, and expect the same. If someone in charge shows this balance, learn from it. If it is you, trust your read of the situation and state it plainly, without apologizing for being accurate.",
"major-08|swords-14":"Authority works best when it is patient. If you are making a ruling, explain the reasons calmly and hear objections before you finalize. If someone above you is deciding, prepare a clear, respectful case. Firm standards and a steady temper make people willing to follow.",
"major-08|pentacles-01":"Something concrete is opening, and calm handling will matter more than force. Answer the offer, the project or the introduction with steady confidence rather than hurry. Patience with a slow start is a skill in itself. Show up the same way each week and let people see your reliability.",
"major-08|pentacles-02":"Several demands pull at once, and staying calm is your best tool. Write down what genuinely needs you this week and let the rest wait without guilt. Treat your schedule and the household budget as habits you keep quietly, not crises you fight each time they flare.",
"major-08|pentacles-03":"Your steady temperament makes you the person a team wants beside them. Do the job properly, credit others, and stay even when someone else is rattled. Craft plus patience earns trust faster than talent alone. Offer to teach a skill this week and notice how the group strengthens.",
"major-08|pentacles-04":"Holding on to what feels secure may be wise, yet fear could be steering the decision. Calm strength asks whether you are protecting something valuable or just refusing to loosen your fingers. Loosen a little on something small, like sharing credit or a tool, and see what the team offers back.",
"major-08|pentacles-05":"A lean stretch can make you feel small, but you have more inner strength than the pressure suggests. Ask for help you are entitled to instead of enduring in silence. Small steady habits, like writing down where each week's effort goes, rebuild confidence one page at a time.",
"major-08|pentacles-06":"Fair exchange is the theme. Be generous with your time or knowledge, and let others give back without brushing it off. A calm conversation about workload or recognition goes further than a quiet grudge. Patience with how long fairness takes to arrive is part of the skill.",
"major-08|pentacles-07":"Look at the long game instead of the daily swings. Gentle persistence, not force, is what ripens a slow project. Ask honestly whether your effort so far points at the result you want, and adjust your habits, not your temper. Patience is a plan when it comes with attention.",
"major-08|pentacles-08":"Skill grows in the repeated hour. Sit down with a calm mind, do the same task slightly better than yesterday, and resist the urge to force a shortcut. Softness with your own mistakes keeps you practicing. Mastery goes to people who can stay gentle with themselves while they work.",
"major-08|pentacles-09":"Your independence and quiet discipline are paying off. Enjoy the ease you have earned instead of rushing to earn more. Steady habits and calm confidence in your own judgment are your real assets. You do not need to prove anything loudly to be respected.",
"major-08|pentacles-10":"Something long-lasting is being built, and gentle steadiness is the mortar. Think about what your effort will mean for the people who come after you: a team, a family, a business run on good habits. Do today's work in a way you would be proud to see repeated.",
"major-08|pentacles-11":"Learning with a calm attitude opens a door. Take the course, ask the questions and make the first real attempt while keeping your temper with yourself. Mistakes made gently teach more than perfectionism does. Set a small practice schedule and stay with it for a few weeks before judging.",
"major-08|pentacles-12":"Reliability combined with composure is a rare pair. Keep going at a measured pace and do not be pulled off course by louder colleagues. Progress may look unglamorous, but it adds up. Finish what you promised, keep your temper level and let your record speak for you.",
"major-08|pentacles-13":"Practical care and quiet strength make a good combination for looking after people and projects alike. This might be a colleague who steadies the room, or a role you play yourself. Use your resources sensibly and treat people with respect. Calm, consistent care outweighs a show of enthusiasm.",
"major-08|pentacles-14":"Authority is strongest when it is calm. Someone experienced, possibly you, can lead through patience and sound habits instead of pressure. Look after what you have been given with generosity and steady judgment. If you manage others, listen first, decide second, and let your consistency set the tone.",
"major-09|major-10":"Change is reaching your field or team, and stepping back to think will serve you better than reacting. Ask yourself one clear question about where you want to be in a year. Let the shifting conditions show you which door opens, and keep learning meanwhile.",
"major-09|major-11":"Take time to weigh a decision on its merits before anyone pushes you. Read the agreement, the policy, or the feedback carefully and by yourself. Once you see clearly, speak plainly and fairly. Careful thought done alone makes your case hard to argue with.",
"major-09|major-12":"Expect a quiet, suspended stretch: no news, no movement, and time to think. Do not fill it with busywork. Study the problem from a new angle, read, and reflect. When things start again you will move with better information and less noise.",
"major-09|major-13":"A chapter of your working life is closing, and reflection matters more than speed. Ask what you learned, what you would keep, and what you would leave. Take a few quiet days if you can. The next step will be clearer once the old one is truly finished.",
"major-09|major-14":"Thoughtful, solitary focus is best mixed with steady collaboration. Do your deep thinking, then share it in measured doses. Balance heads-down time with conversation so your ideas actually land. A patient, even pace helps you finish what you start.",
"major-09|major-15":"Step away from the daily churn and ask why you stay where you stay. Is it comfort, fear, a title, a paycheck? Naming the hold does not force any decision. It just gives you back your choice, which is where any real change begins.",
"major-09|major-16":"An abrupt change has shaken the ground. Before reacting publicly, take some private time to think. What was really at stake, and what was only habit? A clear, quiet review will help you rebuild on firmer footing instead of patching the old cracks.",
"major-09|major-17":"Reflection is leading to renewed direction. Something you thought through in private now feels worth pursuing. Share it with one trusted person and take a small first step. Modest, sincere effort beats a loud relaunch every time.",
"major-09|major-18":"The situation is unclear and you are tempted to withdraw. Do it wisely: gather facts on your own, separate what is known from what is rumored, and then ask direct questions. Do not decide anything major until the picture sharpens.",
"major-09|major-19":"Time spent thinking has paid off in clear, confident work. You know what you are doing and why. Let your insight become visible, whether through a presentation or a small win. Honest self-knowledge makes you easy for others to trust.",
"major-09|major-20":"A private review has led you to a question about your calling. Consider what you want your work to mean. Then act on it in a measured way: talk to someone, update your skills, or look at different roles. Listen first, then respond.",
"major-09|major-21":"Solitary study or long focused effort has led to real completion. You have mastered something. Let others see it, and step out of the back room. Finishing well means presenting well too, so share the result and plan what comes next.",
"major-09|wands-01":"Quiet thinking has produced an idea worth acting on. Before you tell everyone, write down the single question it answers and who needs it. Then take one concrete step, such as a draft or a call. Solitude gave you the spark, so let it lead.",
"major-09|wands-02":"Step back and look at the whole map. Time spent thinking is not wasted when it points to a bigger plan. Pick a direction after weighing the options honestly, and choose the path that matches what you value, not what looks impressive.",
"major-09|wands-03":"The heavy thinking and the first efforts are behind you, and the results need time. Resist filling the gap with busywork. Use it to review what you learned and prepare the next step. Patience is the task, and doing it well is real progress.",
"major-09|wands-04":"A pause has come after long, private effort. Step out of the lab and let others see what you made. A small gathering or team check-in could turn quiet work into shared momentum. Rest, then reconnect, and let your steadiness be visible.",
"major-09|wands-05":"Office friction and competing ideas are drowning out your judgment. Take a walk, then come back with one clear question you want answered. You do not need to win every argument, only to find the useful point inside the noise and act on it.",
"major-09|wands-06":"Recognition is likely to land on thoughtful work rather than loud work. Accept it, and share what you learned along the way so the credit turns into influence. People will listen to you more now, so choose your words with care.",
"major-09|wands-07":"Your reasoning has been tested and you believe your position. Stand by it, but stay open to a real fact that changes it. Prepare your case quietly, then present it evenly. Pressure from several sides is a sign you should sharpen your point.",
"major-09|wands-08":"Emails, updates and deadlines are landing all at once. Slow one step: decide what actually matters before responding. A short pause to think saves you from fast mistakes. Do the important thing well, and let the remaining requests wait their turn.",
"major-09|wands-09":"Fatigue has been building and you have held on anyway. Take a real break and reassess what is worth defending. You do not need to fight every battle. Use what you have learned to choose the two that count, and let the others go.",
"major-09|wands-10":"Your workload has grown past what one thoughtful person can carry. Step back and ask which duties are truly yours. Write the list, cross out what can be delegated, and say the plain truth about capacity. Clarity now prevents burnout later.",
"major-09|wands-11":"A new topic has caught your attention, and your reflective side can go deep with it. Give it a set period of study or experiment. Small, private trials are safe. You will soon see whether it deserves a bigger place in your working life.",
"major-09|wands-12":"Drive is high and the impulse is to sprint, yet the quiet voice says look again. Test the plan against one honest question before you commit. Then go at full speed, knowing you chose the direction on purpose and not in a burst of heat.",
"major-09|wands-13":"Inner clarity and outward confidence are meeting. Speak from what you have really thought through, and people will feel the difference. You do not need to be the loudest in the room. A calm, self-assured word at the right moment carries.",
"major-09|wands-14":"Your vision has matured in solitude, and now you can lead with it. Explain the reasoning behind your direction, not only the goal. People follow someone who has clearly thought it through. Decide what you will build, and share the map.",
"major-09|cups-01":"After a stretch of solitary thinking, something meaningful is offered: a role, project or direction that speaks to what you care about. Check it against your own values before saying yes. Work done alone this week may show what you really want from your career.",
"major-09|cups-02":"One-to-one collaboration works best for you now. Skip the crowded meeting and sit with a single trusted colleague or mentor. Bring your considered thinking and ask for theirs. An agreement made after quiet reflection tends to hold, and a fair deal begins with honest, unhurried talk.",
"major-09|cups-03":"A team event or group success is calling while you would rather think alone. Show up long enough to be seen and congratulate people, then take your solitary time to finish deep work. Balancing visibility with focus keeps colleagues on your side without wearing out your energy.",
"major-09|cups-04":"Disinterest in your current work asks for real reflection, not distraction. Take time alone to ask what you would want to be doing if it felt meaningful. Meanwhile, an overlooked offer or idea may be sitting right in front of you. Look at it once more before dismissing it.",
"major-09|cups-05":"A disappointment sends you inward, and some solitude will help you see clearly what went wrong and what is still yours. Do not isolate for too long, though. Take one insight from the loss, share it with a mentor, and turn towards the tasks and people that remain.",
"major-09|cups-06":"A quiet look back through your career shows the work you found most natural, back when you were less guarded. Let it guide your next move rather than pulling you backward. An old mentor, project or skill may have something valuable to teach if you approach it with curiosity.",
"major-09|cups-07":"Too many appealing options cloud the road, and stepping back is the right response. Spend quiet time separating ideas that suit you from those that only sparkle. Choose a criterion, such as what fits your skills and values, and use it to narrow the list to a single next step.",
"major-09|cups-08":"This is a deliberate departure from a role or routine that has stopped satisfying you, followed by a period of reflection. Do not fear the quiet stretch in between. Use it to define what you want next, keep your professional relationships in good order, and step forward when your direction feels clear.",
"major-09|cups-09":"Quiet satisfaction with work done well, possibly without much applause. You know your own standards and they have been met. Take a moment to enjoy that, then ask the one honest question: what would make the next stage of your working life more meaningful, rather than just bigger?",
"major-09|cups-10":"A workplace where you feel you belong still needs room for your solitary thinking. Ask for focused hours without guilt and offer your insight when the group needs it. Cultures that respect both contribution and privacy tend to hold their people, and you can help shape one.",
"major-09|cups-11":"A new creative idea or inquiry arrives, and it deserves quiet study before you announce it. Try it privately, follow your curiosity, and write down what you learn. A younger or newer colleague may also offer a fresh perspective worth hearing, if you slow down enough to listen.",
"major-09|cups-12":"An appealing proposal shows up just when you needed time alone to think. Slow down. Read the terms, consider whether it fits your long-term direction, and ask a trusted mentor for a view. Charm is not a plan, but a thoughtful yes after reflection can turn out well.",
"major-09|cups-13":"Intuition guided by reflection makes for good professional judgement. You can sense what a client or team is not saying. Give yourself quiet time before reacting, and trust your reading. Set clear boundaries so emotional labor does not become invisible extra work that no one notices or rewards.",
"major-09|cups-14":"A seasoned, thoughtful leader, or the leader in you, is guided by long reflection. Decisions are made with warmth and care but are not swayed by every mood. Offer counsel rather than commands, keep your composure under pressure, and take the time important choices deserve before announcing them.",
"major-09|swords-01":"A single clear idea appears once you step away from the noise. Schedule an hour with no messages and write down what you now see plainly about your direction. Bring that one point to someone whose opinion matters. Focused solitude produces sharper decisions than another meeting.",
"major-09|swords-02":"A stalled decision needs solitude more than more opinions. Set aside time to think alone with the facts, then ask yourself one honest question about what you want from this. Uncovering what you have been avoiding is the first step to moving past the standoff.",
"major-09|swords-03":"A disappointment at work may send you inward, and that is fine for a while. Step back, name plainly what stung, and consider what it teaches you about what you value. Then reach out to a mentor or friend so the lesson does not stay lonely.",
"major-09|swords-04":"Retreat and rest go together here. Take real time away from the noise of work, with your phone off if you can. A stretch of solitude helps you see what matters in your role. Come back with fewer tasks on your list and better reasons for the ones that remain.",
"major-09|swords-05":"When a workplace tussle leaves everyone scratchy, withdraw to think before the next round. Ask yourself what you truly want out of this, beyond being proven right. Return with one constructive proposal. Reflection turns a win-or-lose dispute into a problem both sides can actually solve.",
"major-09|swords-06":"Moving on from a difficult situation goes better with reflection than with speed. Take time to decide what you carry forward: skills, contacts, lessons. Leave the rest behind. A thoughtful transition, guided by one wise person you trust, sets up a calmer working chapter.",
"major-09|swords-07":"Step back from the scheming and look at what is really going on. Reflect on whether a shortcut fits your values, and on who benefits. A quiet review of the facts, perhaps with a mentor, helps you spot what is unspoken. Choose the path you can be comfortable with later.",
"major-09|swords-08":"Feeling trapped at work invites a quiet review. Sit alone with a notebook and write down the restrictions you assume are fixed. Circle those you have never tested. Then talk to a trusted mentor about one. Reflection often shows more open doors than panic did.",
"major-09|swords-09":"Sleepless work worries deserve daylight and a good listener. Take time apart to sort what is real from what is spiraling, then talk to someone you trust. A mentor's outside view can shrink a fear to a manageable task. Small, calm reflection each day steadies you.",
"major-09|swords-10":"A phase of work is over, and reflection is the right response. Spend a quiet day going through what worked, what did not, and what you would keep. Avoid rushing into the next role to escape the feeling. Some clear thinking now guides the next chapter well.",
"major-09|swords-11":"One well-chosen question beats a dozen quick opinions. Spend time alone working out what you really need to understand about your role or project, then ask the person who knows. Studying quietly and asking with care builds respect, even when you are the newest voice.",
"major-09|swords-12":"Charging ahead is tempting, but a pause to think pays back. Step away, review the plan, and ask if speed is serving the goal. If a colleague is pushing hard, hold your ground calmly and ask for time. A considered direction beats a fast wrong one.",
"major-09|swords-13":"Solitary thinking sharpens your judgment. Take a quiet hour to see the situation without wishful thinking, then speak with clarity. Someone in your workplace may be that discerning presence, so listen to what they say. Honest analysis, offered without drama, gets remembered.",
"major-09|swords-14":"An important decision is best made after solitary thought. Go over the facts, consider your principles, and reach a conclusion you can explain. A wise, experienced leader may be a resource: ask for their view. Authority earned through reflection carries more weight than quick verdicts.",
"major-09|pentacles-01":"Step back from the noise and ask one honest question about the opportunity in front of you. What would it really involve, and does it suit how you like to work? Give yourself a few quiet days to think it over. A grounded start beats a hurried one.",
"major-09|pentacles-02":"Too many plans at once cloud your judgment. Withdraw briefly from the juggling and ask which commitment matters most. A quiet hour with a pen and your calendar can settle what a week of scrambling cannot. Then arrange your habits around that answer.",
"major-09|pentacles-03":"Solitary thinking can improve what the team builds. Spend some time alone reviewing your part with a critical eye, then bring it back ready to collaborate. Quality comes from reflection followed by cooperation. Ask a trusted colleague for honest feedback, and give it the same way.",
"major-09|pentacles-04":"Caution has its place, and so does examining it. Ask yourself honestly what you are guarding and why. Reflection can show whether your careful habits protect you or only keep you small. Spend a quiet evening sorting what feels safe from what you could hold more loosely.",
"major-09|pentacles-05":"A hard, lean stretch calls for quiet thought more than panic. Ask one direct question: what is the next small, practical step? Look for support you have overlooked, like a mentor, a colleague or a local program. Being honest about your situation is where recovery begins.",
"major-09|pentacles-06":"Think privately about whether the exchange at work feels fair. Are you giving more than you receive, or the reverse? Once you see it clearly, raise it calmly with the right person. A private review first makes the conversation kinder and shorter. Recognition tends to follow clear, respectful requests.",
"major-09|pentacles-07":"Pause to judge how far your efforts have come. Go through the results with a lamp, not a hammer: what is working, what is not, and what deserves more time? A calm review now saves months of pushing in the wrong direction. Then decide where to keep your energy.",
"major-09|pentacles-08":"Solitary focus is the tool now. Close the door, take on the difficult skill and repeat it until it becomes second nature. Progress may be quiet and unseen for a while. Ask the one honest question: what part of this craft am I avoiding, and what happens if I face it?",
"major-09|pentacles-09":"Independence is serving you well. Enjoy the quiet, self-directed work you have built, and use the calm to think. You do not need approval to trust your own judgment. Ask what a comfortable, sustainable pace would look like, and protect it from other people's urgency.",
"major-09|pentacles-10":"Reflect on the long structure you are building. Ask what you want your work to stand for years from now, and check that today's habits point there. Quiet planning often outperforms frantic activity. Consider consulting an older colleague or mentor whose experience can serve as a lantern.",
"major-09|pentacles-11":"Studying alone can prepare you for the first real attempt. Find a mentor or a solid source, take careful notes, and then try something small. Do not worry about looking like a beginner. Asking one honest question of someone experienced can save you weeks of guessing.",
"major-09|pentacles-12":"Slow, careful work beats a sprint. Think through the method before starting, then keep going without fanfare. It may not be exciting, but it is dependable. Take an evening to plan the next month's steady steps, and follow them even when motivation dips.",
"major-09|pentacles-13":"Quiet, practical wisdom is available to you, perhaps through an experienced colleague or your own good sense. Pause to think about how you are looking after your people and resources. Sensible habits and an honest evaluation beat busywork. Ask advice from someone who has managed this before.",
"major-09|pentacles-14":"Experience is your lamp. Someone seasoned, maybe you or a mentor, can guide decisions on how resources are handled and what deserves protection. Withdraw for a short while to consider the responsible path, then act on it. Thoughtful leadership tends to be trusted for a long time.",
"major-10|major-11":"Timing and consequence line up. A recent change may reward your past reliability, or expose a shortcut. Keep your records straight, treat colleagues fairly, and act honorably while things shift. Decisions made with clean hands hold up when the wheel turns again.",
"major-10|major-12":"External conditions move on their own schedule while you wait for a decision. Rather than push, use the delay to consider the problem differently. Update your skills, listen to the people affected, and be ready to move when the wheel does.",
"major-10|major-13":"A large shift ends one phase of your working life. This could be a restructure, a project wrapping up, or a change of role. Do not cling. Finish tasks cleanly, keep good ties with colleagues, and get ready for what follows.",
"major-10|major-14":"Change is under way, and the best response is measured adjustment. Blend old methods with new ones, spread your effort evenly, and avoid extremes in either direction. A calm hand on budgets and schedules helps you ride the turn without losing your footing.",
"major-10|major-15":"A recurring loop keeps coming around: the same conflict, the same overwork, the same money worry. Timing matters, but so does your part in it. Spot what keeps you attached to the pattern, and change one behavior instead of waiting for luck.",
"major-10|major-16":"Big upheaval is here, and much of it is outside your control. A reorganization, a lost client, or a plan overturned. Focus on what is yours to influence: your reaction, your skills, your relationships. The wheel keeps turning, so keep your options open and your reputation good.",
"major-10|major-17":"Fortunes are improving after a difficult stretch. Use the calmer, brighter mood to restore what was neglected: relationships, routines, plans. Do the modest, sincere things that keep good timing working in your favor, and stay generous with colleagues.",
"major-10|major-18":"Circumstances are shifting and information is patchy. Resist making a major move based on rumor or a hunch. Wait for facts, keep asking questions, and hold to steady habits. Timing will clarify itself faster than speculation will.",
"major-10|major-19":"A positive turn brings visible success: recognition, a good match of person and role, or an unexpectedly smooth stretch. Enjoy it, give credit where it belongs, and use the confidence to take on something worthwhile while conditions favor you.",
"major-10|major-20":"Picture a pivotal moment: evaluation, promotion talk, or a clear fork in your path. Review your record honestly, and answer the moment with intention. Timing is helping you, but only if you respond instead of waiting for it to decide for you.",
"major-10|major-21":"A whole chapter finishes at the right moment. Your efforts and good timing combine into a meaningful completion. Acknowledge what you learned, tie up loose ends, and prepare for the next cycle. Ending well opens the door to what comes after.",
"major-10|wands-01":"A new opportunity is arriving on a wave of good timing. Take the first step while the door is open, but do not assume you control the whole path. Start small, stay adaptable, and remember that luck favors people who are already moving.",
"major-10|wands-02":"The ground is shifting, so your plans need a wider view. Map two or three ways the next year could go and decide how you would respond to each. You cannot control the tide, but you can choose a sturdy boat and a flexible route.",
"major-10|wands-03":"Ships are out and the tide has not turned. Use the interval to prepare rather than fret. Follow up once, politely, then work on something you control. Results tend to come when conditions align, and you can be ready for that moment.",
"major-10|wands-04":"A comfortable stretch has arrived, perhaps through good timing as much as effort. Enjoy it, and thank whoever helped. Then use the calm to strengthen what you have built, because cycles change, and a solid base will carry you through the next turn.",
"major-10|wands-05":"Disagreement is stirring as circumstances change. Do not take it personally. Ask what the friction is trying to point out, then adjust the plan. Teams that argue honestly during a turning point often come out with a much better direction.",
"major-10|wands-06":"A win has come, helped by timing as well as skill. Enjoy the recognition and give credit where it belongs. Then save some of the goodwill for harder days, because tides shift. Your reputation is built by how you act during both the highs and the lows.",
"major-10|wands-07":"Circumstances are changing, and someone may be pushing against your position. Hold what is essential, and let go of what is only habit. Standing your ground works best when you can tell the difference between principle and pride.",
"major-10|wands-08":"The pace is picking up and events are turning fast. Be ready to move when a message or offer arrives. Keep your priorities in mind so speed does not carry you somewhere you did not choose. Good timing rewards those who are prepared.",
"major-10|wands-09":"A long run of shifting conditions has left you tired but intact. Do not quit pacing yourself now. Trim the schedule to essentials, and let the cycle turn without forcing it. Endurance combined with good rest usually outlasts a bad season.",
"major-10|wands-10":"The load feels enormous, but it is a phase and not a permanent state. Look for what can be redistributed while circumstances are moving. Say no to something small this week. Relief often comes as much from a shifted approach as from luck.",
"major-10|wands-11":"A new interest or role is opening at a good moment. Explore it without demanding certainty. Ask questions, try a small task, and see where the current runs. You are early, and early learners often find the doors that others miss.",
"major-10|wands-12":"Change is coming quickly and you feel the urge to charge ahead. Momentum is useful when it points somewhere. Check the direction before you gain speed, and leave room to adapt when the wheel turns again. Bold and flexible beats bold alone.",
"major-10|wands-13":"Self-assurance is your best tool as conditions change. Speak with warmth and clarity, and others will look to you for steadiness. Keep your network friendly, since the next opportunity often comes through a person and not a posting.",
"major-10|wands-14":"A strong vision meets shifting timing. Lead by keeping the goal steady and the plan flexible. Tell your team what will not change and what might. People trust a leader who names uncertainty honestly and still chooses a course.",
"major-10|cups-01":"A change in circumstances delivers a welcome offer, and the timing feels like luck. Say thank you, then check the fit as you would with any opportunity. Cycles turn, so use this favorable moment well: make contacts, show your best work, and be generous without expecting a particular outcome.",
"major-10|cups-02":"A partnership forms or shifts as circumstances turn. Be ready to negotiate fairly, since the balance of power may change over time. Look for agreements that work in good seasons and lean ones alike. A colleague who meets you halfway during a period of change is worth keeping close.",
"major-10|cups-03":"A happy turn for the team brings something worth celebrating together. Acknowledge the roles of others and enjoy the moment, since good cycles do not last forever. Use the goodwill to strengthen working relationships, and remember that shared luck feels better and lasts longer when credit is spread around.",
"major-10|cups-04":"A change is coming through and you feel unmoved. Do not let indifference make you miss what passes by. Stay alert to a new project, contact or offer while your motivation sorts itself out. What feels stale now may be a pause between cycles, not the end of interest.",
"major-10|cups-05":"A reversal of fortune stings, and mourning it is natural. Circumstances shift both ways, though, and this one was not entirely about you. Review what you can still use, ask a colleague for support, and be patient. A lean period at work is a phase, not a verdict on your worth.",
"major-10|cups-06":"A cycle is bringing back something familiar: a type of project, a connection from a former workplace, a skill you once used daily. Treat it as a chance, not an obligation. Ask what worked before and what has changed. Your history is a resource, and the timing may serve you well.",
"major-10|cups-07":"Change opens a fan of options and you cannot yet tell which is real. Do not chase every one while luck is running. Gather facts, test the top two with small steps, and ask people who have done similar work. Substance shows itself over time, unlike glittering prospects.",
"major-10|cups-08":"The cycle has turned and your current path feels finished. Leaving on purpose, with clear reasons, matters more than leaving fast. Prepare quietly, keep your relationships in good condition, and trust that a new season is possible. Moving on with dignity gives fortune something to work with.",
"major-10|cups-09":"Things turn in your favor and a long-held professional wish is nearer to reality. Enjoy the satisfaction and thank those who helped. Cycles continue, so use this stretch to build habits, goodwill and skills that will cushion you when the next change arrives, as it always does.",
"major-10|cups-10":"Your working life is in a stable, happy phase in your working life, with a group you belong to and a purpose you share. Luck helped, and so did your contribution. Keep tending relationships, since environments that feel like home need care to stay that way, especially when leadership or circumstances change.",
"major-10|cups-11":"A creative opportunity or piece of news lands unexpectedly and asks for a light touch. Explore it with curiosity before committing energy. A newcomer, intern or fresh idea may prove more helpful than it looks. Timing seems friendly, so be willing to try something new and see how it develops.",
"major-10|cups-12":"A promising proposal appears at a moment when things are shifting. Charm and timing are on its side, so do your homework. Look at what is offered, who is offering it and what it asks of you. A good opportunity holds up to plain questions, and a fair one welcomes them.",
"major-10|cups-13":"Intuition helps you read where the wind is turning. Trust your sense of people during a period of change, and offer reassurance to colleagues who feel unsettled. A compassionate, steady presence is valued when circumstances shift. Protect your own energy so that caring for others does not leave you depleted.",
"major-10|cups-14":"A steady, diplomatic leader is needed while conditions change. Keep your composure, communicate kindly and make judgement calls based on facts, not mood. Some of what is shifting is beyond anyone's control, so focus on what your team can influence, and reassure people that you will handle it fairly.",
"major-10|swords-01":"The situation is turning, and one sharp idea is showing you where. Note it while it is fresh and act on it when the moment is right rather than early. Timing is partly out of your hands, so prepare your case and stay ready for the opening.",
"major-10|swords-02":"A standoff may resolve when outside conditions change, yet you do not need to wait passively. Gather the information you have been avoiding, decide what you can control, and let the rest turn on its own. Being prepared when the moment arrives beats being frozen in it.",
"major-10|swords-03":"A disappointment stings, but roles, budgets and moods all rotate. Acknowledge what happened without rehashing it. Keep doing quality work while circumstances shift, because the next turn may favor you. Lean on colleagues you trust rather than carrying the letdown alone.",
"major-10|swords-04":"Quiet periods come around, and this one is worth using. Rest, restock and refresh your skills while things are slow. Do not read a lull as failure. When the pace picks up again, you will meet it with more energy than someone who ran through the pause.",
"major-10|swords-05":"Fortunes at work rotate, so how you win matters. Avoid gloating over a small victory and avoid holding a grudge over a small loss. The person you clash with today might be your ally on the next project. Keep the record clean and your reputation generous.",
"major-10|swords-06":"A change of scene is coming, and the timing is largely not yours to set. Prepare what you can: tidy your work, update your skills, keep good relationships. When the opening appears, move steadily. A smoother stretch follows those who transition with care.",
"major-10|swords-07":"Circumstances are moving, and someone may be maneuvering while they do. Keep your own conduct clean and your records clear. Watch for a quiet shortcut that looks tempting as the ground shifts. Reliable habits outlast any single clever play, and you can hold your place on principle.",
"major-10|swords-08":"The sense of being stuck belongs to this stage, not to every stage. Things rotate, and you can shape which way. Identify one option you assumed was closed and test it. Small, repeated tries put you in position when the wheel does move.",
"major-10|swords-09":"Anxious nights about work are a stage, not a verdict. Circumstances rotate, and the pressure you feel now will look different in a season. List what you can influence, do one useful thing about it, and let sleep return. Rest helps you meet the next turn.",
"major-10|swords-10":"A cycle at work is complete. Accept it rather than replaying it, and mark it: thank people, gather lessons, close the loose ends. What comes next is not yours to hurry. Show up ready and useful, and let the next opportunity find you in good form.",
"major-10|swords-11":"Change is coming, and a fresh set of questions helps you prepare. Learn how the shift may affect your role and who is affected with you. Ask instead of guessing. The person who watches carefully while things turn usually finds the best position when they settle.",
"major-10|swords-12":"Momentum is building and you may feel pressure to move first. Check whether you are riding the turn or running ahead of it. A bold step made with a clear plan works better than a rush. Keep your tone measured even if others rush around.",
"major-10|swords-13":"When conditions turn, the person who sees plainly gains ground. Face facts about your position, drop wishful thinking, and communicate directly. Not everything is yours to hold, and clear-headed acceptance lets you spend energy where it matters. Someone with this steady view may help you.",
"major-10|swords-14":"Your authority and standards face changing conditions. Decide by principle instead of by mood, and explain your reasoning as circumstances shift. If someone senior rules on your situation, keep your case factual. Fairness during turbulence builds a reputation that lasts beyond the current round.",
"major-10|pentacles-01":"An opening arrives on a cycle you did not choose. Be ready to act when it appears, with your skills sharp and your paperwork in order. You cannot force the moment, but you can be prepared. Notice which changes in your field are creating room.",
"major-10|pentacles-02":"Shifting priorities call for flexible planning. Keep your schedule loose enough to absorb surprises and organized enough to keep your promises. Watch what is changing around you and adapt without panic. Good habits, like a weekly review of your tasks and spending, carry you through the swings.",
"major-10|pentacles-03":"A change in the team or the season of a project is turning things around. Do good work regardless, because craft keeps its value when circumstances shift. Collaborate generously with whoever the new arrangement brings you. Timing will do part of the job, and your standards will do the rest.",
"major-10|pentacles-04":"Change is moving through your work, and gripping the old ways tightly will wear you out. Keep what is sound, release what no longer fits and stay adaptable. Ask which of your habits give real security and which are merely comfortable. Flexibility is its own kind of safety.",
"major-10|pentacles-05":"A lean patch can turn, though not on command. Meanwhile, reach out for practical support instead of enduring in silence. Keep your habits steady and your skills growing so you are ready when conditions shift. Difficult times are lighter when you share the worry with someone trustworthy.",
"major-10|pentacles-06":"Roles are turning, and today's helper may be tomorrow's helped. Be fair and generous while you are in a strong position, and accept support without embarrassment when you are not. Good reputations are built on how you treat people in both seasons. Keep track of favors kindly, not competitively.",
"major-10|pentacles-07":"Results arrive on a timeline you only partly control. Keep tending your work, and resist the urge to dig up the roots to check. Review progress at a sensible interval, not daily. Patience combined with steady effort usually outlasts luck, and leaves you ready when luck does turn.",
"major-10|pentacles-08":"Fortune turns, and prepared people benefit most. Keep practicing your craft in the quiet hours so you are ready when the change arrives. You cannot control the timing, but you can control the quality of your work. Consistency is your best answer to uncertainty.",
"major-10|pentacles-09":"Circumstances will keep turning, and your self-built stability helps you ride them out. Maintain the habits that got you here, like steady effort and careful budgeting. Do not assume a good season lasts forever, and do not panic when a slower one appears.",
"major-10|pentacles-10":"Long structures move through cycles. A business, a career or a family enterprise will have good years and lean ones. Build habits that suit both. Think about what lasts beyond any single turn of fortune: relationships, reputation and skills others can rely on.",
"major-10|pentacles-11":"A learning opportunity is coming around, and you can be ready for it. Study the basics, and make a first real attempt when the chance shows. The moment is partly luck, but the preparation is yours. Treat early mistakes as part of the ride.",
"major-10|pentacles-12":"Reliability is your advantage when conditions change. Keep doing the job well at a steady pace while others react to every shift. Cycles move on, and a good record travels with you. Stay grounded, finish what you start and let the turning wheel bring what it brings.",
"major-10|pentacles-13":"Changes are underway, and practical care keeps things steady. Look after your people and resources with a calm hand. A capable, reassuring presence is valued most when things shift. Do the sensible, useful thing at each turn, and trust that good management holds up.",
"major-10|pentacles-14":"Fortune shifts, and experienced management weathers it. Draw on your seasoned judgment, keep your affairs in order and stay open to change. You cannot control the timing of good or lean periods, but you can build habits that endure both. Lead calmly.",
"major-11|major-12":"A ruling, review, or negotiation is on hold. Use the interval to examine the case from every side, including the one you dislike. When decisions arrive you will be prepared. Patience with fairness, not pressure, is the wise use of this delay.",
"major-11|major-13":"A role or arrangement ends as a logical consequence of how things developed. Do not fight the outcome, and handle the closing properly. Settle what is owed, be straightforward about what happened, and leave with your integrity intact.",
"major-11|major-14":"A steady, fair way of working is your strength here. Adjust workloads, credit, and expectations gradually so the balance holds. Avoid extremes: neither grand accusations nor silent resentment. Even-handed effort earns quiet respect from the people around you.",
"major-11|major-15":"Check whether the terms you work under are fair, and why you accept them. Fear, habit, or a sense of obligation may be keeping you bound to an unbalanced deal. Read what you agreed to and take a clear-eyed look at your options.",
"major-11|major-16":"Something comes to light and forces a quick reckoning. A mistake, a hidden problem, or an unfair practice is exposed. Stay factual, own your part, and focus on repairs. What gets built afterward needs to rest on honest foundations.",
"major-11|major-17":"A fair outcome brings relief and new optimism. Recognition, correction of an old error, or a more balanced arrangement lifts morale. Use that renewed energy sincerely, keep dealing openly, and plan modest improvements that will last.",
"major-11|major-18":"You have to make a fair call with incomplete information. Do not rule on rumor. Check documents, ask direct questions, and note which pieces are still uncertain. A careful, delayed decision beats a sharp one built on shadows.",
"major-11|major-19":"Fair treatment and clear results line up. Your honest work is recognized, or a dispute resolves in a way that feels right to everyone. Keep being straightforward. A good reputation built on plain dealing is a real asset.",
"major-11|major-20":"A formal or informal evaluation is due. Decisions will reflect real merit and past choices. Prepare by looking honestly at your record, and be ready to acknowledge what fell short. Owning results plainly strengthens your credibility.",
"major-11|major-21":"Fair effort and fair reward meet in a completed piece of work. A settlement, a contract fulfilled, a project closed on good terms. Record the lessons, thank those involved, and let the clean finish be a foundation for what follows.",
"major-11|wands-01":"A new project comes with real consequences, so start it with clear terms. Write down who does what and what success means. Fairness at the start prevents resentment later. Take the first step with your eyes open, and keep your word.",
"major-11|wands-02":"You are choosing between paths, and the sound choice is the one you can defend honestly. List the real pros, cons and costs. Think about who is affected. A well-reasoned plan gives you confidence and makes it easier to explain your decision.",
"major-11|wands-03":"You did your part and now results must arrive. Keep records straight and your commitments honored during the wait. Fairness is measured over time, so stay consistent. When the outcome appears, it will reflect what you put in.",
"major-11|wands-04":"A stable period is here, and it rests on fair dealing. Honor the agreements that got you here, and share credit generously. Use this calm to tidy contracts, roles or budgets so they remain clear. Good structure protects good times.",
"major-11|wands-05":"A dispute is on the table, and the fair path is to name the facts. Separate what happened from how it felt, then propose a balanced fix. Heated discussion is fine if it ends in clarity. Keep your integrity even when others do not.",
"major-11|wands-06":"A win is being recognized, and it is right that it is. Accept the credit and share it truthfully. If someone was overlooked, say so. Being fair when you are on top builds a reputation that lasts longer than any single victory.",
"major-11|wands-07":"You are defending a position, and it helps if it is truly fair. Check your facts, then hold firm. Present evidence, not heat. If the position holds up under scrutiny, it deserves your stubbornness. If it does not, adjust and save your energy.",
"major-11|wands-08":"Work is moving at speed, and fairness can slip in the rush. Take a moment to check that agreements, credit and deadlines are clear. A message or decision sent in haste can have long consequences. Move quickly, but move accurately.",
"major-11|wands-09":"Weariness is a fair signal that your effort and your rewards may be out of balance. Look honestly at what you take on and what you get back. Then renegotiate or simplify. Finishing well means keeping enough strength for the last stretch.",
"major-11|wands-10":"Your load has grown out of proportion, and it is fair to say so. Document what you handle and bring it to whoever assigns work. Ask for a balanced distribution, or a clear priority order. Straight talk now is better than silent overload.",
"major-11|wands-11":"Taking on something unfamiliar is a chance to learn while keeping your standards. Ask what is expected and what you will be judged on. Curiosity paired with clear expectations helps you grow quickly and keeps mistakes small and fair.",
"major-11|wands-12":"Momentum is strong, and someone needs to keep score honestly. Move fast, but keep your commitments and document decisions. Rushing past fairness creates rework later. Aim your energy at a clear, agreed target, and check that your speed is not costing others.",
"major-11|wands-13":"Confidence with integrity earns respect. Speak up with warmth, back it with facts, and treat people even-handedly. A person who can be both friendly and fair, in your team or in yourself, becomes someone others trust with real decisions.",
"major-11|wands-14":"Leadership now needs a clear vision and a clear conscience. Set the goal, define the rules, and apply them to everyone the same way. A leader who is both bold and fair earns loyalty that outlasts any single project.",
"major-11|cups-01":"A fair and attractive offer is on the table. Read the terms carefully, since good intentions still need clear agreements. Ask about responsibilities, expectations and how success will be measured. If it all lines up honestly, your heart and your head can agree, which is the best ground for saying yes.",
"major-11|cups-02":"Here is a partnership built on a fair agreement. Put terms in writing, be honest about contributions and make sure each person's share matches their effort. Mutual respect makes agreements easier to keep. If negotiating with a colleague or client, aim for a deal both sides can honestly feel good about.",
"major-11|cups-03":"The team is in a good mood, and now is the time to make sure credit and workload are shared fairly. Notice who is being overlooked. A celebration means more when everyone who earned a seat is at the table, and fair recognition strengthens the group for the next push.",
"major-11|cups-04":"Dissatisfaction calls for clear thinking, not drifting. List what the job gives you and what it costs, plainly and fairly. Then act on what the list shows: ask for changes, look elsewhere within the field, or recommit. An unnoticed opportunity may sit in the pros column, so read it twice.",
"major-11|cups-05":"A mistake or loss brings consequences, and a fair review is needed. Own what was yours, name what was not, and avoid excess self-blame. Then move to repair what can be repaired. Clear accounting, done kindly, helps you keep your reputation and gets you back to solid ground sooner.",
"major-11|cups-06":"Something from the past, such as an old agreement, a former colleague or a favor owed, calls for fairness now. Honor what you promised and expect the same in return. Experience is an asset, but judge the present by present facts and not by how comfortable the old days felt.",
"major-11|cups-07":"Sort your options by evidence. Some are exciting stories and others are real prospects, and Justice asks for the difference. Check claims, read the fine print and talk with people who know the field. Discarding wishful choices is not pessimism, it is how you find the one that lasts.",
"major-11|cups-08":"A decision to move on has been made after fair thought. Do it properly: honor commitments, hand over work cleanly and speak candidly about your reasons where appropriate. Leaving with integrity protects your reputation and lets you begin the next chapter without leftovers from the last one.",
"major-11|cups-09":"Fair reward for fair effort. You may be seeing recognition that matches the work you did, and it feels earned. Enjoy it, and keep your standards clear and ethical. Satisfaction lasts longer when it rests on solid work and honest dealings with colleagues, clients and yourself.",
"major-11|cups-10":"A workplace or team that treats people fairly earns loyalty. If you belong to one, protect its standards. If you lead, keep rules consistent and pay attention to who carries the load. Lasting belonging at work rests on equity, transparency and recognition as much as on friendly feelings.",
"major-11|cups-11":"A creative idea or a young colleague needs honest, fair feedback. Praise what works, name what does not and avoid both flattery and harshness. If you are the newcomer, ask for clear expectations. Early stages go better when the ground rules are simple, kind and stated aloud.",
"major-11|cups-12":"An appealing proposal needs a fair evaluation. Read the agreement, compare what is promised with what is written and ask direct questions. Someone charming may be entirely sincere, and checking the details respects them as well as you. Decide when you have facts, not just enthusiasm.",
"major-11|cups-13":"Empathy guided by fairness makes a trusted colleague or manager. Listen closely to everyone's side, then decide on principle instead of sympathy alone. Keep confidences, apply rules evenly and let your caring nature show through your actions. People remember being treated both kindly and justly.",
"major-11|cups-14":"A leader or advisor who is both kind and fair. Decisions come from facts and from care for the people affected. If that is you, explain your reasoning openly. If you are working under one, expect straight answers and give them back. This is how trust and results build together.",
"major-11|swords-01":"A clear, fair point cuts through confusion. State the facts and the reasoning in plain words, and make sure you have your records ready. Decisions made from a clean analysis tend to hold. Put the key issue in one sentence before you walk into the room.",
"major-11|swords-02":"A stalemate needs a fair look at both sides, and that includes the side you have not wanted to see. Lay out costs and benefits in plain terms, then decide. Delay itself has a cost. A judgment made honestly is easier to defend than one you avoid.",
"major-11|swords-03":"A painful moment at work asks for an honest accounting. What happened, what part was yours, and what was not? Keep it factual when you speak to others. If you feel wronged, document calmly and raise it with the right person. Fairness restores footing after a letdown.",
"major-11|swords-04":"A pause helps you judge better. Step away from the dispute or decision, rest, and return with a level head. Rested minds weigh evidence more fairly. Do not be pushed into a verdict before you are ready. Proper timing makes a decision cleaner.",
"major-11|swords-05":"A win that bends the rules tends to cost you later. Ask if you would accept the same treatment from a colleague. Repair anything you took more than your share of, and be straightforward with credit. Fair play preserves your reputation longer than any single victory.",
"major-11|swords-06":"A move toward better conditions goes smoother when loose ends are handled fairly. Return what is owed, give proper handover, and be honest in how you describe the past. A clean exit or transition sets up the calmer stretch and protects your name.",
"major-11|swords-07":"Something may be taken or skipped that does not add up fairly. Keep your records straight, and ask direct questions if numbers or credit look off. If you are tempted by a shortcut, weigh what it would cost your integrity. Fair dealing tends to outlast cleverness.",
"major-11|swords-08":"You may be judging your options more harshly than the facts support. Write down the evidence for and against each limit you feel. Ask someone impartial to review it. A fair look at the actual situation often shows more room to move than you thought.",
"major-11|swords-09":"Late-night worry tends to hand down harsh verdicts. In daylight, review what actually happened and what you actually did. Separate facts from fear. A fair accounting, ideally written, often shrinks the problem, and it points to the one thing worth fixing.",
"major-11|swords-10":"A period has finished, and the honest thing is to account for it fully. Note what worked, what did not and who was responsible, without turning it into blame. Close accounts properly. A fair reckoning now gives the next role or project a cleaner start.",
"major-11|swords-11":"Learning the facts before forming a view is the wise move. Ask precise questions about policies, numbers or expectations, and note the answers. A new person asking fair questions can expose what has been vague. Stay curious and impartial until you have the whole picture.",
"major-11|swords-12":"Charging into a decision without weighing all sides invites trouble. Take an hour to check the facts and consider who is affected. A bold move that is also fair earns respect. If a colleague barrels ahead, ask them calmly to slow down and show the reasoning.",
"major-11|swords-13":"Clear sight and fair judgment work well together. Give evaluations that are accurate and balanced, and expect the same. Someone with this quality may be assessing your work, so keep your facts straight. If it is you, decide without favoritism and explain your view plainly.",
"major-11|swords-14":"A formal decision or standard is in play. Prepare your case with facts and be ready to defend your reasoning fairly. If you hold authority, apply the rules consistently and explain them. Consistency and clear principles build trust with the people who depend on your judgment.",
"major-11|pentacles-01":"A genuine opening comes with terms, so read them carefully. Ask clear questions, confirm expectations plainly and be fair about what you are offering in return. Clear agreements protect everyone. If something in the offer seems vague, ask about it before you give your time.",
"major-11|pentacles-02":"Weigh your competing demands with a clear eye. Which ones match your values and what you actually agreed to? Give each a fair portion of your time, and be honest when something has to give. A written weekly plan makes tradeoffs visible and easier to explain.",
"major-11|pentacles-03":"Fair credit and honest standards are the theme. Do your part well, acknowledge others accurately and speak up if recognition is uneven. Quality work done with integrity earns respect. Review the result with clear eyes, and correct mistakes without blame. A fair team is a productive one.",
"major-11|pentacles-04":"Clear sight shows what you are holding on to and whether it is reasonable. Ask what security you genuinely need and what is only fear. Be fair to yourself and others about sharing resources. An honest review of your habits with money and effort will show what is enough.",
"major-11|pentacles-05":"Hard times ask for clear thinking. Look at what happened, what is yours to own and what is not. If you have been treated unfairly, name it calmly and seek appropriate support. Keep tidy notes of your work and your commitments. Fairness is easier to argue when the facts are organized.",
"major-11|pentacles-06":"Fair exchange is on the table. Check that what you offer and what you receive at work are roughly matched, and speak up calmly if they are not. Share credit and workload honestly. When you give, do it without strings. When you receive, say thank you and mean it.",
"major-11|pentacles-07":"Assess your progress without flattery or harshness. What have your efforts produced, and how does that compare with what you hoped? Honest measurement is the first step toward better habits. Ask a trusted colleague for a candid view. Then adjust your plan based on facts, not mood.",
"major-11|pentacles-08":"Your effort deserves fair recognition, and your skills deserve honest measurement. Keep practicing, and keep a clear record of what you have improved. If your training or contribution is being overlooked, raise it calmly with facts. Steady, visible work is the strongest case you can make.",
"major-11|pentacles-09":"You have earned some of your comfort, and clear sight lets you enjoy it honestly. Keep your affairs orderly and your dealings fair. Be honest about what you have and what still needs care. A sensible habit of regular review keeps you steady and unafraid of surprises.",
"major-11|pentacles-10":"Think about what you are building over the long run and whether it is fair to everyone it touches. Clear agreements, honest records and good habits form a strong foundation. Ask what you want to be known for. Decisions made with integrity are the ones you can stand behind later.",
"major-11|pentacles-11":"Study the rules of your field with care and make a first honest attempt. Judge your early results fairly, neither too harshly nor too kindly. Seek feedback and act on it. A record of learning and honest effort carries real weight when you ask for more responsibility.",
"major-11|pentacles-12":"Steady effort and fair judgment suit each other. Do the work at a dependable pace, keep honest records and treat colleagues evenly. Hold yourself to the standard you set for others. Delivering what you promised, without drama, builds a reputation that opens doors on its own.",
"major-11|pentacles-13":"Practical care and honest judgment make a strong pair. Look after your team and your resources with fairness, and be clear about boundaries. Say no when something is not yours to carry. People trust a caretaker who is also straightforward about what can and cannot be done.",
"major-11|pentacles-14":"Authority carries responsibility. If you lead, decide with clear facts and treat people fairly, especially those with less power. If you work under someone, expect honesty and offer it. Well-managed resources and clear rules make a workplace calm. Lead in a way you would be comfortable explaining.",
"major-12|major-13":"You have been in limbo, and it may soon resolve into a clear ending. Surrender the fight to keep things as they were. Consider what you can learn from the outcome, and close out your responsibilities properly. Fresh direction follows willing release.",
"major-12|major-14":"Slow phases like this one, where nothing seems to move. Rather than pushing, stay balanced. Keep steady routines, do small useful tasks, and think about the problem differently. A calm, even approach lets progress appear when the pause ends.",
"major-12|major-15":"You feel suspended, and something familiar is keeping you there: comfort, fear of change, a habit. Step back and view the situation from outside. Ask what you gain by staying stuck, and whether that is worth its cost.",
"major-12|major-16":"An abrupt event halts your progress. Take time before you respond, because a shift in perspective will help more than quick fixes. Ask what the disruption reveals about assumptions you held. The best rebuilding follows a thoughtful pause.",
"major-12|major-17":"A pause is refilling your energy. Hope returns as you step back and see fresh possibilities. Do not rush to prove anything. Spend the quiet time on learning, planning, or restoring balance, and your next move will feel natural.",
"major-12|major-18":"Stalled and unclear is a tough combination. Avoid deciding anything big for now. Use the pause to gather information, question assumptions, and see the situation from a new position. Clarity tends to arrive quietly, once you stop straining for it.",
"major-12|major-19":"Taking a break or waiting for a decision proves beneficial. A new perspective brings clarity and a lighter mood. Trust that stepping back has not cost you. Return to the work refreshed, with a plainer understanding of what matters.",
"major-12|major-20":"Time on the sidelines has changed how you see your path. Now something asks for your decision. Reflect on what you have learned, and step in the direction that feels genuinely right. A considered response beats a reflex.",
"major-12|major-21":"Patience has finished a cycle. What looked like delay was part of the design, and now the whole picture appears. Take a moment to appreciate the result and share it. Then pick your next move consciously.",
"major-12|wands-01":"A new idea is bright, but the best way to use it is to pause and view it from another angle. Ask who would object, and why. Then take the first step with that knowledge. A short delay can improve the launch.",
"major-12|wands-02":"A wide view is available if you stay still long enough to see it. Step out of the daily churn and consider the plan from a different angle. What looks like delay may be the moment you notice the better route.",
"major-12|wands-03":"The work is done and the wait has begun. Rather than chafe, use the pause to review the project from a fresh angle. You may spot a gap or an opportunity you missed while busy. Willing patience is a real skill.",
"major-12|wands-04":"A break has arrived, and it is worth using deliberately. Step away from the task and see it from outside. Share a meal with colleagues or take an afternoon for reflection. Rested eyes often notice what tired ones cannot.",
"major-12|wands-05":"Disagreement in the workplace is easier when you take the other side's view for a minute. Say it back to them fairly, then present your own. The noise can turn useful once each person feels understood, and a willing pause helps that happen.",
"major-12|wands-06":"A win is visible, and you can let it be. Rather than rushing to claim more, pause and consider what the success really shows. Sometimes stepping back lets others credit you naturally. Use the pause to plan how to share the win.",
"major-12|wands-07":"You are guarding your position, and it may help to view the challenge from the other side. Ask what your critic is really worried about. You may find a compromise that costs nothing important. Choose which ground is worth holding, and let the rest go.",
"major-12|wands-08":"News is flying and everyone wants an answer. Hold still for a moment and view the situation from another angle. A brief, deliberate delay can prevent an expensive mistake. Then move quickly with a plan you have examined.",
"major-12|wands-09":"You are near the limit, and stopping to rest is smart, not weak. Use the break to see the whole effort differently. You may find that one habit, not the whole job, is draining you. Adjust that, and finish stronger.",
"major-12|wands-10":"The weight of your tasks may need a new perspective, not more effort. Step back and ask which duties you could drop, share or reshape. A willing pause can show you a lighter route. Do not push through blindly when a change of view is available.",
"major-12|wands-11":"Beginner's eyes give you the chance to look at the work upside down. Ask naive questions, and listen. Fresh curiosity combined with a pause to reflect can turn up ideas that experienced eyes overlook, so let yourself not know.",
"major-12|wands-12":"Drive is pulling you forward, yet a deliberate pause can save you from charging the wrong way. Use a day to look at the plan from another angle, then commit. Impatience corrected early makes your energy far more effective.",
"major-12|wands-13":"You have the assurance to lead, and the wisdom to pause and reconsider. Use both. Listen to the quiet voice in the room before you speak. A confident person who can change their mind gracefully is a valuable presence on any team.",
"major-12|wands-14":"You have a direction and the nerve to lead, but a deliberate pause could make it better. Ask your team what they see from where they stand. Leadership that includes a change of perspective builds trust and often spots problems early.",
"major-12|cups-01":"An appealing offer shows up during a waiting period. Use the pause to look at it from another angle: what does it offer, what does it ask, who benefits? Nothing is lost by taking a little time. Clarity gained now can make the eventual decision confident rather than rushed.",
"major-12|cups-02":"A partnership or agreement benefits from a deliberate pause. Take time to understand the other side's real position before responding. The delay is not stalling, it is homework. A deal reached after both parties have shifted perspective tends to be fairer and sturdier than one reached in a hurry.",
"major-12|cups-03":"The group is celebrating but you sense something is on hold. Join in, and use the quieter moments to see the situation from a colleague's angle. Projects stalled in the middle may benefit from a fresh vantage point. Teamwork resumes more easily when someone has taken time to understand everyone's view.",
"major-12|cups-04":"You are on hold and unimpressed by what is on offer. Instead of pushing against the stall, use it. Rethink your goals from a different angle, ask what you have been overlooking and let motivation return in its own time. A change of perspective may reveal a good option you missed.",
"major-12|cups-05":"A loss has brought your progress to a halt, and the pause is useful if you let it be. Step back and see the setback from another viewpoint. What did it teach, and what remains? Do not force a restart yet. A considered return will hold up better than a hurried one.",
"major-12|cups-06":"A pause in your career invites a look back. Old projects, early mentors and first jobs may reveal what you truly value. Reconsider them from your present view rather than clinging to them or dismissing them. What you learn can steer a slow, deliberate turn toward a better-fitting direction.",
"major-12|cups-07":"Choices multiply and you cannot decide, so you wait. That is fine if the wait is used to gather real information. Look at each option from an unusual angle and ask what it demands in practice. Choosing slowly beats choosing something shiny, and the pause can be your advantage.",
"major-12|cups-08":"You are stepping back from something that no longer satisfies you, and taking your time to decide the next thing. This deliberate pause is fine. Use it to see your work from outside, to plan, and to keep relationships warm. Moving on with a clear head beats moving on in a rush.",
"major-12|cups-09":"You are comfortable with a slower stretch, and you have earned a rest. Use it to appreciate what has been built and to consider your next stage from a fresh viewpoint. Not every good season requires forward motion. Sometimes staying put is how you find out what you actually want.",
"major-12|cups-10":"A willing pause in your own agenda for the sake of the team. It may be a delayed promotion, a shared project or a compromise. Make sure it is your choice and that it is seen. Contributions to belonging deserve acknowledgement, and patience is easier when the group is worth it.",
"major-12|cups-11":"A fresh idea or a message from someone newer to the field lands during a slow period. Take time to consider it from unusual angles. It may offer something your routine has missed. Let it develop without pushing, and ask questions before deciding whether the idea deserves your effort.",
"major-12|cups-12":"An attractive offer arrives while things are on hold. Resist the pull to answer at once. Look at it from several angles, including how it would look in a year, and talk with someone you trust. A deliberate response to a charming proposal shows maturity, and often earns better terms.",
"major-12|cups-13":"Empathy and patience make a powerful pairing for a complex situation. Listening to a client, colleague or team from their position can reveal what is really wrong. Hold back from quick fixes. Your willingness to see things from other angles is a professional strength, even when it looks slow.",
"major-12|cups-14":"A leader who can pause and consider before deciding is a rare asset. Emotional steadiness lets you hear dissent without defensiveness and look at a stalled problem from new ground. If you are that person, use the pause. If you work for one, appreciate a careful decision over a fast one.",
"major-12|swords-01":"A clean idea appears when you stop pushing and look from another direction. Turn the problem over: what if the constraint is the opportunity? Hold the idea a day before acting. A pause often sharpens it, and sharpened ideas are easier to sell.",
"major-12|swords-02":"A stuck decision may loosen if you deliberately step back. Look at it from the other person's chair, and from the customer's. You do not need to choose today. A planned pause with a clear end date is different from avoidance, and it often produces the better option.",
"major-12|swords-03":"After a painful moment, holding off on a response is wise. Take time, look at what happened from another viewpoint, and reply when you are level. A delayed answer is often better than a fast one. This shift in view can turn a setback into useful information.",
"major-12|swords-04":"You are being asked to stop and rest, and to accept it. Let work slow down for a while, even if it feels unproductive. Use the quiet to see your role from a new angle. What you notice while not pushing may change how you approach the next stretch.",
"major-12|swords-05":"Let go of the contest for a moment and look at it from the other side. You may find the dispute matters less than it seemed, or that your rival has a fair point. A chosen delay in responding can protect a working relationship you will need later.",
"major-12|swords-06":"A move to calmer conditions is underway, and it may be slower than you would like. Use the interval to see your work from a fresh angle. What you decide to carry forward matters more than how quickly you arrive. A willing pause during transition brings clarity.",
"major-12|swords-07":"Holding off may expose what a quick move would have hidden. Look at the situation from another viewpoint, and ask what someone gains from the current arrangement. If you are tempted by a shortcut, sleep on it. Waiting a day often reveals whether the plan is sound.",
"major-12|swords-08":"Feeling stuck often comes from seeing only one way through. Deliberately look from a new angle: the customer's, a rival's, or a newcomer's. Then list options you dismissed too soon. A voluntary pause to rethink is different from being trapped, and it opens doors.",
"major-12|swords-09":"Anxious thoughts tighten when you keep looking at them the same way. Turn the worry around: what is the best-case reading? What would you advise a colleague? Put the decision on hold until you have slept. A fresh angle in daylight is often kinder and more accurate.",
"major-12|swords-10":"A chapter is over, and holding still is wiser than scrambling. Let it end and view it from a distance. What looked like failure may look different in a few weeks. Take a deliberate pause before deciding what comes next, and resist rushing to fill the gap.",
"major-12|swords-11":"Learning comes faster when you slow down and question. Ask something you would usually assume, and hear the answer from a new angle. Be willing to wait for information before acting. A newcomer's fresh viewpoint, yours or someone else's, may show what long-timers overlook.",
"major-12|swords-12":"Urgency and patience pull against each other. Rather than rushing ahead, stop and look at the plan from a second angle. A chosen delay can save you from a fast mistake. If a colleague pushes for speed, ask what would change if you waited a week.",
"major-12|swords-13":"Clear, unsentimental thinking works best with a willing pause. Step back, examine the situation from more than one seat, and speak plainly once you have. Someone with a sharp, patient mind may guide you. Withholding your verdict briefly adds weight to it.",
"major-12|swords-14":"A decision needs deliberate delay and another viewpoint before it is final. Test your standards from the other side: how does this look to those affected? Then rule and explain. Authority that has paused to listen is trusted more than authority that hurried.",
"major-12|pentacles-01":"An opportunity is worth a pause. Hold off on answering for a day or two and view it from a different angle: what does the other side need, and what would you do if the terms changed? Willing delay is not indecision. It gives you a better yes or a clearer no.",
"major-12|pentacles-02":"A deliberate pause can untangle overloaded priorities. Step back from the schedule and look at it upside down: which tasks would nobody notice missing? Give yourself a day to think before rearranging everything. Sometimes the working balance comes from doing less, not managing more.",
"major-12|pentacles-03":"Team progress may be on hold, and the pause is useful. Use it to see the project through a colleague's eyes or a customer's. Quality benefits from a second look. Willingly delay the final push until the group agrees, and the result will hold together better.",
"major-12|pentacles-04":"You may be clutching something out of caution. Pause on purpose and view it another way: what would happen if you shared it, changed it or set it down? The delay is a choice, not a defeat. Use it to consider whether security comes from the thing or from your habits.",
"major-12|pentacles-05":"A difficult, stalled period may not be the setback it looks like. Stop pushing for a moment and ask what the situation is teaching you. See it from the angle of people who have been through the same. A willing pause can lead to the practical help you have not thought to request.",
"major-12|pentacles-06":"Pause before you agree to terms or offer help. See the exchange from the other side: what does each person need, and is it fair? A short, willing delay can reveal a better arrangement. Listening carefully to another view often improves the outcome for everyone involved.",
"major-12|pentacles-07":"Stepping back is the whole strategy. Let the work rest, look at it upside down and evaluate what is truly growing. Resisting the urge to check results daily saves energy. A deliberate delay lets you decide from a calmer view whether to stay the course or shift direction.",
"major-12|pentacles-08":"Skill practice sometimes stalls until you change your viewpoint. Stop drilling the same routine, and look at your method from another angle. Ask a mentor how they would see it. The pause is not lost time. A fresh perspective often unlocks the next level of your craft.",
"major-12|pentacles-09":"You can afford a willing pause. Rest in what you have built and view it from a fresh angle: what is really working, and what is only habit? Not every season needs growth. A quiet interval often produces better decisions than another push.",
"major-12|pentacles-10":"Big, lasting structures deserve patience. Pause before locking in a plan and look at it from the angle of those who will inherit it. A willing delay can catch problems early. Ask a colleague who thinks differently to review your long-term direction. Their view may improve the design.",
"major-12|pentacles-11":"Learning is helped by a pause. Take a break from studying, then look at the material from a new angle, such as explaining it to someone else. Your first real attempt does not need to be perfect. A willing delay lets ideas settle so you can act with more understanding.",
"major-12|pentacles-12":"Slow, steady progress may feel like standing still. View it from a new angle: consistency is what wins over time. Let the pause be deliberate, and use it to check that your method is sound. Then resume at the same reliable pace, a little wiser.",
"major-12|pentacles-13":"A practical, caring approach benefits from a step back. Pause before taking on another responsibility and view your workload from outside. What would you tell a colleague in your place? Willing delay protects your energy and your judgment. Good stewardship includes knowing when to stop and look.",
"major-12|pentacles-14":"Mastery includes knowing when to hold off. Look at your responsibilities from the position of those you lead, and delay a decision if you need a better view. Well-run resources reward patience. A leader who can pause and change perspective earns more trust than one who always acts fast.",
"major-13|major-14":"Endings call for a careful transition. Blend the old with the new gradually: hand over duties, train replacements, and pace yourself. Avoid extremes in either direction. A balanced rebuild is more durable than a sudden overhaul.",
"major-13|major-15":"A necessary ending is met with resistance from your own attachments: security, status, routine. Ask what you are afraid of losing. Once you name it, the release becomes easier, and moving on stops feeling impossible.",
"major-13|major-16":"Major, abrupt endings can shake your working life. Be gentle with yourself as you process it. Attend to practical needs first: communication, documents, next steps. The cleared ground is an opportunity, though it may not feel like one yet.",
"major-13|major-17":"Following a closure, a hopeful new direction appears. Let the end of one phase leave you free to pursue what you truly care about. Take small, sincere steps. Optimism plus a clean slate is a good foundation.",
"major-13|major-18":"A transition has left the future unclear. Accept that you cannot see the whole route yet. Keep to simple daily tasks, gather information, and avoid drastic choices while the fog remains. Direction will emerge as you move slowly.",
"major-13|major-19":"A finished phase gives way to something brighter. Letting go of an old role or habit has energized you. Share your enthusiasm, and take on work that suits your renewed confidence. Positive change is under way.",
"major-13|major-20":"A career chapter closes with an evaluation, and something new calls. Review what you have done honestly, then step into a different way of working. Transformation follows when you accept both the ending and the summons.",
"major-13|major-21":"A major stage finishes, and you can see what you built. Celebrate the result while releasing the old role. Document what you learned and pass it on. Endings that come with completion are a strong base for the next step.",
"major-13|wands-01":"A role, project or habit is finishing, and a new idea is ready to begin. Close the old file properly, then make one clear first move. Endings clear the desk. Use the space for something you actually want.",
"major-13|wands-02":"With one thing ending, you can look far ahead. Decide what to keep, what to drop and what to build. A clean look at the whole board beats patching the old plan. Choose your next direction on purpose.",
"major-13|wands-03":"The old approach is finished and your new efforts are in motion. Now comes the wait. Use it to tidy loose ends and prepare, not to second-guess. What you launched needs time to arrive, and clearing the past helps.",
"major-13|wands-04":"A hard transition has passed and a calm spell has begun. Take it. Thank the people who helped you through, and mark the new chapter in some small way. A steady base is what makes the next stage possible.",
"major-13|wands-05":"A restructure or change of plan is causing arguments. Let the disagreement do its job: bring out what people really worry about. Listen for the useful point, drop what belongs to the old way, and move on.",
"major-13|wands-06":"An old chapter closes with a visible success. Take the credit, then hand the past over. Recognition opens doors, but it is only useful if you walk through them. Use this moment to step into work you would rather do.",
"major-13|wands-07":"A change is being resisted, and you may need to defend it. Stay calm, explain why the old way must end, and keep your footing. Not every objection needs a full reply. Hold to the plan while it takes root.",
"major-13|wands-08":"Something is closing and events are moving fast. Sort what is finished from what is urgent. Clear the finished items today, then respond to the new arrivals one at a time. Speed works best on a clean desk.",
"major-13|wands-09":"A hard transition has drained you, yet you kept going. Accept that some energy is gone, and stop spending it on what has ended. Save your strength for the few things that still matter, and give yourself a real recovery period.",
"major-13|wands-10":"The overload has a cause, and part of it is something you can end. Drop a task, a commitment or a habit that no longer earns its place. Relief comes from subtraction. Decide what is finished, and say so.",
"major-13|wands-11":"With the old work finished, a fresh interest is calling. Explore it as a beginner, without pressure to be good right away. Small experiments cost little and reveal a lot. This is a good time to learn something that reshapes your direction.",
"major-13|wands-12":"You want to charge out of the old situation, and energy is high. Aim it. Decide what you are moving toward before you burn the bridge behind you. A clean exit followed by a clear target beats a hasty scramble.",
"major-13|wands-13":"You are guiding others through a change with warmth and certainty. Be honest about what is ending and clear about what is next. People follow someone who faces the loss directly and stays positive. Your steadiness helps the team adjust.",
"major-13|wands-14":"An era is finishing, and you are the one to point toward the next. Name what is over, describe what comes next, and take responsibility for the transition. Leaders who can end things cleanly earn the right to begin new things.",
"major-13|cups-01":"A chapter of your working life closes and a fresh offer appears in the space it left. Do not compare them too quickly. Let go of the old role or routine completely enough to see the new one clearly. An opening that matters to you personally is worth serious attention.",
"major-13|cups-02":"A working relationship changes form. An old arrangement ends and a fairer, more equal one takes its place. Be willing to renegotiate roles and expectations. A partnership rebuilt on honest terms after a clearing often lasts longer than one that never faced the change.",
"major-13|cups-03":"A team or the social side of work is changing shape. Colleagues move on, a group dissolves, a new one forms. Acknowledge the ending with a real farewell, then welcome the new mix warmly. Celebrating a transition properly helps everyone move forward without lingering resentment or confusion.",
"major-13|cups-04":"Your indifference may signal that a role has finished serving you. Take it seriously. Let something end, whether a habit, a project or a way of working, and see what interest returns. Clearing space is often what makes a new offer visible. Keep your responsibilities in order while you decide.",
"major-13|cups-05":"A significant ending brings sorrow, perhaps a lost role, project or working relationship. Allow the loss to be real. When you turn, you will find that skills, contacts and experience remain. Rebuilding starts small, and it starts with acknowledging what you had before you plan what comes next.",
"major-13|cups-06":"An old chapter, such as an earlier career, a familiar company or a former way of working, is completed. Thank it for what it taught you and let it go. Nostalgia is fine as long as it does not stop you from moving on. What you carry forward is skill, not the setting.",
"major-13|cups-07":"It is time to end some of the daydreams and cut the options that never had substance. Choose fewer paths and commit to one. Letting go of glittering possibilities is uncomfortable, and it frees energy for work that can actually be built. Clear decisions turn scattered effort into progress.",
"major-13|cups-08":"This is a definite leave-taking from a role, team or path that has run its course. The ending is deliberate, and the next step is still forming. Close it professionally, keep good ties and give yourself time to rest. The gap between chapters is uncomfortable but not empty.",
"major-13|cups-09":"After a difficult ending comes a sense of contentment. What you released left room for work that suits you better. Let yourself enjoy the results without immediately planning the next change. Transformations at work often bring a quiet satisfaction that is easy to miss if you are always looking ahead.",
"major-13|cups-10":"The shape of your work community is changing, through a reorganization, a departure or a new phase. Grieve what was good in the old arrangement and stay open to belonging in the new one. Lasting connection tends to survive change when people are honest and generous through it.",
"major-13|cups-11":"A fresh idea or a new contact appears after a clearing. It is small and early, and it deserves patience. Let go of the old approach and try a new one with curiosity. A modest beginning, tended with care, can grow into something that fits the person you have become.",
"major-13|cups-12":"A tempting proposal arrives just as one phase of work ends. That timing is worth noticing. Make sure you have properly closed the previous chapter, and check the new offer against practical details. If the fit is real, this can be the change you were waiting for.",
"major-13|cups-13":"Empathy is valuable as your working world changes. You may be the person others turn to during a reorganization or transition, listening and calming. Protect your energy by setting limits. Your intuition about what should end and what should continue is likely more accurate than you assume.",
"major-13|cups-14":"A leader guides others calmly through an ending. Restructuring, closure or a change of direction does not have to be cruel. Communicate clearly, treat people fairly, and keep your own feelings steady. How you handle a transition will be remembered longer than the change itself.",
"major-13|swords-01":"A clean cut is available. Recognize what in your job or approach has run its course, then say so plainly. Drop the process, project or belief that no longer serves. Making the ending explicit clears space, and a new direction usually becomes visible once it is.",
"major-13|swords-02":"A stalemate cannot last, and something is ready to end. Choose which option to let go of, even if it is uncomfortable. The stuck feeling comes from trying to keep everything. Release one thing, and the situation you have been circling can finally move.",
"major-13|swords-03":"A difficult ending, such as a lost role, a closed project or a broken working relationship, hurts and is real. Let yourself acknowledge it. Avoid pretending it did not matter. Clearing the loss thoughtfully makes room for a fresh start, and your grief does not mean you failed.",
"major-13|swords-04":"After something finishes, take a real breather. Do not jump straight into the next big thing. Use the pause to clear out old files, habits and obligations. A rested start on a new phase goes better than a restless one, and there is no prize for speed.",
"major-13|swords-05":"The old rivalry or tug of war has run its course. Stop fighting to win and let that chapter end. Give up the grudge and the scorekeeping. A cleaner working arrangement can grow from the space, and you will spend your energy on things that build something.",
"major-13|swords-06":"A transition is underway and it leads somewhere steadier. Let go of the role, routine or team that is finished, and take the useful skills with you. Handle the move with courtesy. The calmer chapter arrives more readily for someone who did not cling to the old one.",
"major-13|swords-07":"An old way of getting by, whether a workaround, a shortcut or a quiet arrangement, is reaching its limit. Bring it into the open and replace it with something you can stand behind. Ending it on your terms is better than having it end for you.",
"major-13|swords-08":"An old limiting belief about your abilities or options is ready to be retired. Notice the phrase you repeat, such as 'I can't' or 'there's no way,' and let it go. Once that idea ends, the walls turn out to be lower than they looked, and a next step appears.",
"major-13|swords-09":"The anxious stretch is transforming. Identify the concern that has had its day and put it down, perhaps by writing it and closing the notebook. Retire the habit of rehearsing worst cases. Clearing that mental clutter frees energy for the work in front of you.",
"major-13|swords-10":"A phase, role or venture has finished completely, and that is clear. Mark the ending and list what you learned. Do not try to resurrect what is done. Space has opened, and what you build in it can be very different, and better suited to who you are now.",
"major-13|swords-11":"An ending has cleared room, and a good question fills it. What do you want to learn next? Ask around, read up, watch how others do it. Being a beginner again is not a step down. Curious attention helps you choose the next chapter with care rather than by default.",
"major-13|swords-12":"An old approach is ending and you want to move fast. Good, but aim the energy. Decide what you are leaving before you announce it, and keep your delivery respectful. Speed without a destination just creates noise. Pick one clear direction and go at it.",
"major-13|swords-13":"Face plainly what is finished at work, without sentiment or spin. Cut what is not serving, communicate directly and stay fair to the people affected. Someone with a calm, clear eye may help you see it. Honest endings create room for stronger structure.",
"major-13|swords-14":"A firm decision closes one way of doing things. Base it on principle and clear reasoning, and explain it to those affected. If someone in authority makes a change, respond with facts and professionalism. Well-run endings protect people and set up whatever is built next.",
"major-13|pentacles-01":"An ending is clearing the ground for a genuine start. Let the old role, routine or way of doing things finish without clinging. What comes next has practical potential. Take a proper look at the new offer or idea and give it your full, unhurried attention.",
"major-13|pentacles-02":"Something in your schedule or setup has reached its end, and clearing it will ease the juggling. Drop the task, project or habit that has outlived its usefulness. The freed time will settle into a workable balance. Update your routines to match how your life looks now.",
"major-13|pentacles-03":"A team, project or method is winding down, and that is allowed. Honor what was built and move on without bitterness. What comes next can be done more skillfully because of what you learned. Keep the lessons and the good relationships, and let the outdated structure go.",
"major-13|pentacles-04":"It may be time to let go of something you have held tightly. An old way of guarding what you have no longer serves you. Let it end and see what remains. Update your habits around security so they reflect present needs, not old fears.",
"major-13|pentacles-05":"A hard period is closing, and rebuilding can begin. Do not rush it, and do not cling to the hardship as an identity. Accept help that is offered. Make small, dependable changes to how you work and manage the practical side of things. Recovery is built in modest steps.",
"major-13|pentacles-06":"The old arrangement has run its course. Let it go and negotiate a fairer one. Old favors and old resentments can be settled and closed. What follows is a cleaner working relationship. Thank the people who helped you, then move on without carrying a grudge.",
"major-13|pentacles-07":"A long-term effort reaches a natural end or turning point. Assess honestly what worked, and let go of what did not. A clear ending is cleaner than a slow fade. Carry what you learned into the next venture. Pruning is part of healthy growth.",
"major-13|pentacles-08":"The end of a training phase or a familiar routine opens the way to new skill. Let go of methods that no longer serve you. Then start practicing the next step, patiently and without shame about being a beginner again. Mastery is built through many endings and restarts.",
"major-13|pentacles-09":"A comfortable arrangement may be ending, which can feel unsettling even when it is right. Thank it for what it gave. Then clear space for the next form of independence. The habits and discipline you built will come with you into whatever follows.",
"major-13|pentacles-10":"A long-established structure is changing form. That may be a family business, a career path or an old way of doing things. Respect what it built, then let it transform. What you learned from it is the part that carries forward into whatever comes next.",
"major-13|pentacles-11":"An earlier phase of learning is complete, and a new one is starting. Retire the old method or the identity of the newcomer, and pick up fresh study. Do not be discouraged by a clean slate. Each restart builds on habits you already own.",
"major-13|pentacles-12":"A long, plodding phase is closing. What comes next can keep the reliability while dropping the parts that stopped working. Finish what is owed, tidy loose ends and let the routine change. Good habits survive endings, so bring them with you as you shift direction.",
"major-13|pentacles-13":"A role you have played as the capable helper may be transforming. Let go of the duties that drain you and are not yours. Practical care will stay part of who you are, but its form can shift. Clear your obligations and make room for work that suits you now.",
"major-13|pentacles-14":"An era of authority, or a way of running things, is closing. Hand over what is ready to be handed over, and let the old approach end. Mastery survives change if you keep learning. Prepare to lead differently, with the wisdom you gained from what came before.",
"major-14|major-15":"Extremes are tempting, like overwork or avoidance, and both cost you. Find a sustainable rhythm. Set a small boundary, keep it, and adjust gradually. Steady balance loosens the grip of habits that quick fixes never reach.",
"major-14|major-16":"Sudden trouble tests your balance. Keep your head, and avoid overcorrecting. Handle the urgent matters first, then adjust plans gradually. Your calm will help colleagues, and a patient rebuild will hold better than a rushed one.",
"major-14|major-17":"Calm and hopeful, this phase is one where progress comes in steady doses. Keep practicing, learning, and adjusting. Do not expect overnight results. A patient routine, honest self-belief, and modest goals build lasting confidence. Write down one small improvement each week so you can see how far you have come.",
"major-14|major-18":"Ambiguity calls for measured responses. Avoid big moves while details are unclear. Balance caution with steady progress on the tasks you do understand. Keep your communication even, and let clarity arrive by degrees. Put questions in writing and wait for answers before you commit.",
"major-14|major-19":"A comfortable, productive rhythm has arrived. Your effort feels balanced, and people are pleased. Protect it: keep regular hours, share credit, and avoid overloading yourself. Sustainable good results are worth more than intense bursts. When someone asks for more, check the calendar before you say yes.",
"major-14|major-20":"Give a thoughtful review to how you have balanced things. Look at workload, relationships, and priorities, and make calm adjustments. If a decision is called for, weigh it steadily rather than reacting. A moderate, informed step is right.",
"major-14|major-21":"Steady, balanced effort has reached a satisfying finish. The consistency paid off. Enjoy the result and note the methods that worked. Carry that measured approach into the next project, and share it with newer colleagues.",
"major-14|wands-01":"A new idea is bright, and the way to make it last is to blend it gradually into what already works. Begin with a small, balanced trial. Steady effort beats a burst of heat. Take the first step, and pace the second.",
"major-14|wands-02":"A wide view and a moderate approach work well together. Map the long route, then divide it into sustainable stages. Avoid the sprint. Keep several options in play, and blend them into a plan that suits both your ambition and your limits.",
"major-14|wands-03":"The first push is done and the results need steady time. Keep to a moderate, sustainable pace. Do not chase quick confirmation. Keep blending small improvements into your routine, and the outcome will come at its own speed.",
"major-14|wands-04":"A stable period follows patient effort. Keep the mix that got you here: some work, some rest, some teamwork. Celebrate a milestone in a modest way. A balanced routine protects the win and keeps the team healthy.",
"major-14|wands-05":"Conflicting views are pulling in different directions. Look for the mix that serves both. A compromise that borrows the best of each idea is usually stronger than either alone. Keep your tone measured, and do not rush to a verdict.",
"major-14|wands-06":"A win has arrived, and staying balanced is the smart response. Enjoy it, share it, and keep your habits steady. Success that is followed by moderate, consistent effort lasts. Use the moment to strengthen the routines that produced it.",
"major-14|wands-07":"Someone is challenging your position. Answer with measure, not heat. Hold what is essential and blend in what is reasonable from the other side. A balanced response wins more respect than a fierce one, and it keeps the door open.",
"major-14|wands-08":"Events are moving fast, and a moderate hand steadies them. Prioritize, then respond in measured steps. Do not try to do everything at once. Blend quick action with careful review, so speed does not cost you accuracy.",
"major-14|wands-09":"You are near the end of a long effort. Do not sprint or collapse. Pace the final stretch, mixing work with real rest. Small, consistent breaks keep you going. Finishing at a sustainable rhythm beats a heroic last dash.",
"major-14|wands-10":"Too many duties have piled up. Blend delegation, prioritizing and rest until the load is workable. You do not need a dramatic change, only steady adjustments each week. Moderation applied consistently can bring the workload back to a level you can sustain.",
"major-14|wands-11":"A new interest can enrich your work if you fold it in gradually. Set aside a modest, regular window for it. Learn patiently and connect it to your current skills. A slow blend is often more useful than a sudden switch.",
"major-14|wands-12":"Drive is high and impatience follows. Temper it. Divide the goal into stages you can sustain, and pace yourself so you do not burn out before the finish. Fast can be fine when it is measured. Point the heat at a steady target.",
"major-14|wands-13":"Confidence combined with restraint is a strong style. Lead warmly and keep your promises modest enough to keep. People see someone sure of herself who doesn't overreach, and they trust her. Blend friendliness with clear limits.",
"major-14|wands-14":"Vision and moderation suit a leader well. Set the direction, pace the effort, and blend the strengths of your people. Avoid grand pushes that exhaust the team. A steady, well-mixed approach carries a project through its long middle.",
"major-14|cups-01":"An appealing new opportunity is best blended in gradually. Try it in a small way, such as one project or a few hours a week, before reshaping your whole schedule. Balance the excitement against your existing commitments. Steady integration usually beats an overnight overhaul.",
"major-14|cups-02":"Cooperation works when both parties adjust. In a partnership, negotiate a middle that suits both, then keep tuning it as you go. Avoid extremes, whether of demand or concession. A balanced working relationship depends on steady, moderate communication, and it strengthens with every fair, small correction.",
"major-14|cups-03":"Team harmony comes from balancing different personalities and needs. Celebrate wins without letting them turn into complacency or noise. Encourage a healthy blend of collaboration and quiet focus. A well-mixed group pulls together, and the mood at work gets better when nobody is asked to be someone else.",
"major-14|cups-04":"Flat feelings about work respond to modest changes. Adjust your schedule, swap one task or take on something slightly different. Avoid overhauling everything in a mood. Steady, thoughtful tuning often restores interest better than a big reaction, and it keeps you open to the offer you may have overlooked.",
"major-14|cups-05":"Recovery from a setback is gradual. Combine acknowledgement of the loss with steady attention to what remains. Avoid the extremes of denial and despair. Small, regular efforts rebuild momentum and confidence, and a moderate pace lets you learn from what happened without being ruled by it.",
"major-14|cups-06":"Experience and fresh thinking combine well. Draw on what you learned earlier in your career and mix it with current methods. A mentor from the past or an old skill can be applied in a modern setting. Honor the history without letting it limit what you try next.",
"major-14|cups-07":"Plenty of options, and none is solid yet. Test a few in small, low-cost ways rather than diving into one. Blend curiosity with practical checks: time, skills, interest. Over several weeks, patient experiments reveal which possibility has substance, and a gradual approach keeps you from burning out on any of them.",
"major-14|cups-08":"Expect a well-paced departure. Rather than a sudden exit, you plan a smooth handover, maintain relationships and keep some stability while you look ahead. Gradual change is easier on you and everyone else. Give the process the time it needs, and adjust the plan as new information arrives.",
"major-14|cups-09":"A balanced working life brings quiet satisfaction. Effort, rest and reward are in decent proportion. Enjoy that, and do not tip the scales by overcommitting. Sustainable contentment comes from steady routines and moderate ambition, and you can protect it by saying no to what would upset the balance.",
"major-14|cups-10":"A stable, cooperative workplace is built by people who adjust to one another. Contribute to the mix, remain flexible and keep workloads in proportion. Belonging lasts when the balance between individual needs and group goals is maintained continuously, not just fixed once and forgotten.",
"major-14|cups-11":"A young idea or a beginner's enthusiasm benefits from steady, moderate support. Offer encouragement and practical guidance in equal measure. Whether it is your own project or someone else's, allow it to develop gradually. Regular small steps will turn a promising first draft into something dependable.",
"major-14|cups-12":"An appealing offer calls for a measured reply. Balance enthusiasm with practical questions, and blend the promise with a realistic view of the workload. Take time, ask for details and adjust expectations as you learn. A well-paced yes to a good opportunity tends to work out better than a rushed one.",
"major-14|cups-13":"Emotional intelligence paired with moderation makes a dependable colleague. Someone who listens, mediates and keeps the peace without exhausting themselves is valuable. Divide your energy across people and tasks sensibly, and keep boundaries in place. Sustained care for a team works best when it is paced.",
"major-14|cups-14":"A balanced, diplomatic leader blends feeling and judgement. Decisions come with care for people and attention to facts. If that is you, stay consistent, avoid extremes and keep your composure under pressure. If you work with one, expect fairness, and reciprocate with steady, moderate professionalism.",
"major-14|swords-01":"A sharp idea works best when it is blended into existing plans instead of forced through. Mix it in steadily, test it in a small way, and adjust. The long middle rewards the person who introduces clarity in measured doses, not a single dramatic announcement.",
"major-14|swords-02":"A compromise is likely hiding inside the standoff. Look for a blend of both options, and test it in small steps. Moderation is not weakness. Bring both sides to a table with actual numbers, and be willing to adjust the recipe until the mixture works.",
"major-14|swords-03":"A setback is best absorbed at a moderate pace. Acknowledge it, then return to a steady routine. Balance work with rest and talk to people who help you feel level. Slow, consistent repair rebuilds confidence and standing better than overcompensating.",
"major-14|swords-04":"A rhythm of work and recovery keeps you sustainable. Protect the pause, then return at a measured pace. Do not swing between exhaustion and overdrive. The long middle of a project is where even, steady effort with real rest wins.",
"major-14|swords-05":"A conflict cools when both sides give a bit. Skip the victory lap and look for a workable middle. Trade something you care less about for something you need. Even-tempered negotiation preserves the working relationship, which lasts longer than any single win.",
"major-14|swords-06":"A steady, unhurried transition is under way. Blend what worked before with what the new setting needs, and do it in stages. Balanced change avoids burnout. Keep your routines steady while circumstances shift, and calmer conditions will settle in on their own.",
"major-14|swords-07":"Steady, transparent practice outperforms clever maneuvers. If you notice a quiet shortcut in play, respond by staying measured and documenting things. Choose the balanced approach that you would be comfortable explaining to everyone. Over the long middle, reliability wins.",
"major-14|swords-08":"The way out of a stuck spot is a slow, mixed approach: a little learning, a little asking, a little trying. Do not wait for a dramatic escape. Steady small moves change the picture over weeks. Moderation keeps you from swinging between panic and passivity.",
"major-14|swords-09":"Anxiety at work eases with rhythm. Keep regular hours, take breaks, and blend focused effort with real downtime. Handle worries in small doses instead of all at once. Steady, moderate habits give a racing mind something to hold onto and let you sleep better.",
"major-14|swords-10":"After a complete ending, recovery is a patient process. Blend what you learned with new routines and pace yourself. Do not try to make up for the loss overnight. Steady, balanced effort over the coming weeks rebuilds momentum and standing.",
"major-14|swords-11":"Learning is a slow blend of asking and doing. Ask one good question, try the answer, adjust, and repeat. Moderate steady practice makes you skilled faster than cramming. Someone junior may be doing this well, so give them room and patient feedback.",
"major-14|swords-12":"Drive needs pacing. Keep your speed but add rest and checkpoints so you do not burn out or steamroll others. If a colleague rushes, propose a rhythm the team can keep. Fast and moderate are compatible when the direction is clear.",
"major-14|swords-13":"Honest assessment delivered in measured doses lands well. Give clear feedback, but blend it with acknowledgment of what works. A calm, discerning person may be a useful ally. Over the long middle, balanced truth-telling builds a team that can handle it.",
"major-14|swords-14":"Sound judgment with a moderate temper is the ideal mix. Set standards, then apply them with flexibility where it counts. Lead by steady example instead of by ruling hard. Over time, balanced authority earns loyalty and keeps quality high without exhausting people.",
"major-14|pentacles-01":"An opening comes at a time for measured effort. Do not overcommit or underplay. Mix ambition with realism, and take it in steady stages. Blend your skills with what the opportunity actually needs, and let the long middle do its work before you judge.",
"major-14|pentacles-02":"Moderation solves the juggling. Combine tasks that belong together, spread effort evenly, and avoid extremes of overwork and slacking. A sustainable weekly rhythm beats heroic bursts. Build the habit of adjusting small amounts regularly, and your working balance will hold without constant effort.",
"major-14|pentacles-03":"Blending different skills makes the whole team better. Mix your approach with a colleague's, and moderate the extremes on both sides. Craft improves when temperaments combine well. Patience with the middle of a project, where nothing dramatic happens, turns individual talent into good group work.",
"major-14|pentacles-04":"Moderation applies to security, too. Hold what you need, share what you can, and avoid the extremes of hoarding and overspending your energy. A steady middle path with your habits is the most sustainable. Adjust a little at a time, and let comfort come from balance.",
"major-14|pentacles-05":"A lean season eases through slow, sensible adjustments. Combine practical help with steady habits and avoid drastic moves out of stress. Moderate, consistent effort will carry you through the long middle. Ask for support where you need it, and blend patience with practical steps.",
"major-14|pentacles-06":"Fair exchange comes from steady, moderate adjustment. Blend your generosity with healthy limits. Offer help without overextending, and accept help without guilt. Over time, a balanced pattern of give and take builds trust and a reputation for fairness. Small course corrections work better than large ones.",
"major-14|pentacles-07":"Judging progress in moderation avoids both panic and complacency. Blend patience with regular, calm reviews. Let long-term efforts mature in stages, adjusting the mix of effort and rest. The long middle of a project is where most real growth happens, so pace yourself for it.",
"major-14|pentacles-08":"Steady practice in balanced doses is what builds mastery. Combine focused work with rest, and avoid burning out on intensity. The repeated hour is most effective when it is sustainable. Adjust your routines a little at a time until they feel natural, and trust the process.",
"major-14|pentacles-09":"Comfort and discipline are finding a balance. Blend enjoyment of what you have earned with continued modest effort. Avoid the extremes of coasting and overworking. A sustainable pace, sensible habits and a sense of enough keep your independence healthy and your mind at ease.",
"major-14|pentacles-10":"A lasting structure is built by blending steady effort across years. Balance short-term needs with long-term ones, and avoid swinging between extremes. Moderation is the underrated skill of long careers. Make small, regular adjustments to your habits and your plans, and the structure will hold.",
"major-14|pentacles-11":"New learning goes best in balanced portions. Mix study with practice, and effort with rest. Try a first attempt at a modest scale and adjust as you go. Patience with the long middle of learning is what turns a beginner into someone reliable.",
"major-14|pentacles-12":"Reliable effort blended with flexible thinking gets results. Keep your steady pace, and adjust methods as needed without dramatic overhauls. The long middle is where dependable people win. Moderation in workload, and a habit of small improvements, will keep you steady.",
"major-14|pentacles-13":"Practical care and moderation work well together. Look after people and resources with a balanced hand, avoiding both neglect and overextension. Blend generosity with sensible limits. Consistent, moderate care over a long period builds a healthy workplace and a sustainable pace for you.",
"major-14|pentacles-14":"Seasoned leadership blends firmness with flexibility. Manage resources with moderation, avoiding both extravagance and rigidity. Combine experience with openness to new ideas. Over the long middle of your career, small, consistent, balanced decisions add up to a reputation for sound judgment.",
"major-15|major-16":"A restrictive situation breaks open. A job, arrangement, or habit that trapped you falls apart, perhaps not on your terms. Focus on stability first, then recognize the freedom in it. Plan next steps with clear eyes.",
"major-15|major-17":"There is a way out of the rut, and you can see it. Hope helps you break a discouraging routine or an unhealthy pull, like overwork or people-pleasing. Choose one small change today. Encouragement and steady effort do the rest.",
"major-15|major-18":"Uncertainty makes it easy to stay in a bad arrangement, and worry can exaggerate the risks. Write down what you know and what you only fear. Ask directly for information. Decisions made in less fog will feel more free.",
"major-15|major-19":"Energy and clarity show a way out of a tight spot. What once felt like an obligation now looks optional. Move toward work that brings visible, honest satisfaction. Freedom arrives as you notice what you are actually happy doing.",
"major-15|major-20":"You are being asked to face what has kept you tied to a situation, and to answer. Review your commitments honestly. Choosing differently is possible, and the reckoning may be the very thing that frees you.",
"major-15|major-21":"A long entanglement reaches completion. You have outgrown a role or a constraint, and the door is open. Finish cleanly, acknowledge what you gained, and choose the next phase deliberately, not out of habit. A short note of thanks to the people who helped along the way closes the chapter well.",
"major-15|wands-01":"A new opportunity is exciting, and it may also be tempting you toward an old pattern such as overwork or chasing approval. Ask what is driving you before you start. Begin only what you would choose without the pressure to prove something.",
"major-15|wands-02":"Step back and look at what keeps you tied to a role or routine that no longer serves you. Map an alternative honestly, then choose the first small change. Seeing the loop clearly is where freedom begins.",
"major-15|wands-03":"You have put in the effort and the results are still out of sight, which makes it tempting to cling to one safe arrangement. Check whether you are patiently waiting or simply stuck in a familiar role. Send the follow-up, ask about the timeline, and widen your view.",
"major-15|wands-04":"A steady spot on a good team is worth appreciating, and it can also become a golden cage. Enjoy the stability while asking whether you stay because you choose it or because leaving feels frightening. Have one honest conversation about what growth here would look like.",
"major-15|wands-05":"Constant friction at work can feel energizing, and sometimes it is only a habit. Notice who benefits when a meeting turns into a scrap, and whether you feed it. Bring one specific disagreement to a calm, structured conversation, and see if the noise settles into something useful.",
"major-15|wands-06":"A win in public can tie you to the need for applause. Enjoy the recognition you earned, and then check whether you are now working to protect an image instead of doing good work. Choose one project this month that no one will clap for, and do it well.",
"major-15|wands-07":"Holding your ground is fine when you are right and costly when it has become a reflex. Sort your stances into the ones that protect your values and the ones that protect your pride. Give up one small argument this week, and save your energy for the point that truly matters.",
"major-15|wands-08":"Everything is moving quickly, and the speed itself may be the hook. Before you say yes to the next urgent request, ask whether it is truly important or just loud. Pick the one task that actually moves your goals forward, finish it, and let the rest wait a day.",
"major-15|wands-09":"Exhaustion can turn into a badge you wear, and it may also be keeping you tied to a situation that has stopped rewarding you. Notice whether you are pushing on from commitment or from fear of letting go. Ask for help on one task, and take a real day off.",
"major-15|wands-10":"You have said yes to so much that the load itself now has a grip on you. List every commitment, then mark the ones you took on to be liked or to feel needed. Hand off or decline one this week, and say plainly what you can actually deliver.",
"major-15|wands-11":"New ideas are tempting, especially when the current work feels heavy and stuck. Explore the fresh thing, but keep it honest: is it a real direction or a comfortable escape from a problem you have been avoiding? Give the curiosity a small trial, a couple of hours, while you handle the overdue task.",
"major-15|wands-12":"Momentum feels great, and it may be carrying you past a tie that deserves a closer look, like a habit, a shortcut, or an obligation. Move fast where you are clear and stop where you are not. Before you sprint on the next goal, check who and what you are dragging along.",
"major-15|wands-13":"A confident, magnetic person, maybe you, is drawing others in, and it matters what she does with that pull. Lead with warmth, and notice whether you rely on charm to avoid hard feedback. Ask a trusted colleague one direct question about how you come across, and listen without defending yourself.",
"major-15|wands-14":"Vision and authority are strong here, and so is the temptation to make your way the only way. If you lead, invite one honest objection this week and take it seriously. If you report to someone like this, decide which of their demands is real pressure and which is habit.",
"major-15|cups-01":"A generous offer arrives with strings you may not see yet. Read the terms twice and ask what it would cost you in freedom, hours, or favors owed. Enthusiasm is fine, but let a colleague you trust look at the details before you say yes.",
"major-15|cups-02":"A working partnership feels balanced on the surface, though one side may depend on the other more than either admits. Put roles, credit, and expectations on paper. Mutual respect holds up better when nobody feels trapped by the arrangement or by unspoken debts.",
"major-15|cups-03":"Office camaraderie feels wonderful, yet it can turn into a habit that keeps everyone comfortable and stuck. Enjoy the team, but watch for gossip, long lunches that replace decisions, or loyalty that stops anyone from speaking up. Friendly and honest can coexist.",
"major-15|cups-04":"The job feels flat, yet something keeps you tied to it: a paycheck rhythm, a title, an old fear. Name what actually holds you there. Once you can say it plainly, you can weigh a new offer or a new skill without panic driving the choice.",
"major-15|cups-05":"A setback stings, and you keep circling it: the lost client, the botched project, the boss's comment. Rumination can feel like responsibility, but it drains the energy you need. Write down what you learned, close the file, and turn toward the work still standing.",
"major-15|cups-06":"Old ways of doing things feel kind and familiar, and that familiarity can quietly keep you from growing. A former boss, an outdated process, or a comfortable role may be holding more sway than it deserves. Honor the good years, then update one habit.",
"major-15|cups-07":"Many shiny options are on the table and one habit of thinking keeps you from picking any. Perhaps it is fear of missing out, perhaps a hunger for the perfect title. List three concrete choices, price the effort each needs, and pick one to test this month.",
"major-15|cups-08":"You may be ready to leave a role, team, or routine that no longer feeds you, yet a grip of habit or fear keeps you at the desk. Naming the hold is step one. Update your materials and talk quietly with people in the field before any big move.",
"major-15|cups-09":"Things are going well, the wish is nearly granted, and there is a temptation to coast or overspend on rewards. Enjoy the win. Then check that your satisfaction does not rest on one habit, one client, or one perk that could vanish.",
"major-15|cups-10":"You belong somewhere, and the team feels like family, which is lovely and a little risky. Family-style loyalty can excuse overwork or unclear pay. Enjoy the community while keeping boundaries about hours, duties, and what you agreed to in writing.",
"major-15|cups-11":"A friendly, creative idea or a young colleague's message opens a door, while a familiar routine tries to keep it shut. Take the small invitation seriously. Reply, sketch the idea, or ask the question. Old habits fade when a fresh channel gets real use.",
"major-15|cups-12":"A smooth, appealing proposal comes your way, perhaps a partnership, a perk-filled role, or a flattering pitch. Charm is not a contract. Ask for specifics on pay, duties, and exit terms, and see how the other side reacts when you slow the conversation down.",
"major-15|cups-13":"You may be the emotional anchor of your workplace, absorbing everyone's stress and staying late to smooth things over. That is real skill, and it can bind you to a role nobody chose to pay for. Name your limits and let others carry their share.",
"major-15|cups-14":"A calm, fair-minded approach is your edge when office politics, money pressure, or a manipulative habit tugs at you. Lead with clear rules and even temper. Kindness to colleagues does not mean giving up your terms, and leadership grows from that balance.",
"major-15|swords-01":"One sharp realization about a routine, a client, or a paycheck that keeps you in place could change how you see the whole arrangement. Write the thought down plainly before it fades. Then test it with a concrete step, such as a direct question to your manager, instead of just feeling relieved.",
"major-15|swords-02":"A job, a deal, or a working habit holds you while you pretend both options are equal. The stalemate is comfortable, and comfort is the trap. List what each path really costs you in energy, then remove the blindfold long enough to read that list before the next deadline arrives.",
"major-15|swords-03":"A hard truth about a workplace you felt tied to has landed, perhaps a broken promise or a role that never grew. The disappointment is real. Let it inform your next move instead of feeding resentment. Ask what you were hoping the job would give you, then look for it honestly.",
"major-15|swords-04":"Being always available has become a chain, and a pause is exactly the medicine. Protect a few days of real rest, or a boundary on after-hours messages, before you decide anything about your future here. Tired people confuse habit with loyalty, so recover first and judge the job afterward.",
"major-15|swords-05":"A rivalry or a tense negotiation feeds on itself, and everyone ends up a little smaller. Check whether you are chasing the win because it matters or because backing down feels like losing. A fair compromise that lets the project move often beats a triumph nobody wants to celebrate.",
"major-15|swords-06":"A move away from a draining situation is underway or close, perhaps a new team, a new role, or simply a healthier way of working. The old pressures may follow you in your head for a bit. Bring only what is useful, leave the guilt at the dock, and let the calmer pace settle.",
"major-15|swords-07":"Watch for quiet maneuvering, whether a colleague's private agenda or your own temptation to take a shortcut because the pressure feels relentless. Cleverness that avoids the real issue only deepens the trap. Keep your paper trail tidy, say the uncomfortable thing directly, and choose the route you would be fine explaining aloud.",
"major-15|swords-08":"You may believe you have no options, yet the walls are largely made of your own assumptions about money, reputation, or what others expect. Write down each reason you are stuck and mark which are facts. Then take one modest step, such as a conversation or an application, to see how firm they are.",
"major-15|swords-09":"Dread about money, a boss, or a role you feel bound to can grow loud once the office is quiet. Much of it is repetition, not new information. Put the worries on paper, pick the one that needs action, and let the rest wait until you have slept and had a meal.",
"major-15|swords-10":"A draining arrangement, whether a role, a contract, or a way of working, has come to a definite close. It may feel abrupt and unfair. Yet the loop that kept you tied there is over, and that frees energy. Update your résumé, thank the good people, and treat the low point as the floor.",
"major-15|swords-11":"A newly alert eye is picking up how you stay tied to a job, a client, or an unhealthy routine. Ask plain questions of yourself and others, such as what is actually keeping me here and what would change if I asked for more. Gather facts before acting, and take notes as you go.",
"major-15|swords-12":"Impatience with a situation that has held you back could push you to make a sudden, blunt move. The frustration is fair, but blunt tactics can burn bridges you later need. Channel the energy into a direct, prepared conversation or a clear proposal, and let your facts do the pushing instead of your temper.",
"major-15|swords-13":"A composed, unsentimental view of your circumstances shows exactly where habit, fear, or convenience keeps you attached. Trust that clear read. Draw a firm line around your time or duties, state it evenly, and do not apologize for it. Being fair and direct is enough to shift how others treat you.",
"major-15|swords-14":"Authority, whether yours or someone else's, is tied up with an arrangement that no longer serves anyone. A fair, reasoned decision is needed, guided by principle rather than by who has the leverage. Set out clear terms, apply them evenly, and be willing to release a habit or an obligation that does not hold up.",
"major-15|pentacles-01":"A real opening has appeared, and something in you wants to grab it for the wrong reasons: fear of missing out, or a need to be needed. Read the terms slowly. Ask what would keep you tied to this role and whether that tie is your choice.",
"major-15|pentacles-02":"Too many plates are spinning, and part of you likes the frenzy because it feels important. Busy is not the same as balanced. List what you carry, mark what you volunteered for out of habit, and hand back one thing this week. Your time needs a budget as much as your money does.",
"major-15|pentacles-03":"A team can be a home or a cage. You may be staying in a group because it is comfortable, or because someone there holds sway over your confidence. Do the craft well, but notice who benefits when you stay small. Good work should leave you freer, not more dependent.",
"major-15|pentacles-04":"Security has turned into a chain. Maybe it is a comfortable routine, a title, or a habit of never allowing yourself anything. Notice where safety has become fear. You do not have to change everything, but name what you are guarding and whether it still deserves that much energy.",
"major-15|pentacles-05":"Scarcity thinking has a strong grip right now. Fear of lack pushes you toward poor bargains, silence about mistreatment, or hoarding effort out of dread. Notice when worry is making your decisions. Simple habits, like tracking what comes and goes each week, give you facts in place of dread.",
"major-15|pentacles-06":"Favors and obligations may be tying you down. A boss who helps you and then expects loyalty, or a colleague who keeps score, can turn kindness into leverage. Look at who owes whom, and whether you agreed to those terms. Fair exchange is clear about what each side gives.",
"major-15|pentacles-07":"Years of effort make changing course hard, even when the results are thin. The pull to stay because you have already given so much is strong. Judge the harvest honestly instead of by hours sunk. Ask what this path looks like in three years if nothing shifts, and act on your answer.",
"major-15|pentacles-08":"Routine can become a rut, and diligence can hide a compulsion to keep grinding. Ask if you are working hard because the craft needs it or because stopping feels unsafe. Keep the discipline, but set a firm end to the day. Real mastery includes the ability to put the tools down.",
"major-15|pentacles-09":"Ease and independence are pleasant, and they can also hide a dependency: on a title, a client, a lifestyle you fear losing. Enjoy what you built, but ask what you would do if it changed. Freedom means you could leave the garden and still know who you are.",
"major-15|pentacles-10":"An old structure, whether a family business, a long-held role or an unspoken code at the office, has a hold on you. Loyalty is admirable, but it should be chosen. Ask who wrote these rules and whether they serve the next ten years. Change what you can, quietly and steadily.",
"major-15|pentacles-11":"A student's enthusiasm is tangled up with a temptation, perhaps a shortcut, an easy promise, or a mentor who enjoys being needed. Keep learning, and keep your own notes. Try something small and real before you commit to any big claim. An honest first attempt protects you from grand promises.",
"major-15|pentacles-12":"Reliable effort is a strength, and here it may have become stubbornness or a way to avoid change. Someone at work, maybe you, keeps plodding along the same route because it is familiar. Steady is good. Check that the road still leads where you want to go before the next mile.",
"major-15|pentacles-13":"A capable person, you or a colleague, has taken on everyone's problems and enjoys the power that comes with it. Competence is real, but so is the habit of making yourself indispensable. Teach someone else one of your tasks this month. Sharing skills is a sign of strength, not loss.",
"major-15|pentacles-14":"Authority and material comfort tempt you to guard your position at any cost. A leader, you or someone above you, may be tied to status more than to purpose. Ask what mastery is for. The strongest managers share credit and information, and do not use fear to hold a team.",
"major-16|major-17":"After a shake-up comes renewal. The disruption has cleared the way, and you can rebuild with a lighter heart. Start with honest, small steps. Encourage colleagues, and take the chance to redesign what did not work.",
"major-16|major-18":"Upheaval plus unclear information is stressful. Avoid reacting to rumors and gather actual details. Focus on the tasks in front of you. It will make more sense in time, and steady action beats panic. Keep notes on what you learn so the next decision rests on something solid.",
"major-16|major-19":"A disruption leads to a better outcome than expected. The old structure fell, and the new one works better. Enjoy the relief, be gracious about the change, and use your renewed energy for something worthwhile.",
"major-16|major-20":"A shake-up brings a reckoning and a call to change direction. Look honestly at what collapsed and what you contributed. Respond to the moment decisively but thoughtfully, and let it lead toward more authentic work.",
"major-16|major-21":"A sudden change closes a major chapter. Although unexpected, it completes a cycle. Wrap up what you can, acknowledge what you achieved, and prepare for the new phase. You bring your experience with you. A short list of what worked and what did not will make the next start easier.",
"major-16|wands-01":"A disruption is clearing away a structure that was not sound, and a fresh idea is ready to move into the space. Do not rebuild the old plan out of habit. Take the first practical step on something new, such as a draft or a call, while the ground is still open.",
"major-16|wands-02":"A sudden change has knocked out the plan you were working from, and now you can see further than before. Draw up two or three routes forward and weigh them calmly. Resist choosing the first exit, and talk with someone who has a wider view of the field than you do.",
"major-16|wands-03":"A shake-up has landed and the results of your earlier effort are still on their way. Use the interval to look at what the disruption showed you about your setup. Keep sending signals outward, like updates and check-ins, so that the right people know you are still steady and reachable.",
"major-16|wands-04":"Upheaval has hit, and a reliable team or stable ground is still there to lean on. Take a moment to thank the people who helped you through it. Once the mood settles, ask what a stronger version of your setup would look like, and start with something small and visible.",
"major-16|wands-05":"A shake-up tends to bring competing voices, and everyone wants their idea to win. Treat the noise as raw material: listen for which point is actually correct, even if it comes from someone annoying. Pick one clear priority and ask the group to test it, instead of debating everything at once.",
"major-16|wands-06":"A public stumble or shake-up may make your standing feel fragile, and honest work can rebuild credibility quickly. Do not chase back the old reputation. Deliver one thing that is solid and visible, and let people see how you handle a hard moment. That earns more respect than looking untouched.",
"major-16|wands-07":"Change hits and you feel outnumbered, so you dig in. Choose carefully what to defend: the parts of your work that are sound deserve a firm stand, and the parts that were already failing may not. Make your case calmly with evidence, and let go of what the shake-up has already exposed.",
"major-16|wands-08":"A disruption has set things moving quickly, with news, messages, and deadlines all landing at once. Sort them by what truly matters and reply to that first. Speed is useful when you know your direction, so take ten quiet minutes to name it before you answer everything.",
"major-16|wands-09":"After an upheaval you are tired, watchful, and still showing up. That resilience counts, and it needs replenishing. Do not treat every new email as another blow. Rest properly, protect your energy for the last stretch of the problem, and accept a colleague's offer of practical help.",
"major-16|wands-10":"The collapse may show how much you had piled onto yourself, and the crash can be a chance to put some of it down. Look at which duties were never truly yours. Speak to your manager or team about redistributing them, and name the one project you cannot responsibly keep doing alone.",
"major-16|wands-11":"The old structure has fallen and something new is asking to be tried. Follow a small curiosity: a short course, an unfamiliar tool, a conversation with someone in a different field. You do not need a full plan yet. A modest, low-stakes experiment can show you where your energy actually wants to go.",
"major-16|wands-12":"Upheaval plus impatience makes for hasty moves. You want to charge forward, and the ground is still uneven. Take a day to clarify what the shake-up has changed before you commit to a new push. Channel the energy into one concrete step, then check the results before the next.",
"major-16|wands-13":"The rug has been pulled and you, or someone near you, still carries the room with poise. Let your composure steady others without pretending nothing happened. Speak plainly about what changed and what happens next. People will follow calm honesty more readily than forced cheerfulness in a difficult moment.",
"major-16|wands-14":"The shake-up asks for real leadership, and that does not mean acting as if you have all the answers. Tell your team what you know and do not know, then set a direction for the next few weeks. If you follow this person, offer clear information and honest questions instead of silence.",
"major-16|cups-01":"Sudden change removes an old structure, and a fresh offer or opportunity appears at almost the same moment. Take a breath before treating it as rescue. Check what it asks of you and whether it fits the person you are becoming, not just the panic you feel.",
"major-16|cups-02":"A shock hits a working relationship or project, and the way partners respond decides what survives. Meet the problem side by side: share facts, divide the repair, and drop blame. A good partnership often proves itself after the surprise, not before it.",
"major-16|cups-03":"An upheaval rattles the team, and the way people gather afterward matters more than the news itself. Hold an honest huddle, share what you know, and celebrate the small things that still work. Morale rebuilds through shared effort, not forced cheer.",
"major-16|cups-04":"Boredom and complacency are broken by an unplanned event: a restructure, a lost account, a sudden new boss. Resist the urge to sulk. The shake-up is showing you what was stagnant. Pick one skill or contact to develop now that the old routine has cracked.",
"major-16|cups-05":"A blow lands, whether a layoff scare, a failed launch, or a harsh review, and disappointment is real. Allow a short stretch of grief, then take inventory of what survived: skills, references, relationships. Rebuilding is more useful than replaying the fall.",
"major-16|cups-06":"Things you thought were stable are shifting, and you may long for how the workplace used to be. Take the fond memory as information about what you valued: trust, kindness, clear roles. Then rebuild those qualities into whatever comes next.",
"major-16|cups-07":"Sudden events puncture a stack of vague plans and shiny promises. Good news hides in it: you can finally tell which options are real. Keep the ones with concrete steps, dates, and people behind them, and let the smoke drift off.",
"major-16|cups-08":"A workplace change pushes or frees you to leave a situation you had outgrown. It may feel abrupt, though part of you already knew. Gather your records, talk to trusted people, and choose your next direction with a level head rather than a flash of anger.",
"major-16|cups-09":"A goal that felt secure hits turbulence, such as a delayed promotion or a budget cut. Disappointment is natural. Ask whether the plan depended on one fragile thing, and diversify your skills and contacts so the next win has better footing.",
"major-16|cups-10":"A shake-up in the organization tests the sense of belonging you have built. Stay close to the colleagues you trust, share information openly, and remember that a team is people, not a structure. What you built together can be rebuilt in a new form.",
"major-16|cups-11":"In the wake of a surprise, a creative idea or friendly message brings quiet hope. Follow the small lead: reply, draft, ask. Fresh ideas often come from the cleared ground, though they need patient testing before anyone builds on them.",
"major-16|cups-12":"A tempting offer lands in the middle of upheaval. It might be real, and it might be a well-dressed distraction. Ask for details, timelines, and how it fits what you truly want next. Good opportunities can wait a day for a clear answer.",
"major-16|cups-13":"You are the calm, empathic presence that others turn to when things fall apart at work. That matters, so protect it. Set aside quiet time to recover, delegate the emotional labor now and then, and keep your own goals from getting lost in everyone else's storm.",
"major-16|cups-14":"When plans collapse, a measured leader stands out. Stay fair, listen first, and make decisions that consider people as well as numbers. You do not have to hide concern. Showing calm and care together helps a shaken team find its footing.",
"major-16|swords-01":"A shake-up at work, such as a reorganization, a lost account, or a plan collapsing, comes with a piercing insight about what was never sound. Grab that insight while the dust is still up. Draft a simple, honest assessment of what failed and what should replace it, and share it with someone who can act.",
"major-16|swords-02":"You have been holding two options in careful balance, and circumstances are about to tip the scale for you. Rather than wait to be pushed, consider which direction you actually want. Use the disruption as permission to choose, and decide with a clear head, listing facts on paper before the next meeting.",
"major-16|swords-03":"Bad news, such as a cancelled project, a cut in hours, or a betrayal by a colleague, lands hard and fast. Give yourself a day to feel it before responding. Then ask what the situation revealed about what was built on weak ground, and start rebuilding one solid piece at a time.",
"major-16|swords-04":"A sudden disruption calls for a real breather before any big response. Rushing to patch things exhausts you and leads to more mistakes. Take the rest you are owed, keep your commitments minimal for a short while, and return with a level head. Plans made after recovery tend to hold up better.",
"major-16|swords-05":"Upheaval has people scrambling and blaming, and some are eager to come out on top at others' expense. Try not to join the scramble. Protect your integrity and your working relationships, since you will need those after the shake-up. Choose cooperation over point-scoring, even when a small victory is on offer.",
"major-16|swords-06":"Following a sharp disruption, you are moving toward steadier conditions, maybe a new role, a fresh team, or a calmer way of working. The transition may feel slow. Pack your best skills, leave the blame behind, and accept help from a trusted colleague. Steady effort, not haste, carries you to the far shore.",
"major-16|swords-07":"Chaos can tempt people into quiet side deals, hidden plans, or getting out with the best of the spoils. Be wary of that, from others and from yourself. Keep records straight, tell your manager what you know, and refuse shortcuts. Integrity during a shaky stretch will be remembered long after the noise fades.",
"major-16|swords-08":"A sudden setback has left you feeling trapped, convinced every route is closed. Look again. Many of the barriers are assumptions formed in the first shock. Write down what is truly fixed and what is not. Then pick one constraint to test this week, whether by asking a question or requesting an alternative.",
"major-16|swords-09":"After an upheaval at work, the worry keeps running long after the office has gone quiet. Most of those thoughts are what-ifs, not facts. Give them a set time on paper, sort out what you can act on tomorrow, and ask for support where you need it. Sleep is part of the plan.",
"major-16|swords-10":"A sudden and total ending, such as a closed department, a finished contract, or a project that fell apart, leaves you standing in what remains. It feels heavy, and it is also complete. Nothing more can go wrong with that chapter. Gather your notes, thank those who helped, and begin planning what comes next.",
"major-16|swords-11":"Following a disruption, your sharp attention is an asset. Ask direct questions about what changed and why, read the new documents carefully, and observe how leadership behaves. Collect facts before you form an opinion. A well-placed question in a meeting may earn you more respect than a quick reaction ever would.",
"major-16|swords-12":"Upheaval is pushing you to act fast and bluntly, maybe with a sharp email or a hasty decision. Speed can help in a crisis, but only if it points somewhere useful. Pause long enough to check your facts and your tone. Direct is good, reckless is not.",
"major-16|swords-13":"A shake-up is exposing what was weak in the plan, and you can read it without flinching. Trust that clear judgment. Say what you see in measured terms, focus on what can be rebuilt, and skip the blame. Your steady, honest voice may be exactly what a rattled team needs right now.",
"major-16|swords-14":"In a period of upheaval, decisive and fair judgment is needed, whether from you or from someone above you. Set aside emotion and weigh the facts. State the new terms clearly, apply them evenly, and own the outcome. Leadership during a shaky moment is measured by calm reasoning more than by loud confidence.",
"major-16|pentacles-01":"A plan collapsed, and in the space it left an actual opportunity is appearing. Do not rush to rebuild what fell. Look at the new offer or idea on its own merits, and start small and concrete. Sturdier work often begins after the false version is gone.",
"major-16|pentacles-02":"The careful juggling act just got interrupted by a surprise, perhaps a schedule blown up, a colleague leaving, or a budget rearranged. Drop the tasks that were only holding up the illusion. Rebuild your week around fewer commitments and keep a small cushion of time, since the next surprise will come.",
"major-16|pentacles-03":"A team plan or a job structure just fell apart. It hurts, but poor foundations were probably already showing cracks. Get together with the people you trust and rebuild in a sounder way, with clearer roles and honest standards. Craftsmanship after a collapse tends to be more careful and more lasting.",
"major-16|pentacles-04":"Something you clung to for safety, a position, a routine, a comfortable arrangement, has been disrupted. Gripping harder will not put it back. Ask what part of that security was real and what part was habit. Rebuilding thoughtfully, with room to adapt, is safer than clutching the ruins.",
"major-16|pentacles-05":"A shock has left you feeling exposed and short of resources. Lean on what you have: skills, allies, a support network, the habits that get you through a tough week. Ask for help in specific terms. The lit window nearby is worth knocking on, and asking is not weakness.",
"major-16|pentacles-06":"After an upheaval, the balance between what you give and what you get needs resetting. Colleagues may step up, or you may have to. Keep it fair and out in the open. Mentoring, sharing, or accepting real support helps the group recover, and a clear agreement about roles heads off resentment.",
"major-16|pentacles-07":"A shock has interrupted a long project just as you were assessing its results. Frustrating, yes, but it also offers a rare chance to see what was real. Review honestly what was working and what was hollow. Then replant with better information and a steadier pace. Patience is still your ally.",
"major-16|pentacles-08":"A sudden setback is answered best by returning to fundamentals. Practice your craft, one focused hour at a time, and let the noise settle. Skill survives upheaval when the habit is strong. Rebuild your routine deliberately, and let steady effort restore your confidence before you make large decisions.",
"major-16|pentacles-09":"Your comfortable, self-built setup has been disrupted, and that stings. But what you learned in building it is still yours. Take a clear look at what worked, what was overly cushy, and what needs bolstering. You can rebuild a solid working life from your own experience and habits.",
"major-16|pentacles-10":"An institution you counted on, a company, a family business, a long-standing way of doing things, is shifting under you. Not everything built to last will last. Protect the relationships and skills you can carry forward. Think in terms of what you would want to inherit and to leave behind.",
"major-16|pentacles-11":"Everything you thought you knew has been shaken, and you are a student once more. Use it. Pick one skill or subject and give it real, patient attention. A small, honest first attempt at something new will teach you more than another round of worrying about what fell down.",
"major-16|pentacles-12":"When things blow up, the person who keeps working at a measured pace is the one everyone leans on. That may be you or a colleague. Do the next necessary task well, then the next. Slow and reliable is a sound response to chaos, and it earns respect.",
"major-16|pentacles-13":"Chaos calls for a capable pair of hands. Someone in the workplace, perhaps you, has the practical sense to sort what matters from the noise and keep people supported and organized. Use that ability. Look after the team's basics first, since good morale and clear routines make rebuilding manageable.",
"major-16|pentacles-14":"A leader's position or an established way of running things has been shaken. If that is you, lead through the change with honesty, not bravado. Drop what was for show, keep what is sound, and be open about the new plan. People follow authority that admits what happened and acts calmly.",
"major-17|major-18":"Optimism meets ambiguity. Keep your goals in view even when details are unclear. Take small steps, ask questions, and trust your sense of direction while verifying facts. Patience helps you find your path. Checking one assumption a day keeps hope honest.",
"major-17|major-19":"Recognition, optimism, and clear success come together. Your sincere efforts are visible and appreciated. Share your good spirits with the team, and keep aiming at goals that matter to you. Confidence is well earned now.",
"major-17|major-20":"A renewed sense of purpose responds to a summons. You are evaluating your path and feel drawn toward meaningful work. Act on it in small, honest ways: learn something new, talk to mentors, refine your goals.",
"major-17|major-21":"Faith and effort have finished a significant piece of work. You have earned real satisfaction. Take a day to look back at how far the project came. Celebrate sincerely, thank your supporters, and let your renewed confidence guide your next ambition.",
"major-17|wands-01":"A gentle recovery and a new idea arrive together. Give the idea a modest first step, such as a rough sketch, a call, or a small test. You do not need to be fully recovered to begin. Start where you are, and keep the pace steady so the energy lasts.",
"major-17|wands-02":"The pressure has eased enough for you to look ahead. Take an afternoon to map where you want your work to go over the next year, and list the two or three moves that would open the most options. Show the plan to someone whose judgment you trust.",
"major-17|wands-03":"The effort is in place, and you can wait for the results with a calmer mind. Keep your outreach steady, like a follow-up message or an updated portfolio, and let others respond in their own time. Use the quiet to rest and refresh, so you are ready when the answers come.",
"major-17|wands-04":"After a hard stretch, the team and the workplace feel steadier. Take a moment to acknowledge what everyone got through and share a small celebration. Then ask what made it stable so you can protect it: a clearer process, a kinder tone, or a better rhythm of meetings.",
"major-17|wands-05":"Lively debate after a rough patch is a healthy sign. Ideas are being tested again. Listen for the useful point in each argument and keep the tone respectful. Ask the group what they need from the process, and agree on how you will decide, so that the energy leads somewhere concrete.",
"major-17|wands-06":"Your quiet, steady effort is getting noticed. Accept the praise gracefully and thank the people who helped. Do not rush to top it with the next big thing. Let the recognition remind you what kind of work you want to be known for, and take the next step from there.",
"major-17|wands-07":"You can defend your position more gracefully now that the worst has passed. Explain your reasoning plainly and skip the sharp edges. Where others push back, ask what they are worried about. A steady, hopeful tone makes your firm stance easier to accept, and makes a compromise easier to reach.",
"major-17|wands-08":"Messages, offers, and answers may begin arriving in a quick run after a quiet period. Meet the pace with a clear head: reply to the important ones first and take a breath before agreeing to anything. Your renewed confidence helps, and a short list of priorities keeps it from scattering.",
"major-17|wands-09":"You have almost made it through a long haul, and your reserves are low. Hope is real, so protect it with rest, clear limits, and a smaller list. Ask a colleague to take one task off your plate, and remember that finishing steadily counts for more than finishing fast.",
"major-17|wands-10":"The heavy load can start to lift if you let it. Choose what you can hand off, delay, or drop, and speak to whoever assigned it. Restoring your energy is not selfish, since it makes the work you keep better. Aim for a workload you could keep up for months.",
"major-17|wands-11":"Open-minded interest is returning, and it can lead somewhere good. Say yes to a small chance to learn, like a workshop, a shadowing day, or a project outside your usual lane. Keep expectations light. Enthusiasm plus a calmer mind helps you notice what really suits you.",
"major-17|wands-12":"You are ready to move, and this time your energy has some peace behind it. Set a direction that you believe in, then go step by step. Speed is fine when you check your bearings at each stage, and a teammate can help keep your enthusiasm pointed at real results.",
"major-17|wands-13":"Steady warmth and calm confidence carry weight at work. Whether it is you or someone you know, this energy helps others feel supported without losing their edge. Share credit, speak with clarity, and let your assurance be a quiet example for the people who watch how you handle the day.",
"major-17|wands-14":"Someone with vision and a healing touch is worth following, and it may be you. Lead by setting a hopeful, realistic direction and by treating people fairly. Share the reasons for your choices so others can help build them, and make sure you look after your own energy along the way.",
"major-17|cups-01":"Renewed hope meets a genuine opportunity, perhaps a role, a project, or a collaborator that fits who you are becoming. Reach out with clear, modest requests. Patience and steady effort suit this moment better than grand promises or overnight ambition.",
"major-17|cups-02":"A cooperative, even-handed partnership is possible and refreshing. Share credit, divide the tasks, and check in regularly. After a rough patch, an honest collaboration can restore confidence, and a fair exchange of effort and respect brings steady, calm progress.",
"major-17|cups-03":"A supportive team and a hopeful mood make this a good time to recover from a rough season. Celebrate small wins together and invite input. Colleagues who cheer each other on tend to solve harder problems, and the good feeling can carry into the next quarter.",
"major-17|cups-04":"Things feel uninspiring, yet a quiet renewal is underway. Do not dismiss the small offer nearby because it lacks sparkle. Try a modest change: new duties, a course, a conversation with a mentor. Motivation often returns after you take a small, honest step.",
"major-17|cups-05":"Disappointment gives way to a gentler outlook. Acknowledge the loss, then notice what still stands: skills, allies, ideas. Recovery at work often looks like small, steady tasks done well. Rebuild confidence one modest win at a time and forgive yourself the setback.",
"major-17|cups-06":"Fond memories of a mentor, a first job, or a simpler season can renew your sense of purpose. Reach out to someone who helped you early on, or borrow the good habits of that time. Old goodwill is a quiet asset for your next chapter.",
"major-17|cups-07":"You have a handful of appealing paths, and calm reflection can sort them. Write down what each requires and what you would enjoy on an ordinary Wednesday. Hope is best used to test one option in a small way, not to keep all of them glowing.",
"major-17|cups-08":"You may be ready to leave a stale situation for something that suits you better. Hope is guiding you, so plan with care: gather references, update your portfolio, and speak to people already doing the work you want. A thoughtful exit sets up a better arrival.",
"major-17|cups-09":"Things are settling into a good place, and you can enjoy the progress with a clear conscience. Thank the people who helped. Keep your standards steady rather than chasing the next thing at once. Contentment is a solid base for the next round of ambition.",
"major-17|cups-10":"A workplace or community where you truly fit is coming into focus. Contribute generously, ask for what you need, and notice the people who lift the room. Long-term satisfaction usually grows from feeling valued and useful, not just well paid.",
"major-17|cups-11":"Inspiration arrives in a modest form, like a note from a colleague, a creative prompt, or a hunch about a better process. Give it a little room. Sketch it, share it, and let curiosity, not pressure, decide how far it goes.",
"major-17|cups-12":"An appealing proposal arrives when your outlook is bright. Enjoy the enthusiasm, then check the fit: does it match your values, workload, and pay expectations? Ask for specifics and a fair timeline. Good ideas and good people can stand a bit of scrutiny.",
"major-17|cups-13":"Your empathy and calm are professional strengths right now. Use them to mediate, mentor, or make decisions that respect people's needs. Take quiet time to recharge, because a steady presence at work depends on you staying replenished.",
"major-17|cups-14":"Fair-minded leadership shines. You can listen with compassion and still make the tough call. Be the person who keeps the team calm, gives clear feedback, and treats money matters transparently. Trust follows those who combine heart and good judgement.",
"major-17|swords-01":"A bright, focused idea lands after a rough patch, and it feels both hopeful and sensible. Write it down and give it shape. Outline a simple next step and share it with someone you respect. Renewed confidence paired with clear thinking is a good foundation for a fresh proposal or plan.",
"major-17|swords-02":"You feel hopeful about the future but cannot yet pick between two options, and you may be avoiding the choice. Give yourself a short, fixed window to gather facts, then decide. Optimism is a good resource here, so use it to look directly at what each path would ask of you.",
"major-17|swords-03":"A painful disappointment at work is out in the open, and while it stings, hope is quietly returning. Acknowledge the loss plainly, then focus on repair: a candid conversation, a revised plan, a fresh start with new people. Recovery after honest grief usually leaves you steadier than before.",
"major-17|swords-04":"A restful pause is exactly what your energy needs, and the outlook ahead is brighter than it feels. Step back from the constant push, take real time off if you can, and let your mind settle. When you return, your ideas and patience will both be sharper and much easier to trust.",
"major-17|swords-05":"A tense contest or a sour disagreement can give way to something better if you stop trying to win. Take the first step toward fairness, whether that means a shared credit or a straightforward apology. Restoring trust with a colleague is worth more than the last word, and it gives the team fresh energy.",
"major-17|swords-06":"A calmer season is drawing near as you shift away from strain. Perhaps a new role, a lighter workload, or a healthier team is on the way. Trust the direction, and take the practical steps: update your materials, tell a mentor, and keep your pace steady. Progress here comes from patient rowing, not sprinting.",
"major-17|swords-07":"Optimism about your prospects meets a temptation to be clever or secretive about how you get there. Steer toward plain dealing. Share your plan with the people it affects, and keep your methods above board. Slow, honest progress builds a reputation that quick maneuvers never will.",
"major-17|swords-08":"You may feel stuck in a role or a plan, blindfolded by your own doubts, even as encouragement gathers nearby. Ask someone you trust for an outside view. Then try one modest step that tests your limits, like a stretch assignment or a candid request. Small experiments reveal how much room you actually have.",
"major-17|swords-09":"Restless nights about your future are wearing you down, though genuine grounds for optimism remain. Give your worries a scheduled slot, then close the notebook. Take one concrete step, such as a budget check-in or a candid chat with a mentor. Rest and steady action calm the loudest fears.",
"major-17|swords-10":"A chapter has closed for good, and although it hurt, the sky is clearing. Take a moment to honor what you learned. Then sketch the next step with fresh, modest optimism: a skill to build, a person to reach, a smaller goal. Renewal starts from whatever ground you are standing on.",
"major-17|swords-11":"A fresh, hopeful outlook is paired with a keen eye and honest questions. Use it to learn: ask how decisions are made, read up on the field, and observe what successful colleagues do. Gathering information now builds confidence for later, and asking sincerely rarely costs you respect.",
"major-17|swords-12":"Renewed optimism has you pushing ahead fast, ready to pitch, decide, and act. That drive is an asset if it stays aimed. Check your facts, hear one objection before charging forward, and keep your tone friendly. Speed works best when a steady hand is on the wheel.",
"major-17|swords-13":"A calm, discerning mind is paired with real optimism about what comes next. Use your candor to help the team, naming problems fairly and proposing repairs. You are trusted because you neither flatter nor despair. Keep offering that balanced view, and consider mentoring someone who could use your steadiness.",
"major-17|swords-14":"Sound judgment and confidence in the future support each other. Whether you are the one deciding or advising the one who does, keep the reasoning open and the tone encouraging. Fair rules, plainly explained, help a team recover from a rough patch. Lead with clarity, and let optimism show in how you speak.",
"major-17|pentacles-01":"After a rough stretch, a genuine opening arrives, and you are calm enough to see it clearly. Accept it with patience, not haste. A modest, real start built on your renewed clarity is worth more than a grand one. Let hope guide your effort while your feet stay on the ground.",
"major-17|pentacles-02":"Calm is returning, and you are learning to handle several demands without frantic energy. Set a steady rhythm. Keep the budget of your time and money simple and honest. Small course corrections each week work better than heroic overhauls, and a more hopeful outlook makes the workload feel lighter.",
"major-17|pentacles-03":"A hopeful, cooperative period is opening. People are ready to build something well, together. Bring your best craft and welcome others' input. Recovery from an earlier setback shows up in the quality of what the group makes now. Speak up about your ideas, and expect them to be heard.",
"major-17|pentacles-04":"Recovery is under way, but old caution about resources may still be running your choices. That instinct helped you through a hard time. It is now okay to relax it slightly. Look at your habits around saving and spending with calm eyes, and keep what is sensible while letting go of fear.",
"major-17|pentacles-05":"Hard times are easing, and help is reachable. Even if you still feel short of what you need, the outlook is improving. Ask for support and accept it without shame. Keep your habits small and steady. Hope will not fix everything overnight, but it gives you energy to take the next step.",
"major-17|pentacles-06":"Fair give and take is what renews morale now. Share your knowledge freely, and accept guidance when it is offered. A workplace that is recovering benefits from open credit and honest thanks. Pay attention to whether effort and recognition match up, and speak calmly if they do not.",
"major-17|pentacles-07":"Long, patient effort is starting to show, and you can look at the results with hope instead of anxiety. Assess what is growing well and what needs more care. Keep the pace steady. Progress you can trust is rarely fast, and your renewed outlook helps you keep going without exhausting yourself.",
"major-17|pentacles-08":"Hope shows up as discipline. Put in the focused hour, then another tomorrow, and let progress accumulate. You are recovering your confidence through the work itself. Choose one skill to sharpen and give it steady attention. Improvement that comes from practice is quiet, and it lasts.",
"major-17|pentacles-09":"You are rebuilding a comfortable, self-directed working life, and it is going well. Enjoy what you earned through patience and effort. Keep your habits sound and your outlook open. Independence works best when you still ask others for ideas, so seek feedback before the next big choice.",
"major-17|pentacles-10":"Recovery now feeds something durable. You can see how today's choices shape a career or organization that lasts. Put steady effort into practices that will outlive this year: clear records, good relationships, fair habits. Hope grounded in structure keeps you motivated during the slow parts.",
"major-17|pentacles-11":"Hope makes a good student. You are ready to study something new and give it a sincere try. Pick a course, a skill or a small trial project, and commit some regular time to it. Modest, honest first attempts build the confidence that a large leap could not.",
"major-17|pentacles-12":"Calm, persistent effort is carrying you through recovery. Nothing flashy, just the next task done well. Trust the plodding pace and the routine that goes with it. A patient colleague who follows through may be the best example available, so learn from their consistency and copy their habits.",
"major-17|pentacles-13":"Resourceful, calm care is your strength. You can look after people and projects with a quiet competence that inspires trust. Keep your workspace and your budgeting habits organized and unhurried. Others will follow your example. Take time to rest as well, because sustainable care starts with yourself.",
"major-17|pentacles-14":"Authority and hope combine well. A leader who is secure, fair and generous can build something durable. If that is you, share credit and plan for the long haul. If it is someone above you, notice how they handle resources with care, and learn what makes their approach trustworthy.",
"major-18|major-19":"Confusion gives way to clarity. Information arrives and the situation becomes understandable, so questions that felt heavy now have simple answers. Share what you learned, own any misreads, and move forward with confidence. Things look simpler in daylight.",
"major-18|major-20":"An inner nudge about your direction is getting louder, though the details are murky. Investigate: ask questions, try small experiments. Respond to the call carefully. A clearer path follows intentional, informed action. Talk it over with someone whose judgment you respect before you settle on a direction.",
"major-18|major-21":"A project is close to completion, but uncertainty lingers. Finish what you started, check details, and do not let anxiety undermine your accomplishment. Clear up remaining loose ends, then celebrate. A final checklist, gone through calmly, will quiet most of the doubt.",
"major-18|wands-01":"An idea is sparking in a fog of unknowns. Rather than launching at full speed, take one small exploratory step, such as a question to an expert or a rough test. Collect facts before you commit, and keep your excitement, since it will help once things get clearer.",
"major-18|wands-02":"Your plans are forming, and important details are still hidden. Take time to research the terrain, ask questions, and list what you still do not know. A wider view of the landscape will do more for you now than a quick decision. Test your assumptions with someone outside the project.",
"major-18|wands-03":"You have done the groundwork, and the outcome remains unclear. Do not read too much into silence or vague messages. Follow up politely, ask for specifics, and use the wait to sharpen your next steps. Clarity is coming, and steady curiosity will serve you better than anxiety.",
"major-18|wands-04":"The team is settled, and something beneath the surface is not fully discussed. Enjoy the stability, and gently ask whether anyone has a concern they have kept quiet. A relaxed check-in over lunch or a short survey can bring what is hidden into view before it grows into a bigger issue.",
"major-18|wands-05":"Confusion and friction feed each other. People argue because they are working from different assumptions. Ask everyone to say what they think the goal is, and write it down. Many disputes fade once the misunderstanding is visible, and the remaining ones become real problems you can solve.",
"major-18|wands-06":"Praise or status may not show the whole picture, yours or someone else's. Ask for specifics: what exactly went well, and what is still unclear? Do not build your confidence on flattery, and do not dismiss recognition either. Measure your progress against your own goals, and get a second opinion.",
"major-18|wands-07":"Holding firm is harder when the facts are unclear. Before you dig in, confirm what you actually know and what you only suspect. Defend the parts you are sure of, stay flexible on the rest, and give yourself permission to change your mind once more information comes in.",
"major-18|wands-08":"Events are speeding along and your picture of them is incomplete. Read messages twice before answering, and avoid committing to anything you cannot yet explain. Ask a clarifying question or two and give yourself a night to think. A brief delay now can prevent a larger mistake later.",
"major-18|wands-09":"Fatigue makes uncertainty look bigger than it is. Before you decide the whole project is failing, get real sleep and look at the facts in the morning. Ask one trusted colleague for an honest read, and finish the next small piece, since a completed step often brings clearer thinking.",
"major-18|wands-10":"Burden and confusion feed on each other: too many tasks, and not enough clarity about which ones matter. Write out everything on your plate and mark what is truly required. Bring the unclear items to whoever set them and ask for specifics, so you carry only what is yours.",
"major-18|wands-11":"A new interest calls, and much is still unknown. Explore it gently, and treat early impressions as questions, not answers. Talk to people already doing the work, read what they read, and ask about the downsides as well as the exciting parts. Interest grows into direction when it meets real information.",
"major-18|wands-12":"You want to charge ahead, but the route is hazy. Hold your speed until you have checked the facts. Ask the questions you have been skipping, and confirm what the goal really is. Momentum works in your favor when it follows a clear picture, and against you when it follows a hunch.",
"major-18|wands-13":"A confident person with sharp instincts is at work, whether that is you or someone near you. Trust gut feelings as prompts to investigate, not as final conclusions. Speak up about what seems off, do it with warmth, and back it with a question or two so it lands well.",
"major-18|wands-14":"Vision and unclear conditions test leaders. Share your direction while admitting what remains uncertain. Invite the team to report what they are seeing on the ground, since your view may be missing something. If you work under someone like this, offer real observations rather than guesses about what they want.",
"major-18|cups-01":"Fog surrounds a new offer, and enthusiasm may run ahead of facts. Request details in writing, ask what has been left unsaid, and give yourself a little time. Opportunities that are real can withstand a few careful questions about scope, pay, and expectations.",
"major-18|cups-02":"A partnership looks promising, but some details are murky or assumed. Clarify roles, deadlines, and who owns what before the excitement carries you along. Straight conversations at the start prevent quiet resentments later, and fair terms make collaboration easy.",
"major-18|cups-03":"Office gatherings and team chatter can hide what is really going on. Rumors may swirl. Enjoy the company, hold your opinions loosely, and check facts with the source before reacting. Good colleagues are the ones who are the same in private as in public.",
"major-18|cups-04":"Dissatisfaction is real, though its cause is unclear. Is it the tasks, the boss, the pay, or your own fatigue? Track your energy for a week and note what drains or lifts it. Better information will tell you whether to change roles or simply reshape your days.",
"major-18|cups-05":"A disappointment leaves you unsure what went wrong, and anxious thoughts fill the gaps. Ask for feedback, gather facts, and write down what you know versus what you fear. Clear information shrinks a problem to a workable size.",
"major-18|cups-06":"Nostalgia for a past job or team blurs your view of the present. Old glories may be brighter in memory than they were. Take the good lessons, ask what you truly want now, and review your current role with fresh, unsentimental eyes.",
"major-18|cups-07":"Too many vague options cloud your judgement, and promises may be bigger than the plans behind them. Ask for numbers, timelines, and names. Choose the path with tangible steps, and treat everything else as speculation until it earns a place on your calendar.",
"major-18|cups-08":"Something tells you to move on from a role or project, though the path ahead is dim. Do not leap blindly. Research, network, and keep steady income and routines in place while you scout. A clear plan lets intuition and practicality work together.",
"major-18|cups-09":"Things look good on the surface, though something unseen may need attention. Review the details behind the success: contracts, workloads, assumptions. Enjoying progress is fine, and a quiet audit now can protect what you have built.",
"major-18|cups-10":"The team seems close, yet some tensions or unspoken expectations sit below the surface. Bring them into the light kindly, in a one-to-one chat or a planned meeting. A workplace that feels like a community can handle honest feedback and be stronger for it.",
"major-18|cups-11":"A creative idea or an intriguing note from a colleague appears, but you cannot tell yet if it holds up. Sleep on it, then test it in a small way. Inspiration is valuable when it survives a practical look, and it usually does if it is truly good.",
"major-18|cups-12":"A polished proposal or flattering invitation comes in, but the details are thin. Ask what you would be doing day to day, who pays for what, and how success is measured. If answers come easily and clearly, good. If they wobble, take your time.",
"major-18|cups-13":"Your intuition reads the workplace mood accurately, though not every hunch is fact. Use it to know where to look, then confirm with evidence. Guard your energy, since absorbing everyone's stress clouds your own judgement about what needs doing.",
"major-18|cups-14":"Ambiguity is high, and your steady, fair-minded approach is needed. Gather facts, listen to all sides, and delay big decisions until the picture clears. A leader who stays calm in uncertainty earns trust that outlasts any single outcome.",
"major-18|swords-01":"Amid uncertain information, a crisp insight appears. Treat it as a good lead rather than a final answer. Verify with a few simple facts before you act, then state your conclusion plainly to those who need it. Clarity earned from a foggy situation is valuable, so protect it.",
"major-18|swords-02":"Vague facts and a reluctance to choose have you idling between options. The fog will not lift on its own. Gather two or three concrete details, such as numbers, dates, or a clear statement from someone in charge, and set a deadline for deciding. Even a modest step beats staying in suspended animation.",
"major-18|swords-03":"Disappointing news arrives without a full explanation, and imagination fills in the rest. Resist building a story out of rumor. Ask for the details, in writing if you can, and allow yourself to feel let down without acting on it immediately. Facts, once you have them, usually hurt less than speculation.",
"major-18|swords-04":"Nothing about the current situation is clear, so hold off on big moves. Use this period to rest, tidy your notes, and gather your strength. Resist the pull to force a decision from incomplete information. When you return refreshed, the picture is likely to look simpler and your choices sharper.",
"major-18|swords-05":"Misread signals and unclear goals are breeding a needless contest with a colleague. Before you fight it out, check whether you are even arguing about the same thing. Restate your understanding, invite theirs, and settle on shared facts. Sorting out the misunderstanding matters more than getting the upper hand.",
"major-18|swords-06":"A move toward calmer conditions is happening even though many details remain unclear. Do not wait for perfect information to begin. Take the practical next step, such as accepting a transfer, changing routines, or leaving a murky project. Clarity tends to arrive along the way rather than before departure.",
"major-18|swords-07":"Murky circumstances create a perfect cover for hidden agendas and shortcuts. Stay alert to information that seems to be missing, and hold yourself to transparency. Document decisions, confirm important details, and avoid maneuvers you would not want examined. When things are unclear, straightforward conduct is your best protection.",
"major-18|swords-08":"Uncertainty has convinced you that you are stuck, though the boundaries may be largely imagined. Sort what you know from what you assume. Ask a colleague or mentor what they see. Small, concrete inquiries can reveal exits your worried mind overlooked, and they often bring quick relief.",
"major-18|swords-09":"Anxious speculation about your position, your finances, or your reputation keeps you awake. Much of it is projection, not information. Separate what has really happened from what you fear might. Make a short plan for the genuine items and give yourself permission to rest from the rest.",
"major-18|swords-10":"A situation has finished, and some of it remains unexplained. Resist the urge to reconstruct every detail. Note the lessons you can be sure of, wrap up loose ends, and step toward what is next. Sometimes an ending is just an ending, and a fresh start does not require a full explanation.",
"major-18|swords-11":"Your attention is sharp in a setting where information is incomplete. Put it to use by asking simple, honest questions and noting what does and does not add up. Keep a running list of open items. Careful observation, rather than guesswork, will give you the footing to speak up with confidence.",
"major-18|swords-12":"Pressure to act quickly meets unclear facts. Moving fast without a map can send you in the wrong direction. Take a short pause to confirm the essentials, then go. Direct action is welcome once you know what you are actually acting on, and your colleagues will trust it more.",
"major-18|swords-13":"Amid rumor and shifting information, your composed judgment is a valuable anchor. Sift what is confirmed from what is guessed, and say so evenly. Refuse to join speculation. Others will lean on your fairness and your ability to say I do not know yet, but here is what we can verify.",
"major-18|swords-14":"Decisions loom while the picture is incomplete. Good judgment here means naming the gaps openly and refusing to pretend certainty. Gather the missing facts, consult advisors, and set a fair timeline for deciding. A principled choice made after honest review will stand up much better than a confident guess.",
"major-18|pentacles-01":"An opportunity has shown up wrapped in uncertainty. The details are vague and your gut is sending mixed signals. Do not decide on excitement or anxiety alone. Get the specifics in writing, ask a trusted person to look them over, and wait for the fog to lift before you commit.",
"major-18|pentacles-02":"Juggling is harder when you cannot see the whole picture. Priorities keep changing and information is incomplete. Write down what is known and what is only rumor or worry. Work on the known items first. A simple weekly plan keeps your energy from draining into guesswork.",
"major-18|pentacles-03":"The team's picture is murky. Roles, expectations or quality standards are not clear, and people are guessing. Call a meeting to set out what is actually being asked. Careful, honest craft is the best answer to fog. Document decisions so that nobody has to rely on memory or hints.",
"major-18|pentacles-04":"Uncertainty is making you cautious and protective. You may hang onto what you have because you cannot judge what comes next. That is a reasonable reflex, up to a point. Gather information calmly. Once the picture is clearer, you can decide what is worth defending and what can be loosened.",
"major-18|pentacles-05":"Anxiety about lack is louder than the facts, at least for now. Sort real problems from imagined ones by writing them down. Speak to someone reliable about what worries you. Clear information usually shrinks a fear, and seeking help is a practical step, not an admission of failure.",
"major-18|pentacles-06":"Unclear terms around who owes what can breed resentment. A favor, a bonus, a shared credit or a handful of borrowed hours may not be as fair as it looks. Ask questions until the arrangement makes sense. Fair exchange starts with honest information, so put the agreements in writing.",
"major-18|pentacles-07":"A long project has entered a foggy stage. You cannot tell yet whether the effort is paying off. Resist rash changes. Set a date for a calm review, and collect real measurements before that day. Patience with clear checkpoints beats panic and it beats blind hope.",
"major-18|pentacles-08":"Keep practicing while the direction is unclear. Skill grows in fog too. Do the repetitive tasks, sharpen the basics, and let understanding arrive in its own time. You may feel unsure of the purpose today, and doing the work well will show you what to do next.",
"major-18|pentacles-09":"You have built a comfortable place to work, but a vague unease is nagging. Pay attention to it without letting it run the show. Independence is an advantage when you use it to think carefully. Take quiet time to look at what is really bothering you and what is only a mood.",
"major-18|pentacles-10":"The long-term picture is hazy. You sense that something about your career path or an inherited business arrangement is not what it seems. Ask about history, structure and unspoken rules. Learning the whole story of how things got here gives you a firmer footing for what to build next.",
"major-18|pentacles-11":"You are new to something and it is confusing. That is normal. Take notes, ask simple questions, and expect several imperfect attempts. Fog thins with experience. Do not pretend to understand more than you do, since honest questions earn more respect than confident guesses.",
"major-18|pentacles-12":"Keep going at a steady pace even though the route is unclear. Reliable, unglamorous effort carries you through fog better than dramatic moves. Do the next reasonable task, note what you learn, and adjust. Colleagues who see you calm and consistent will trust you when things are murky.",
"major-18|pentacles-13":"Your instincts about people and situations are sharp right now. Pair them with practical checks: numbers, facts, calendars. A capable, caring approach helps you navigate a situation nobody has explained well. Trust your read, verify it, and look after the people who depend on your steadiness.",
"major-18|pentacles-14":"Someone with authority or resources is not being fully open, or you are not, and the situation is murky as a result. Ask direct questions and keep good records. Leadership needs honesty about what is known and unknown. Confirm before building any plan on someone's word.",
"major-19|major-20":"Success meets reckoning. Recognition is due, and you can review your path with confidence. Answer the call to a more meaningful role, and share your enthusiasm. Clear results speak well for you. Keep a record of those results for the next time you are asked what you bring.",
"major-19|major-21":"Real achievement and real joy arrive together. A significant goal is met, and you are proud of it. Celebrate, credit the team by name, and let the confidence carry into the next challenge without hurry.",
"major-19|wands-01":"A clear head and a fresh idea make a strong start. Put the idea into a first, visible step this week, whether a draft, a call, or a small prototype. You have good energy and good judgment together, so share your plan early with someone supportive and let their reaction help you.",
"major-19|wands-02":"You can see the field clearly and choose where to go next. Set a goal for the coming months, list the resources and people you need, and make one concrete move toward it. Confidence built on real information is worth acting on, and your plan gets better when others review it.",
"major-19|wands-03":"Your groundwork is solid, and the results are on their way. While you wait, keep a positive, active presence by staying in touch with the right people and sharing progress. Use the time to broaden your reach, for example by learning a skill or building a connection that will help later.",
"major-19|wands-04":"A supportive team and a stable base make good work feel easy. Mark a shared accomplishment, however small, with a real thank-you or a small gathering. Then ask what has made things go well so that you can keep it going when the next challenge arrives.",
"major-19|wands-05":"Energetic, open competition among people who respect each other can improve the work. Encourage your colleagues to share bold ideas and challenge each other kindly. Make sure the group agrees on the shared goal, and keep the tone friendly, so that the debate builds something better than any one view.",
"major-19|wands-06":"Success is visible and people are applauding. Take in the praise, thank those who helped, and do not shrink your achievement. Then ask what you want next. A win is a good platform, and this is a good moment to talk to leadership about the role or responsibilities you would like.",
"major-19|wands-07":"You are in a good position and someone may still challenge it. Stay calm and back your view with facts and results. A sunny, confident manner makes firmness easier to accept. Concede minor points gladly, and keep your attention on the outcome that matters most to the whole team.",
"major-19|wands-08":"Things are moving fast in a helpful direction. Answer messages promptly, take advantage of open doors, and keep your priorities in view so speed does not scatter your effort. A short daily review of what is done and what is next will help you enjoy the pace without feeling rushed.",
"major-19|wands-09":"You are nearly through a demanding stretch, and your spirits can stay up. Pace yourself for the final push and accept help where it is offered. Note what you have learned along the way. Once the effort ends, take a proper break, since your best work comes from a rested mind.",
"major-19|wands-10":"Success has brought more work, and clarity shows you what you can pass on. Sort your tasks into what only you can do and what someone else can learn. Delegate one item this week, explain what a good result looks like, and trust the person you asked.",
"major-19|wands-11":"Fresh enthusiasm is paired with a clear head. Say yes to a chance to learn something new, and share your ideas openly. Even a beginner's view can help a team see something familiar in a new way, so ask your questions early and enjoy being a newcomer.",
"major-19|wands-12":"Drive and optimism are strong, and you can get a lot done. Aim the energy at a specific goal and check in with the people affected by your pace. Share the enthusiasm generously, since it can lift a team, and make sure quieter voices have a chance to speak.",
"major-19|wands-13":"A confident, cheerful presence lifts a workplace. Whether it is you or a colleague, this energy suits leading a project, welcoming a new hire, or smoothing a tense meeting. Use it to include others and share the spotlight, and it will strengthen the trust people place in you.",
"major-19|wands-14":"Vision that comes with warmth is a real asset. Set a clear direction, explain the why, and celebrate progress out loud. If you work for someone like this, learn what they are aiming at and offer ideas that serve it. Leadership is at its best when people feel included.",
"major-19|cups-01":"A promising, honest opportunity comes with good energy behind it. Say yes to the conversation, share your strengths openly, and ask for what fair looks like. Clarity and goodwill on both sides suggest a start that is worth the effort.",
"major-19|cups-02":"A cooperative, sunny partnership is possible. Work with someone whose goals fit yours, divide responsibilities fairly, and celebrate progress together. Trust and transparency make collaboration productive, and a colleague like this is worth appreciating in words.",
"major-19|cups-03":"Team spirit is strong and results are worth toasting. Mark the milestone, thank the people who made it happen, and let the good mood carry into the next task. Recognition given freely builds loyalty that no bonus structure can replace.",
"major-19|cups-04":"Boredom is real, and the sun shows there is more on offer than you noticed. A task, a colleague, or a side project could be worth fresh attention. Try approaching your day as a newcomer would, and see what quietly interests you.",
"major-19|cups-05":"A setback has hurt, and brighter days are close. Acknowledge the disappointment, then focus on what still works: skills, relationships, savings of energy. Progress resumes when you shift from what was lost to what can be built, one visible step at a time.",
"major-19|cups-06":"Good history supports a bright present. Old mentors, early wins, and familiar friendships can lift your work now. Reconnect with someone from your past who believes in you, and let their goodwill remind you of your own talent and staying power.",
"major-19|cups-07":"Clarity arrives about a crowded set of options. Weigh each one plainly against your goals, skills, and schedule. The best choice tends to look obvious once the glitter is gone. Commit to one, tell the people involved, and move forward with energy.",
"major-19|cups-08":"Moving on from a role or routine can be a confident, well-lit decision. You know why you are going and what you hope to find. Say thanks to those who helped, leave things tidy, and step into the next chapter with your head high.",
"major-19|cups-09":"Success is in reach, and you can enjoy it honestly. Celebrate your effort, share credit, and treat yourself in a sensible way. A satisfying result now builds confidence for future projects, so record what worked and carry the method forward.",
"major-19|cups-10":"A workplace where you thrive and belong is pictured here. Results and relationships support each other. Keep contributing, speak up for the team, and let people know they matter. Careers that are anchored in respect and shared purpose tend to last.",
"major-19|cups-11":"Fresh, cheerful creativity is available. A colleague's kind note or your own playful idea can spark something useful. Share it early, ask for reactions, and keep the mood light. Good work often begins as a happy experiment that someone was willing to try.",
"major-19|cups-12":"A good-natured proposal or partnership offer merits a fair hearing. It seems well-intentioned and clear. Ask practical questions about duties, hours, and pay, and let your enthusiasm run alongside your common sense. Sincere offers stand up to a little scrutiny.",
"major-19|cups-13":"Your caring and clear-headed approach brightens the workplace. Colleagues bring you concerns because you listen. Use this trust to mentor, resolve friction, and support fair practices. Keep your own workload sensible so the warmth you give does not run dry.",
"major-19|cups-14":"Wise, warm leadership is highlighted. Set fair expectations, praise real effort, and handle money and deadlines with transparency. Teams flourish when their lead is both approachable and dependable, and your example can shape the culture for years.",
"major-19|swords-01":"A brilliant idea arrives and it is both smart and straightforward. Say it aloud. Share it with someone who can help you build it, and act while the energy is high. Clear thinking joined with real optimism makes an excellent start for a proposal, a pitch, or a fresh goal.",
"major-19|swords-02":"Good conditions are on the table, but you hesitate between choices or avoid looking closely. The picture is brighter than your caution suggests. Study the details honestly, weigh the options, and commit. A confident decision made in favorable light usually beats an endless deferral and rewards you with momentum.",
"major-19|swords-03":"A disappointment is being faced honestly, and encouraging conditions are close behind. Feel the letdown for a moment, then look at what still works. Praise from a colleague, a good result elsewhere, or a supportive manager can help you recover. Speak candidly about what went wrong and move on to what can go right.",
"major-19|swords-04":"A break is well earned and will pay you back. Take real time off, or at least protect your evenings, and enjoy it without guilt. You are doing well and can afford to recharge. Coming back rested will make your work sharper and your mood a genuine asset to the team.",
"major-19|swords-05":"A rivalry or a tense negotiation has room to become friendly if you stop trying to score points. The atmosphere is generally favorable, so use it to share credit and reach a fair deal. Collaboration under good conditions builds relationships that outlast the project and make later disputes easier.",
"major-19|swords-06":"A move toward a better situation, whether a new position, team, or simply a happier routine, is underway and going well. Bring your skills, leave the old friction behind, and stay open to friendly faces along the way. Confidence and clear planning make this transition smooth and even enjoyable.",
"major-19|swords-07":"Conditions are bright enough that you do not need to sneak around. Play it straight, share information generously, and let your work speak for itself. If a colleague seems to be cutting corners, raise it plainly. Transparency in a healthy environment earns trust, and trust is the best resource you can bank.",
"major-19|swords-08":"You believe you are stuck, while circumstances are more open than they appear. Remove the blindfold of old assumptions and look around. Ask for what you need, apply for the opportunity, or start the conversation. Favorable conditions reward those who step forward, and the bindings usually turn out to be looser than feared.",
"major-19|swords-09":"Anxiety about a project or your standing is louder than the facts justify. Things are going better than your midnight thoughts suggest. Write down the real evidence of your progress, ask for feedback, and get outside for a while. A little perspective and daylight go a long way.",
"major-19|swords-10":"A difficult chapter is finished, and things are turning bright. Acknowledge what it cost, then move toward the good. A new project, a friendlier team, or simply a lighter mood is available. Recognize your resilience, tell a trusted colleague what you learned, and start the next phase with energy.",
"major-19|swords-11":"An eager, observant mind meets encouraging conditions. Ask the questions you have been holding, request a walkthrough, and take notes on how things are done. Learning is easy to welcome right now. A new skill or a smarter process is within reach if you stay curious and speak up.",
"major-19|swords-12":"Momentum and sunny confidence make this a good time to push a plan forward. Be direct with your team, ask for what you need, and get moving. Keep your tone generous so the speed feels energizing rather than steamrolling. Quick, honest progress can win real goodwill.",
"major-19|swords-13":"A sharp mind and a warm manner make you an easy person to trust. Give feedback that is fair and specific, and deliver it with encouragement. Your candid, friendly assessments can steer a project cleanly. If you mentor others, this is a wonderful moment to share what you have learned.",
"major-19|swords-14":"Sound, fair decision-making meets a positive climate. If you are in charge, explain your reasoning openly and invite questions. If you advise someone who is, offer your view plainly and kindly. Transparent rules and a cheerful tone help a team trust the outcome and get on with good work.",
"major-19|pentacles-01":"A clear, promising opening arrives with good energy behind it. Say yes to it if the terms are plain and fair, and put your full attention into a strong start. Confidence is warranted. Share the news with people who back you and let their encouragement carry you through the early days.",
"major-19|pentacles-02":"You are handling several demands with good humor and a clear head. The workload is real, but you can manage it. Keep your priorities visible and your budget habits cheerful and consistent. Ask for what you need in plain terms, and take a moment to enjoy what is going well.",
"major-19|pentacles-03":"Good collaboration is in reach, with clear roles, skill and cheerful energy. Present your best work openly and give credit generously. A team that enjoys itself produces better results. Take pride in doing things properly, and let your colleagues see that you appreciate their contributions.",
"major-19|pentacles-04":"Things are going well, and the impulse to clutch everything tightly is not needed. Enjoy your stability without letting it make you stingy or rigid. Share what you know, treat others generously and stay open to improvements. Security feels better when you are confident enough to be flexible.",
"major-19|pentacles-05":"A lean, worrying time is giving way to clearer skies. Your effort and patience are recognized, and support is more available than you thought. Do not dwell on the hard stretch. Build steady habits now, and gratitude for the help you received will strengthen your working relationships.",
"major-19|pentacles-06":"Fair exchange is working nicely. You share your time and skills, and you are fairly recognized. Keep it honest and transparent. Mentoring someone or accepting help gladly builds goodwill that lasts. Notice what is going right in your working relationships and say so out loud.",
"major-19|pentacles-07":"The harvest looks good. Long effort is producing visible, encouraging results, and you can assess them with clear eyes. Note what worked so you can repeat it. Stay steady rather than rushing to expand, since patient growth tends to last longer than a sudden burst.",
"major-19|pentacles-08":"Steady practice is bringing real skill, and enthusiasm makes it enjoyable. Keep showing up for the work, one focused stretch at a time. Craft that you love improves quickly. Let the satisfaction of progress motivate you, and share what you have learned with someone earlier on the same road.",
"major-19|pentacles-09":"Satisfaction from self-made success is real, and you have earned it. Enjoy the comfort your habits created. Stay generous, and keep your standards high. Independence combined with a positive outlook makes you a fine colleague or mentor, and you can invite others into your well-kept garden.",
"major-19|pentacles-10":"A sturdy, lasting foundation for your career or business looks well lit. Things you built with care are thriving and can support others. Think about mentorship, succession and sound practices. Celebrate the long work behind this success, and keep planning steadily for years ahead.",
"major-19|pentacles-11":"Enthusiasm for learning is high, and so is your energy. Start the course, the practice project or the apprenticeship-style task you have been eyeing. Share your progress with a supportive mentor. Eager beginners with clear goals often surprise everyone, and the first real attempt is likely to go better than you fear.",
"major-19|pentacles-12":"Steady, dependable effort is well rewarded now. You may not be the loudest person in the room, but your reliability shows. Keep to your pace and finish what you start. A cheerful attitude about unglamorous tasks makes you a favorite among colleagues and supervisors alike.",
"major-19|pentacles-13":"Practical care and good spirits make you a valued presence. You can look after people, plans and resources with warmth. Keep your work environment welcoming and orderly. Sharing your organizing gifts helps everyone, and acknowledging others' contributions turns competence into real team spirit.",
"major-19|pentacles-14":"Confident, generous leadership is favored. Master your craft, share credit, and use your position to support others. Good stewardship of time and resources shows in the atmosphere at work. If you are watching a leader like this, note how they combine competence with decency, and learn from it.",
"major-20|major-21":"Reckoning leads to completion. A review or decision has brought a phase to a satisfying close. Accept the results, mark the achievement, and move on with a clear sense of purpose. Take a day to rest before the next thing begins.",
"major-20|wands-01":"An honest review of your working life may point to a new beginning. Ask what you have outgrown and what you truly want to build next, and take one small action toward it. The energy for a fresh start is available, so give it a shape by writing the first step.",
"major-20|wands-02":"A candid assessment of where you stand opens up options. List what you have accomplished, what you still want, and the two or three paths that fit. Talk with a mentor or someone who knows the field, and avoid deciding alone. A clear plan beats a vague ambition.",
"major-20|wands-03":"You reviewed your work honestly, took action, and now results are still forming. Stay visible and keep your commitments while you wait. Use the time to fill gaps you noticed in your review, such as a skill or a contact, so that when responses come, you are more ready than before.",
"major-20|wands-04":"A frank review has cleared the air, and the team can settle into a healthier rhythm. Say out loud what you learned and what will change. Then celebrate that you got through it. Stable ground after an honest conversation is worth appreciating, and it makes room for new goals.",
"major-20|wands-05":"Frank feedback is bringing disagreement to the surface. Treat it as information instead of an attack, and let each person make their case. Then decide what will change. Contentious reviews are uncomfortable, and they often clear away unspoken frustrations that have been holding up the team.",
"major-20|wands-06":"An honest review may lead to well-earned recognition. Prepare by gathering specific examples of what you accomplished, and present them plainly. Give credit to those who helped. If the acknowledgment does not come, ask for a candid conversation about how your work is viewed and what next steps are open.",
"major-20|wands-07":"You are being evaluated, or evaluating yourself, and you have a stance to defend. Gather facts, listen to critics, and revise whatever does not hold up. Stand firmly on the parts that do. Willingness to change your mind on small points strengthens your credibility on large ones.",
"major-20|wands-08":"News arrives at speed and calls for a clear response. Take a moment to assess honestly before you reply, then act promptly on what you decide. A fast review of your priorities can keep you steady when events move quickly, and it prevents you from reacting only to the loudest voice.",
"major-20|wands-09":"You are near the end of a tiring stretch, and it is time to assess. What did it teach you, what would you do differently, and what do you need now? Write your answers down, share them with someone you trust, and rest enough to make the final push sensibly.",
"major-20|wands-10":"An honest tally of your commitments is overdue. List each responsibility and ask whether it is yours, whether it still serves a purpose, and whether you can do it well. Have the difficult conversation about what to release. Dropping what no longer fits is a sign of good judgment.",
"major-20|wands-11":"A moment of reflection is followed by an appetite to try something new. Use what your honest review taught you to choose one thing to explore, whether a project, a course, or a conversation. Beginners with self-knowledge learn quickly, so start small and ask questions freely.",
"major-20|wands-12":"You have seen something clearly and you want to move immediately. Channel that drive into a plan with steps instead of a burst of action. Explain your reasoning to those affected, and give them time to respond. Speed is a strength when it carries people with you.",
"major-20|wands-13":"Confidence grounded in an honest self-review carries weight. Whether it is you or a colleague, this person can give clear feedback with warmth and own their mistakes without drama. Model that openness in your next meeting by naming one thing you would do differently, and invite others to do the same.",
"major-20|wands-14":"Leadership faces an honest audit here. Look at your decisions and results, credit what worked, and admit what did not. Share your conclusions with the team and describe changes you will make. If you report to someone like this, offer your candid observations respectfully, since they are ready to hear them.",
"major-20|cups-01":"A chance to change direction appears, and it asks you to review your history honestly. What have you done well, and where do you want to go? Answer the call with a clear plan, ask thoughtful questions, and decide on merit, not on nerves.",
"major-20|cups-02":"A fair review of a partnership or team arrangement is due. Discuss what each person contributes and what should change. Go in with specifics, listen well, and aim for terms both sides can respect. Straight talk now saves years of quiet friction.",
"major-20|cups-03":"Team gatherings and reviews bring honest reflection. Celebrate what went well, and address what did not without blame. A group that can laugh and look at itself squarely improves faster. Use the meeting to agree on one clear change.",
"major-20|cups-04":"You are being asked to face your dissatisfaction rather than coast. Review your role, goals, and habits with honesty. Maybe you need a new challenge, a raise conversation, or a fresh skill. Naming the problem clearly is the first move toward solving it.",
"major-20|cups-05":"After a loss or failure, a clear review is worth doing. Ask what actually happened, what was in your control, and what you would do differently. Keep it factual and fair. Learning from an error is more valuable than hiding from it.",
"major-20|cups-06":"Looking back at your career story helps you choose the next chapter. Notice which past roles, mentors, or projects gave you energy. Use that insight to shape your goals, and reconnect with a former colleague if it feels natural and useful.",
"major-20|cups-07":"An honest review of your options is overdue. List the choices, note what evidence supports each, and drop the ones built on hope alone. Then commit to a next step and tell the relevant person. Decisions get easier once you stop circling.",
"major-20|cups-08":"After honest reflection, you may be ready to leave a role or path behind. Make sure the decision rests on facts: your goals, your workload, your growth. Prepare well, speak respectfully, and move on with a plan that you feel good about.",
"major-20|cups-09":"Reviewing your progress reveals real accomplishment. Look over what you have built and appreciate it. Then decide what the next stretch should look like. Satisfaction and ambition can sit together when you are honest about both.",
"major-20|cups-10":"Consider the culture of your team or company, and ask if it matches your values. Where you belong, contribute openly and speak up about what could be better. Where you do not, that clarity is useful information for your next steps.",
"major-20|cups-11":"A creative nudge or friendly message may point to a new path. Take it seriously. Reflect on what it says about your talents, and give it a modest test run. Some callings begin as a small, gentle idea that will not go away.",
"major-20|cups-12":"A proposal comes your way and asks for a considered response. Review what it would mean for your goals, hours, and pay. Ask questions until you feel informed. A thoughtful answer, whether yes, no, or not yet, reflects well on you.",
"major-20|cups-13":"Reflect on how your empathy serves you at work, and where it costs too much. Set gentle limits, and use your insight to guide teams and clients wisely. A frank self-assessment can turn sensitivity into one of your best professional tools.",
"major-20|cups-14":"A mature review of your leadership or decisions is timely. Ask for feedback, weigh it fairly, and adjust. Balance compassion with clear standards when giving reviews or handling money matters. Fair, warm accountability builds a team that trusts you.",
"major-20|swords-01":"A candid review of your path produces one crisp insight about what you want and what no longer fits. Write it down and let it steer your next step. A direct conversation with a supervisor, or an updated plan for the year, can turn that clarity into something practical.",
"major-20|swords-02":"A moment of reckoning about your direction is here, and you are still trying to keep every option open. Set aside an hour to list what each path asks and gives. Face the answer honestly. Postponing the review only extends the tension, while a clear choice lets you move.",
"major-20|swords-03":"A frank review of your work life shows a disappointment you have been avoiding. It stings, yet naming it is the start of real change. Take responsibility for your share, and ask for support with the rest. A candid talk with someone you respect can turn the sadness into a plan.",
"major-20|swords-04":"A review of your path is due, and it goes better after a proper pause. Step away from the daily rush, get some sleep, and think about what you actually want from work. When you return, your assessment will be steadier and your next steps easier to name.",
"major-20|swords-05":"Review of a recent conflict reveals that winning cost more than it gained. Own your part, apologize where it is due, and propose a fair way forward. The workplace notices those who can end a feud gracefully, and doing so can turn a rivalry into a working partnership.",
"major-20|swords-06":"After an honest review of where you are, you are making a considered move toward better conditions. It might be a change of team, a new approach, or a career shift you have thought through. Pack your lessons, leave old resentments behind, and keep a steady, unhurried pace.",
"major-20|swords-07":"A reckoning about your conduct is called for. Have you cut a corner, kept something back, or watched someone else do so? Face it and put it right, disclosing what needs disclosing. Coming clean on your own initiative is far better than being caught out, and it rebuilds trust.",
"major-20|swords-08":"A candid look at your situation shows that the barriers are partly ones you built from doubt. Write down each reason you cannot move and question it. Which are facts, and which are habits of thought? Then try one small experiment, like a conversation or a class, that tests the limit.",
"major-20|swords-09":"Late-night self-criticism about a performance or a decision is out of proportion. A fair review is balanced: what went well, what could improve, what you will do next. Put that on paper and share it with a trusted colleague. Balanced honesty settles the mind far better than dread.",
"major-20|swords-10":"A period of your working life is complete, and the reckoning has arrived with it. Honor what you achieved, acknowledge what did not work, and close the file. A fresh start becomes possible once the ending is fully accepted. Make an updated list of goals and step toward the next one.",
"major-20|swords-11":"You are called to look at your career with fresh, alert eyes. Ask questions about what you want, what the market values, and where your skills fit. Read, listen, and observe before deciding. A thoughtful, well-informed review now sets up a stronger direction for the coming months.",
"major-20|swords-12":"The call to reckon with a lingering issue makes you want to act now and act boldly. Channel that into one well-prepared, honest conversation instead of scattered confrontations. Bring your facts and stay on point. Direct, timely action can resolve what has been pending and free you to move ahead.",
"major-20|swords-13":"A clear-eyed assessment of your career, free of flattery and excuses, is possible now. Trust your ability to see it fairly. Identify what has worked, what has not, and what you would change. Share that view calmly with a mentor or a manager, and let it guide your plan.",
"major-20|swords-14":"Authority and review meet. Whether you are evaluating others or being evaluated, fairness is the point. Use clear criteria, apply them evenly, and explain your reasoning. If it is your own performance under review, accept the verdict honestly and use it to plan. Principled decisions build lasting respect.",
"major-20|pentacles-01":"An honest review of your working life reveals a genuine opening. Listen to the call and check that it fits who you have become. Prepare well and start with a solid, modest step. A new beginning built on clear self-assessment tends to last.",
"major-20|pentacles-02":"A moment of reckoning about your workload and priorities has arrived. Review what you are carrying and whether it matches your values. Some tasks may deserve a firm no. Recalibrate your schedule and budget habits so that they reflect what you actually care about.",
"major-20|pentacles-03":"The team's performance and your own contribution deserve a fair review. Look at what is done well and what falls short, without blame. Renew your commitment to quality craft and open communication. Feedback given kindly and received calmly can raise the standard for everyone.",
"major-20|pentacles-04":"Time for a frank review of your relationship with security. Are your habits around saving, spending and holding on serving your goals, or are they driven by fear? Reflect, adjust, and let go of what is no longer useful. A clear plan is better than clenching.",
"major-20|pentacles-05":"After a lean or discouraging period, an honest review shows you where you stand and where help exists. Face the facts without shame. Reach out for the support or guidance you need. This reckoning can be the start of a steadier stretch, built on clear information rather than dread.",
"major-20|pentacles-06":"A fair reckoning of who gives and who gets at work is due. Are contributions recognized? Are you helping others in ways that feel right? Speak plainly, propose a fairer arrangement, and be ready to hear feedback. A clear agreement now prevents resentment later.",
"major-20|pentacles-07":"A long effort is up for evaluation. Look at the harvest with honest eyes: what grew, what did not, what you learned. Decide whether to continue, adjust or change direction, based on the evidence and not on hope alone. Your patience has earned an accurate answer.",
"major-20|pentacles-08":"Review your skills and routines honestly, and answer the call to raise your standard. What have you practiced enough, and what have you avoided? Commit to a steady schedule of improvement. Real mastery comes from reflecting on the work and then doing it again, with intention.",
"major-20|pentacles-09":"Take an honest look at what your self-built success has cost and provided. You have earned comfort through discipline. Now consider whether it aligns with your deeper values. Reviewing it lets you enjoy what is working and adjust what feels hollow or lonely.",
"major-20|pentacles-10":"The long view calls for an honest review. Where is your career or family enterprise heading, and does it reflect what you value? Assess the structure you are part of and what you will leave behind. Then act on what you learn with steady, deliberate steps.",
"major-20|pentacles-11":"Reflection has shown you what to study next. Answer the call by enrolling in a course, finding a mentor or setting up a practice project. Give it honest, regular effort. A sincere first attempt informed by hard-won self-knowledge is a strong place to start.",
"major-20|pentacles-12":"A fair look at your work ethic shows where consistency has served you and where it has become plodding. Recommit to dependable effort, and ask whether your route still makes sense. The call is toward patient, thorough work that earns lasting trust.",
"major-20|pentacles-13":"A frank review of your capability and care is due. You look after people and resources well, so ask whether you get proper recognition. Hear the call to use your practical gifts where they matter most. Evaluate your habits with kindness and refine them.",
"major-20|pentacles-14":"Leadership and stewardship are up for honest review. Look at how you treat people, share credit and manage resources. Answer the call to lead with integrity. If you follow someone in charge, evaluate what you see plainly and learn what you would keep or change.",
"major-21|wands-01":"One cycle of work is done, and another idea is ready to go. Celebrate what you finished, then pick a first move for the next project while your confidence is high. Completion gives you a foundation to build on, and a small first step keeps the momentum.",
"major-21|wands-02":"A goal has been reached, and a wider horizon shows what comes next. Review your accomplishments and think about where they could take you, whether that is a new region, industry, or role. Discuss the options with people whose advice you trust and set a direction.",
"major-21|wands-03":"A large piece of work has finished, and you are waiting to see its effects spread. Stay engaged by sharing results and keeping in touch with people who will benefit. Use the interval to plan what you want to do with your new skills and reputation.",
"major-21|wands-04":"A finished project and a supportive team make a natural time for celebration. Acknowledge the contributions everyone made and enjoy the sense of accomplishment. Then consider what made this success possible, such as clear communication or trust, so you can repeat it in your next effort.",
"major-21|wands-05":"The work is nearly done, and last-minute disagreements are common. Treat them as a final quality check. Let people voice concerns, settle the important ones, and let go of small ones. Finishing well often means accepting a slightly imperfect result that everyone can support.",
"major-21|wands-06":"Completion and recognition arrive together. Accept the credit that is yours, share it with those who helped, and record what you accomplished for future reference. A finished project is a strong basis for asking about new challenges or responsibilities that suit where you now stand.",
"major-21|wands-07":"Having reached a goal, you may find others questioning it. Present your results calmly and let the work speak. You do not need to prove everything, so pick which challenges deserve an answer. A secure position is easier to defend when you also stay open to learning.",
"major-21|wands-08":"Projects are wrapping up quickly and news is coming in. Keep track of what is finished, what is pending, and what is next. A brisk ending is satisfying when you have organized it well, so take a moment afterward to write down what you learned before the next task arrives.",
"major-21|wands-09":"The end is near and your energy is low. Keep going at a steady pace, accept help, and plan a proper rest once you finish. What you have learned through this effort is valuable, so take time to record it. Completion is close and worth honoring.",
"major-21|wands-10":"A demanding project reaches its end, and you can start releasing the weight of it. Hand over the pieces that others can manage and document the rest. Then take real time off. The next chapter will go better if you enter it rested, with a clearer sense of what you will and will not take on.",
"major-21|wands-11":"A finished cycle opens the door for a beginner's mindset. You have proven your skills, and you are free to try something unfamiliar without fear of failing. Pick a new topic or side of your field to explore, and treat it as a low-pressure experiment.",
"major-21|wands-12":"One goal is achieved and you are eager for the next. Before you race ahead, take a day to review what worked and set a direction. Bring your team into the plan so your speed does not leave them behind. Fast movement is valuable when it is well aimed.",
"major-21|wands-13":"A confident, accomplished person, quite possibly you, is at the peak of a cycle. Use your standing to mentor others, share credit, and set high but fair standards. Your example influences your workplace, so let it be one that includes people and celebrates their contributions.",
"major-21|wands-14":"Mastery and vision come together here. You have finished something significant and can now guide others. Share what you have learned, set a direction for the next phase, and delegate meaningfully. A leader who can let go of past success is free to pursue what comes next.",
"major-21|cups-01":"A cycle of effort is finishing, and a fresh offer or opening follows. Honor what you completed, then step into the next opportunity with readiness. Tie up loose ends, thank those who helped, and begin the new role or project with a clear head.",
"major-21|cups-02":"A collaboration reaches a satisfying high point. Both sides contributed, both sides benefit. Acknowledge the achievement together and discuss how to keep the partnership fresh. This is a good moment to formalize an arrangement that works well.",
"major-21|cups-03":"A project or season wraps up with good company and reason to celebrate. Hold a proper send-off, name each person's contribution, and share the credit. Marking the finish well leaves everyone ready and motivated for what comes next.",
"major-21|cups-04":"A big goal is met, and you may feel let down rather than thrilled. This is common after finishing something large. Take a real break, reflect on what mattered, and let your next ambition form naturally rather than out of restlessness.",
"major-21|cups-05":"A chapter closes with regret still attached, perhaps a project that ended short of your hopes. Accept what it was, and take the lessons with you. Completion does not require perfection. Finish the paperwork, say goodbyes, and prepare for the next chapter.",
"major-21|cups-06":"Your career comes round to a place that echoes where you started, but with skills and wisdom you did not have then. Reconnect with early influences, and bring their best lessons into your current work. Experience turns nostalgia into mastery.",
"major-21|cups-07":"The long stretch of weighing options is ending. You have gathered enough information to choose a direction. Commit, plan the steps, and tell the people involved. Completing the decision phase frees your energy for the actual work ahead.",
"major-21|cups-08":"A chapter of your working life has run its course. Wrap up your responsibilities with care, leave good records, and take the next step deliberately. Leaving well, having completed what you set out to do, keeps doors open in the future.",
"major-21|cups-09":"Success arrives, and it is complete enough to enjoy. Look at what your work has produced, feel proud, and treat yourself sensibly. Then note what you learned so the next project can build on it. A well-earned finish deserves a moment of rest.",
"major-21|cups-10":"Your working life aligns with your values and your people. Results, relationships, and purpose all fit together. Notice how rare that is, and protect it by treating colleagues well and keeping healthy limits. This is a place to build something long-lasting.",
"major-21|cups-11":"With one stage finished, a small creative spark points to what comes next. Follow it lightly: a sketch, a note, a conversation. You have proven yourself, so you can afford to play. The next chapter often starts with a modest, curious step.",
"major-21|cups-12":"After finishing something significant, an attractive new proposal appears. You are in a strong position to negotiate and choose. Ask questions, compare it with your goals, and give an answer when you feel ready. Your track record backs you up.",
"major-21|cups-13":"Your empathy and experience come together as real professional maturity. Use them to guide teams, advise clients, or shape your organization's culture. You have completed a major learning cycle, and others can benefit from what you now understand.",
"major-21|cups-14":"You have reached a level of leadership where heart and judgement work as one. Guide others fairly, handle money matters openly, and mentor with patience. A cycle of learning is complete, and you can share its results while staying open to growth.",
"major-21|swords-01":"A large effort reaches its finish, and a sharp insight arrives about what to do with it. Capture the lesson while it is fresh. Write a short summary of what worked and propose the next goal. Completion with clear thinking sets you up for a confident new start.",
"major-21|swords-02":"A project or a phase is close to completion, and you are stalling over one last decision. The delay keeps the finish line out of reach. Gather what you need, weigh the options fairly, and commit. Crossing the line will feel far better than continuing to hover just before it.",
"major-21|swords-03":"A major chapter is done, and the completion carries a pang of loss or disappointment. Allow that feeling. Not every ending is a triumph, though it is still an achievement to have finished. Thank the people who helped, note what you learned, and bring that with you into the next role.",
"major-21|swords-04":"A big stretch of work is finished, and rest is the right response. Take actual time off before you plan the next phase. Reflect on what you accomplished and let your energy return. Coming back with a full tank helps the next project begin well.",
"major-21|swords-05":"A project or a relationship at work comes to its end, and a lingering rivalry tempts you to argue about credit. Skip it. Close gracefully, give fair acknowledgment to others, and leave on good terms. A generous finish protects your reputation more than a hard-won point ever could.",
"major-21|swords-06":"You have arrived after a long transition, and conditions are notably steadier. The move you made has worked. Settle in, get to know your new surroundings gradually, and build routines that keep the calm. This is a good moment to acknowledge how far you have come.",
"major-21|swords-07":"A project is wrapping up, and you should make sure everything is accounted for and above board. Tie off loose ends, disclose what needs disclosing, and avoid shortcuts on the final stretch. A clean, honest finish protects your name and hands you a straightforward start on whatever is next.",
"major-21|swords-08":"A milestone is within reach, but self-doubt tells you the final step is impossible. Check that belief against evidence. What is actually blocking you? Often a small request or a fresh approach opens the door. Take the step, and let the achievement you have earned register.",
"major-21|swords-09":"A large effort has wrapped up, and worry about how it will be judged keeps you awake. Trust the work you did. Note your contributions, collect any feedback that comes, and give your mind a chance to settle. Completed work deserves your rest as much as it deserved your effort.",
"major-21|swords-10":"A long phase of your working life is over, cleanly and completely. It may sting, and it is also an accomplishment. Mark the occasion, gather your lessons, and thank the people who helped. With nothing left hanging, you can plan the next stage with a clear desk.",
"major-21|swords-11":"As a big project or phase closes, your attention turns to what you want to learn next. Ask sharp questions of people who have gone further, and watch how they work. Keep notes. Treat the finish as a launch pad for new curiosity, not just a place to rest.",
"major-21|swords-12":"A successful conclusion fills you with drive to charge into the next goal. Good, but do a quick debrief first so the lessons are not lost. Then move fast, speak plainly, and bring your team along. Momentum after completion is valuable when it is built on what you just learned.",
"major-21|swords-13":"With a major stage complete, your fair, unsparing view of what happened is a real asset. Write an honest summary of the result, credit others accurately, and note what should change. Colleagues will value your straight assessment and probably ask for it again when the next plan takes shape.",
"major-21|swords-14":"A cycle of work is complete, and your judgment carries weight in deciding what happens next. Review the results fairly, set out the principles you want to keep, and communicate the verdict with clarity. A well-reasoned close gives the team confidence and a solid base for the next phase.",
"major-21|pentacles-01":"A chapter of your working life is complete, and a solid new opening follows. Finish what needs finishing and acknowledge what you achieved. Then step into the next phase with a practical first move. Endings done well make better beginnings.",
"major-21|pentacles-02":"A demanding period is settling into a sustainable rhythm. You handle competing tasks with skill. Recognize the accomplishment, and keep your habits of planning and budgeting time steady. Balance is not a finish line, so keep adjusting gently.",
"major-21|pentacles-03":"A team effort reaches a well-crafted conclusion. Take pride in the finished product and in how the group worked. Note lessons for the next project. Recognizing everyone's role builds goodwill, and a job done properly is its own reference for future collaboration.",
"major-21|pentacles-04":"You have arrived at a stable, secure place. Enjoy it, and avoid freezing it in place. Security lasts longer when it can adapt. Keep your good habits, keep learning, and hold your position with an open hand rather than a clenched fist.",
"major-21|pentacles-05":"A hard, lean stretch is finishing, and you have made it through. Acknowledge that. You gained resilience and a better sense of what help is available. Use that knowledge going forward. Small, steady habits from this period are worth keeping.",
"major-21|pentacles-06":"Fair exchange has come full circle. Contributions are recognized and shared success is real. Thank the people who helped, and pass along what you have learned. Generosity within a workplace strengthens it, and good relationships are the truest result of the effort.",
"major-21|pentacles-07":"The long wait is over and the harvest is in. Look over the results, learn from them, and decide what to plant next. Your patient approach has proven itself. Rest a little before starting again, since a completed cycle deserves recognition.",
"major-21|pentacles-08":"Mastery has arrived through repetition. What once took effort is now a skill you own. Recognize this and consider how to use it, teach it or extend it. Finish the current project with pride, then choose a new craft challenge to continue growing.",
"major-21|pentacles-09":"Independence and steady effort have brought you a well-rounded working life. Savor what you built. You do not need to prove anything further this month. Rest, appreciate, and then decide what you would like to explore next, from a place of security rather than pressure.",
"major-21|pentacles-10":"A long-term structure reaches maturity. Your work is part of something that endures, be it a career, a business or a body of skill. Honor the years behind it. Think about what you want to pass on and how to prepare the next people who will carry it.",
"major-21|pentacles-11":"A stage of learning has come to a satisfying close, and a new area of study beckons. Celebrate your progress, then pick the next skill to practice. Early attempts will feel awkward, and that is acceptable. Each completed cycle prepares you to begin the next one wisely.",
"major-21|pentacles-12":"Consistent, patient work reaches a satisfying finish. You did what you said you would do, and it shows. Enjoy the recognition and take a brief rest. Then look at the next steady task ahead, confident that your reliable style has proven its worth.",
"major-21|pentacles-13":"Practical care and skill have come together in a job well completed. People trust you, and results are sound. Take a moment to acknowledge the effort you put into the details. Then share your methods with others so that the good practices become part of the group.",
"major-21|pentacles-14":"Mastery and stewardship come together in a completed, well-run venture. Recognize what you have built and how you have led. Share credit and plan for those who will follow you. A leader who finishes well leaves a lasting, respected example.",
"wands-01|wands-02":"An idea has arrived and you are already seeing the bigger map. Before you chase the whole vision, choose the single first move and put a date beside it. Planning is useful only once something real is underway, so start the smallest piece this week.",
"wands-01|wands-03":"Your effort is out in the world and the ships are still at sea. Resist the urge to restart everything. Follow up once, politely, then use the quiet to prepare for what comes back. Early momentum counts for more when you stay steady through the gap.",
"wands-01|wands-04":"A new project starts with a supportive team behind it. Mark the launch with a short gathering, even a lunch, so everyone feels part of it. A stable base lets your enthusiasm turn into steady work, so lean on the colleagues who already trust you.",
"wands-01|wands-05":"Somebody's fresh idea lands in a room full of opinions. Expect pushback, overlapping proposals and some rivalry over who leads. The noise is useful if you let it sharpen the plan. Write the goal in one sentence and judge each argument by whether it serves that goal.",
"wands-01|wands-06":"A bold first step is earning you credit. Colleagues or a manager see the initiative, so let them see the result too. Share early progress plainly, without boasting or shrinking. Recognition now makes it easier to get backing when the next phase comes around.",
"wands-01|wands-07":"A new idea is under attack before it has had a chance to prove itself. Keep your position, but bring evidence instead of heat. Build a small working example that answers objections better than an argument can, and protect your time for that version.",
"wands-01|wands-08":"An idea is about to move faster than you expected. Emails, replies and decisions can come in a rush. Get the basics ready: the brief, the contacts, the next three steps. Speed rewards the prepared, so do the most important task first before the day fills up.",
"wands-01|wands-09":"You began strong and the project has since worn you down. The idea is still good. Protect your energy by cutting one task and finishing the rest in smaller pieces. Do not drop the goal just because you are tired. Rest first, then resume with a cleaner plan.",
"wands-01|wands-10":"A promising start has attracted more tasks than one person can hold. Every extra yes is now a weight. Choose the one thing that matters most, hand off or delay the rest, and say so plainly. A new flame lasts longer when you are not smothering it.",
"wands-01|wands-11":"An eager new interest has just met a strong first impulse. Try a small experiment: a short course, a side task, a chat with someone in the field. Learning by doing beats planning here. Keep notes on what energizes you so the next step is easier to choose.",
"wands-01|wands-12":"A burst of drive and a fast horse: use it on one target instead of five. Impatience can push past details that matter, so have someone look over what you send before it goes out. Big pushes work best when there is a clear finish line.",
"wands-01|wands-13":"A new idea meets someone who can make a room feel welcome and still hold the line. That may be you, or a colleague worth recruiting. Lead with enthusiasm and back it with a clear standard. People follow confidence that is friendly, so share your plan with energy and specifics.",
"wands-01|wands-14":"An idea and the leadership to back it have shown up together. Put the vision in plain terms, name the first milestone, and ask for help with the specifics. Good leaders start something and then let others shape it. Give a timeline you can actually keep.",
"wands-02|wands-03":"The plan is made and sent out, and now comes the wait for returns. Use the gap to build the next stage: contacts further afield, a backup timeline, skills the expansion will need. Patience is easier when your attention is on something productive.",
"wands-02|wands-04":"Stable ground lets you plan bigger. The team behind you is solid, so use that security to sketch the next stage. Hold a planning session that feels like a celebration, with food and good humor, and treat it as an opening for ambition, not just maintenance.",
"wands-02|wands-05":"Options are open and everyone has a view on which way to go. Competing visions can feel messy, but they are how a plan gets stress-tested. Collect the proposals, compare them against one goal, and decide by a set date so debate does not turn into delay.",
"wands-02|wands-06":"A plan you designed is starting to pay off in visible ways. Credit may come from above or from peers. Accept it, then use the goodwill to propose a larger step. A reputation built on follow-through makes the next ask much easier.",
"wands-02|wands-07":"Your strategy has critics. Stay on your ground, but prepare: know the numbers, the reasons and the alternatives you turned down. Defending a plan works better when you can show you weighed the other side. Decide which objections to answer and which to let pass.",
"wands-02|wands-08":"The plan you drew up is about to move. Messages, approvals and deadlines may arrive all at once. Keep your map handy and sort tasks by what the big picture needs. Fast movement suits you now, since you already know where you are headed.",
"wands-02|wands-09":"The vision is clear and your reserves are low. Do not scrap the plan. Trim it into smaller stages and finish the nearest one first. Near the end of a long push the temptation to stop is strongest, so take a real break and return with a shorter list.",
"wands-02|wands-10":"A large plan sits on far too many tasks for one person. Before growing, delegate. List what you are holding, circle the parts only you can do, and pass the rest on. Expansion built on an overloaded person tends to stall.",
"wands-02|wands-11":"A curious beginner and a long-range planner share one head here. Learn the basics of a new field, but keep your larger goals in view. Try a small test project that fits the big plan. The questions you ask now shape what you build next.",
"wands-02|wands-12":"With a goal in sight and the drive to charge at it, take ten minutes to plan the route before you ride out. Rushing toward the wrong target burns energy fast. Check the plan against the goal, then let the momentum work for you.",
"wands-02|wands-13":"A confident leader with a wide view is what this moment asks for, and you may be that person or need one. Present your plan with warmth and specifics, and ask the room to join. Charisma carries a strategy further when the numbers behind it are solid.",
"wands-02|wands-14":"Strategy and command of the room arrive together. Sketch where the work should stand a year from now, then name the two or three moves that lead there and who owns each. Leaders who share a clear map get far more effort from people than those who simply give orders.",
"wands-03|wands-04":"What you sent out is starting to return, and there is room to celebrate. Mark the milestone with your team before the next cycle begins. A pause here is not lost time. It keeps the people who did the work feeling that the work matters.",
"wands-03|wands-05":"Your work is out there, and meanwhile the office is loud. Disagreements about credit or direction can distract you from what you already set in motion. Stay out of the noise you do not need. Follow up on your project and let it speak for itself.",
"wands-03|wands-06":"Your long game is getting noticed. A project that took months of steady work is landing well, and people are saying so. Receive the praise without waving it off. Then note what built this success so you can repeat it on the next venture.",
"wands-03|wands-07":"A long project is out in the world and critics are showing up. Hold your position calmly. You did the groundwork, so point to it instead of arguing. Keep watching the horizon for results and stay steady while the rest of the room talks.",
"wands-03|wands-08":"What you launched is about to come back in a hurry. Replies, offers or delivery dates can arrive all at once after a quiet spell. Clear your schedule a little so you can respond well. Work you prepared earlier pays off best when you answer promptly.",
"wands-03|wands-09":"Results are taking longer than planned and your stamina is thin. You are close, though. Conserve energy, stick to the essential tasks, and do not reopen decisions you already made. A short, real break now keeps you going for the last stretch.",
"wands-03|wands-10":"The project is out the door, yet the burdens keep piling up at your desk. Waiting does not mean carrying everything alone. Pick the tasks that can be shared or dropped while results come in, and protect the hours you need to handle what returns.",
"wands-03|wands-11":"Between launches, a new interest catches your eye. Give it a small, curious look instead of shelving it. Meanwhile, keep an eye on the returns from your earlier effort. A learner's mind during the waiting stretch often finds the next project.",
"wands-03|wands-12":"One instinct says sit and let the work return, the other says charge ahead and force results. Choose a middle path: one firm follow-up, one new push. Bursts of energy go further when aimed at something you can finish before the replies arrive.",
"wands-03|wands-13":"A self-assured leader can stand on the cliff and watch the ships come in without panic. Stay visible and cheerful while outcomes are pending, and your team will borrow your calm. Prepare the next steps, and be ready to lead when the results arrive.",
"wands-03|wands-14":"Good leaders launch and then wait well. You set the direction and sent people out, so trust them while the results travel back. Review what you know, plan the next expansion on paper, and be ready to back the returns with real decisions.",
"wands-04|wands-05":"A comfortable team hits a stretch of bickering. Because the base is sound, the friction can be sorted out without real damage. Call a short meeting, let each person be heard, then return to the shared goal. Healthy teams argue and still stay together.",
"wands-04|wands-06":"Good work has finished and the team is recognized. Hold a proper celebration and name who did what. Credit shared honestly builds loyalty for the next push. Leaders who make space for this find that people stay motivated longer than any bonus memo could manage.",
"wands-04|wands-07":"You have found stable ground and now others want to shake it. Stand up for the working conditions and relationships you built. Do it with facts, not heat. A team that has just earned its footing will back you if you speak plainly.",
"wands-04|wands-08":"Things that were settled now start moving. A deadline or an announcement arrives fast, but the team behind you is ready. Lean on the routines and relationships you built, and use their steadiness to handle the speed without feeling scattered.",
"wands-04|wands-09":"Your team is solid and you are running low. Accept that support: share a task, admit you need a break, and let people cover you. Being tired near a good base is the easiest kind of tired to recover from, if you actually take the time.",
"wands-04|wands-10":"A solid team has drifted into leaning on one person. If that is you, redistribute. Name the tasks, assign them, and ask for honest feedback on what is too much. The group is strong enough to take on the load once it can see it.",
"wands-04|wands-11":"A new hire or a fresh interest finds a welcoming workplace. Take time to show them around and invite their questions, since beginners spot what the old hands stopped noticing. A stable team with curiosity in it finds better ways to do familiar work.",
"wands-04|wands-12":"A comfortable team meets someone with urgent, forward-charging energy. It could be you or a new colleague. Welcome the drive, but help it fit the group's rhythm. Give the eager person a clear job and a deadline, and the whole team benefits.",
"wands-04|wands-13":"A confident, welcoming leader can turn a workplace into a place people want to be. Set a tone of friendly standards: clear expectations, real appreciation, a good gathering now and then. Teams built like that keep their best people.",
"wands-04|wands-14":"A leader with vision has built something stable. Use that position to set the next goal, and bring the team into it at a gathering rather than through an announcement. People back a direction they helped shape on ground that feels secure.",
"wands-05|wands-06":"After a scramble of competing ideas, one clear result gets recognized. If that is you, share the credit with the people who pushed you. If it is someone else, congratulate them honestly. Rivalry handled well becomes a stronger team, not a grudge.",
"wands-05|wands-07":"Competition and criticism are coming from several sides at once. Decide which fights matter, because you cannot answer them all. Stay calm, keep your evidence organized, and let your steady results do most of the speaking.",
"wands-05|wands-08":"Conflict in the room gives way to a sudden deadline or a burst of news. Use the pressure to focus the group. Sort out who does what in a few clear lines and get moving. Fast work with clear roles settles most of the old arguments.",
"wands-05|wands-09":"After too many skirmishes at work you are tired and defensive. Step out of the ones that do not matter. Keep your energy for the one or two that do, and find a colleague you trust to share the stress with. Stamina lasts longer when you stop re-fighting old battles.",
"wands-05|wands-10":"Too many tasks and too many voices are pulling at you at once. Clear the load before trying to win the argument. Hand off what you can, turn down what you must, and pick one conversation to settle properly. Conflict shrinks as workload does.",
"wands-05|wands-11":"A newcomer or a new idea stirs up the usual order. Questions from a beginner can irritate the old guard, yet they also uncover weak spots. Answer curiosity with patience and let the noise show you what needs fixing.",
"wands-05|wands-12":"A rush of competing energy makes the office feel like a race. Drive is high, so point it at the goal and not at each other. Divide the work so people are not running the same lane, and agree on the rules before the sprint.",
"wands-05|wands-13":"A self-assured presence can turn a group's noise into useful debate. Listen first, then name the decision and the reason for it. Warmth with a firm standard makes it safe for people to disagree and still cooperate.",
"wands-05|wands-14":"Strong opinions fill the room, and a leader must steer them. Set the goal, ask each person for their best argument, and then decide in a set time. The strongest leaders make use of conflict without being threatened by it.",
"wands-06|wands-07":"Recognition has arrived, and so have people who want to challenge it. Keep your footing by staying factual about what you did and why. Accept the praise, answer critics briefly, and keep working. Reputation lasts when it is defended calmly.",
"wands-06|wands-08":"Your win is about to be talked about everywhere. Expect quick messages, fast follow-up requests and new opportunities. Prepare a short summary of what you achieved so you can respond crisply. Momentum from recognition fades if you wait too long to use it.",
"wands-06|wands-09":"The win is real, but it cost you. Take the recognition, then schedule actual rest before the next challenge. Do not let applause convince you that you must keep going at the same pace. Resilience counts more when it is paced.",
"wands-06|wands-10":"Success brings extra duties. You are praised, so you are given more, and the load grows heavier than the reward. Decide what the recognition should actually buy you: fewer tasks, better support, a clearer role. Ask for that in plain words.",
"wands-06|wands-11":"Your success draws fresh eyes. A junior colleague or a new contact wants to learn from you. Share what worked, and let their questions sharpen your own thinking. Mentoring, even informally, extends the reach of your win.",
"wands-06|wands-12":"Recognition can make you impatient for the next win. Channel that drive into a defined goal instead of several at once. Bring your team along, since a dash ahead leaves people behind. Fast is fine so long as the group arrives with you.",
"wands-06|wands-13":"A public win in the hands of a warm, sure leader builds loyalty. Thank the team by name, be straightforward about what comes next, and let your standards show in how you treat people. Reputation made with kindness tends to open doors on its own.",
"wands-06|wands-14":"A visible victory gives a leader credibility, and credibility is worth using. Set out the next ambitious goal while people are listening. Give credit widely and be specific about the vision, so your team knows what winning again would look like.",
"wands-07|wands-08":"Criticism is one thing, a speeding deadline is another, and both are here. Prioritize the few tasks that matter most and answer objections in short, factual replies. Holding your ground is easier when you are not distracted by every rushed message.",
"wands-07|wands-09":"Long resistance has left you worn but still standing. You do not have to win every point. Choose the stand that matters, let go of smaller ones, and lean on a colleague who has your back. The finish line is closer than exhaustion makes it feel.",
"wands-07|wands-10":"You are holding your position against critics while also buried in obligations. Something has to give, and it should be the extra load. Name what is essential, decline or delegate the rest, and keep your defense short and factual.",
"wands-07|wands-11":"A newcomer or a fresh idea challenges your way of doing things. Before you defend, listen. Sometimes the naive question exposes the weak point. Hold firm where your experience is solid, and stay open where the beginner sees something you missed.",
"wands-07|wands-12":"A fast, pushy push is going up against a firm defender. Both may be right about part of it. Put the actual facts on the table, set a pace you can both live with, and avoid letting speed or stubbornness decide the outcome.",
"wands-07|wands-13":"Confidence under pressure is the point here. When challenged, respond with warmth and firmness together. Explain your reasoning without defensiveness, and keep your attention on what you are building. People respect someone who holds a position and stays kind.",
"wands-07|wands-14":"You are leading under challenge. Keep the vision steady, present your reasons clearly, and welcome the useful criticism while ignoring the noise. Leadership earns trust by defending the direction and adjusting the details.",
"wands-08|wands-09":"A rush of activity is hitting a worn-out person. Triage ruthlessly: pick the tasks that truly need you now and push the rest to later. Being tired near the finish line is normal, and one sharp, rested push will beat a scattered week.",
"wands-08|wands-10":"Tasks are flying in faster than you can carry them. Do not try to hold it all. Put everything on one list, rank it, and hand off a third. Quick movement works only when you are not bent under the weight.",
"wands-08|wands-11":"News arrives, and a beginner's energy wants to run with it. Good, as long as someone reads the details. Follow the new lead quickly, test it small, and learn as you go. Speed and curiosity together find openings others are too slow to see.",
"wands-08|wands-12":"Nonstop motion is the mode here. Fast news, fast decisions, high drive. That can produce a lot in a short period, as long as someone keeps an eye on details. Set a clear target and a pause point so speed does not turn into scramble.",
"wands-08|wands-13":"When the pace picks up, a composed, confident person becomes the anchor. Stay visible and upbeat as news arrives, and make clear calls. Others take their cue from you, so your calm makes the whole effort faster and cleaner.",
"wands-08|wands-14":"Fast events need a leader with a clear vision. State the goal, assign roles and keep the information flowing. Speed only helps when everyone knows the direction. Make your decisions early, and trust people to run with them.",
"wands-09|wands-10":"You are close to the finish and overloaded, too. This is a signal, not a character flaw. Cut scope, delegate loudly, and protect your sleep. A smaller, finished project is better than a large one that breaks the person carrying it.",
"wands-09|wands-11":"Tired veterans and eager beginners can help each other. If you are the veteran, pass on what you know, and let the newcomer's energy lift the room. If you are the beginner, respect what stamina has taught the people around you.",
"wands-09|wands-12":"Your energy is low while the project demands speed. Do not sprint on empty. Pick the single most important push, do it, and then rest. Better to burst once with focus than to keep pushing while exhausted and make mistakes.",
"wands-09|wands-13":"Experience and self-assurance make a steady pair. You have seen the hard stretches and you know your worth. Lead with calm, rest where you need it, and mentor those who are newer. Confidence built on endurance carries weight in any room.",
"wands-09|wands-14":"Seasoned leadership means knowing when to rest and when to push. You have the vision and the stamina, but not infinite supply. Delegate the parts that wear you down, keep the strategic decisions, and finish the long project with a clear head.",
"wands-10|wands-11":"An overloaded person meets a beginner with energy to spare. Delegate something real and explain it well. Fresh eyes often simplify tasks that veterans have piled up. Letting someone else learn part of your work is good for both of you.",
"wands-10|wands-12":"Fast energy and too many tasks make a risky mix. Slow down long enough to choose priorities. Pushing harder will not lighten the load, so reduce it. Decide what you finish, what you delegate and what you let go of entirely.",
"wands-10|wands-13":"A self-assured person can also admit the load is too big. Use your authority to redistribute tasks and set realistic boundaries. Good leaders show the team that asking for help is normal. Your example makes it safe for others to speak up too.",
"wands-10|wands-14":"A leader with a vision who holds every task will burn out. Build the team you need and hand over real responsibility, not just chores. Your role is direction, not doing everything. Delegation is what lets the vision grow beyond one person.",
"wands-11|wands-12":"Enthusiasm and drive are running together, which gets projects off the ground quickly. Add a little structure: a goal, a deadline, a person who checks details. Quick starts work best when someone is also tracking where everything lands.",
"wands-11|wands-13":"A beginner with questions benefits from a confident guide. If you are the guide, be generous with what you know. If you are the beginner, ask directly and accept the feedback. Mentorship here is warm, practical and quick to pay off.",
"wands-11|wands-14":"A learner and a leader make good partners. If you are the learner, come prepared with questions and show initiative. If you are the leader, give clear direction and room to experiment. The new ideas can grow when the vision is shared openly.",
"wands-12|wands-13":"Drive and composure work well together. One person pushes forward, the other keeps the big picture and the team's morale. Split the roles clearly. Set the goal together, then let energy run inside boundaries that confident judgment has set.",
"wands-12|wands-14":"Raw momentum meets a clear vision. Give the energetic person a defined mission and deadline, and let the leader steady the course. Hot drive needs a destination. With both in place, big things can be completed rather than just started.",
"wands-13|wands-14":"A leader with vision and a leader with charisma can guide a group together. Divide the roles so one sets direction and the other builds morale. Agree on who decides what. United, they make a workplace people want to join.",
"cups-01|cups-02":"Goodwill is offered and a willing partner takes it. A joint project, a mentor, or a client who likes working with you fits here. Write the terms down early so the warmth has a frame, then let the shared effort build on it.",
"cups-01|cups-03":"A new wellspring of enthusiasm lands in a team ready to celebrate it. Share the idea at a gathering, a lunch or a kickoff, where energy travels. Give credit freely. Morale is the asset here, and a good mood works best when it is named and used.",
"cups-01|cups-04":"A fresh opportunity is waiting while you sit through a flat stretch. Check whether it is the job that bores you or only this week. Open the message, read the proposal, take the meeting. Dullness can make a good offer look like more of the same.",
"cups-01|cups-05":"A new chance sits behind the setback you keep replaying. Acknowledge the loss, a lost client or a scrapped plan, then look at what remains. Ask a colleague what they see that you cannot. The fresh offer will still be there once you have turned around.",
"cups-01|cups-06":"Goodwill from an old colleague or former boss turns into a fresh offer. Reconnect with someone you once worked well with. Their trust is a head start, though the new job is not the old one. Bring what you learned then and stay open to a different shape.",
"cups-01|cups-07":"One real offer stands among a cloud of possibilities. Write down the choices and mark which have a name, a date and a next step. The real ones usually have those. Give the concrete offer a fair hearing before dreams of ten other paths pull you off it.",
"cups-01|cups-08":"You are leaving something behind, and a fresh offer appears as you go. Test it gently rather than taking it as proof that you were right. Leave the old role on good terms, keep your references warm, and ask what this opening gives you that the last one could not.",
"cups-01|cups-09":"A welcome offer arrives and you are already comfortable enough to judge it well. You can afford to be choosy. Accept what fits your actual needs, not just the appealing title. Enjoy the recognition, then ask the practical questions so the good feeling has something solid under it.",
"cups-01|cups-10":"A single generous start can grow into a place where people truly belong. Look for roles where culture matters as much as pay. If you lead, a good opening gesture, such as a welcome note or a shared meal, sets the tone. Build the team the way you would a household.",
"cups-01|cups-11":"An early, unpolished spark of an idea needs a gentle messenger. Share it informally: a note, a quick voice message, a sketch. Expect friendly feedback rather than a decision. Keep the first version modest, and pay attention to what makes you excited about it.",
"cups-01|cups-12":"A proposal is delivered with style. A client, recruiter, or partner is making a pitch that sounds attractive. Enjoy it, then ask for details and a timeline in writing. Charm is a good sign, but follow-through is the test. Respond promptly and politely either way.",
"cups-01|cups-13":"A new opening comes with a person who reads the room well. Seek counsel from a mentor or colleague with real emotional intelligence. If you are that person, offer steady, private support. The best outcomes come from listening first, then deciding what the situation needs.",
"cups-01|cups-14":"A generous offer comes under a leader who is fair. Good for a role where you will be treated well and expected to pull your weight. If you are the decision maker, extend the opportunity with clear limits. Kindness and firm terms belong in the same contract.",
"cups-02|cups-03":"A strong working pair becomes the heart of a wider team. Two people who click can set the tone for the department. Include others early in the plan so the partnership does not turn into a clique. A shared win is worth marking with the group.",
"cups-02|cups-04":"The partnership is fine; the restlessness is yours. Before you leave a collaboration that works, ask what you are actually missing: variety, challenge, acknowledgment. Say it to your partner directly. A bored half of a good pair can usually be re-engaged with one honest conversation.",
"cups-02|cups-05":"A workable partnership is overshadowed by a recent disappointment. Do not blame your collaborator for what went wrong before they arrived. Name the setback, agree what you will do differently, and keep the shared goal in view. The two cups still standing are worth your attention.",
"cups-02|cups-06":"A good old working relationship can be renewed, perhaps with a former colleague or client. Reconnect and see whether you can be equals now. Loyalty built years ago is an asset. Do not let nostalgia set the terms, though: agree on roles that fit today's work.",
"cups-02|cups-07":"A dependable partner offers a real deal while you entertain a dozen maybes. Compare honestly: which alternatives have terms, dates and someone willing to sign? Commit to the partnership that is actually on the table, or say clearly why you are not. Indecision costs the other person time.",
"cups-02|cups-08":"A collaboration that looks fine on paper may no longer feed you. Before you step away, have a frank conversation with your partner about what has shifted. If the answer is still no, part on respectful terms and take the lessons. Go toward something, not only away.",
"cups-02|cups-09":"A reliable partner and a satisfying result belong together here. Good for celebrating a deal, a joint venture or a mutually beneficial arrangement. Keep the terms fair so both sides feel well rewarded. Enjoy the success and thank the person who helped you reach it.",
"cups-02|cups-10":"A good partnership is maturing into an institution. Two people who trust each other are building something others can join, such as a business, a program or a steady client base. Write down how decisions are made so it survives growth and everyone feels they belong.",
"cups-02|cups-11":"A young idea finds a partner who likes it. A fresh hire, intern or collaborator may bring unexpected, sincere enthusiasm. Pair that eagerness with your experience, and keep the first project small. Mutual respect between beginner and veteran makes the work better for both.",
"cups-02|cups-12":"A proposal gets a matching response. A pitch, an offer or a partnership deal may move smoothly when both sides show respect. Keep the agreement specific, since a good mood is not a contract. Follow up with a short written summary and confirm next steps.",
"cups-02|cups-13":"A balanced partnership gains a wise, empathic presence. A trusted colleague or mentor can help two sides meet in the middle. If you are the mediator, listen to both before proposing anything. Emotional steadiness is a professional skill, and it is what this situation rewards.",
"cups-02|cups-14":"A fair deal, overseen by a calm authority. Negotiations, contracts and partnerships go best when both sides feel respected and the decision maker is steady. Put expectations in writing, stay courteous when you disagree, and look for an outcome that both can honestly live with.",
"cups-03|cups-04":"The team is celebrating and you feel unmoved. Notice whether that is burnout or a sign the work has stopped fitting. Attend the event, stay briefly, and then use the space to think about what would interest you. Your colleagues' energy may not be yours, and that is useful information.",
"cups-03|cups-05":"Colleagues are celebrating a win while you are grieving a setback. Be honest about both, without spoiling the mood or hiding your disappointment. One trusted coworker can be told the truth. Their support helps more than an act, and the next success is easier to join afterwards.",
"cups-03|cups-06":"A reunion with former coworkers or a team that feels like family. The warmth is real and so are the useful contacts. Use the goodwill: ask for advice, share what you know. Do not let the comfort of the old crew keep you from new skills or new rooms.",
"cups-03|cups-07":"Lots of friendly people, lots of ideas, and no single direction. A brainstorm or team event can produce enthusiasm but not decisions. Close it with three items, an owner for each and a date. Good mood is a fine start, and it still needs to be converted into a plan.",
"cups-03|cups-08":"The team is cheerful, and you sense it is time to move on. Do not let the goodwill of colleagues make the choice for you. Weigh what you would gain and what you would lose. If you go, leave gracefully, say thank you, and keep the friendships.",
"cups-03|cups-09":"A successful stretch, celebrated with people who matter. A project closing, a goal reached, a team that works well. Mark it properly with a meal or a thank you, and record what made it work. Satisfaction is fuel, so let it carry you into the next effort.",
"cups-03|cups-10":"A happy team that feels like a family is the pattern here. Make room for rituals, shared meals and proper thanks. If you are hiring or joining, weigh culture heavily. The people you laugh with matter a great deal, though you should still read the contract closely.",
"cups-03|cups-11":"Good news spreads through the group, starting with a young or new person's enthusiasm. Give a newcomer a chance to present at the next gathering. A friendly, informal channel, such as a chat thread or team lunch, carries the idea better than a formal memo.",
"cups-03|cups-12":"An attractive offer is made in a room full of colleagues. A client dinner, networking event, or team outing may bring a proposal. Enjoy yourself and keep your head: follow the conversation up the next morning with a written summary and a few clear questions.",
"cups-03|cups-13":"A lively team relies on someone who quietly looks after morale. Be that person, or thank the one who is. Check in privately with colleagues who go silent at gatherings. Emotional attentiveness keeps a good atmosphere from being only surface.",
"cups-03|cups-14":"A happy group with a fair leader. A manager who is warm but clear keeps the morale high and the standards intact. If that is you, celebrate wins publicly and deliver hard news privately. If it is your boss, appreciate a workplace where kindness and accountability coexist.",
"cups-04|cups-05":"Disappointment has turned into disengagement. You may be pulling back after a setback, and calling it boredom. Name the loss, talk to someone you trust, and look for one small task that interests you. Motivation often returns once the grief is acknowledged rather than avoided.",
"cups-04|cups-06":"You are bored, and the past looks better than the present. Before you romanticize an old job or a former team, list what you actually disliked there. Some of what you miss is being younger. Bring one thing you loved from that time into your current routine.",
"cups-04|cups-07":"Dissatisfied with the present and overflowing with ideas, you risk starting many things and finishing none. Pick the one that would make a real difference, and give it a fixed amount of time. Discuss it with someone who will ask for results, not only for enthusiasm.",
"cups-04|cups-08":"The disinterest has lasted long enough to be information. After sitting with it, you are ready to seek something better suited. Prepare the move: update your materials, talk to people in the field, and leave on good terms. A purposeful exit beats a slow fade.",
"cups-04|cups-09":"Something is comfortable and something is dull, and the two feel alike. Ask whether you are satisfied or merely unchallenged. If you are well placed, add a small stretch goal. If the ease hides stagnation, raise it with your manager before your interest fades.",
"cups-04|cups-10":"A stable workplace where you belong may have gone flat. Before assuming you need to leave, ask for a new project or responsibility. Belonging is hard to find and worth protecting. Small changes in role can reawaken interest without losing the good culture around you.",
"cups-04|cups-11":"A quiet chance to engage has arrived in the form of a new idea or junior colleague. Your boredom may be hiding it. Take a few minutes to hear them out. A fresh perspective can reconnect you to why you chose this work in the first place.",
"cups-04|cups-12":"An attractive proposal reaches you in a flat season. Do not accept simply to escape boredom, and do not reject it for the same reason. Get the details, sleep on them, and ask whether it fits what you want. Restlessness is a poor judge of opportunity.",
"cups-04|cups-13":"A perceptive colleague or mentor can help you understand your disengagement. Talk it through with someone who listens well before making changes. If you are the listener, give space and few answers. Often the most useful thing is to help someone name what is missing.",
"cups-04|cups-14":"A flat patch under a steady leader invites a serious conversation about direction. Request a meeting about growth, responsibilities or a change of scope. If you lead someone who seems bored, ask what they need. Careful, fair guidance can turn indifference into interest.",
"cups-05|cups-06":"After a setback, a familiar face or place offers relief. Ask a former mentor or colleague for perspective, and let their steadiness help. Do not hurry back to old habits because they feel safe. Take the comfort, then bring it forward into what comes next.",
"cups-05|cups-07":"A disappointment has set your mind wandering among grand alternatives. Hold off on big moves while you are hurting. Write down the options, mark which are realistic, and revisit the list in a few weeks. Clear thinking returns after the sting has faded.",
"cups-05|cups-08":"After a loss, you turn away from something that no longer works. Leaving can be wise once the disappointment is processed. Do not flee in the first heat. Take notes on what you learned, confirm a next step, and go with your professional relationships intact.",
"cups-05|cups-09":"A recent setback is giving way to a sense of having enough. Acknowledge what you lost, and then acknowledge what you built. A modest, satisfying result after a hard period is worth savoring. Let the recovery be unhurried, and keep your confidence.",
"cups-05|cups-10":"A setback looms large, but a wider, supportive community stands behind it. Lean on team members, mentors or family for practical help. The loss is real and so is the support. Giving your attention to the latter shortens the former.",
"cups-05|cups-11":"A small, sincere piece of encouragement reaches you after a disappointment. Take it in. A junior colleague or friendly message may be the first sign that things are not as bleak as they feel. Reply kindly, and look at what is still possible.",
"cups-05|cups-12":"A charming proposal turns up just as you are discouraged. Take your time with it. You may be tempted to grab it as relief. Ask questions, compare it with what you actually want, and answer when your head is clearer than your mood.",
"cups-05|cups-13":"After a disappointment, a steady, empathic person is your best resource. Seek a mentor or supportive colleague who can listen. If you are that person, resist rushing to solutions. Processing the setback properly means the next attempt comes from clarity rather than hurt.",
"cups-05|cups-14":"A setback handled by a cool head. A manager or mentor can help you put a loss in perspective and decide on a sensible response. If you lead, acknowledge the disappointment openly, then set clear next steps. Feeling the loss and planning recovery can go together.",
"cups-06|cups-07":"The past and imagination are competing with the present. Before you chase a former role or a dream career, look at what is concrete. Nostalgia edits out the hard parts, and fantasies skip the effort. Choose one realistic move and treat it as a test.",
"cups-06|cups-08":"Saying goodbye to a place that once fit. A comfortable role or team may have been outgrown. Thank the people who taught you, take what you learned, and step toward something larger. Leaving with gratitude keeps doors open and eases the transition.",
"cups-06|cups-09":"A long record of good work is now paying back in satisfaction. Reflect on how far you have come, and let colleagues know what they meant to you. Enjoy the comfortable stretch, and keep one small goal in mind so contentment does not turn into coasting.",
"cups-06|cups-10":"A workplace that feels like home, with traditions and trust. Such cultures are worth protecting. If you help new people join, pass on the stories and habits that make the team what it is. Mentorship and continuity are the strengths here.",
"cups-06|cups-11":"A childlike curiosity is welcome here. A junior colleague, a new approach or a playful exercise may bring fresh energy. Let the newcomer show you something. Your experience and their openness together make a good team for a small, friendly experiment.",
"cups-06|cups-12":"An offer arrives from someone you know or from an old connection. A former client, colleague or contact may suggest a project. Familiarity helps with trust, but read the proposal as you would a stranger's. Ask about scope, timing and what has changed since you last worked together.",
"cups-06|cups-13":"An experienced, empathic colleague connects to the history of the work. A long-standing mentor or client understands the background and cares about you. Ask for their perspective on a current decision. If you are that person, share institutional memory generously and listen carefully.",
"cups-06|cups-14":"Loyalty to a long-standing relationship meets a wise leader. A veteran manager or long-term client appreciates what you have delivered. Use that trust to ask for what you need. Keep the warmth and be professional: a good history is a base, and it is not a substitute for clear terms.",
"cups-07|cups-08":"After weighing illusory options, you decide to go. Good: it means you have stopped juggling maybes. Choose a direction based on values, not glamour, and plan the exit. Notify the right people and tie up loose ends so your next step begins cleanly.",
"cups-07|cups-09":"You have a range of options and enough comfort to choose well. Sort them by real merit, not by glitter. Being settled lets you negotiate from a steady place. Decide which wish is worth the effort, and set a first step within the week.",
"cups-07|cups-10":"Among many tempting paths, one offers community and stability. Consider how the team treats its people and whether you would want to be there in five years. Glamour fades, and belonging does not. Compare the options on daily life, not just on the brochure.",
"cups-07|cups-11":"A fresh, unproven idea has joined a long list of ideas. Test it cheaply: a sketch, a short trial or a conversation with someone who would use it. Enthusiasm is a good start. Let the evidence, rather than the daydream, decide how far it goes.",
"cups-07|cups-12":"A polished pitch arrives among many possibilities. Ask for specifics, such as deliverables, timeline and who is responsible. Appealing presentation does not equal substance. Compare it with your other options on the same criteria, and make your decision in writing so that illusions do not creep back in.",
"cups-07|cups-13":"With too many choices, a steady advisor is the useful one. Ask someone with good judgement to question your list. If you are advising, ask what the person wants before offering opinions. Good listening turns a pile of options into a short list.",
"cups-07|cups-14":"A wide field of options needs a decision maker with clear criteria. Set your priorities before looking at opportunities, and stick to them. If a leader is guiding you, accept realistic limits. Good judgement narrows possibilities to the ones that can actually be delivered.",
"cups-08|cups-09":"Part of you is ready to walk away while another part is close to feeling satisfied. Before deciding anything, write down what the work gives you and what it costs you. Talk with someone you trust about what would make it fulfilling. Clarity about what you want matters more than how quickly you move.",
"cups-08|cups-10":"You are leaving a role that does not suit you, and what you want is a place where you belong. Seek out cultures that match your values. Ask questions about how people treat each other. Moving on is easier when you know what kind of team you are moving toward.",
"cups-08|cups-11":"Just as you are leaving, a new, modest opportunity appears. Hear it out, since it could be interesting. Do not turn back only from habit or flattery. Weigh it against your reasons for going, and decide on substance.",
"cups-08|cups-12":"An appealing offer arrives as you are walking out. Good timing can mean luck, or it can be a distraction. Review it against your reasons for leaving, and ask for specifics. If it fits, say yes with open eyes. If it does not, go ahead with your plan.",
"cups-08|cups-13":"A trusted advisor helps you handle a departure with grace. Speak with someone who has seen career changes and will not flatter you. If you are the one being left, listen without defensiveness. A well-managed exit preserves relationships and leaves everyone clearer.",
"cups-08|cups-14":"A well-considered exit, guided by sound judgement. Plan the timeline, handle handovers properly, and leave on professional terms. If a senior person is your advisor, ask how they would approach it. Acting deliberately, rather than impulsively, protects your reputation and your peace.",
"cups-09|cups-10":"A well-earned satisfaction meets a supportive community. This suits reaching a long-term goal with a team that shares in it. Celebrate properly, thank those who helped, and consider how you can make the place better for the next group. Shared success is lasting success.",
"cups-09|cups-11":"A comfortable stretch of work gets a fresh spark from a newcomer or a small new idea. You are secure enough to hear it without feeling threatened. Try a little experiment. A modest, playful addition can make a good situation better.",
"cups-09|cups-12":"A gratifying proposal arrives while you are doing well. You can afford to be selective. Ask for details, and accept only if it adds something you want. Enjoy the recognition of the offer, and answer graciously whichever way you go.",
"cups-09|cups-13":"Satisfaction supported by a wise, caring presence. A good mentor, colleague or client keeps the atmosphere healthy. If you are that person, continue to pay attention to others' needs. A workplace that feels good usually has someone quietly making it so.",
"cups-09|cups-14":"A satisfying position under steady, fair leadership. Use the stability to plan for the long term, such as learning, savings habits and sensible goals. If you lead, enjoy the result without complacency. Keep standards high and your door open.",
"cups-10|cups-11":"A close-knit team welcomes a young, enthusiastic voice. Give a newer colleague a real chance to contribute and introduce them around. Fresh ideas work best in a culture that is secure enough to listen. Both sides benefit: they learn, and you are reminded why you started.",
"cups-10|cups-12":"An offer comes to a stable, well-integrated team or to a person who is already settled. Consider how it affects the people around you. Discuss it with those you trust. A good proposal respects the community as well as the individual.",
"cups-10|cups-13":"A caring, wise person anchors a close team. Acknowledge the colleague who looks after people, or be that person with boundaries. Culture depends on such attention. Make space in meetings for people to say how things are going, and act on what you hear.",
"cups-10|cups-14":"A well-run organization with a fair leader. This is a place where people feel looked after and are also held to a standard. If you are in charge, protect both the welfare and the accountability. If you are applying, look for this combination.",
"cups-11|cups-12":"A fresh idea has found a messenger who can deliver it. A proposal may move from informal first contact to a formal invitation. Keep both stages honest: share the concept plainly, then confirm details in writing. Enthusiasm is an advantage when matched with follow-through.",
"cups-11|cups-13":"A young idea or employee meets an empathetic mentor. Offer your guidance without taking over. If you are the newcomer, ask the wiser person to look at your first draft. Patient feedback at the start shapes everything that follows.",
"cups-11|cups-14":"A creative young idea meets a level-headed authority. Present it to a decision maker in a way that respects their concerns. If you lead, encourage the new thinker while setting boundaries. A fair mentor can turn a sweet idea into a workable plan.",
"cups-12|cups-13":"A charming proposal is weighed by a wise, empathic person. Present your pitch to someone who will listen to what is underneath it. If you are the evaluator, ask about values and needs as well as details. Good decisions account for feelings and facts.",
"cups-12|cups-14":"An attractive pitch reaches a measured decision maker. Expect questions and prepare answers: costs, risks, timeline. If you are the decider, give the pitch a real hearing and keep your criteria. Good ideas tend to survive scrutiny, and good leaders enjoy the exercise.",
"cups-13|cups-14":"Empathy and authority form a strong leadership pair. Listening well and deciding well belong together. If you work with this combination, let the feeling person surface the concerns and the steady one set the course. Teams thrive when care and clear judgement share the work.",
"swords-01|swords-02":"The fog around a stuck decision can lift once you write down the real question in one sentence. Two options have been weighing on you equally because you never named what matters. Name it, tell the person waiting on your answer, and let the stalemate close this week.",
"swords-01|swords-03":"A disappointment at work is easier to handle once you describe it accurately. Skip the vague complaint and state what happened, what it cost you, and what you need next. That plain summary makes an useful email, a fair conversation with a manager, or a cleaner decision.",
"swords-01|swords-04":"A sharp idea has arrived at a tired moment. Capture it in a few lines, then step away and rest before you present it or act on it. Good thinking needs a clear head to deliver well. Protect a real break this week, then come back and decide.",
"swords-01|swords-05":"Being correct in the meeting is one thing, and leaving a colleague humiliated is another. Your argument is sound, yet the way you land it decides whether anyone follows it. Make your case once, cleanly, and let the other person keep some dignity in the room.",
"swords-01|swords-06":"Once you see your situation plainly, a move toward better conditions becomes practical. That might be a transition to a new team, a slower pace, or a role with fewer daily conflicts. Write down what you are leaving behind and what you are heading toward, then plan the crossing step by step.",
"swords-01|swords-07":"You can now see the angle behind a quiet maneuver at work, whether a colleague is working around the team or you have been tempted to. Use the clarity to choose the open route. Document what you know, ask direct questions, and skip the shortcut that depends on nobody noticing.",
"swords-01|swords-08":"You have described your situation to yourself as a trap, and a sharper look may show unlocked doors. List the actual constraints, then mark which ones are facts and which are assumptions. Often the job is less sealed than it feels. Pick one assumption and test it with a single question this week.",
"swords-01|swords-09":"Worry about work has been loud after hours, and clear thinking is the answer. Sit down in the morning and sort the dread into three piles: things you can act on, things you can ask about, and things outside your control. Acting on the first pile lets you sleep better.",
"swords-01|swords-10":"A phase of your working life has run its course, and you can tell why. Resist reopening it or arguing the case one last time. Summarize what you learned, tie off loose ends, and use the clean slate to decide what kind of work you want to build next.",
"swords-01|swords-11":"An eager learner, perhaps you or a junior colleague, is asking exactly the right question, and a sharp answer is within reach. Do the research before speaking in the meeting. Bring one precise observation instead of several vague ones. Sharing your findings with the right person will earn more respect than volume.",
"swords-01|swords-12":"A driven colleague, or a version of you, is charging ahead with a sharp idea and little patience for process. The idea may be good. Test it before the whole team jumps in. Give the plan a short check for missing steps, then move with confidence once it holds.",
"swords-01|swords-13":"An experienced, unsentimental person, possibly a mentor, a senior colleague or your own best judgment, offers a clean read on a work problem. Take the honest version over the flattering one. Ask for specifics, write them down, and use them to decide your next move instead of protecting your pride.",
"swords-01|swords-14":"A sharp idea and an authority figure are meeting. You may be the one delivering a ruling, or waiting for one. Come prepared with facts and a clear argument, and be willing to hear the other side. A decision grounded in standards is easier to defend and to live with.",
"swords-02|swords-03":"You have been holding off on a tough discussion, and the tension is beginning to show. Something disappointing happened, yet you keep acting as if the question is still open. Stating the letdown aloud, to a colleague or your manager, ends the stalemate and lets you choose a direction.",
"swords-02|swords-04":"An unresolved choice has worn you out, and the best next step may be to stop for a moment. Take a real day off from the question and let your mind recover. You will weigh the options better when you are not exhausted. Set a day to return and decide.",
"swords-02|swords-05":"A deadlocked negotiation or team disagreement is shifting from a problem-solving exercise into a fight about who wins. Step back and ask what outcome you actually need. A compromise that lets both sides save face may serve you better than a victory nobody forgets.",
"swords-02|swords-06":"After weeks of going back and forth, a decision is finally carrying you toward steadier ground. Accept that not every detail can be settled and choose the path that lowers stress. A project hand-off, a team change or a simpler schedule may be the calmer shore you have been wanting.",
"swords-02|swords-07":"An impasse may be hiding a quiet side agreement, an unspoken plan or information someone has left out. Ask direct questions in writing so the facts are on record. If you are the one holding back, consider whether telling the whole story would speed up the decision.",
"swords-02|swords-08":"You are waiting for a perfect answer while telling yourself there are no good options. Both the indecision and the feeling of being trapped are mental habits. List three actions you could take within your control, choose the smallest, and do it before the weekend to break the loop.",
"swords-02|swords-09":"An undecided career question is now costing you sleep. The worry has grown bigger than the decision itself. Set a deadline, gather the two or three facts you truly need, and ask one person who has been there. Choosing with good information will quiet the night thoughts faster than waiting.",
"swords-02|swords-10":"A question you have been weighing for months has effectively been answered by events. Stop revisiting it and accept that a chapter has closed. Take a short pause to mark the ending, update your plans, and tell the people who have been waiting on your decision.",
"swords-02|swords-11":"A stalemate may be broken by a fresh set of eyes, perhaps a newer teammate or a simple question you had not asked. Do some quiet research, gather facts, and look at the options without taking a side first. Curiosity often unlocks what arguing could not.",
"swords-02|swords-12":"A long deadlock meets a fast-moving person who wants an answer now. That can be a relief or a risk. Push for progress, but check that the decision has real information behind it. A quick, clean resolution is fine, as long as it is not a reaction to impatience.",
"swords-02|swords-13":"A stalemate is being assessed by someone who sees it without sentiment, a sharp colleague, a mentor, or your own clear mind. Listen to the unvarnished version of the problem. The answer is often simpler than the debate suggests. Follow the facts, and trim the options to two.",
"swords-02|swords-14":"An indecisive stretch needs a ruling, either from an authority figure or from your own principles. Set criteria, such as fairness, workload or long-term fit, and score the options honestly. A decision based on stated standards is easier to explain, and it ends the waiting.",
"swords-03|swords-04":"A workplace disappointment, like a rejection, a harsh review or a lost project, asks for recovery before response. Take a short break from the situation, protect your sleep, and skip the reply you would send tonight. You will handle the next step better once the sting has eased.",
"swords-03|swords-05":"A conflict at work ended with a winner and a colleague left hurt, and the fallout may linger. If you were the winner, acknowledge the damage. If you were hurt, say so calmly to the right person. A short, direct conversation can prevent lingering resentment from shaping the team.",
"swords-03|swords-06":"A painful episode at work is behind you, and you are moving to something steadier. A transfer, a new role or simply a calmer season is carrying you away from disappointment. Bring the lessons but leave the resentments. Give yourself credit for getting through.",
"swords-03|swords-07":"A disappointment at work may trace to a hidden move, such as credit taken quietly, information withheld or a plan you were not told about. Gather facts before reacting. A calm, documented conversation beats a public accusation. If you have been holding back yourself, consider coming clean.",
"swords-03|swords-08":"After a painful setback, you may feel boxed in and certain that nothing can change. That belief deserves a gentle challenge. List what has actually closed and what remains possible. Ask one trusted colleague for an outside view, and try one small move this week.",
"swords-03|swords-09":"A work disappointment is echoing loudly after hours, costing you sleep and peace. Try giving the worry a set time during the day, then close the laptop and do something grounding. If the stress stays heavy, talk with someone you trust. Many fears shrink once they are said aloud.",
"swords-03|swords-10":"A painful stretch at work has reached its ending, whether a project collapse, a restructure or a role that ran its course. The hurt is real, and so is the clean slate. Write down what you learned, thank the people who helped, and let the old chapter close before planning the next.",
"swords-03|swords-11":"A disappointment at work has sharpened your attention. You are watching, asking questions and learning how things really operate. Use that alertness well: take notes, clarify expectations in writing and ask a colleague what they noticed. Curiosity after a setback can turn into real skill.",
"swords-03|swords-12":"A careless, fast-moving colleague, or a hasty choice of your own, has caused some hurt. Stating the plain facts is not the same as handling them kindly. Reach out, acknowledge the impact, and agree on a steadier pace for the next discussion so the same sting is not repeated.",
"swords-03|swords-13":"A setback is being read by a sharp, unsentimental person, possibly a seasoned colleague or your own mature judgment. They see what went wrong and do not soften it. That honesty hurts and helps. Use the feedback to draft a practical plan and keep your dignity while you do.",
"swords-03|swords-14":"A painful setback meets an authority figure or a rule book. A performance review, a policy decision or a boss's ruling may sting, and it is also clear. Ask for the reasoning in detail, separate the facts from the feelings, and respond on the record with calm.",
"swords-04|swords-05":"After a draining fight over who was right, the most useful move is a real break. Do not rehash it over email tonight. Give the conflict a day, let the adrenaline fade, and return with a simple aim: settle the practical question and get on with the work.",
"swords-04|swords-06":"You are moving from a stressful phase toward steadier conditions, and you are allowed to recover on the journey. Take the vacation, the lighter workload or the reset week without guilt. A calmer role or rhythm is coming into view, and your energy will return as you settle into it.",
"swords-04|swords-07":"Stepping back from the noise gives you a chance to spot an unspoken move at work. Take a day to review the facts, reread emails and think about who benefits from the current plan. Rest first, then address anything odd in a calm, specific conversation.",
"swords-04|swords-08":"You are tired, and tiredness is telling you there is no way out of a work situation. Before you accept that, recover. Take real time off, then list your options with a fresher mind. Many walls shrink after a proper rest, and the ones that do not can be planned around.",
"swords-04|swords-09":"Anxious nights about work are draining you, and the fix starts with protecting your sleep. Put the tasks on paper before bed so your mind can let go. Take a real break over the weekend. Clear, rested thinking will show which concerns are real and which were only noise.",
"swords-04|swords-10":"A rough chapter is over, and recovery is the job now. Take leave, slow your pace, or at least reduce your commitments while you regain strength. Do not jump into the next project to outrun the last one. A rested restart will carry you further than a rushed one.",
"swords-04|swords-11":"You are in a learning pause, taking in information before you act. A quiet week of reading, shadowing someone or asking thoughtful questions is time well spent. Resist the urge to prove yourself right away. Rest and research now will make your first real move sharper.",
"swords-04|swords-12":"Momentum is tugging at you while your energy runs low. Rushing ahead tired usually means redoing work later. Hold off on the big send or the bold pitch until you have rested. If a pushy colleague wants an instant reply, tell them when you will answer.",
"swords-04|swords-13":"A calm break gives you a clear read on your situation. Away from the pressure, you notice which commitments are pointless and which matter. Trust that unsentimental view. Return with a short list of what to keep, what to drop and what to ask your manager about.",
"swords-04|swords-14":"You are taking time to reflect before a judgment or a policy decision, and that restraint is wise. Review the rules, the facts and your priorities while rested. A ruling made on a calm day stands up better. Return with a clear, fair position and explain it plainly.",
"swords-05|swords-06":"A bitter workplace dispute is behind you, and a calmer arrangement is on the horizon. Let go of needing the last word. A transfer, a new project or simply a change of tone carries you out of the fight. Take your good work with you and leave the grudge.",
"swords-05|swords-07":"Competition and quiet maneuvering are in the air, with a colleague trying to come out ahead, possibly at another's expense. Keep your own conduct clean and keep records. If you are tempted to take a shortcut to win, remember that reputation outlasts any single victory.",
"swords-05|swords-08":"After a conflict, you feel boxed in by a reputation or a grudge. The situation may be less fixed than your mind says. Look at what really changed, and make one small gesture of repair, such as a cooperative email, before you assume the damage is permanent.",
"swords-05|swords-09":"A workplace clash is still ringing in your head at night. You may worry about how you came across or what others now think. Write down what you did well and what you would do differently. Then let that be enough, and plan one repair conversation for tomorrow.",
"swords-05|swords-10":"A protracted conflict has reached its final page. The rivalry, the dispute or the office politics has finished, and the costs are visible. Resist the urge for one more parting shot. Close the file, thank the people who stood by you, and aim your energy at new work.",
"swords-05|swords-11":"A sharp-eyed newcomer or a fresh question is exposing how a rivalry really works. Watch how credit and blame move around your team before you take sides. Ask neutral questions and keep notes. Understanding the dynamics first will help you avoid a fight that cannot be won.",
"swords-05|swords-12":"An aggressive, competitive push is underway, and someone is charging at a colleague or a rival to come out on top. Before you join, ask what you actually want from the outcome. Fast wins often leave lasting resentment. Advance your work on its merits and let the noise pass.",
"swords-05|swords-13":"A workplace dispute meets a cool, perceptive mind that can see what each side wants. Use that clarity to cut through the posturing. Name the shared goal plainly, acknowledge one fair point from the other side, and propose a solution. You can be honest without humiliating anyone.",
"swords-05|swords-14":"A conflict now meets authority, such as a manager, an HR process or a clear policy. If you are in the right, let the facts speak without gloating. If you are in the wrong, own it early. A fair ruling respected by everyone settles a fight more durably than a win.",
"swords-06|swords-07":"You are leaving a difficult situation, and someone may be managing how it looks. Keep your own exit tidy: hand over clearly, share the information that belongs to the team and avoid any trick. A transparent transition protects your name and leaves the next phase unburdened.",
"swords-06|swords-08":"A move toward a better situation is underway, and your thoughts may keep saying you are stuck. Look at the evidence: you have already changed teams, tasks or routines. Give that progress a fair hearing, and name the next small step so the old story loses its grip.",
"swords-06|swords-09":"You are leaving a rough patch at work, and anxious thoughts have come along for the ride. Notice that the circumstances are already improving. Jot down the worries before bed, plan a slow first week in the new rhythm, and give your mind permission to catch up.",
"swords-06|swords-10":"A chapter has finished, and the passage to something calmer has started. Accept that you do not need to resolve everything before you go. Hand over what you can, say your goodbyes, and head for work that suits the person you are now.",
"swords-06|swords-11":"You are heading toward calmer conditions, and there is much to learn on the way. Keep asking questions in the new environment, observe how the team works and take notes. A curious, humble approach during a transition earns trust faster than proving yourself.",
"swords-06|swords-12":"You are on the way to a calmer role, and a fast-moving colleague or your own restlessness may want to speed things up. Keep the transition orderly. Finish the handover, ask the questions and let the new position settle before you push for results.",
"swords-06|swords-13":"A straight-talking mentor, or your own steady judgment, is helping you transition to a more peaceful setting. Take the advice that is hard to hear. Name what you will no longer tolerate, carry only what is useful to the next role, and tell people what you need plainly.",
"swords-06|swords-14":"A transition is being guided by clear rules and sound judgment. You may be the one who sets the terms of a move, or a steady leader is managing it well. Put the plan in writing, be fair in the handover and trust that an orderly departure builds a good reputation.",
"swords-07|swords-08":"Quiet maneuvering has left you feeling boxed in, perhaps by office politics or by a half-truth you keep repeating. Separate what is actually known from what is assumed. Bring one question into the open with someone you trust, and see which bindings were imaginary.",
"swords-07|swords-09":"A secret, a shortcut or a rumor is wearing on your nerves at work. If you are holding something back, consider the relief of coming clean early. If you suspect a colleague, collect facts before assuming the worst. Either way, bring the matter to a fair person in the morning.",
"swords-07|swords-10":"A plan that depended on staying quiet has collapsed, whether it was yours or someone else's. The ending is painful and clean. Tell the truth plainly, take responsibility for your share and avoid making up new explanations. A straight account is the quickest route to rebuilding trust.",
"swords-07|swords-11":"A sharp-eyed junior colleague, or your own alertness, catches a detail that does not add up. Keep notes, ask a neutral question and check your facts before raising it. A careful, well-documented observation protects everyone better than a loud suspicion would.",
"swords-07|swords-12":"A swift, blunt colleague may be using speed to cover a shortcut, or you feel tempted to. Moving fast and staying quiet is how mistakes get buried. Take a moment to double-check the facts and share your plan with someone. Transparent speed beats clever speed every time.",
"swords-07|swords-13":"An experienced, sharp-minded person, possibly a senior peer or your own instinct, notices where a plan is being quietly bent. Ask pointed questions and do not accept a polished answer in place of a real one. You are well placed to protect the team by asking plainly.",
"swords-07|swords-14":"Authority and a quiet scheme collide. A leader or a rule book is about to deal with a shortcut someone took. If it was yours, come forward before you are found out. If it was someone else's, bring the facts to the proper person and let a fair process work.",
"swords-08|swords-09":"Feeling trapped at work keeps you awake, and lost sleep makes the trap look tighter. Break the cycle with one concrete action, like updating a resume outline, asking a colleague for advice or scheduling a conversation with a manager. Action quiets the spiral better than more thinking does.",
"swords-08|swords-10":"A period of feeling boxed in at work is ending, perhaps through a change in circumstances or your own decision to see the situation differently. Let the old constraint go. Write down what you learned about your limits and choose a next step that matches who you are now.",
"swords-08|swords-11":"You feel restricted, and a fresh question could loosen it. Ask someone in a different role how they handle a similar limit. A little research on options, such as a new team, a course or a conversation about duties, often shows more room to move than you had assumed.",
"swords-08|swords-12":"Impatience with a restrictive situation makes you want to charge out. A fast reaction can feel like freedom, but a plan is better than a burst. Identify exactly what is boxing you in, then make a direct request or a clear change. Decisive and prepared works best.",
"swords-08|swords-13":"A sharp mentor or your own unsentimental thinking is poking holes in a belief that you are trapped. Listen to it. Make two lists: what is truly fixed and what is merely habit. Most of the second list can be changed with a direct conversation or a small experiment.",
"swords-08|swords-14":"You feel stuck, and a fair-minded authority, perhaps a mentor or your own judgment, points to the rules that actually apply. Learn your real options, including contracts, policies and boundaries. Knowing the facts reduces the sense of entrapment. Then decide firmly what you will ask for.",
"swords-09|swords-10":"A sleepless period of work stress has reached its low point and its close. The dread is at its loudest just before something ends. Do what you can today, lean on support and avoid making sweeping decisions in the dark. A lighter stretch is coming once this one is fully over.",
"swords-09|swords-11":"Worry at work is being met by a simple, honest question. A junior teammate, a new manager or your own curiosity may ask what exactly is going wrong. Write the answer down. Naming specific problems is the quickest way to shrink a vague, sleepless dread.",
"swords-09|swords-12":"Stress and speed make a risky pair. A sleepless worry can drive a hasty email, or a fast-moving colleague may be adding to your dread. Hold off on reactions until you have slept. Then respond with a short, factual message and a clear next step.",
"swords-09|swords-13":"An experienced, level-headed figure can help you separate real work risks from the ones your mind manufactures. Ask a seasoned colleague to look at the situation with you. Their honest read, without panic, may be exactly the relief you have been wanting.",
"swords-09|swords-14":"Anxiety meets authority and sound judgment. A manager, a mentor or your own clear standards can bring order to sleepless concerns. Set out the facts, weigh them against the rules or goals and make a reasoned call. A decision on paper calms the mind better than circling.",
"swords-10|swords-11":"A chapter has closed, and an inquisitive, watchful mood is taking over. Ask thoughtful questions about what you want next, and talk to people in roles you admire. A fresh beginner's mindset after a finish is useful. Collect information before you commit to the next move.",
"swords-10|swords-12":"Something just finished, and you want to leap into the next one at full speed. A short pause to process the ending will make the new start stronger. Review what went wrong, what went right and what you want differently. Then move quickly and with a plan.",
"swords-10|swords-13":"With one phase over, a clear-minded person, such as a mentor, an experienced colleague or your own maturity, is helping you look at it squarely. Write a plain account of what occurred, including your share. Honesty with yourself is what makes the next role better.",
"swords-10|swords-14":"A chapter ends with a clear ruling, such as a final decision from a leader, a closed contract or a finished policy. Accept the verdict, ask about proper next steps and keep your dignity. Take the standards you believe in into your next opportunity.",
"swords-11|swords-12":"An observant learner and an impatient driver are sharing a project, whether in your team or inside you. Let the learner gather facts while the driver sets the pace. Agree on a short deadline and one person responsible for each step. Together they can move quickly without missing details.",
"swords-11|swords-13":"A newer or junior person is learning from a seasoned, discerning one. You may be either. Ask direct questions, take notes and be ready for frank feedback. If you are the mentor, be specific and fair. The relationship works best when honesty goes both ways.",
"swords-11|swords-14":"An eager learner is facing an authority figure, perhaps a new hire with a manager, or your questioning side and your decision-making side. Prepare thoughtful questions and respect the boundaries set. Authority can be open to good reasoning, and good questions often earn a real hearing.",
"swords-12|swords-13":"A charging colleague and a measured, skeptical one are working side by side, or you are toggling between the two modes. Let the fast one draft and the careful one review. Agree on who has final say, and you will get speed with accuracy.",
"swords-12|swords-14":"An energetic, blunt driver and a rule-minded authority are in the same room. Channel the speed toward goals that leadership has approved, and let the authority's standards shape the route. Present your proposal crisply, with facts, and be prepared to hear a firm answer.",
"swords-13|swords-14":"A discerning advisor and a decisive leader, which may be two colleagues or two sides of you, make a strong pair. Combine candid analysis with clear authority, and be sure that both are heard. Watch for coldness, and thank people for the work behind the decision.",
"pentacles-01|pentacles-02":"A solid new opening lands on top of tasks you are already juggling. Decide what it replaces before you say yes, because stacking it on the pile will blur everything. Write down your weekly hours, find the honest slot for the new piece, and tell whoever needs to know what you are setting down.",
"pentacles-01|pentacles-03":"A fresh project comes with a team attached. Do the first piece properly and let colleagues see the craft, since early standards set the tone for everything after. Ask who will check your work and who will build on it, then agree how the parts fit before anyone rushes ahead.",
"pentacles-01|pentacles-04":"A genuine opportunity appears, and the habit of guarding what you already have tugs against it. Name what you are protecting, whether a routine, a title or a corner of the office, and ask if it truly needs protecting. Try the new thing in a small contained way first.",
"pentacles-01|pentacles-05":"A way out of a hard patch is closer than it looks. Reach for the one real offer, whether a referral, a training slot or a friendly manager, instead of assuming nobody would want you. Ask a mentor or former colleague a direct question this week, and accept a plain answer.",
"pentacles-01|pentacles-06":"A fresh beginning raises a question of fairness. When a new role, client or project starts, settle who gives what and who is credited or paid for it before the work grows. Share a skill freely where it helps someone, and ask plainly for what is owed to you.",
"pentacles-01|pentacles-07":"A sturdy start needs time before it shows anything. Set one review date a few weeks away and judge the project then, not daily. Between now and then, keep up the ordinary care: answer messages, fix small problems, and jot down what you try so the review has facts.",
"pentacles-01|pentacles-08":"A real opening wants skill, and skill comes from repetition. Pick one task in the new role and do it at the same hour every day until it stops feeling clumsy. Ask for feedback early, since small corrections now will save you a great deal of rework later.",
"pentacles-01|pentacles-09":"Your independence makes this new opening easy to weigh on your own terms. You have habits, skills and steady footing, so you can negotiate from calm instead of need. Check that the role fits the life you have already built, then say yes or no without apologizing.",
"pentacles-01|pentacles-10":"A first step here carries weight for years. Treat the new start as the foundation of something others will lean on, so write down what you do and build it so a colleague could take it over. Ask who this work is for beyond this quarter.",
"pentacles-01|pentacles-11":"A beginner's eagerness has met a real opening. Whether you are the new hire, the apprentice, or the person training one, take it seriously: keep notes, ask the odd question, and finish one small real piece of work. Someone studying beside you may become a steady ally.",
"pentacles-01|pentacles-12":"A solid chance arrives, and the slow reliable route is the right one. Plan the steps, do the dull ones first, and keep your promises to colleagues even when it feels unglamorous. A steady coworker or supplier may be the person who makes this opening hold.",
"pentacles-01|pentacles-13":"A capable, caring figure, perhaps a manager, a mentor or you in that role, opens a door. Practical support counts more than speeches here: look after the people and supplies around the new project and make sure the basics are covered before launch.",
"pentacles-01|pentacles-14":"A seasoned, resourceful person, a boss, an owner or the version of you who runs things, is backing a new start. Bring a clear plan and figures you can explain out loud. Respect the mentor's time, and show you can carry responsibility from the first week.",
"pentacles-02|pentacles-03":"You are busy and collaborative at once. Several tasks are in your hands while a team depends on the quality of each. Pick the piece where your craft shows most, hand off the rest in writing, and check in with one colleague daily so nothing slips between people.",
"pentacles-02|pentacles-04":"Juggling and clutching are pulling in opposite directions. You keep many tasks in the air and refuse to hand any off, which leaves you worn down. Choose the two things only you can do, and delegate or release the rest this week.",
"pentacles-02|pentacles-05":"You are stretched thin and feeling out in the cold. Juggling gets harder when you feel unsupported, so name the gap to someone who can help, such as a supervisor or a mentor. Ask for one concrete piece of relief, like a moved deadline or an extra pair of hands.",
"pentacles-02|pentacles-06":"Balance comes from fair exchange. As you handle competing demands, be clear about what you give and what comes back, in hours, credit and pay. Help a colleague this week and ask for a matching favor, so the load moves around instead of piling up on you.",
"pentacles-02|pentacles-07":"Several tasks are in motion while one long project quietly matures. Keep the daily juggling from crowding out the slow work by blocking a regular hour for the project that pays off later. Step back soon and look at what is actually growing.",
"pentacles-02|pentacles-08":"Busy work plus repetition makes you faster. The juggling is annoying but instructive: choose the routine you repeat every day and tighten it with a checklist or fixed order. In a few weeks the same tasks may take far less of your attention.",
"pentacles-02|pentacles-09":"You are handling many things gracefully and have earned some independence. The balance now is between workload and your own rhythm, so protect one quiet hour to work on your own terms. Notice the skills that keep several tasks steady, and claim credit for them.",
"pentacles-02|pentacles-10":"Short-term juggling sits on top of a long-term structure such as a family business, a company or a career path. Keep the daily tasks in service of the bigger picture. Ask which of today's items will still matter next year, and give those your best attention.",
"pentacles-02|pentacles-11":"Learning to juggle is the job right now. A newcomer, or you in a new role, is keeping a few tasks going while studying the craft. Write a simple weekly plan, ask one experienced colleague how they manage it, and accept that dropping a ball or two is part of learning.",
"pentacles-02|pentacles-12":"Plodding consistency can tame a scattered workload. A dependable coworker, or your own patient side, handles one task after another in order instead of everything at once. Try it this week: pick the top item, finish it, then move on. Steady rhythm beats frantic multitasking.",
"pentacles-02|pentacles-13":"A resourceful, practical person keeps many things moving. It might be a manager looking after a team while wrestling with deadlines, or you in that role. Protect time for your own work, since caring for others can quietly eat your day. Say no to one request that is not yours.",
"pentacles-02|pentacles-14":"An experienced leader is handling many pieces at once. That might be your boss, a business owner, or you when you carry more than your share. Keep priorities visible by listing the three outcomes that define success this month, then delegate the rest with clear instructions.",
"pentacles-03|pentacles-04":"Good team craft is meeting a habit of protecting turf. Notice whether you are holding back information, credit or tools from people who could help the job come out properly. Share one method or template with a colleague this week, and see whether the team's result improves.",
"pentacles-03|pentacles-05":"You have skill, but you may be feeling shut out of the group doing the work. Speak to whoever runs the team and ask how you can contribute to a specific piece. Offer the craft you are good at. Being visibly useful is the quickest way back inside.",
"pentacles-03|pentacles-06":"Good teamwork and a fair flow of credit and reward are both in play. Watch who gives and who receives in your group. Thank the colleague who made your part easier, and speak up if recognition is lopsided. Mentoring, or being mentored, fits this moment.",
"pentacles-03|pentacles-07":"A team project has been underway long enough to judge. Gather the group for an honest review of what is working and what is not worth more effort. Appreciate the craft that went in, and decide together where to put the next few weeks.",
"pentacles-03|pentacles-08":"You are doing serious craft and serious practice. You are refining skills in front of a team, and the quality shows. Keep the daily repetition going, and invite a senior colleague to critique one piece of your work. The aim is to improve, not to impress.",
"pentacles-03|pentacles-09":"Skilled teamwork and personal independence are both on show. You do your part well and need little supervision. Offer to lead a small piece, and also guard time for focused solo work. Your reputation grows when you are both reliable and self-directed.",
"pentacles-03|pentacles-10":"Quality work that will outlast your own shift. A team is building something with a long lifespan, such as a process, a product or a workplace culture. Ask what the next person will need to carry it on, and write it down. Careful craft now saves trouble later.",
"pentacles-03|pentacles-11":"You are a learner among skilled people. You may be the newcomer on a team, or the one training one. Ask for a small real task, do it carefully, then ask for a review. Teams teach best through actual work, so watch how the experienced ones do it.",
"pentacles-03|pentacles-12":"A dependable colleague, or your own plodding side, carries a team project through to the end. No flash, just thorough, correct work delivered on schedule. Do the boring checks, hand the piece over clean, and let your reputation speak for you.",
"pentacles-03|pentacles-13":"A skilled, caring colleague, or you in that role, makes the team actually function. They notice who needs what and sort out practical details without being asked. Support the person keeping things running, and do the same for others. Quality improves when someone looks after the people doing the work.",
"pentacles-03|pentacles-14":"A master of the trade leads a skilled crew. That might be a senior colleague, an owner, or you when you take the lead. Set clear standards and explain them, then let people use their own skill within them. A well-run crew is the result.",
"pentacles-04|pentacles-05":"Fear of scarcity is making you grip what you have, and it is isolating you. Notice where you withhold help, information or trust because resources feel thin. Ask one colleague for support and offer something back. Connection is a resource too, and it costs less to start than you think.",
"pentacles-04|pentacles-06":"You are learning to share after years of holding back. Practice giving time, credit or know-how to a teammate, and let others do the same for you. Fair exchange needs both hands open. Notice whether you keep a tally, and ask yourself if it is actually fair.",
"pentacles-04|pentacles-07":"Careful guarding meets a long wait. You may be hoarding your time and hesitating to move, telling yourself more assessment is needed. Set a date to review your progress, then act on what you find. Protect what truly matters, and let the rest go.",
"pentacles-04|pentacles-08":"Steady routines and careful keeping produce solid work. The danger is that caution hardens into rigidity, and a method becomes a fence. Keep the disciplined practice, but test one new approach this month. Protect your craft and attention without shutting out fresh ideas.",
"pentacles-04|pentacles-09":"You have built security through your own effort, and it shows. The question is whether caution keeps you from sharing it, whether that means expertise, credit or leadership. Pick one way to bring a colleague into your methods this month, and ease your grip on the rest.",
"pentacles-04|pentacles-10":"Protecting an inheritance, whether a role, a family business or a reputation, can slide into clinging. Ask whether the structure you keep still serves the people in it today. Talk with someone younger or newer about what should change, and hold tradition with open hands.",
"pentacles-04|pentacles-11":"A cautious learner is on the scene. You may be protecting your time and effort while starting something new, or training someone who holds back. Try a first real attempt anyway, in a low-risk way. Keep notes, and accept that early mistakes are the price of skill.",
"pentacles-04|pentacles-12":"A cautious, dependable colleague is in the picture. The work is solid and change is slow. If that is you, deliver on your promises and speak up once about what you need. If it is someone near you, know that reliability is their way of saying they care.",
"pentacles-04|pentacles-13":"A resourceful person who looks after people also guards the supplies. Notice whether caring has turned into controlling. Let a teammate take over a task and do it their own way. Good stewardship includes trusting others with what you have built.",
"pentacles-04|pentacles-14":"An established authority is protecting what they have built. A boss, an owner or you may be holding on to control. Ask what could be delegated without harm. Good leadership includes letting others carry weight, and teaching them how to carry it.",
"pentacles-05|pentacles-06":"A hard stretch comes with help within reach. A colleague, a mentor or a program may offer support if you ask. Accept it without shame. When you are steadier, pass the same help along. Fair exchange over time is how teams get through lean periods.",
"pentacles-05|pentacles-07":"Lean times and a slow project overlap. You are not seeing much result yet, and discouragement is natural. Do an honest review of what you have planted: what needs another season, and what should be dropped. Seek one colleague's view on where to focus.",
"pentacles-05|pentacles-08":"When things feel scarce, steady craft is your best answer. Put your attention on one skill you can improve every day, and let the discipline steady you. Seek a mentor's view. Quality of work builds a path back in, slowly and surely.",
"pentacles-05|pentacles-09":"You may be moving from lean to comfortable, or the other way round. Your independence is a real strength, and it may hide how isolated you feel. Keep your steady routines and also ask for company, such as a coworker lunch or a mentor call.",
"pentacles-05|pentacles-10":"You feel excluded from an established group while a well-rooted organization carries on. Ask who can open the door, such as a sponsor or mentor. Show what you can add to the longer story, and be patient with how long trust takes.",
"pentacles-05|pentacles-11":"A beginner is out in the cold. You may be new and feeling shut out, or training someone who feels that way. Give a clear first assignment and a clear way to ask questions. Study helps, and so does one person saying they are glad you are here.",
"pentacles-05|pentacles-12":"A steady, practical person works through a hard stretch without complaint. That may be you, or a colleague near you who deserves notice. Keep doing the next task well, and say once if you need backup. Steadiness does not mean suffering in silence.",
"pentacles-05|pentacles-13":"A resourceful, caring colleague notices who is being left out, and acts. Be that person, or let them find you. Bring someone into the conversation, share a resource, or pass a client along. Good workplaces are made of such small rescues.",
"pentacles-05|pentacles-14":"A person with authority meets someone who is struggling. A boss, an owner, or you in a senior role can change a hard situation simply by noticing it. Offer fair terms and clear support. If you are the one struggling, go to the person with the power to help and ask directly.",
"pentacles-06|pentacles-07":"Fair exchange over the long term is the theme. You have invested effort, credit and help to others, and now you wait to see what comes of it. Weigh honestly what you received against what you gave, and adjust your next commitments from that. There is no need to rush.",
"pentacles-06|pentacles-08":"Skilled work and fair dealing go together. Practice your craft every day, and treat the people around you generously and justly. Offer to teach a junior colleague a technique, and ask for a matching favor. Repetition makes you good, and fairness makes you trusted.",
"pentacles-06|pentacles-09":"Independent success comes with a willingness to share it. You can be generous with knowledge and time because you are secure. Mentor someone, or take a colleague's side in a fair dispute. Keep your own time protected, and let generosity be a choice rather than a duty.",
"pentacles-06|pentacles-10":"Fair sharing in a long-lasting enterprise is the question. Ask whether rewards and responsibilities in your organization are divided in a way that would still feel fair in ten years. Support a colleague who is newer, and accept help from someone who has been there longer.",
"pentacles-06|pentacles-11":"A student receives help and then learns to give it. If you are new, accept guidance gladly and repay it in real effort. If you teach, be fair with your time and credit. A trainee who is treated well often becomes the best colleague.",
"pentacles-06|pentacles-12":"A reliable colleague delivers and shares fairly. Consistent work paired with honest dealings builds trust. Be the person who does what was promised and splits credit correctly. If you are waiting on someone's help, ask plainly for a timeline.",
"pentacles-06|pentacles-13":"A warm, capable person spreads resources well. That could be a manager who looks after the team, or you in that role. Make sure support flows both ways, and that you ask for what you need. Fair treatment is the core of a good team.",
"pentacles-06|pentacles-14":"A leader with means to share, and the fairness to do it well, is in view. A senior person may extend help or set terms. Look at how rewards and responsibilities are divided, and ask clear questions. If you lead, be open about why decisions are made.",
"pentacles-07|pentacles-08":"A long project and a repeated routine sit together. Keep the daily hour of practice going while the larger result matures. Every few weeks, step back to see what has actually grown, and adjust your method. Skill and patience together beat either alone.",
"pentacles-07|pentacles-09":"Patient effort is starting to pay off as independence. Look over what you have cultivated: skills, contacts, reputation. Enjoy it without instantly planning the next big push, and decide which plants deserve another season.",
"pentacles-07|pentacles-10":"Generational thinking applies to a slow-growing project. Judge what you have grown against a horizon of years, not weeks. Decide which part deserves protection and which should be pruned. Write down what you have learned so the next person does not start from zero.",
"pentacles-07|pentacles-11":"A newcomer has planted something and is waiting. The early months involve learning, and results come slowly. Ask for a review date with your manager so you know what is expected, and keep studying between tasks. Patience plus curiosity is the apprentice's best tool.",
"pentacles-07|pentacles-12":"A methodical colleague is on a long haul. Progress is slow and reliable. Keep a record of what you have done, and take the long view in meetings where others want quick wins. Patience is a professional skill, and it is yours.",
"pentacles-07|pentacles-13":"A capable, caring person tends something over time, such as a team, a client relationship or a business. Check what is thriving and what needs more attention. Give the people involved time to grow, and do not hurry results.",
"pentacles-07|pentacles-14":"A veteran reviews the long record. A boss or an owner, or you, looks at what has grown and decides where to put the next effort. Bring evidence of results over time, not hopes. Good leaders prune as well as plant.",
"pentacles-08|pentacles-09":"Years of practice have brought a measure of independence. You have earned the right to work at your own pace in a place you control. Continue the craft, and give yourself a rest without guilt. Skill and comfort are compatible.",
"pentacles-08|pentacles-10":"Craft is turning into legacy. Your daily work is building something others will inherit, such as a method, a reputation or a business. Keep the quality high, and document what you do. Mentor someone so the skill outlives the task.",
"pentacles-08|pentacles-11":"An apprentice and a craftsperson are working side by side. Whether you are learning or teaching, this is the repeated-hour pairing: small tasks done carefully, again and again. Ask for feedback, give it kindly, and accept that improvement is slow. The skill is yours if you keep the routine.",
"pentacles-08|pentacles-12":"Methodical effort, done the same careful way every day, wins out. A colleague or you will finish the work properly while others scramble. Keep your routine, check your results, and accept being unglamorous. Consistent delivery is how you become trusted.",
"pentacles-08|pentacles-13":"A practiced, caring professional knows both the work and the people. You or a colleague keeps things running through skill and attention. Pass on your methods, look after the team's basics, and protect time to refine your own craft.",
"pentacles-08|pentacles-14":"A master of the trade, after many years of practice, leads by example. That may be a boss, a senior colleague, or you as the expert. Teach what you know, keep your standards, and respect the next person's learning curve.",
"pentacles-09|pentacles-10":"Individual success sits inside a larger lasting structure. Enjoy the comfort you have earned, and think about how it connects to the wider organization or family enterprise. Share what you know with the next generation of colleagues.",
"pentacles-09|pentacles-11":"A self-sufficient person has a beginner at their side. You might be teaching someone, or studying under an independent expert. Offer a real task, and let the newcomer make mistakes safely. Pass on the habits that make your way of working comfortable.",
"pentacles-09|pentacles-12":"A reliable worker has built real independence. Colleagues count on the steady output, and you have earned trust and some autonomy. Keep the standards up, and choose what you want to work on next instead of drifting.",
"pentacles-09|pentacles-13":"A self-possessed, resourceful person makes a workplace comfortable. You or a colleague has built reliable skill and a pleasant working environment. Keep caring for the details, and do not let helping others crowd out your own projects.",
"pentacles-09|pentacles-14":"A capable, self-made leader is in view. You or someone near you has earned authority through sustained effort. Lead with calm, credit those who helped, and keep learning. Comfortable leaders stay curious.",
"pentacles-10|pentacles-11":"A newcomer is joining an established organization. If that is you, study the culture, ask questions, and do one small piece of work well. If you are established, welcome the newcomer and explain how things work. Continuity depends on it.",
"pentacles-10|pentacles-12":"A dependable hand works inside a long-lasting structure. Steady, consistent effort maintains what has been built. Be the colleague who keeps the machinery running, and ask that this work be recognized. Stability is a contribution.",
"pentacles-10|pentacles-13":"A caring, capable person holds an established organization together. A manager, a founder's right hand, or you keeps the practical things in order and looks after the people. Make sure the work is acknowledged, and that you have help.",
"pentacles-10|pentacles-14":"An established head of a long-lasting enterprise is in charge. The owner, the senior partner, or you in that role carries responsibility for continuity. Think about succession, training and standards. Good stewardship is about what remains after you.",
"pentacles-11|pentacles-12":"A keen learner and a methodical finisher can carry a project from first question to done. The learner spots what could be improved, and the steady one makes sure it actually ships. Whichever you are, borrow the other's gift: ask one more question, or commit to one more reliable deadline this week.",
"pentacles-11|pentacles-13":"A student of the craft has found someone generous and practical to learn from. The best lessons here are hands-on: how resources are handled, how people are looked after, how a place is kept running. Ask to shadow the person whose work you admire, or offer that chance to someone newer than you.",
"pentacles-11|pentacles-14":"A promising newcomer has caught the eye of someone who has built something lasting. That can become an apprenticeship if you handle it well. Bring a clear proposal and proof of the work you have already done. If you are the established one, give the beginner a real task and an honest review.",
"pentacles-12|pentacles-13":"A steady pair of hands and a resourceful, caring manager make work feel sustainable. Routines hold because someone keeps them, and people stay because someone notices them. Share credit between both roles. If you are the one grinding quietly, let the person who looks after the team know what you actually need.",
"pentacles-12|pentacles-14":"Consistent effort is being noticed by someone who values results over noise. The patient worker earns trust by delivering, and the established leader rewards what is proven. Keep a simple record of what you have finished. If you lead, make sure your most dependable people are the ones who hear thanks first.",
"pentacles-13|pentacles-14":"Practical wisdom and earned authority are sitting at the same table. One side tends the people and the details, the other sets the direction and keeps the structure sound. Decide clearly who owns which calls. If you hold both roles, protect time for each so neither the people nor the plan gets neglected."};
